  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910017" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S4 Maddington Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Stepping Back In Time - Maddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Maddington Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;While you are sitting, what is the TransPerth stop number you are at?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f604a0ca-aea7-429e-9484-722ce27a788d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a seat and look across the road at what's left of Maddington Park.
Maddington Farm was established by John Randell Phillips. In 1833 he sold it to Major William Nairn, who renamed it Maddington Park and built a large two-storey house that was a fine example of colonial architecture. The Harris family owned the property from the 1870s until 1983. Vegetables were always grown at Maddington Park but when electricity was connected in 1927,  production increased as pump fed irrigation could be introduced. Cauliflowers and cabbages were the main winter crops, watermelons and rockmelons in summer. Potatoes were also grown.  From the  1950s  to  1970  the property was producing an average of 180,000 cauliflowers per year. Suburban sprawl began to encroach on the market garden and much of the property was sold in 1978 for the Maddington shopping complex. In 2004 the house was devastated by fire.
Sound bite:
https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_7_maddington.mp3&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9fa098ca-9d15-4a5b-aff4-1176c9630a25" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to a short flashback to the origins of Gosnells and Maddington.
Follow the footsteps of the early settlers and learn about the agricultural beginnings of the Southern River and Canning River Area from the first settlement in 1829.
We recommend walking between the stages to immerse yourself in the history of this place.
Please take note of the bonus information given at each stage, helping you to find the bonus box (GC8ZGT8).
We hope you enjoy this little walk back in time with us.

Throughout this adventure, we have linked sound bites from the City of Gosnells relating to the stations where available. To listen to the sound bites, mark and open the links in each location.

Listen to a brief introduction here: https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_1_intro.mp3  


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7d47871e-b704-4538-972b-ef295efc46f7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;You bonus letter is: O

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
