  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory</url>
  <urlname>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S3 The Southern River by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910015" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S3 The Southern River</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Stepping Back In Time - Maddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Southern River&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the northern end of the board walk you will find a "Riverbank Project" sign. What is the last word written in Noongar language?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5b2b220f-7d12-4cfc-a40b-2988a2e027b6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Southern River is known as the Wungong River in its higher reaches. 
Paperbarks (Melaleuca sp) and Flooded Gums (Eucalyptus rudis) are the most prolific trees in the area. Aboriginal people traditionally used the paperbark leaves for medicinal purposes, including chewing the young leaves to alleviate headaches and other ailments. It was used for cradles, as bandages, sleeping mats, as a building material, for wrapping food and as a disposable raincoat.
Just slightly to the south of Burslem Bridge the Southern River joins the Canning River.
Nyungar people call the Canning River "Djarlgarra". The river provided plentiful food to the Beeliar and Beloo people – fish, eggs, snakes, lizards, tortoises, marron, gilgies and birds were found here.

Sound bite:
https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_4_southern.mp3&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9fa098ca-9d15-4a5b-aff4-1176c9630a25" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to a short flashback to the origins of Gosnells and Maddington.
Follow the footsteps of the early settlers and learn about the agricultural beginnings of the Southern River and Canning River Area from the first settlement in 1829.
We recommend walking between the stages to immerse yourself in the history of this place.
Please take note of the bonus information given at each stage, helping you to find the bonus box (GC8ZGT8).
We hope you enjoy this little walk back in time with us.

Throughout this adventure, we have linked sound bites from the City of Gosnells relating to the stations where available. To listen to the sound bites, mark and open the links in each location.

Listen to a brief introduction here: https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_1_intro.mp3  


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/082d18ec-d960-4fc1-a15f-70ef32be6304" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Your bonus letter is: E&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
