 <wpt lat="-32.06285" lon="115.9798">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgPhV-02</name>
  <desc>SteppingBackIn2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory</url>
  <urlname>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S2 Grave of John and Frances Davis by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910013" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S2 Grave of John and Frances Davis</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Stepping Back In Time - Maddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Grave of John and Frances Davis&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the name of the ship the Davis family arrived on in 1829?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e9c9f89-65be-481d-b583-2c4e206412b6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John Okey Davis was one of the original settlers in the Canning area and was allocated this land in 1829. Davis and his wife Frances and seven children set sail from London and arrived in the Swan River Colony in 1829. 
They had been attracted to advertisements in England for cheap land and were allocated a staggering 7026 acres that covered almost all of what we know today as Gosnells including Huntingdale and Southern River. 

The Davis family named their farm Chenies and planted wheat and vegetables. Life was hard and isolated in their new home. Frances died in 1835 only six years after arriving and John died a year later after a long illness.  Both were buried here on their property. 
The headstone on their grave is a replica of the original which has been stored for preservation. In 1983 their great-grandchildren planted a tree at their gravesite.

Listen to their history here: https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_3_davis.mp3
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9fa098ca-9d15-4a5b-aff4-1176c9630a25" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to a short flashback to the origins of Gosnells and Maddington.
Follow the footsteps of the early settlers and learn about the agricultural beginnings of the Southern River and Canning River Area from the first settlement in 1829.
We recommend walking between the stages to immerse yourself in the history of this place.
Please take note of the bonus information given at each stage, helping you to find the bonus box (GC8ZGT8).
We hope you enjoy this little walk back in time with us.

Throughout this adventure, we have linked sound bites from the City of Gosnells relating to the stations where available. To listen to the sound bites, mark and open the links in each location.

Listen to a brief introduction here: https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_1_intro.mp3  


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fd8951ff-2deb-401a-8b4b-b00bc5b89076" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Your bonus letter is: N&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.063567" lon="115.9816">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgPhV-01</name>
  <desc>SteppingBackIn1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory</url>
  <urlname>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S1 Wilkinson Homestead by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910011" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Stepping Back In Time - Maddington : S1 Wilkinson Homestead</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Stepping Back In Time - Maddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wilkinson Homestead&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MaddingtonHistory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Just inside the Homestead garden, you will see three vehicles. What is the make of the 1st one (on the right)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/42289aad-55e3-46b7-a08e-c1187f71e03b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wilkinson  Homestead  was  built  in  1912  on  the  banks of  the  Canning  River  for  John Wilkinson. John, a tailor from Ballarat, relocated with his wife Emma in 1905.  Their  27  acre  property  produced  large  amounts  of  citrus  fruit,  stone  fruit  and  almonds. Like  all  early  properties  fronting  the  Canning  River,  was  irrigated  from  the  river.  Their property is typical of the period with a high pitched roof and sweeping verandah.  Today  the  Wilkinson's  property  offers  a  chance  to  step  back  in  time  and  discover  the history  of  the  area.  The  museum  features  a  collection  of  textiles,  agricultural  machinery, household  items,  furniture,  photographs  and  a  1927 Essex  motor  vehicle.  The  shed  is made  from  material  salvaged  from  the  Maddington  Centenary  Hall,  the  1929  Gosnells Road  Board  building  and  the  Orange  Grove  brickworks.  

https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_2_wilkinson.mp3 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9fa098ca-9d15-4a5b-aff4-1176c9630a25" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to a short flashback to the origins of Gosnells and Maddington.
Follow the footsteps of the early settlers and learn about the agricultural beginnings of the Southern River and Canning River Area from the first settlement in 1829.
We recommend walking between the stages to immerse yourself in the history of this place.
Please take note of the bonus information given at each stage, helping you to find the bonus box (GC8ZGT8).
We hope you enjoy this little walk back in time with us.

Throughout this adventure, we have linked sound bites from the City of Gosnells relating to the stations where available. To listen to the sound bites, mark and open the links in each location.

Listen to a brief introduction here: https://www.gosnells.wa.gov.au/files/assets/public/audio_files/heritage/walk_trail/part_1_intro.mp3  


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0ac499c2-9289-4c5d-92b6-0d75e2c9ffed" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Your bonus letter is:  M&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.027167" lon="115.942383">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdp7y-02</name>
  <desc>PlacesOfIntere2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi</url>
  <urlname>Places of interest - City of Canning : S2 Woodloes Homestead by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-152" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Places of interest - City of Canning : S2 Woodloes Homestead</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Places of interest - City of Canning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Woodloes Homestead&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the red object that you can see in the gardens?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0106fe5f-1d1a-4333-bd6e-e48f77af793e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally the home of Francis and Maude Bird built in the early 1870s. Bird was an architect and a partner of the Mason Bird Timber Company with access to excellent timbers; some of which can be seen in the door surrounds within the house.  The house has an interesting history having been through many owners until acquired by the Town of Canning for restoration and opened as a local museum in 1978.  At the rear of Woodloes is a reproduction of the Congregational Church with the Chimney which stood on Albany Highway until 1988 when it was demolished due to the widening of the highway.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed637c44-87e6-4cba-8944-4e209837ba79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to our Adventure Lab which will take you on a journey to some places of interest around the Canning River.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0255" lon="115.9384">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdp7y-03</name>
  <desc>PlacesOfIntere3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi</url>
  <urlname>Places of interest - City of Canning : S3 Mason's Landing by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-153" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Places of interest - City of Canning : S3 Mason's Landing</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Places of interest - City of Canning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mason's Landing&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt; Where was the timber felled and drawn from? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8b92e8df-caa6-40d1-bb51-555c64634c65" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1864 Ben Mason obtained a permit to cut timber in the Darling Ranges and within two years was employing 100 men, both at the mill site in Carmel and at the Landing. By 1869 a steam-powered sawmill was established by the riverside to process the timber before it was stacked onto barges and taken down the river to Perth and Fremantle. Francis Bird joined him as a partner in 1871. A wooden railway (later capped with iron) joined the mill in the hills with the landing. Timber wagons were hauled by horses and the route was approximately along that now occupied by Bickley Road. Masonmill Road is in Carmel and sawpits were still in the area when a rose nursery was established on the hills mill site after WWII.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed637c44-87e6-4cba-8944-4e209837ba79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to our Adventure Lab which will take you on a journey to some places of interest around the Canning River.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.071033" lon="115.99965">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMybc-04</name>
  <desc>PTAPublicArtAr4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA</url>
  <urlname>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S4 Gosnells Station by Granett</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-149" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S4 Gosnells Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Granett</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Granett</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;PTA Public Art (Armadale Line)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gosnells Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many trees in the top row?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bf8b622b-4374-4272-9210-5aafd146292f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The train station … in between

Landscape scenes of trees and houses sculptured from galvanised steel have been incorporated into the station fences. Outside the station two bench seats have been adorned with the same theme, you'll need to inspect the one on the Lissiman st side.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/791572a5-4bd6-47e9-924f-496371c4f927" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Transport Authority Public Art

While the PTA is predominately about moving people from one place to another, they have commissioned some spectacular pieces of art which can be contemplated while you wait for your train to arrive.

This Adventure Lab takes you to some examples along the Armadale line. The geofences are fairly generous and you might be able do some stages while on the train. However a closer inspection of these works is highly recommended.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.019967" lon="115.927117">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpd8h-05</name>
  <desc>CanningCivicCe5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC</url>
  <urlname>Canning Civic Centre  : S5 Riverside Observation Deck by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000335" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Canning Civic Centre  : S5 Riverside Observation Deck</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Canning Civic Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Riverside Observation Deck&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the number plate attached to this boardwalk and use these numbers as your answer.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d304103d-ebc9-4eb6-b301-22a2d6902d3d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This small observation deck provides an excellent vantage point to view the flow of the Canning River. Some of the abundant bird life of this area may be on show, and if not, you could take a seat and enjoy the relaxing and peaceful setting. This location  has been included in this adventure lab to emphasize the significance of the river in the City of Canning and the current redevelopment the City Centre on the theme of the 'Riverine City".&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e752fde-7f3a-4e23-9641-68ba854fbcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you for a pleasant stroll in and around around the Canning City Civic area  which showcases a variety of interesting and unique cultural and natural features within a well planned and beautifully presented garden and wetland setting.  All locations are pedestrian and wheelchair accessible and can be completed in any order.

There is or will be an optional bonus mystery cache for you to find.  You will need to use the clues provided in the journal at each stage to work out the five key words required to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates. Good luck and have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0d260bda-b53a-4a47-93ab-fd1e70d7cabc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Too easy! Well done!
Your fifth and last word: As quiet as a ........?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.017333" lon="115.929817">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpd8h-03</name>
  <desc>CanningCivicCe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC</url>
  <urlname>Canning Civic Centre  : S3 Ouro by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000331" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Canning Civic Centre  : S3 Ouro</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Canning Civic Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Ouro&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The surname of the author of the poem at the base of Ouro is?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7d7c52cd-042f-4553-970d-65130e1bcc19" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You are standing before a nature themed sculpture called "Ouro".This work was created by Lorenna Grant  and reflects a perspective and understanding of nature as a 'cyclic' form. Lorenna took her inspiration, in part, from the constructed wetlands surrounding the administration building as something of a 'return' to what was at the site in the past, a naturally occurring wetland. 
The spiral is an ancient symbol that speaks of the life, death and rebirth cycle. This symbol is found on every inhabited continent and is used in Aboriginal art as a symbol of flowing water and the Ouroboros or snake eating its own tail (representing the same events occurring over and over- cyclically)
The poetry embedded in the base of the work speaks of a return to the beginning.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e752fde-7f3a-4e23-9641-68ba854fbcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you for a pleasant stroll in and around around the Canning City Civic area  which showcases a variety of interesting and unique cultural and natural features within a well planned and beautifully presented garden and wetland setting.  All locations are pedestrian and wheelchair accessible and can be completed in any order.

There is or will be an optional bonus mystery cache for you to find.  You will need to use the clues provided in the journal at each stage to work out the five key words required to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates. Good luck and have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/48162041-e190-4354-9a86-a1891289c988" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Correct! The second puzzle word completes this phrase: As blind as a......?

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.017667" lon="115.929033">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpd8h-04</name>
  <desc>CanningCivicCe4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC</url>
  <urlname>Canning Civic Centre  : S4 Canning ANZAC Centenary War Memorial by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000333" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Canning Civic Centre  : S4 Canning ANZAC Centenary War Memorial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Canning Civic Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Canning ANZAC Centenary War Memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This memorial was officially opened in what year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea3ab776-d4a7-4b5f-816e-ded9d732e967" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This site is now considered to be the City's main point of commemoration, hosting Anzac Day and Remembrance Day services within the City of Canning. The original War Memorial was situated in front of the Town Hall building on Albany Highway but vandalism caused it to be moved to Memorial Park at the corner of Albany Highway and Manning Road.  As this existing memorial is squeezed onto a small parcel of land between two very busy roads and with the number of attendees at Anzac Day increasing, major events now take place in this safer and more expansive location. 
The memorial fountain, made from sandblasted granite, is the work of Susanna Castleden. The Memorial itself consists of four elements: The Memorial Path, the Sites of Conflict, Ataturk's Tribute and the Wish for Peace. Further details of these elements can be found on the plaque to the right of the fountain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e752fde-7f3a-4e23-9641-68ba854fbcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you for a pleasant stroll in and around around the Canning City Civic area  which showcases a variety of interesting and unique cultural and natural features within a well planned and beautifully presented garden and wetland setting.  All locations are pedestrian and wheelchair accessible and can be completed in any order.

There is or will be an optional bonus mystery cache for you to find.  You will need to use the clues provided in the journal at each stage to work out the five key words required to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates. Good luck and have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b79de91-4cf2-4167-a670-8b77986a77fa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;You nailed it!
The  fourth word: As graceful as a .......?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.016383" lon="115.929867">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpd8h-02</name>
  <desc>CanningCivicCe2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC</url>
  <urlname>Canning Civic Centre  : S2 Leaf Sculpture by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000329" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Canning Civic Centre  : S2 Leaf Sculpture</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Canning Civic Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Leaf Sculpture&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many tapering metal bars crisscross the base of this sculpture?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f08180f4-c3b1-4994-8eb3-8b0fe2e6fd3e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
You are standing in front of the city's focal sculpture.  This interesting piece of art was created by Jahne Rees  whose brief was to reflect aspects of the the general culture and heritage of the City of Canning and the specific site location.  Jahne chose to explore the themes that connected the historical use of the site (as a garden nursery) and the current and ongoing role of the City in the life of the community. The work is intended to evoke a range of interpretations including the fertile earth nurturing the seed and a sense of opening and striving upward in a relationship of mutual support and growth.  
The City has had to make some "adjustments" to the base of the work to deter local skateboarders whose 'community use' of the sculpture was causing considerable damage.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e752fde-7f3a-4e23-9641-68ba854fbcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you for a pleasant stroll in and around around the Canning City Civic area  which showcases a variety of interesting and unique cultural and natural features within a well planned and beautifully presented garden and wetland setting.  All locations are pedestrian and wheelchair accessible and can be completed in any order.

There is or will be an optional bonus mystery cache for you to find.  You will need to use the clues provided in the journal at each stage to work out the five key words required to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates. Good luck and have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9855ec7d-4d3b-4bd8-8006-1f9c27cbe8c6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;That's it!  Well done! 
If you wish to solve the puzzle, the first word of the answer completes the following phrase:  As brave as a......?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.016017" lon="115.929967">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpd8h-01</name>
  <desc>CanningCivicCe1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC</url>
  <urlname>Canning Civic Centre  : S1 Town Hall  and Fence Railings by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000327" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Canning Civic Centre  : S1 Town Hall  and Fence Railings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Canning Civic Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Town Hall  and Fence Railings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CCC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was the Town Hall built?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f70e1c56-3914-43d0-8bff-51eca0dcccee" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Canning Town Hall is a heritage listed building that has undergone extensive renovation and restoration  and has been used in the past not only as a centre of local government but also as a dance hall, movie theatre, youth centre and offices for a Seniors' Centre. The railing in front of the building is the work of Peter Zappa whose brief was to develop a railing to replace the existing functional but plain steel post railing that was installed in the 1980's. This artwork celebrates continuous movement and change but also the steadfast qualities of local government. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e752fde-7f3a-4e23-9641-68ba854fbcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you for a pleasant stroll in and around around the Canning City Civic area  which showcases a variety of interesting and unique cultural and natural features within a well planned and beautifully presented garden and wetland setting.  All locations are pedestrian and wheelchair accessible and can be completed in any order.

There is or will be an optional bonus mystery cache for you to find.  You will need to use the clues provided in the journal at each stage to work out the five key words required to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates. Good luck and have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca895a39-7dbf-4e39-9203-160aa710edf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;That's it. Your third word is: Gentle as a.......?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.00735" lon="115.938767">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMybc-02</name>
  <desc>PTAPublicArtAr2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA</url>
  <urlname>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S2 Queens Park Station by Granett</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-147" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S2 Queens Park Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Granett</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Granett</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;PTA Public Art (Armadale Line)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Queens Park Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many crows in the 'tree'?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/128fce39-5ef0-4cd0-bb18-3705b39e14df" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As the Crow Flies

A stylised tree of colourful crows decorated with designs translated from artwork created by Saint Joseph Primary School students. The artwork also doubles as a bench with an engaging perforated pattern of crows in flight which cast differing shadows depending on the time of day. The inspiration comes from the historic phrase used by travellers and people giving directions to describe the shortest route between two points travelling in a direct line.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/791572a5-4bd6-47e9-924f-496371c4f927" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Transport Authority Public Art

While the PTA is predominately about moving people from one place to another, they have commissioned some spectacular pieces of art which can be contemplated while you wait for your train to arrive.

This Adventure Lab takes you to some examples along the Armadale line. The geofences are fairly generous and you might be able do some stages while on the train. However a closer inspection of these works is highly recommended.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.034117" lon="115.910633">
