 <wpt lat="-32.02985" lon="116.038833">
  <ele>98.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKVRs-01</name>
  <desc>MasonAndTheBir1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird</url>
  <urlname>The Mason and the Bird : S1 Are we there yet ?!? by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909945" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Mason and the Bird : S1 Are we there yet ?!?</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Mason and the Bird&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Are we there yet ?!?&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How far will you have to walk to reach the Mason Bird Tramway Bridge (no spaces, including units)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3f481058-31c1-4e46-bf09-641e45a1c277" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the 1850’s and 60’s the Perth region was growing fast, and the demand for timber was high. Clever businessman Benjamin Mason saw an opportunity. First, he harvested timber along the Canning River and established a steam-driven mill at Mason’s landing in Cannington. Soon the Cannington area was cleared and Mason obtained a licence to harvest timber near Bickley Brook, 15km from the mill. In 1864 Mason built a second timber mill in what is today the suburb of Carmel (Mason’s Mill). In 1870 architect Francis Bird joined the operation and led the construction of a timber tramway to transport timber from Mason’s Mill to Mason’s landing on horse-drawn trolleys. From where you stand, the tramway followed down Hardinge Rd, then Bickley Rd all the way to Cannington.
Walking uphill towards the location of where the mill used to be, this walk will take you on a little historic adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/438b35a2-abe2-41b5-b41e-a4336c444901" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This lab cache invites you to follow the “Mason and Bird Heritage Trail”, following the first 4 km of what used to be the original timber tramway, built in 1870 by the Mason and Bird Timber company to transport timber downhill to Mason's landing in Cannington. 
The one-way trail has a length of about 4.5 km, 9 km return, this will take you about 2 hours one way (4 hour return). We recommend parking at the Hardinge Park (observe gate opening times). 
Wear appropriate shoe wear and carry sufficient water!
Locations 1 to 3 follow a well-prepared path. Between location 3 and 4 you will have to climb a hill and the path is covered in loose rocks. If you prefer not to walk that section, you have the option to park at the end of the trail and complete location 4 and 5 from the other side. 
Mobile reception can be a little patchy throughout the trail, so we have increased the geofence to make sure you receive all data you need. 
Where appropriate, we state exact coordinates to find the required information.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/084d1424-ee9a-4715-9709-3bfb714246bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.032183" lon="116.04905">
  <ele>122.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKVRs-02</name>
  <desc>MasonAndTheBir2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird</url>
  <urlname>The Mason and the Bird : S2 Boy's Brigade Camp (or what's left of it) by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909947" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Mason and the Bird : S2 Boy's Brigade Camp (or what's left of it)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Mason and the Bird&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Boy's Brigade Camp (or what's left of it)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The blue sign refers to a marker on the ground. What are the 2 letters and 3 numbers on that marker (no spaces)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27a89d88-a15e-40ca-b821-cfd1ecc74e06" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Along the path you will spot a wooden sign of the Mason and Bird Heritage Trail #3.

If you are interested, further down the hill (S32 01.922 E 116 02.934) you will find remains of a foundation.
The foundations here are thought to have supported a brick building that was used by the Boy's Brigade which was active here in the 1960's. 

To answer the question you will find a blue sign on a tree stump to your left (S32 01.931 E116 02.943). &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/438b35a2-abe2-41b5-b41e-a4336c444901" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This lab cache invites you to follow the “Mason and Bird Heritage Trail”, following the first 4 km of what used to be the original timber tramway, built in 1870 by the Mason and Bird Timber company to transport timber downhill to Mason's landing in Cannington. 
The one-way trail has a length of about 4.5 km, 9 km return, this will take you about 2 hours one way (4 hour return). We recommend parking at the Hardinge Park (observe gate opening times). 
Wear appropriate shoe wear and carry sufficient water!
Locations 1 to 3 follow a well-prepared path. Between location 3 and 4 you will have to climb a hill and the path is covered in loose rocks. If you prefer not to walk that section, you have the option to park at the end of the trail and complete location 4 and 5 from the other side. 
Mobile reception can be a little patchy throughout the trail, so we have increased the geofence to make sure you receive all data you need. 
Where appropriate, we state exact coordinates to find the required information.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.024783" lon="115.8846">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdp7y-05</name>
  <desc>PlacesOfIntere5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi</url>
  <urlname>Places of interest - City of Canning : S5 Convict Fence by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-155" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Places of interest - City of Canning : S5 Convict Fence</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Places of interest - City of Canning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Convict Fence&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/poi&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Convict Fence granted permanent state government protection?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f72b4140-95d2-495d-bc9a-91f37dc04a36" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located between Salter Point and Shelley Bridge the convict fence was originally constructed in 1866, during the convict era of W.A. (1850 - 1868) and the remnants of the fence are clearly visible.  It was built to keep a channel open for timber barges from the Mason Bird Company to travel up and down the river.  It is known that convicts, standing waist deep in the water used shovels to keep the channel free of mud, throwing the mud over the fence.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed637c44-87e6-4cba-8944-4e209837ba79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to our Adventure Lab which will take you on a journey to some places of interest around the Canning River.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.032133" lon="116.052383">
  <ele>122.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKVRs-03</name>
  <desc>MasonAndTheBir3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird</url>
  <urlname>The Mason and the Bird : S3 Building a bridge by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909949" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Mason and the Bird : S3 Building a bridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Mason and the Bird&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Building a bridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Until what year was the bridge used for motorised traffic?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4f9b2695-de46-434b-9298-36803718e2f7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;To the right of your path, you will spot the possibly oldest surviving wooden bridge in Australia.
Built by convicts in 1871/72 it formed part of the Mason and Bird Timber Tramway. The Jarrah decking has been replaced but the piles below are thought to be original Wandoo which supported the Jarrah rails and sleepers over the Munday Brook. Remains of the convict-built embankment and roadway leading to the bridge are still visible.

Find the larger plaque at the Western end of the bridge (big boulder).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/438b35a2-abe2-41b5-b41e-a4336c444901" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This lab cache invites you to follow the “Mason and Bird Heritage Trail”, following the first 4 km of what used to be the original timber tramway, built in 1870 by the Mason and Bird Timber company to transport timber downhill to Mason's landing in Cannington. 
The one-way trail has a length of about 4.5 km, 9 km return, this will take you about 2 hours one way (4 hour return). We recommend parking at the Hardinge Park (observe gate opening times). 
Wear appropriate shoe wear and carry sufficient water!
Locations 1 to 3 follow a well-prepared path. Between location 3 and 4 you will have to climb a hill and the path is covered in loose rocks. If you prefer not to walk that section, you have the option to park at the end of the trail and complete location 4 and 5 from the other side. 
Mobile reception can be a little patchy throughout the trail, so we have increased the geofence to make sure you receive all data you need. 
Where appropriate, we state exact coordinates to find the required information.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Turn around here if you do not want to climb the hill straight ahead of you (slightly left). Otherwise, continue straight (up the hill).&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.02945" lon="116.0638">
