 <wpt lat="-32.0496" lon="115.8587">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBinUo-05</name>
  <desc>DefenceOfPerth5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence</url>
  <urlname>The Defence of Perth : S5 Aviation Heritage Museum by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-362" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Defence of Perth : S5 Aviation Heritage Museum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Defence of Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Aviation Heritage Museum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the alpha-numeric code on the stripe near the tail? (2x letters and 3x numbers)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b8797040-f2bd-494a-ba4f-0abf5d178fa2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Aviation Heritage Museum of Western Australia is owned and operated by the Royal Australian Air Force Association Western Australian Division (RAAFA) and is housed in the Association’s Memorial Estate in the suburb of Bull Creek, approximately 15km south of the Perth CBD.

Despite being owned and operated by an ex-service organisation, the museum’s focus is not restricted to military aviation and the collection features a lot of exhibits related to civil aviation in Western Australia. Most of these items which include uniforms, insignia, aircraft are housed in the South Wing, which also includes their First World War collection. The North Wing is home to the larger aircraft in the collection and has a greater emphasis on the Royal Australian Air Force and its operations during peace and war. 

In addition to the two display hangars the museum also has a separate library, photo archive, model aeroplane club-room and of course, a gift shop.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58814ab7-66e9-4885-b1d4-1399d838349f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has some significant military locations, which have played an important role in the country’s wartime history. There are many military museums from Albany in the South, Broome in the North, and Kalgoorlie in the East. 

This series looks at some of the local military sites in the Perth area, which includes World War II Tunnels, local Telecommunications, Spitfires, Tanks and Artillery. 

Most museums have restricted opening hours, however the WWII Tunnels and tours of HMAS Ovens are temporarily suspended due to Covid-19. 

Important: 
The WWII Tunnels location in Mosman Park will take some physical effort to reach the site, however there is a path for wheelchairs and people with walking aides. Please follow the Directional Signs from the Boundary Road Carpark to the Heritage Site - approximately 350 metres.




https://scoop.com.au/military-museums-to-visit-across-wa


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/54741995-0ed8-48df-9a32-6af3f838762e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations! 
If you have time, check out the Museum which includes the Avro Lancaster Mk VII pictured and is found in the North Wing.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.971917" lon="115.902483">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMybc-01</name>
  <desc>PTAPublicArtAr1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA</url>
  <urlname>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S1 Victoria Park Station by Granett</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-146" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S1 Victoria Park Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Granett</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Granett</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;PTA Public Art (Armadale Line)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Victoria Park Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many bells are there?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/52aefaa6-2d93-4ff6-a30d-8e0272102a96" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mute and Bright

This work consists of two major objects, a large self-patinating steel speaker-bell and a smaller, similarly-shaped speaker in stainless steel. The two objects are linked by an interactive audio program developed by visually-impaired sound artist Rob Muir, referencing the important link the station has with patrons of the nearby Association for the Blind.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/791572a5-4bd6-47e9-924f-496371c4f927" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Transport Authority Public Art

While the PTA is predominately about moving people from one place to another, they have commissioned some spectacular pieces of art which can be contemplated while you wait for your train to arrive.

This Adventure Lab takes you to some examples along the Armadale line. The geofences are fairly generous and you might be able do some stages while on the train. However a closer inspection of these works is highly recommended.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.02795" lon="116.0749">
  <ele>238.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKVRs-05</name>
  <desc>MasonAndTheBir5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird</url>
  <urlname>The Mason and the Bird : S5 Mason's Mill by LAXA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909953" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Mason and the Bird : S5 Mason's Mill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LAXA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LAXA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Mason and the Bird&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mason's Mill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/MasonAndBird&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the 3 big numbers on top of the valve?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/96d571b4-a339-4861-ae38-1c532021905d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this end of the track, straight ahead, is the site believed to be the location of Mason's Mill. It is now a privately owned orchard.
At the hight of the Mason and Bird Timber Company, 138 men worked here, utilising 40 horses and 80 bullocks, producing up to 20,000 feet of timber a day. 
Up to 400 people lived in the community which formed around the mill and included shops, a school and a sports ground.

Go to S32 01.677 E116 04.494 (water valve)


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/438b35a2-abe2-41b5-b41e-a4336c444901" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This lab cache invites you to follow the “Mason and Bird Heritage Trail”, following the first 4 km of what used to be the original timber tramway, built in 1870 by the Mason and Bird Timber company to transport timber downhill to Mason's landing in Cannington. 
The one-way trail has a length of about 4.5 km, 9 km return, this will take you about 2 hours one way (4 hour return). We recommend parking at the Hardinge Park (observe gate opening times). 
Wear appropriate shoe wear and carry sufficient water!
Locations 1 to 3 follow a well-prepared path. Between location 3 and 4 you will have to climb a hill and the path is covered in loose rocks. If you prefer not to walk that section, you have the option to park at the end of the trail and complete location 4 and 5 from the other side. 
Mobile reception can be a little patchy throughout the trail, so we have increased the geofence to make sure you receive all data you need. 
Where appropriate, we state exact coordinates to find the required information.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/75adeb5c-c863-49bc-bbe5-9589e3229a7a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.967783" lon="115.886667">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsno8-01</name>
  <desc>PerthWaterSout1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside</url>
  <urlname>Perth Water – Southside : S1 Tram Stop by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000437" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Water – Southside : S1 Tram Stop</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Water – Southside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tram Stop&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the two names of the Mayor at the time?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9d2709ac-92ad-49ee-ac40-134298cfb0c8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Perth’s first electric tram network was opened in 1899. At its maximum extent in the 1930s the network consisted of over 50 km of track and operated along 35 different routes.

Lines were built all over the inner suburbs of Perth, including to:
o	Victoria Park (via the Causeway and Albany Hwy)
o	South Perth Zoo (via the Causeway and Angelo St)

This site celebrates the nearby tram stop.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6104c3a2-b08e-4acc-82d8-d3a248650aec" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you by foot or bike (or other non-motorised transport) along what is surely one of the nicest paths in Perth – the southern shores of “Perth Water”.

The tour is best done by walking or cycling, although it is possible to drive from site to site even though that would be defeating the purpose. You can start at either end, or wherever you choose. This Adventure Lab does not require you to visit the sites in any ordered sequence.

The pathways along the foreshore provide outstanding views across Perth Water to the city. Along the way you’ll be able to learn a bit about various activities and land uses that occurred on and near the South Perth foreshore over the years.

Please note that some of the paths are for cyclists only, and some are for pedestrians only. The length of the tour is about 4.7km (if walking) from the Causeway bridge to the Narrows (one way) and a little bit more if cycling. There are plenty of opportunities for rest and refreshments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations !!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.993717" lon="115.858317">
