</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9639" lon="115.88115">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-10</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander21</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S10 Causeway by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910121" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S10 Causeway</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Causeway&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the left side of the informative sign as you face it what is year at the beginning of the 6th line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/41ff05f6-0fbe-4071-a1bb-adea676a6253" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Following the settlement of the Swan River Colony in 1829, the Swan River was the main transportation link between Perth and the port of Fremantle. Land transportation was difficult as the only river crossing near Perth, a ford at the eastern end of the town, was often impassable for wagons and carts. The next crossing point was 20 miles (32 km) upstream at Guildford, a major detour.

The first causeway was opened in 1843 and was originally a toll road.  (Perhaps the only one ever to be in WA.)

The second causeway was opened in 1867 after severe flooding damaged the original causeway.

The third and current causeway was built between 1947 and 1952 consisting of 2 bridges meeting at Heirisson Island after the Heirisson Islands were turned into a single island. The bridges were the "first truly modern bridges" built in Perth after World War II, being the first bridges in Western Australia to use steel composite construction, and only the second (and third) in Australia.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.153183" lon="116.013067">
  <ele>57.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBxh8r-03</name>
  <desc>EotLS3ArmadaleS</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL</url>
  <urlname>End of the Line : S3 Armadale Station by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909855" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>End of the Line : S3 Armadale Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;End of the Line&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Armadale Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many circular poles support the platform roof? ( _ _ )&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9b48ab0e-9838-4b4a-9aa7-ed06d341add7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Armadale Station is the final stop of the Perth Rail Network along the Armadale  Line.

It is located 30.0km from Perth. The original station was opened May 2nd 1893, the current station was opened on November 6th 2004&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b725a41a-cce1-431d-909c-cab48dd07c51" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Please be aware the locations for this Adventure Lab are quite a distance from the map marker, make sure you check your nearest Lab point before commencing.

The metropolitan Perth rail network is comprised of five major lines connecting the four Metropolitan Development Corridors and Fremantle to the Perth CBD.

Over the coming years the network is set to expand even further to accomade travelers from Ellenbrook, Forrestfield and extending lines past Armadale to Byford and continuing north to Yanchep.

This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the various lines of the Perth Rail Network. You are more than welcome to catch the trains to the stops (it can be done in a single day) if you wish but this is not a requirement, in fact all information can be gained and answered without entering the stations themselves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4faefbff-4a20-445b-9659-5468092739ad" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The clue you need for the bonus final is:
 E&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.974717" lon="115.86505">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsno8-03</name>
  <desc>PerthWaterSout3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside</url>
  <urlname>Perth Water – Southside : S3 Coode St Jetty by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000441" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Water – Southside : S3 Coode St Jetty</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Water – Southside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Coode St Jetty&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the date on the top photo?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/12d58022-254d-43ab-b382-adf457014b14" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Before construction of the Coode Street Jetty in 1896, the Coode Street foreshore was used as a landing point for local South Perth residents.

The first ferry service to operate from Coode Street was managed by W. F. Tubbs however it was very irregular. This service was replaced in 1898 by local residents Rowland Pennington and Fred Bailey, who formed a public company, the River Ferry Company, in hopes to bring some regularity to the service. The company had two sailing boats in action, the Mary Queen and the Gladys, however the venture was a failure.

In 1904, Jack Olsen and Claes (Harry) Sutton developed a thriving ferry business on the Swan River, including regular ferries to Coode Street.The Sutton and Olsen families continued to run the service until 1935 when they sold the business to the Swan River Ferries Company.

The private ferry service was eventually merged into the State Transport system and the jetty was rebuilt in 1990.

(Source: https://southperthlocalhistory.wordpress.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6104c3a2-b08e-4acc-82d8-d3a248650aec" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you by foot or bike (or other non-motorised transport) along what is surely one of the nicest paths in Perth – the southern shores of “Perth Water”.

The tour is best done by walking or cycling, although it is possible to drive from site to site even though that would be defeating the purpose. You can start at either end, or wherever you choose. This Adventure Lab does not require you to visit the sites in any ordered sequence.

The pathways along the foreshore provide outstanding views across Perth Water to the city. Along the way you’ll be able to learn a bit about various activities and land uses that occurred on and near the South Perth foreshore over the years.

Please note that some of the paths are for cyclists only, and some are for pedestrians only. The length of the tour is about 4.7km (if walking) from the Causeway bridge to the Narrows (one way) and a little bit more if cycling. There are plenty of opportunities for rest and refreshments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations !!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.153417" lon="116.013467">
  <ele>59.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMybc-05</name>
  <desc>PTAPublicArtAr5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA</url>
  <urlname>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S5 Armadale Station by Granett</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-150" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>PTA Public Art (Armadale Line) : S5 Armadale Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Granett</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Granett</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;PTA Public Art (Armadale Line)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Armadale Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PTA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many poles make up the artwork?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/522a7e2f-cd9b-487e-9a77-5e25eead025a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Signal Grove

Five tall, coloured sculptures stand outside the main entrance to the station in a piazza area with old wooden railway sleeper platforms. The works were designed to provide a sense of identity that reflect the nature of the site, its locality and history. This is not done in an overtly narrative way but incorporates symbols and images that refer to, rather than illustrate the ideas. The references are to forests and forestry, rail history and related artefacts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/791572a5-4bd6-47e9-924f-496371c4f927" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Transport Authority Public Art

While the PTA is predominately about moving people from one place to another, they have commissioned some spectacular pieces of art which can be contemplated while you wait for your train to arrive.

This Adventure Lab takes you to some examples along the Armadale line. The geofences are fairly generous and you might be able do some stages while on the train. However a closer inspection of these works is highly recommended.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
