 <wpt lat="-31.959017" lon="115.878483">
  <ele>39.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-02</name>
  <desc>RedCatS02WestAu</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S02 West Australian Cricket Association - WACA by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910111" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S02 West Australian Cricket Association - WACA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;West Australian Cricket Association - WACA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the last three digits of the light pole's number opposite the museum entrance?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/af40d630-4c51-43f9-a051-c8d73ea05107" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Red Cat Stop 1 - Nelson Cr 

The WACA ground was officially opened in 1983, it occupies a site of old swamp land. It is referred to as Western Australia's "home of cricket".

The pitch at the WACA is regarded as the quickest and bounciest in the world. Interestingly four of the nine fastest Test centuries have been scored at the WACA.
In 1932, Donald Bradman played at the ground for the first time and attracted a crowd in excess of 20,000

There is a fantastic museum at the WACA and tours of the grounds are conducted Monday to Friday 10am and 1pm – Duration 90 minutes&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;I recommend walking to the 'Queens garden' location from here.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.959217" lon="115.8764">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-03</name>
  <desc>RedCatS03Queens</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S03 Queens gardens by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910113" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S03 Queens gardens</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Queens gardens&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the bench directly behind Peter Pan is a park bench, what is the first name mentioned?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/effed476-d7c7-4a82-8a47-ab2fc478e844" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Red Cat stop 29 - Queens Gardens.

Queens Gardens is a beautiful park, i have always noticed a strange calming effect when I have visited, it makes me feel like sitting down and forgetting about what I was doing and taking it all in. It seems to have the same effect on the other people I have noticed in the park. 

The history of the site is quite interesting, it is associated with the development of the City of Perth, but was originally used for recreation purposes including horse racing. Later on it was used as a clay mine and brickwork. Bricks that were produced here are part of some of Perth’s most prominent buildings, these include The Perth Town Hall, Barracks Arch and the Cloisters Building. The water lily filled lakes are the old clay pits.

The park features a replica of the Peter Pan statue located in Kensington Gardens in London. There were only six replicas of the statue made and relocated to various parts of the world.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here hop onto the Red cat at stop 29 - Queens Gardens. The next location is the Perth Mint.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.956717" lon="115.87845">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-09</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander29</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S09 East Perth Cemetery by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915767" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S09 East Perth Cemetery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;East Perth Cemetery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last word on this 7th line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e87265e0-9a15-411c-bb57-b07d0ac71631" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From the establishment of the first cemetery in 1829 to the closure of the seven cemeteries in 1899 almost all of the people who died in Perth were buried here.

More than 90% of the grave headstones and markers were lost through decay, neglect or wanton destruction since then. Only 800 remain. The headstones tell stories of bravery, tragedy, illness and accident; of success and suffering.

In the middle of the Cemeteries stands a simple Gothic chapel designed by colonial architect, Richard Roach Jewell. Built in 1871 as a mortuary chapel, St Bartholomew’s became a parish church in 1888. After a period of neglect, it is still a consecrated church.

Originally located on the edge of the town, the Cemeteries today are a tranquil haven within bustling inner-city East Perth. They are open on Sundays between 2pm and 4pm.

Find the entrance sign to the right of the main entrance to the Cemetery and Church.  Counting the title lines find the 7th line on the sign.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.030883" lon="115.82825">
  <ele>45.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB37UA-04</name>
  <desc>S4SouthernAncho</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark</url>
  <urlname>Wireless Hill Park : S4 Southern Anchor Block by jinta29</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002483" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wireless Hill Park : S4 Southern Anchor Block</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jinta29</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jinta29</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wireless Hill Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Southern Anchor Block&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What shaped girders were used? (letter only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/548b4ae0-e485-4ba8-a5c4-bc9c8b5cae3c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a stroll across to the Southern Anchor Block where the information board will give you the answer to this question.

Photo © State Library of Western Australia&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/083f9189-6cb2-4e26-8615-0cc8889e0398" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is one of our favourite places in Perth to visit. 

Also known as Yagans Lookout, this area has always been a significant place for communication by the Beeliar Nyoongars. In days gone by, fires were used for communication often at times when fish were moving along the Swan River.

In 1912, the Wireless Hill Station was built here, one of Australia's first telecommunication stations, using Morse Code to communicate with ships off the Australian coast using 112 metre tall mast.

In 1968 the Station was vacated and it is now listed on the Western Australian Register of Heritage Places.

The park now boasts BBQ's, Toilets, a Nature Playspace, Museum, heritage trails, some fantastic geocaches and a lookout to the CBD.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4f7a2859-37ac-4bc9-b3a6-7d1283c392be" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great skills!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.03045" lon="115.828017">
  <ele>45.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBinUo-04</name>
  <desc>DefenceOfPerth4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence</url>
  <urlname>The Defence of Perth : S4 Wireless Hill Telecommunication Museum by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-361" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Defence of Perth : S4 Wireless Hill Telecommunication Museum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Defence of Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wireless Hill Telecommunication Museum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many insulators are on the front of the entrance wall, in digits? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1b32b3a9-9f0f-4e47-b98c-d09cd54c49c5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wireless Hill has been a place of communication for thousands of years, as it was previously used by the Noongar Beeliar Aboriginal people as a lookout and a place from which to send smoke signals. 

The Museum building is located within the old Engine Room of the original Wireless Station established in 1912. The Wireless Station was one of Australia’s first links with radio technology, used for telecommunications during World War One and World War Two.

Using Morse code, the Station communicated with ships off the Australian coast using a mast that was 120 metres (396 feet) tall. In 1968 the Wireless Station was vacated and it is now listed on the Western Australian Register of Heritage Places. 

The museum features several radio sets and describes the role they played in the defence of Australia and also providing essential communication links for organisations such as the Royal Flying Doctor Service. 

https://www.melvillecity.com.au/things-to-do/museums-arts-and-culture/wireless-hill-museum
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58814ab7-66e9-4885-b1d4-1399d838349f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has some significant military locations, which have played an important role in the country’s wartime history. There are many military museums from Albany in the South, Broome in the North, and Kalgoorlie in the East. 

This series looks at some of the local military sites in the Perth area, which includes World War II Tunnels, local Telecommunications, Spitfires, Tanks and Artillery. 

Most museums have restricted opening hours, however the WWII Tunnels and tours of HMAS Ovens are temporarily suspended due to Covid-19. 

Important: 
The WWII Tunnels location in Mosman Park will take some physical effort to reach the site, however there is a path for wheelchairs and people with walking aides. Please follow the Directional Signs from the Boundary Road Carpark to the Heritage Site - approximately 350 metres.




https://scoop.com.au/military-museums-to-visit-across-wa


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/be4bd760-9c8d-4f87-9ed0-3dba73724b39" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations! 
In this photo you can see a rare World War II spy radio (SOE Transmitter Receiver Type3 Mk II) that is inside the museum.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.973883" lon="115.856967">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsno8-04</name>
  <desc>PerthWaterSout4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside</url>
  <urlname>Perth Water – Southside : S4 Dreamer by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000443" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Water – Southside : S4 Dreamer</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Water – Southside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dreamer&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the three hyphenated words at the top of the plaque? (Note: no hyphens needed, but answer should be in upper case)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1324d80d-fe26-4f63-b6c0-45c95241d30b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The statue depicts Sri Chinmoy (1931-2007), an Indian-born visionary, poet, musician and artist who dedicated his life to Peace. According to the plaque, the statue stands as a universal symbol of humanity’s hope that one day the dream of world peace shall inundate the entire world.

The plaque reads “May all who view the statue in this tranquil natural setting of the Scented Gardens, by the banks of the Swan River, be inspired to meditate and reflect on peace.”

This is lovely place to sit in the shade and rest before you continue on with this Adventure Lab tour of the southern shores of Perth Water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6104c3a2-b08e-4acc-82d8-d3a248650aec" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you by foot or bike (or other non-motorised transport) along what is surely one of the nicest paths in Perth – the southern shores of “Perth Water”.

The tour is best done by walking or cycling, although it is possible to drive from site to site even though that would be defeating the purpose. You can start at either end, or wherever you choose. This Adventure Lab does not require you to visit the sites in any ordered sequence.

The pathways along the foreshore provide outstanding views across Perth Water to the city. Along the way you’ll be able to learn a bit about various activities and land uses that occurred on and near the South Perth foreshore over the years.

Please note that some of the paths are for cyclists only, and some are for pedestrians only. The length of the tour is about 4.7km (if walking) from the Causeway bridge to the Narrows (one way) and a little bit more if cycling. There are plenty of opportunities for rest and refreshments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations !!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.094667" lon="115.837683">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBW3tb-04</name>
  <desc>ExploringBibra4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Bibra Lake : S4 Walliabup Skate Park by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915791" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Bibra Lake : S4 Walliabup Skate Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Bibra Lake&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Walliabup Skate Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the last four digits for the phone number for Co Safe&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9cf10793-cb1e-42fd-80bb-085c33f5a4d2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Walliabup skate park is one of the newer features of Bibra Lake, after a petition by local school kids who wanted to see the run-down local skatepark upgraded. The skate park takes up an area of 1,200 square metre and includes basketball area, natureplay, shade shelters and amenities. The park is surrounded by 
paperbark Melaleuca raphiophylla and Flooded Gum Eucalyptus rudis and other wetland plant species. The area has a seasonally high water table and planted swales have been incorporated to allow hydrological and fauna movement through the site and to accommodate increased stormwater runoff from the skate park’s concrete surfaces.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceffc579-0079-4b5f-a7e7-f423f8052655" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Bibra Lake.
Bibra Lake is fourth lake (going north to south) of a string of lakes which combined comprise the Beeliar Regional Park. Bibra Lake has many environmental, heritage and recreational values. Bibra Lake was first known as "Walliabup" but was later named "Bibra" after Benedict von Bibra who brought land in the area in 1843. 
Bibra lake has an interesting history of supporting a dairy farm, post office ,orchard and market garden. It was also home to a battery attached to the 66 Searchlight stationed here in World War 2 along with the regimental headquarters of the 116 Light Anti-Aircraft. This was the base for many women who served in the Australian Women's Army Service.
Today Bibra Lake offers many recreational activities whilst also providing a conservation reserve for our birds and wildlife. This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the area and can be completed by walking, riding or by taking the car but to really enjoy the area we recommend making use of the bike path.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3380e3e0-6f0e-476d-b18d-7097580c9abc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Enjoy watching how clever and brave these kids are on their skateboards and scooters.
This is another spot for the geokids to enjoy.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.030583" lon="115.827483">
