  <desc>S5Telecommunica</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark</url>
  <urlname>Wireless Hill Park : S5 Telecommunications Tower by jinta29</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002485" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wireless Hill Park : S5 Telecommunications Tower</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jinta29</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jinta29</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wireless Hill Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Telecommunications Tower&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the FM frequency in MHz (two numbers, fullstop, one number)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d0dc3a5f-33ec-42ea-8f0d-3f1bcc4d5ba5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You will now need to go to the site of the original antenna mast. The nearby information board will provide you with the answer to the next question.

Photos © State Library of Western Australia and City of Melville&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/083f9189-6cb2-4e26-8615-0cc8889e0398" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is one of our favourite places in Perth to visit. 

Also known as Yagans Lookout, this area has always been a significant place for communication by the Beeliar Nyoongars. In days gone by, fires were used for communication often at times when fish were moving along the Swan River.

In 1912, the Wireless Hill Station was built here, one of Australia's first telecommunication stations, using Morse Code to communicate with ships off the Australian coast using 112 metre tall mast.

In 1968 the Station was vacated and it is now listed on the Western Australian Register of Heritage Places.

The park now boasts BBQ's, Toilets, a Nature Playspace, Museum, heritage trails, some fantastic geocaches and a lookout to the CBD.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/250aff16-59ab-41d7-be6a-2aa7ba14b33c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Woohoo!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
