   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wireless Hill Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lookout Tower&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WirelessHillPark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last name of the photographer?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86cb33e8-7285-4258-a32b-e135329fe3bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Head up to the top of the lookout for some great views across to the CBD. You will need to read the information board for the answer to this question.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/083f9189-6cb2-4e26-8615-0cc8889e0398" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is one of our favourite places in Perth to visit. 

Also known as Yagans Lookout, this area has always been a significant place for communication by the Beeliar Nyoongars. In days gone by, fires were used for communication often at times when fish were moving along the Swan River.

In 1912, the Wireless Hill Station was built here, one of Australia's first telecommunication stations, using Morse Code to communicate with ships off the Australian coast using 112 metre tall mast.

In 1968 the Station was vacated and it is now listed on the Western Australian Register of Heritage Places.

The park now boasts BBQ's, Toilets, a Nature Playspace, Museum, heritage trails, some fantastic geocaches and a lookout to the CBD.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1bb5bf30-9ac4-4f00-9140-b9999bd0110e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
