  <urlname>Icons of South Perth : S2 Perth Zoo by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-212" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Icons of South Perth : S2 Perth Zoo</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Icons of South Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perth Zoo&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the price of admission for a Mini Group? (numerals plus the decimal point)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bf93a9af-dc1f-4f3d-b686-8460309649d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Perth Zoo is a 41-acre zoological park in South Perth, Western Australia. The zoo first opened in 1898 and by 2011 housed 1258 animals of 164 species and an extensive botanical collection. It is a full institutional member of the Zoo and Aquarium Association and the World Association of Zoos and Aquariums. Source: Wikipedia)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/91a05639-9d06-4fe3-9f93-17c7b2e5bc4f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you to a number of iconic structures in the City of South Perth - some in South Perth and some in Como. The tour can be driven, walked or cycled.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/35c5c960-616e-496c-ba4b-5f9a421da826" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.023167" lon="115.825817">
  <ele>-1.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLwUn-04</name>
  <desc>LMelvilleHisto4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S4 German Jetty by Chwiliwr</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002505" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S4 German Jetty</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Historical Places of Interest.&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;German Jetty&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the bolded word on the 2nd line of the 3rd paragraph on the information sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/59ce11b7-9306-4bf7-8b51-b0c8759e2c4f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the site where the German Jetty once stood.  It was here that the mast and other materials for the construction of the Applecross Wireless Station were landed, and then carted up the hill.  The station commenced operations in 1912.

There were no adequate roads for transportation of materials to the site.  Ferries were still the main source of public and commercial transport to and from Perth.

After the construction of Applecross Wireless Station the jetty was used for recreational pursuits for a number of decades.

The remains of the jetty were demolished in 1965.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e5c1644-f91d-452f-826e-26100c031ecd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Melville has had a long association with the Swan River that borders its northern boundaries.

This adventure takes you on a trip along the Swan River visiting some historical places of interest along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/76eb61a9-8256-4bb1-92a0-5ad684affd8c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.
Enjoy the view of the river from here.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9732" lon="115.852283">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB9bRQ-03</name>
  <desc>IoSPS3WindsorHo</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9</url>
  <urlname>Icons of South Perth : S3 Windsor Hotel by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-213" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Icons of South Perth : S3 Windsor Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Icons of South Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Windsor Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The plaque on the wall of the hotel says the main hotel has changed very little in how many years?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/671cb92b-89ce-4d45-9888-1103fabff704" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Windsor Hotel is a heritage-listed building in South Perth, Western Australia. Designed by J. J. Talbot Hobbs, the building is of Federation Filigree style. Built in 1898, it is one of the earliest surviving hotels in Perth's southern suburbs. The Windsor is sited to take advantage of patrons visiting Perth Zoo, coming via ferry along the Swan River. Since its construction, the hotel has had several major renovations. (Source: Wikipedia)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/91a05639-9d06-4fe3-9f93-17c7b2e5bc4f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you to a number of iconic structures in the City of South Perth - some in South Perth and some in Como. The tour can be driven, walked or cycled.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86b04569-726a-46a6-9847-218ba34c4ce6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.957533" lon="115.86915">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-04</name>
  <desc>RedCatS04ThePer</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S04 The Perth Mint by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910115" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S04 The Perth Mint</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Perth Mint&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Attached to the left hand entrance gate it a plaque, what is the last word?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1dc39483-0cb4-4250-92db-3ddbd478b4cb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; Red Cat Stop 6 - Perth Mint

The Perth Mint was Established on 20 June 1899, two years before Australia's Federation in 1901, it was built to refine gold from the gold rushes and to mint gold sovereigns and half-sovereigns for the British Empire. 
Along with the Royal Australian mint the Perth Mint is the older of the two mints issuing coins that are legal tender in Australia.

The tour here is well worth it, they conduct tours every sixty minutes, each day, beginning from 9:30 am, with the last tour starting at 3:30 pm&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here head back to Red Cat stop 6. The next location is the Perth Library.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.007083" lon="115.830267">
  <ele>14.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLwUn-05</name>
  <desc>LMelvilleHisto5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S5 Majestic Hotel Site by Chwiliwr</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002507" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S5 Majestic Hotel Site</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Historical Places of Interest.&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Majestic Hotel Site&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What month is mentioned on the wall plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8090855b-a5e1-4e21-94d2-7fb8d8e63696" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A house was built on the Point by A. P. Matheson in 1897 as a gift to the State Governor in 1897, but the Governor declined to accept it. The house became the Hotel  Melville and had many owners.  The  Stack  family purchased the Hotel in 1924 and renamed it  'Majestic  Hotel'  in 1925.  The Stack family ran the  Hotel until 1981.  The Hotel was also used as a  residence for prominent identities. 

Point Dundas is now subdivided and developed as residential lots. There is no physical trace of the original house nor the Hotel.  The modern Boardwalk around the base of the high ground of the Point is a timber  deck and handrail on timber sub-frame  into  the  foreshore.  The  Applecross Jetty is  a  timber-decked   narrow  walkway  supported  on  timber  framing,  a timber handrail, and piles into the Swan River.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e5c1644-f91d-452f-826e-26100c031ecd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Melville has had a long association with the Swan River that borders its northern boundaries.

This adventure takes you on a trip along the Swan River visiting some historical places of interest along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8bc7834e-8a16-489a-b1ff-d0411a9c13fa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.
Afraid you can no longer have a drink here unless you can afford to buy one of the houses.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.957567" lon="115.8655">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-10</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu10</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S10 John Septimus Roe by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-225" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S10 John Septimus Roe</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;John Septimus Roe&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look at the plaque beneath the sculpture. What year did Roe retire?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9aa0839-db3a-428e-b68f-4445f56485a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The bronze statue commemorates John Septimus Roe (8 May 1797–28 May 1878) who was the first Surveyor-General of Western Australia.

He was a renowned explorer, and a Member of Western Australia's Legislative and Executive Councils for nearly 40 years. The sites of Perth and Fremantle were chosen on his recommendation, and he was responsible for laying out the towns.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.086633" lon="115.8269">
  <ele>14.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBW3tb-05</name>
  <desc>ExploringBibra5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Bibra Lake : S5 Narma Kullarak Broadwalk by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915793" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Bibra Lake : S5 Narma Kullarak Broadwalk</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Bibra Lake&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Narma Kullarak Broadwalk&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What month was this boardwalk officially opened?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/44e0f737-377c-4886-bc78-a0413705fc5f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bibra Lake has a wide range of habitats making it an ideal place for many water and bush birds.
The open water has a good variety of ducks including Musk Duck, Blue-billed Duck and Australasian Shoveller. The boardwalk contains a shelter that provides avid bird watchers a great vantage point to observe
the wild life. If you are lucky enough you may even spot a turtle or two.
 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceffc579-0079-4b5f-a7e7-f423f8052655" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Bibra Lake.
Bibra Lake is fourth lake (going north to south) of a string of lakes which combined comprise the Beeliar Regional Park. Bibra Lake has many environmental, heritage and recreational values. Bibra Lake was first known as "Walliabup" but was later named "Bibra" after Benedict von Bibra who brought land in the area in 1843. 
Bibra lake has an interesting history of supporting a dairy farm, post office ,orchard and market garden. It was also home to a battery attached to the 66 Searchlight stationed here in World War 2 along with the regimental headquarters of the 116 Light Anti-Aircraft. This was the base for many women who served in the Australian Women's Army Service.
Today Bibra Lake offers many recreational activities whilst also providing a conservation reserve for our birds and wildlife. This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the area and can be completed by walking, riding or by taking the car but to really enjoy the area we recommend making use of the bike path.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cf38e7a7-98cc-475a-96fa-9d74dfb1797a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Take the time to enjoy the scenery and the bird life that call this place home.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
