  <desc>CityIsWadjukC05</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC</url>
  <urlname>This city is Wadjuk Country : S05 Yandellup by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909783" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>This city is Wadjuk Country : S05 Yandellup</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;This city is Wadjuk Country&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Yandellup&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the name of the first hospital built on this site in 1855?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cf983039-80f2-4f77-b005-b6d7dcfad3b9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This area is known as Yandellup, which means the reeds are on and near this place, it was cultivated and harvested through an understanding of seasonal changes. 

‘The yanjid was a thing that you could eat as well, the bottom roots of it. But you could only do it in seasonal turns, not every day of the week. If you pulled it out certain times, it would cause storms. 
Big storms would come. That was the aboriginals ways of acknowledging that rains were going to come.
The fires were used for clearing the area so food can be plentiful the following year.’

Karla is an integral part of Wadjuk Noongar existence, highly symbolic, in Noongar karla means campfire, it also implies the home where people gather to meet around the fire. The Wadjuk Noongar were fire-stick farmers and only Wadjuk Noongars with responsibility for boodjar could burn it. 

Today the site is consumed by health, mental and religious facilities.

Photo credit: Parkacru
Final image credit: ABC Education Resources&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79cbb012-2a74-4686-afba-7d425fd49400" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The City of Perth is Wadjuk Boodjar (Country) covering the area between Gargatup, the place down by the hillside (in Kings Park), and Matagarup, the place of the leg holes (Heirisson Island), where Noongars would cross the river. This area, near the lowest and easiest crossing point on the Swan River (at Matagarup), was an important communication and trade centre between the coast and the islands. Whadjuck/Balardong custodian Tom Bennell referred to Perth as ”Beerit” meaning ”pathways” due to its importance for meetings, travel and trade.

This Adventure Lab Cache will take you on an approximate 12km journey around Wadjuk Boodjar  depending on the route you take, you may walk, ride, drive (parts) or use public transport to get around, we recommend you give yourself a good half of a day to complete the adventure. 

Photo credit: Parkacru

VIEWER ADVICE
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander viewers are advised that the following Adventure Lab Cache may contain images of people who have died.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4e820b92-62e2-4cd5-b592-baa155f76a9b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
