 <wpt lat="-31.955917" lon="115.861867">
  <ele>39.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnmX8-03</name>
  <desc>PerthsTheatreS3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre</url>
  <urlname>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S3 Playhouse Theatre (Original Site) by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-233" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S3 Playhouse Theatre (Original Site)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth’s Theatre Scene&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Playhouse Theatre (Original Site)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the two letters encircled at the top of the inscription?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6559cb13-0907-456e-933d-5102cbc11d4f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Playhouse Theatre  was purpose-built for live theatre in the 1956 and remained one of the city's principal venues for performing arts for over half a century until replaced by the State Theatre Centre of Western Australia in January 2011. It was demolished in October 2012 as part of a redevelopment of Cathedral Square. The site is now occupied by office buildings. 

The Playhouse was the home of the National Theatre Company (née Repertory Theatre Club),  the Playhouse Theatre Company (1984-85), the West Australian Theatre Company (1985-91) and the Perth Theatre Company (1995 - 2009).

The coordinates will take you to an inscription in the new building’s stone wall facing Pier St.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ac167c3-0dba-4363-bb05-0e8eac02c57a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Since the time of Settlement, the Perth CBD has been home to a vibrant theatre scene and this short Adventure Lab will take you to some of this city’s key live performance locations, both historical and current. The seven locations are in close proximity and you should easily be able to complete the series within two hours. Take your time to appreciate some of the wonderful architecture of the contemporary theatres as well as the remnants of those lost to development. 

Given the building density within this area, the listed coordinates may not be as accurate as desired. However there should be sufficient information in each location’s description for you to find what you need once you have navigated to the general area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a720344f-9999-4c56-8fa7-789675ea2595" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;CORRECT!!

Congratulations. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.030433" lon="115.8165">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLwUn-03</name>
  <desc>LMelvilleHisto3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S3 Atwell House and Gallery by Chwiliwr</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002503" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S3 Atwell House and Gallery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Historical Places of Interest.&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Atwell House and Gallery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many gumnuts on the branches around the female statue in the painting on the front of the eastern building?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e15373b-7e48-4003-b90d-24aff13025e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ownership of Lena Blacker’s Alfred Cove property was transferred to Walter David Atwell on 15 June 1926.

Oral history interviews with David (Walter Atwell’s second son), his wife Connie and their daughter Dianne in 1998 provide a detailed description of the house, its furnishings and the lifestyle of the family.  From the  interviews, it is clear that Atwell House was designed to show the prominence of its owners, being a substantial brick and tile residence for the time with an elaborate verandah, bay windows and leadlight window and door features. 

On 29 May 1982, Mayor of Melville, J.F. Howson Esq. OBE JP, officially opened the MCAA’s  Atwell Studio Gallery building.This was constructed for the purposes of providing a gallery space for the permanent  display of the artwork created by Association members.  The building was specifically designed to match  the scale, style and materials of the adjacent Atwell House. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e5c1644-f91d-452f-826e-26100c031ecd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Melville has had a long association with the Swan River that borders its northern boundaries.

This adventure takes you on a trip along the Swan River visiting some historical places of interest along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc6b0586-4cd1-4947-a12f-3285c4b2c2b5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.
The Gallery should be visited if you have time.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.957033" lon="115.8593">
  <ele>54.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-06</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculpt065</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S06 5 Pens by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-221" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S06 5 Pens</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;5 Pens&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of this sculpture (see nearby plaque) (2 words)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a4f22a7a-ce9e-47f4-bace-3b5867665260" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 2006, the City of Perth Art Foundation commissioned artist Anne Neill, in collaboration with landscape architects Blackwell &amp; Associates and heritage consultant Barbara Dundas to design an artwork for this entry to Stirling Gardens.

The five 3.5m high cast aluminium pen nibs are dedicated to the planners who designed the Stirling Gardens (Western Australia's first Botanical Garden). It also honours those who wrote the recorded history of the gardens.

This sculpture `Memory Markers` was inspired by the early recording of memories about Stirling Gardens. The shapes are based on the familiar ink dipping nib which expresses the essence of the Victorian Era on which the original formal planting patterns were based.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.956133" lon="115.860317">
