   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Kangaroos on the Terrace&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Have a close look at the tail of the first Kangaroo in the line of 3, what year was the sculpture made?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1bcede13-ad3f-4d0e-bfbd-d244103db31d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Three large male Western Grey kangaroos in different natural positions, `drinking`, `alert` and `in flight`, created and cast in silicone bronze along with a Joey and Mother by artists Charlie Smith and Joan Walsh-Smith.

This is one of many sculptures and installations in and around the Stirling Gardens.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9559" lon="115.860617">
  <ele>32.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-01</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo01</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S01 Point Zero by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-191" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S01 Point Zero</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Point Zero&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was was the sculpture dedicated?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f7ff90dd-bb01-4111-98b1-436a77a017a2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In today's world of GPS enabled devices distances are measured from where you are to where you are going.

Travelers of the past used road distances and road maps to find their way around.  Each state of Australia had their 'Point Zero' to measure road distances from a central point.

Unlike most states where road distances were measured from the General Post Office located in the state capital city the road distances in Western Australia from 1925 were measured from the corner of the building where the Point Zero plaque is on display.

NE of the Point Zero plaque is a sculpture in the St George's Cathedral lawn area.  Locate the dedication plaque for this sculpture.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95885" lon="115.8568">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-05</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu05</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S05 Bessie Mabel Rischbieth OBE by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909869" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S05 Bessie Mabel Rischbieth OBE</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bessie Mabel Rischbieth OBE&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What type of bird is standing nearby?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4d8e55ca-4b48-44de-b00c-cba841ce113e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bessie Mabel Rischbieth, OBE (16 October 1874 – 13 March 1967) was an influential and early Australian feminist and social activist. A leading or founding member of many social reform groups, such as the Women's Service Guilds, The Australian Federation of Women Voters and their periodical The Dawn, she sought to establish international campaigns for social change and human rights.

Rischbieth promoted a Citizens Committee for the Preservation of Kings Park and the Swan River and successfully prevented an olympic swimming pool being built for the 1962 Empire Games in Kings Park.

She remained active in social issues until her death at Bethesda Hospital in Claremont, Western Australia on 13 March 1967, aged 92.

The coordinates will take you to a wonderful part of the new Elizabeth Quay precinct.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.087833" lon="115.822183">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBW3tb-02</name>
  <desc>ExploringBibra2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Bibra Lake : S2 Bibra Lake Regional Playground by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909961" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Bibra Lake : S2 Bibra Lake Regional Playground</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Bibra Lake&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bibra Lake Regional Playground&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many wooden poles are located at the entrance to the park?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2ade0571-9d67-4ab3-adf1-a342cd0a6e4b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This great playground opened in 2016 has kept many a child entertained, so if you do bring the geokids along for this adventure you may be here for awhile.
The playground design reflects Bibra Lake's rich and significant Aboriginal Heritage and is also inspired by local wildlife and fauna. This is reflected in the features this playground offers:
- Aboriginal fish traps - large basket-like climbing frames and cubby houses
- Giant talking rocks telling local Nyungar stories
- Giant nest of turtles and eggs.
- Double flying fox
- Tree top aerial rope walk
- Rope obstacle course and climbing frames
- Water spurting bullrishes and water play area with sandpit
- In ground trampolines
- Seesaws and birds nest swing
- 25,000 year old Diprotodon fossil dig

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceffc579-0079-4b5f-a7e7-f423f8052655" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Bibra Lake.
Bibra Lake is fourth lake (going north to south) of a string of lakes which combined comprise the Beeliar Regional Park. Bibra Lake has many environmental, heritage and recreational values. Bibra Lake was first known as "Walliabup" but was later named "Bibra" after Benedict von Bibra who brought land in the area in 1843. 
Bibra lake has an interesting history of supporting a dairy farm, post office ,orchard and market garden. It was also home to a battery attached to the 66 Searchlight stationed here in World War 2 along with the regimental headquarters of the 116 Light Anti-Aircraft. This was the base for many women who served in the Australian Women's Army Service.
Today Bibra Lake offers many recreational activities whilst also providing a conservation reserve for our birds and wildlife. This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the area and can be completed by walking, riding or by taking the car but to really enjoy the area we recommend making use of the bike path.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4e0a3ce3-7323-4271-95cf-b98b961facce" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations. We think this park was a great addition to Bibra Lake.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9549" lon="115.861283">
  <ele>31.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-05</name>
  <desc>RedCatS05CityOf</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S05 City of Perth Library by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910117" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S05 City of Perth Library</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;City of Perth Library&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;looking at the front of the building, walk around to the left, what would you need to cross to continue to the back?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6236a6e-6f83-4d12-a178-4eee3301f282" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Red Cat stop 8 - Town Hall

The library is the first civic building to be built by the City of Perth since the Perth Concert Hall in 1972, It was completed in 2016. 
It is well worth exploring the building, you will discover many interesting aspects, such as the ceiling features a mural by artist Andrew Nicholls, there is a 13 meter tall vertical garden, the tallest in Perth, with more than 3500 plants! It also has an outdoor terrace and free wifi.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here complete the 'Town Hall' location and then walk to the 'London Court' location&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.943667" lon="115.87675">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2021-05-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBAmy9-04</name>
  <desc>HISTORYOFTHEIN4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc</url>
  <urlname>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S4 Engineering Heritage National Landmark by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-169" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S4 Engineering Heritage National Landmark</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Engineering Heritage National Landmark&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the year it was Founded ? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c3a44e86-5a51-49af-9717-9a04eab3aa04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Note: The board with the description and the marker were not found on Google maps, but it is inside this building. If you find it, please consider measuring the right location and make a copy of the marker.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29448b92-ccf2-4fed-9423-76f521005c21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1890s
As Australia marched toward Federation, the promise of a transcontinental railway to link the isolated west with the fast growing east coast persuaded Western Australia to align with the rest of the colonies. At the time, a skeleton rail link existed but the west to east line was missing a section between Kalgoorlie and Port Augusta spanning almost 2000 kilometres.
1912 – 1917
In one of Australia’s most remarkable surveying and engineering feats, two construction teams worked for more than five years to fill the 2000 kilometre gap. On 17 October 1917, the two construction teams – one starting from Port Augusta and other from Kalgoorlie – finally met after working for five years on what is the longest straight stretch of railway ever built in Australia. It was constructed using the most basic tools – pick and shovel, carthorse and camel. The teams joined the line at what is now known as the tiny South Australian siding of Ooldea on the Nullarbor Plain.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4076e376-3da8-4b8a-b1cf-34b22e642164" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.943667" lon="115.876817">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2021-05-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBAmy9-05</name>
  <desc>HISTORYOFTHEIN5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc</url>
  <urlname>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S5 The Train Carriage by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-170" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S5 The Train Carriage</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Train Carriage&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The carriage was sold to whom? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4616a2d0-8d0b-4dd7-9b2a-cbee53eb8bb8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The carriages or "cars" were built by the Metropolitan Railway Carriage and Wagon Company of Birmingham, England. They each had one first class and two second class compartments, seating 22 passengers (six in first class and sixteen in second class). First class had comfortable padded seats. Second class had wooden benches. Lighting was by oil lamps. The coachwork and frame was made of teak and the interior and roof was made of pine. Each coach was 4.9 metres long, 2.3 metres wide, 3.2 metres high and weighed 4.25 tonnes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29448b92-ccf2-4fed-9423-76f521005c21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1890s
As Australia marched toward Federation, the promise of a transcontinental railway to link the isolated west with the fast growing east coast persuaded Western Australia to align with the rest of the colonies. At the time, a skeleton rail link existed but the west to east line was missing a section between Kalgoorlie and Port Augusta spanning almost 2000 kilometres.
1912 – 1917
In one of Australia’s most remarkable surveying and engineering feats, two construction teams worked for more than five years to fill the 2000 kilometre gap. On 17 October 1917, the two construction teams – one starting from Port Augusta and other from Kalgoorlie – finally met after working for five years on what is the longest straight stretch of railway ever built in Australia. It was constructed using the most basic tools – pick and shovel, carthorse and camel. The teams joined the line at what is now known as the tiny South Australian siding of Ooldea on the Nullarbor Plain.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b9701c48-a5e2-4732-982b-b9e35651d1b7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this lab caches. Hope you enjoyed your day.  
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.943533" lon="115.87675">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2021-05-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBAmy9-03</name>
  <desc>HISTORYOFTHEIN3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc</url>
  <urlname>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S3 The Indian Pacific by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-168" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S3 The Indian Pacific</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Indian Pacific&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This Plague was unveiled by whom?  (3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bb4cada2-2233-401d-9023-4d9895a6c2fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;23 February 1970
It was not until 1969 that an uninterrupted rail link from the Indian Ocean in the west to the Pacific Ocean in the east existed. The newly named Indian Pacific train embarked on its inaugural transcontinental journey on 23 February 1970, arriving in Perth. It is this rich history that has made the Indian Pacific one of Australia’s most treasured icons.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29448b92-ccf2-4fed-9423-76f521005c21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1890s
As Australia marched toward Federation, the promise of a transcontinental railway to link the isolated west with the fast growing east coast persuaded Western Australia to align with the rest of the colonies. At the time, a skeleton rail link existed but the west to east line was missing a section between Kalgoorlie and Port Augusta spanning almost 2000 kilometres.
1912 – 1917
In one of Australia’s most remarkable surveying and engineering feats, two construction teams worked for more than five years to fill the 2000 kilometre gap. On 17 October 1917, the two construction teams – one starting from Port Augusta and other from Kalgoorlie – finally met after working for five years on what is the longest straight stretch of railway ever built in Australia. It was constructed using the most basic tools – pick and shovel, carthorse and camel. The teams joined the line at what is now known as the tiny South Australian siding of Ooldea on the Nullarbor Plain.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f1e0c9c9-01d4-416f-8ceb-f776b6f2dd11" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.955033" lon="115.86055">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-07</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander27</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S07 Perth Town Hall by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915763" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S07 Perth Town Hall</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perth Town Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The same year is mentioned on each plaque.  What is this year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/75e6b67a-3565-4430-bfdb-6a59229b6f68" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One of Perth’s heritage jewels, the Town Hall took some time to construct. The foundation was laid on 24 May 1867 by Governor Hampton, but it was not opened until 1 June 1870 by Governor Weld. The design was a joint production of James Manning and Richard Roach Jewell. Manning was responsible for all of the woodwork, including the magnificent roof, while Jewell designed and supervised the erection of the main building.

In addition to Council activities, the Town Hall has also hosted concerts, exhibitions, bazaars, lectures, dances, skating, and stage shows. It was also the place that generations gathered every 31 December to hear the New Year rung in by the bells of its clock.

It is near this spot that the felling of a tree marked the foundation of the City on 12th August 1829.

Look for the exposed foundations on the David Malcolm Justice Centre side (south side) of the town hall heading west away from Barrack Street.   Above them are two plaques on the wall.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9433" lon="115.876733">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2021-05-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBAmy9-02</name>
  <desc>HISTORYOFTHEIN2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc</url>
  <urlname>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S2 WA Standard Gauge Railway Kalgoorlie to Perth by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-167" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S2 WA Standard Gauge Railway Kalgoorlie to Perth</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WA Standard Gauge Railway Kalgoorlie to Perth &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This Plague was unveiled by whom?  (4 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4f277d5a-2f90-451b-8376-3fbed7411105" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The original 635 km narrow gauge railway from Perth to Kalgoorlie was built in the 1890s to mainly serve the Eastern Goldfields. Since the beginning of the 20th century the standardisation of the railway gauge throughout Australia received the investigation and consideration which such an important national problem deserved. However, apart from the Trans Australian Railway, completed in 1917 as a Federation commitment, little was achieved. In 1912 the Commonwealth Government proposed a standard gauge (4 ft 8½ in or 1435 mm) railway from Fremantle to Kalgoorlie, to be completed at the same time as the Trans Australian Railway, but it did not proceed due to a lack of finance.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29448b92-ccf2-4fed-9423-76f521005c21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1890s
As Australia marched toward Federation, the promise of a transcontinental railway to link the isolated west with the fast growing east coast persuaded Western Australia to align with the rest of the colonies. At the time, a skeleton rail link existed but the west to east line was missing a section between Kalgoorlie and Port Augusta spanning almost 2000 kilometres.
1912 – 1917
In one of Australia’s most remarkable surveying and engineering feats, two construction teams worked for more than five years to fill the 2000 kilometre gap. On 17 October 1917, the two construction teams – one starting from Port Augusta and other from Kalgoorlie – finally met after working for five years on what is the longest straight stretch of railway ever built in Australia. It was constructed using the most basic tools – pick and shovel, carthorse and camel. The teams joined the line at what is now known as the tiny South Australian siding of Ooldea on the Nullarbor Plain.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29c45217-276c-43db-8d7c-b7b861e01595" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.954733" lon="115.860433">
  <ele>35.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-06</name>
  <desc>RedCatS06TownHa</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S06 Town Hall by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000417" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S06 Town Hall</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Town Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last word on the 'City of Perth Coat of Arms' plaque&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4a4c1aa7-f038-41be-8e63-14d7717b08bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Red Cat stop 8 - Town Hall

Perth Town Hall is Australia's only Gothic style town Hall. It was not specifically designed as a Council chamber or administration center, but rather as an all-purpose meeting place for the people of Perth.

The coloured bricks came from the East Perth clay pits (now ornamental ponds at Queen’s Gardens) and were laid in chequered Flemish bond. Free settlers laid the foundations for the Hall and then convicts completed the building.

The Hall is open to the public from 10am to 4pm, Monday to Saturday, unless hired for a private function. 
It is well worth taking a look inside.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here complete the 'City of Perth Library' location (if you haven't) and then walk to the 'London Court' location.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.958967" lon="115.855167">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJj4A-06</name>
  <desc>CityIsWadjukC06</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC</url>
  <urlname>This city is Wadjuk Country : S06 Derbarl Yerrigan by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-206" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>This city is Wadjuk Country : S06 Derbarl Yerrigan</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;This city is Wadjuk Country&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Derbarl Yerrigan&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who is the author of ‘First Contact’?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0e45a629-d63a-44dc-b94e-911c0ec3b5af" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Elizabeth Quay is situated on the Derbarl Yerrigan (Swan River) it’s now busy with restaurants, bars, and landmark sculptures, long before the hustle and bustle it once belonged to the Wadjuk Noongars, the shallow reed beds is where the Wadjuk Noongar People fished before the ‘first contact’ in 1829.

First Contact by _____ _____
‘They first saw a boat with white sails coming to shore, which they haven’t seen before, you know it would have looked like a big white bird, and the sailors being Wadjellas, made the noongar people think it was their ancestor’s spirits coming back from the dead, from the sea.’ 

Image credit: ABC Education Resources
Final image credit: Parkacru &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79cbb012-2a74-4686-afba-7d425fd49400" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The City of Perth is Wadjuk Boodjar (Country) covering the area between Gargatup, the place down by the hillside (in Kings Park), and Matagarup, the place of the leg holes (Heirisson Island), where Noongars would cross the river. This area, near the lowest and easiest crossing point on the Swan River (at Matagarup), was an important communication and trade centre between the coast and the islands. Whadjuck/Balardong custodian Tom Bennell referred to Perth as ”Beerit” meaning ”pathways” due to its importance for meetings, travel and trade.

This Adventure Lab Cache will take you on an approximate 12km journey around Wadjuk Boodjar  depending on the route you take, you may walk, ride, drive (parts) or use public transport to get around, we recommend you give yourself a good half of a day to complete the adventure. 

Photo credit: Parkacru

VIEWER ADVICE
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander viewers are advised that the following Adventure Lab Cache may contain images of people who have died.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9ec8d488-22f8-46cc-9a50-48b725fa3f95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.955267" lon="115.859217">
  <ele>66.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-08</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu08</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S08 Footsteps in Time by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-223" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S08 Footsteps in Time</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Footsteps in Time&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who is the 5th person, see nearby plaque (3 words)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c205ed6a-1b71-4835-a751-59418df31ae2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The five unique bronze statues, "Footsteps in Time" were commissioned by St Martins Properties Pty Ltd to commemorate the 175th anniversary of Western Australia and symbolise the business people who have built the Central Business District.

Inspired by the fact that St. Martins Centre occupies the site of the first businessman`s club of WA, this public artwork recognises their involvement on significant historical moments during Perth`s development.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.96635" lon="115.8471">
