  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-211" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Icons of South Perth : S1 The Old Mill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Icons of South Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Old Mill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/360c5218-28a5-4aa0-a1dc-db2d4cd7c7b9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did milling operations cease?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/61002008-0a3f-44cc-8c05-8672468e2770" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill (originally called Shenton's Mill) is one of the oldest surviving colonial buildings in Western Australia. It was constructed in 1835 by Lockyer &amp; Sons for William Shenton. It replaced an earlier timber-framed mill built in 1833.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/91a05639-9d06-4fe3-9f93-17c7b2e5bc4f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you to a number of iconic structures in the City of South Perth - some in South Perth and some in Como. The tour can be driven, walked or cycled.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9442f259-4837-4575-b654-7c0893db543d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.942033" lon="115.87745">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2021-05-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBAmy9-01</name>
  <desc>HISTORYOFTHEIN1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc</url>
  <urlname>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S1 Steam Locomotives by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-166" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC : S1 Steam Locomotives</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;HISTORY OF THE INDIAN PACIFIC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Steam Locomotives&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2d2209af-7bb3-4abd-9309-89ba4c67d8fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year is it?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e8c8e087-737f-4d43-a098-ff52d46eb551" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Locomotive is maintained by the Australian Railway Historical Society, W.A. Davison (inc).
The Rail Transport Museum is located at Bassendean adjacent to Ashfield Station. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29448b92-ccf2-4fed-9423-76f521005c21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1890s
As Australia marched toward Federation, the promise of a transcontinental railway to link the isolated west with the fast growing east coast persuaded Western Australia to align with the rest of the colonies. At the time, a skeleton rail link existed but the west to east line was missing a section between Kalgoorlie and Port Augusta spanning almost 2000 kilometres.
1912 – 1917
In one of Australia’s most remarkable surveying and engineering feats, two construction teams worked for more than five years to fill the 2000 kilometre gap. On 17 October 1917, the two construction teams – one starting from Port Augusta and other from Kalgoorlie – finally met after working for five years on what is the longest straight stretch of railway ever built in Australia. It was constructed using the most basic tools – pick and shovel, carthorse and camel. The teams joined the line at what is now known as the tiny South Australian siding of Ooldea on the Nullarbor Plain.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/734835fc-6907-4df2-b321-659f37d3536c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
