 <wpt lat="-31.91805" lon="115.9791">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2021-02-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBTnrz-02</name>
  <desc>SpookyWanderin2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1b385469-ed4b-4f73-9866-b6696aeb2af8</url>
  <urlname>Spooky Wanderings : S2 Guildford Cemetery by TeamBarritts</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002491" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Spooky Wanderings : S2 Guildford Cemetery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>TeamBarritts</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">TeamBarritts</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Spooky Wanderings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Guildford Cemetery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1b385469-ed4b-4f73-9866-b6696aeb2af8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1b385469-ed4b-4f73-9866-b6696aeb2af8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the given coordinates you will find a plaque, what is the stated opening date? (As shown on the plaque)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/03162f8d-b641-43e6-a77b-1662837865ba" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Guildford Cemetery was first established in the late 1800s. Today the cemetery remains the chosen resting place of many people wishing to be buried in a traditional setting, alongside some of Western Australia’s most notable pioneers.  

An earlier cemetery in Guildford was established early in the history of the Swan River Colony in James Street and some headstones were moved to Guildford Cemetery when it was established.

It located in the southern part of Guildford, between the Great Eastern Highway Bypass and Kalamunda Road adjacent to the Perth Airport. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d145caeb-98e9-4811-8d6d-8e37e1682ce8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you on a short tour of some of the oldest and most interesting cemetery’s around East Perth and the Hills area's.  This Lab should take about 2 hours to complete and all sites should have good phone coverage. 

While you are visiting the various locations please take the opportunity to read a few of the headstones, finding about the area and the history of the people who are laid to rest there.  Sometimes it may give a hint as to what they did and where they originated from. 

Very importantly, please remember where you are and show the appropriate respect for the residents and people visiting them.

There is also an optional bonus mystery cache to find once you have finished.  You will need to collect the clues provided in the journals to solve the puzzle and get the cache coordinates.  

Good luck and have fun! 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7bd8e553-d005-4636-9619-e3f74c7cf628" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations, well done!  Now to the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.954333" lon="115.85935">
  <ele>42.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-09</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu09</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S09 Percy by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-224" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S09 Percy</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Percy&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the plaque beneath the sculpture, what year did the Mirror dress Percy up in a suit?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e80e19e-7e88-4910-8614-352bfc2c7263" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Percy Button was a local street entertainer and one of Perth's best known faces from the 1920's to the 1950's. Performing somersaults and handstands, Percy entertained people for a few shillings while they waited to see films, newsreels and theatrical performances at the theaters that were concentrated in what is now the Hay Street Mall, the Theater Royal, the Ambassador and His Majesty's Theater.

Percy was renowned for his grubbiness and in November 1929 local newspaper, The Mirror, dressed Percy up in a long-tailed suit and ran a front page competition asking readers to guess the identity of the cleaned up man. The Mirror offered a guinea's worth of goods for the first opened letter giving the man's name. The newspaper later asked Percy to write the 1929 Centenary Christmas Message.

This artwork celebrates the spirit of the street.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.954383" lon="115.859383">
  <ele>42.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnmX8-04</name>
  <desc>PerthsTheatreS4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre</url>
  <urlname>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S4 Theatre Royal (Facade) by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-234" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S4 Theatre Royal (Facade)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth’s Theatre Scene&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Theatre Royal (Facade)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the last two digits on the City of Perth light pole directly in front of the Theatre Royal facade?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc3d23fe-0fd0-4a0b-ac06-173c04480212" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Theatre Royal was the first purpose-built theatre in Western Australia. Then one of the city’s largest and most magnificent buildings, it was built for Thomas Molloy, builder, speculator and local politician. Although the Town Hall, St George’s Hall and, sometimes, the Swan River Mechanics’ Institute were used to stage live theatre productions, the need for a purpose-built theatre in Perth had been long felt in the community and, as a result, the news that Molloy was planning to build Perth’s first theatre was received with much enthusiasm. The Theatre Royal was opened on 19 April 1897. It should have opened the previous Saturday night, but was prevented from doing so “owing to the late arrival of S. S. Rockton with the actors and scenery on board”. The opening performance was of the ‘Silver King’ by the Stanford &amp; Barnes Company. Later in the 1980’s it became adjoined as part of Hoyts Centres Cinema Four but was closed in April 1999. 
The facade survived and the building now houses retail shops.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ac167c3-0dba-4363-bb05-0e8eac02c57a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Since the time of Settlement, the Perth CBD has been home to a vibrant theatre scene and this short Adventure Lab will take you to some of this city’s key live performance locations, both historical and current. The seven locations are in close proximity and you should easily be able to complete the series within two hours. Take your time to appreciate some of the wonderful architecture of the contemporary theatres as well as the remnants of those lost to development. 

Given the building density within this area, the listed coordinates may not be as accurate as desired. However there should be sufficient information in each location’s description for you to find what you need once you have navigated to the general area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5d92855a-638d-4dbe-9d31-4f0332874f79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;CORRECT!

Congratulations. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.964717" lon="115.848">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsno8-05</name>
  <desc>PerthWaterSout5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside</url>
  <urlname>Perth Water – Southside : S5 The Narrows by mmaherinwa</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000445" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Water – Southside : S5 The Narrows</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>mmaherinwa</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">mmaherinwa</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Water – Southside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Narrows&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Southside&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the date on the bottom photo?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc2c3162-b523-430a-b8b8-f9e3a72faa2d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The interpretive information at this site states:

“Hot summer nights at the Point.

We always looked forward to crabbing. On hot steamy summer nights we piled into the family car and headed for Mill Point. We always wore old sandshoes to protect us from a nip on the toes.

The river in those days was teeming with delicious blue manna crabs, easily seen on what was then a clear river bed. Father and older brother armed themselves with scoop nets for the catch. Younger brother dragged the large metal tub along to hold the captured crabs, while I carried the hurricane lamp. We filled a large kerosene tin with water from the river and when the water was boiling, in would go the unfortunate crabs. Munching into our meal was sheer paradise and we were, with full tummies, more than pleased to leave the beach in its previous orderly state”.

(Source: sign board at site)
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6104c3a2-b08e-4acc-82d8-d3a248650aec" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you by foot or bike (or other non-motorised transport) along what is surely one of the nicest paths in Perth – the southern shores of “Perth Water”.

The tour is best done by walking or cycling, although it is possible to drive from site to site even though that would be defeating the purpose. You can start at either end, or wherever you choose. This Adventure Lab does not require you to visit the sites in any ordered sequence.

The pathways along the foreshore provide outstanding views across Perth Water to the city. Along the way you’ll be able to learn a bit about various activities and land uses that occurred on and near the South Perth foreshore over the years.

Please note that some of the paths are for cyclists only, and some are for pedestrians only. The length of the tour is about 4.7km (if walking) from the Causeway bridge to the Narrows (one way) and a little bit more if cycling. There are plenty of opportunities for rest and refreshments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations !!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95415" lon="115.859267">
