 <wpt lat="-32.02585" lon="115.812317">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2020-05-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrNtN-01</name>
  <desc>CaringForWater1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard</url>
  <urlname>Caring for Waterbirds : S1 Wetland Lookout by fairwaydrivers</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-288" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Caring for Waterbirds : S1 Wetland Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Caring for Waterbirds&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wetland Lookout&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many benches are there on the lookout platform? Write your answer in word form.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dd9aff52-fc48-4acd-9fbb-b580a5d2938d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From this lookout platform you can sit quietly and watch the waterbirds. You can see their behaviors as they search for food or rest. Have a look at the habitat. What do you see? What sort of plants? What are the birds doing?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6292440-19d6-4fcb-a1bb-d28838755318" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan Estuary and its foreshore provide habitats for a diversity of waterbirds. The sedges, samphires and trees around this area are essential for maintaining the health of the estuary and the waterbirds.The seagrass meadows provide a nursery habitat for fish and crustaceans. Dead and decaying seagrass provides food for invertebrates that are food for waterbirds. The shallow mudflats are exposed during low tide which may only be for a few hours each day. These mudflats hide mussel deposits and are home to worms, aquatic snails, insects and other minute crustaceans sought after by waterbirds.Other waterbirds trap small fish in the open water.
This adventure is best done on a lovely bike ride which will give you beautiful views of the Swan River and the many waterbirds. See how many different species you can count!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done. We hope you loved this place and saw many waterbirds.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95" lon="115.860883">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJj4A-03</name>
  <desc>CityIsWadjukC03</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC</url>
  <urlname>This city is Wadjuk Country : S03 The pinjar by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909779" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>This city is Wadjuk Country : S03 The pinjar</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;This city is Wadjuk Country&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The pinjar&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What percentage of original wetlands on the Swan River coastal plain are still intact? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/29093584-5959-4b9e-a874-2e862d3279bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between an area to the north of where the railway line is now and the Swan River to the south there are ancient wetlands, the train station and cultural precinct are situated on one of a series of pinjar (lakes). 

According to Balardong Wadjuk Noongar boordiya Cedric Jacobs, the pinjar were essential for hunting and collecting food.

“This was a supermarket of the Noongar people.”

Food harvested depended on the season and the water levels. Popular animals are gilgie (fresh water crayfish), yaagan (turtles) and koolya (frogs).

An early settler George Grey watched Wadjuk yorga harvesting frogs and turtles. In 1841 he wrote:

“women with their long sticks, and their long thin arms, which they plunge up to the shoulder in the slime, managing to drag them out.”

Today you can see a small wetland outside the Art Gallery, frogs and lizards are returning to a place where they once thrived. 

Photo credit: Parkacru
Final image credit: ABC Education Resources &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79cbb012-2a74-4686-afba-7d425fd49400" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The City of Perth is Wadjuk Boodjar (Country) covering the area between Gargatup, the place down by the hillside (in Kings Park), and Matagarup, the place of the leg holes (Heirisson Island), where Noongars would cross the river. This area, near the lowest and easiest crossing point on the Swan River (at Matagarup), was an important communication and trade centre between the coast and the islands. Whadjuck/Balardong custodian Tom Bennell referred to Perth as ”Beerit” meaning ”pathways” due to its importance for meetings, travel and trade.

This Adventure Lab Cache will take you on an approximate 12km journey around Wadjuk Boodjar  depending on the route you take, you may walk, ride, drive (parts) or use public transport to get around, we recommend you give yourself a good half of a day to complete the adventure. 

Photo credit: Parkacru

VIEWER ADVICE
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander viewers are advised that the following Adventure Lab Cache may contain images of people who have died.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e41f587f-521f-4e5c-84a5-4c033a4e4e69" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.025383" lon="115.81215">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-05-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrNtN-02</name>
  <desc>CaringForWater2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard</url>
  <urlname>Caring for Waterbirds : S2 Nesting Boxes by fairwaydrivers</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-289" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Caring for Waterbirds : S2 Nesting Boxes</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Caring for Waterbirds&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Nesting Boxes&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What creatures are the nesting boxes for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/854ee9e7-3283-4012-a236-77b0b3e76482" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Very near the birdwatching platform are some trees. in two of these are nesting boxes. On the box is a website. It is very interesting to read with lots of pictures.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6292440-19d6-4fcb-a1bb-d28838755318" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan Estuary and its foreshore provide habitats for a diversity of waterbirds. The sedges, samphires and trees around this area are essential for maintaining the health of the estuary and the waterbirds.The seagrass meadows provide a nursery habitat for fish and crustaceans. Dead and decaying seagrass provides food for invertebrates that are food for waterbirds. The shallow mudflats are exposed during low tide which may only be for a few hours each day. These mudflats hide mussel deposits and are home to worms, aquatic snails, insects and other minute crustaceans sought after by waterbirds.Other waterbirds trap small fish in the open water.
This adventure is best done on a lovely bike ride which will give you beautiful views of the Swan River and the many waterbirds. See how many different species you can count!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Isn't it exciting that we have these creatures in our neighbourhood. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.954267" lon="115.854733">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJj4A-02</name>
  <desc>CityIsWadjukC02</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC</url>
  <urlname>This city is Wadjuk Country : S02 Jeena middar boodjar by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909777" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>This city is Wadjuk Country : S02 Jeena middar boodjar</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;This city is Wadjuk Country&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Jeena middar boodjar &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What number is the building where the jeena middar were once held?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f94c8108-c201-4f9b-bff9-d980aeee98f8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jeena middar boodjar - gathering feet dancing on this land

Jeena middar (corroborees) are songs and dances that record the katijin, moort and boodjar of Aboriginal people. They were often held when families gathered together. 

In March 1833 two Minang Noongar people from Albany visited, a jeena middar was held to welcome them to Wadjuk boodjar. 

The Wadjuk Noongar prepared for the jeena middar by painting themselves with ritual patterns. The Wadjuk Noongars sang a kangaroo hunting song, led by Wadjuk Noongar boordiya (leader) Yagan the “master of ceremonies”

Buildings now stand on the ground that was once used for jeena middar, you will need to locate the building at the given coordinates.

Image credit: Western Australian Newspapers
Final image credit: Parkacru&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79cbb012-2a74-4686-afba-7d425fd49400" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The City of Perth is Wadjuk Boodjar (Country) covering the area between Gargatup, the place down by the hillside (in Kings Park), and Matagarup, the place of the leg holes (Heirisson Island), where Noongars would cross the river. This area, near the lowest and easiest crossing point on the Swan River (at Matagarup), was an important communication and trade centre between the coast and the islands. Whadjuck/Balardong custodian Tom Bennell referred to Perth as ”Beerit” meaning ”pathways” due to its importance for meetings, travel and trade.

This Adventure Lab Cache will take you on an approximate 12km journey around Wadjuk Boodjar  depending on the route you take, you may walk, ride, drive (parts) or use public transport to get around, we recommend you give yourself a good half of a day to complete the adventure. 

Photo credit: Parkacru

VIEWER ADVICE
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander viewers are advised that the following Adventure Lab Cache may contain images of people who have died.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2d4a37e8-263d-4070-b3d4-d55535c1a782" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9509" lon="115.85815">
