  <urlname>This city is Wadjuk Country : S01 Yagan Square by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909775" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>This city is Wadjuk Country : S01 Yagan Square</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;This city is Wadjuk Country&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Yagan Square&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TCIWC&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The tower’s 14 ‘reeds’ represent ____ ____ ____ of the Noongar people.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cdf70e80-0690-45a3-be42-02488c999c5b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yagan Square is a component of the Perth City Link in Perth, Western Australia which is situated between the Perth railway station, Horseshoe Bridge and the Perth Busport in the eastern part of the Perth City Link precinct, occupying 2.7 acres. 

Construction of the square began in February 2016, and it was opened on 3 March 2018.

Designed to acknowledge the significance to the indigenous people, the Noongar people of Perth, the land it is built on as well as the lakes that used to exist under what is now the Perth railway station and Wellington Street.

On the south-western corner of Yagan Square is a 45-metre-high "digital tower", with a wrap-around display screen 30 metres in diameter and 14 metres high with fourteen tall columns rising. 

The design of the Digital Tower, with its distinctive tall columns, is inspired by the bulrushes once found around the area up until the early 1800s. 

You will need to locate the plaque near the digital tower.

Photo credits: Parkacru&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79cbb012-2a74-4686-afba-7d425fd49400" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The City of Perth is Wadjuk Boodjar (Country) covering the area between Gargatup, the place down by the hillside (in Kings Park), and Matagarup, the place of the leg holes (Heirisson Island), where Noongars would cross the river. This area, near the lowest and easiest crossing point on the Swan River (at Matagarup), was an important communication and trade centre between the coast and the islands. Whadjuck/Balardong custodian Tom Bennell referred to Perth as ”Beerit” meaning ”pathways” due to its importance for meetings, travel and trade.

This Adventure Lab Cache will take you on an approximate 12km journey around Wadjuk Boodjar  depending on the route you take, you may walk, ride, drive (parts) or use public transport to get around, we recommend you give yourself a good half of a day to complete the adventure. 

Photo credit: Parkacru

VIEWER ADVICE
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander viewers are advised that the following Adventure Lab Cache may contain images of people who have died.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ee5bfa7-5ca9-42f3-bbc3-6274cf73b4f7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
