  <name>LBnmX8-01</name>
  <desc>PerthsTheatreS1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre</url>
  <urlname>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S1 State Theatre Centre of Western Australia by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-231" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth’s Theatre Scene : S1 State Theatre Centre of Western Australia</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth’s Theatre Scene&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;State Theatre Centre of Western Australia&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Theatre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look at the first panel. Which theatre group occupied the ground floor of the Arts House? (4 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b84ad7f2-3b6a-4892-9c1a-6784d11148b1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The State Theatre Centre of Western Australia is a theatre complex located within the Perth Cultural Centre.  The largest of three dedicated performance areas is the 575 seat Heath Ledger Theatre, named in honour of the late Academy Award winning actor. After an international design competition in 2005, attracting more than 40 designs worldwide, was won by Kerry Hill Architects, construction on the Theatre Centre began in late 2007. In July 2008 the Western Australian government announced that the complex would be named after Heath Ledger, however after a change of government, that decision was modified in December 2008 to apply only to the main auditorium. The centre was officially opened on 27 January 2011.

The coordinates will take you to a laneway running from the Perth Cultural Centre, past the Courtyard  to the Theatre Foyer (NB: access A/H is only via the Cultural Centre). Along the wall of the laneway is a stainless steel art installation called “Interval” by  Arif Satar and Audrey Fernandes-Satar.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ac167c3-0dba-4363-bb05-0e8eac02c57a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Since the time of Settlement, the Perth CBD has been home to a vibrant theatre scene and this short Adventure Lab will take you to some of this city’s key live performance locations, both historical and current. The seven locations are in close proximity and you should easily be able to complete the series within two hours. Take your time to appreciate some of the wonderful architecture of the contemporary theatres as well as the remnants of those lost to development. 

Given the building density within this area, the listed coordinates may not be as accurate as desired. However there should be sufficient information in each location’s description for you to find what you need once you have navigated to the general area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0a49e174-88e2-4dc1-875a-ee6536cb9254" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;CORRECT!

Congratulations. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.098017" lon="115.820083">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBW3tb-01</name>
  <desc>ExploringBibra1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Bibra Lake : S1 Mary Ann Tapper Park by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909959" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Bibra Lake : S1 Mary Ann Tapper Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Bibra Lake&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mary Ann Tapper Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BibraICNothing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Daniel and Rosina raised 12 children in a house called?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/540e10a8-4afb-41cc-a05a-44f596220361" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This area was initially owned by Benedict von Bibra to use as a camping place to shorten the trip between his two carpentry businesses in Perth and Fremantle. The land was then sold to a subsequent passive investor before Mary Ann Tapper purchased the land. Mary along with her son Daniel and wife Rosina 
starting out as market gardeners, Daniel eventually included dairy cows in order to supplement the families living. Eventually the site of their homestead became the center of the local community with a petrol pump, telephone exchange and post office. Tappers remained on this property until 1965 when it went into government ownership. The site you are now standing on is the location of the Tapper homestead, which included three houses, that of Mary-Ann, her son Daniel, and her daughter, Florence Korten, &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceffc579-0079-4b5f-a7e7-f423f8052655" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Bibra Lake.
Bibra Lake is fourth lake (going north to south) of a string of lakes which combined comprise the Beeliar Regional Park. Bibra Lake has many environmental, heritage and recreational values. Bibra Lake was first known as "Walliabup" but was later named "Bibra" after Benedict von Bibra who brought land in the area in 1843. 
Bibra lake has an interesting history of supporting a dairy farm, post office ,orchard and market garden. It was also home to a battery attached to the 66 Searchlight stationed here in World War 2 along with the regimental headquarters of the 116 Light Anti-Aircraft. This was the base for many women who served in the Australian Women's Army Service.
Today Bibra Lake offers many recreational activities whilst also providing a conservation reserve for our birds and wildlife. This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the area and can be completed by walking, riding or by taking the car but to really enjoy the area we recommend making use of the bike path.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79e7e4b8-1ecb-4dee-88bd-3b842e5cef2a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;We hope you enjoyed this little bit of history and the admired the lovely fig trees&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9533" lon="115.854283">
