   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;It feels like home&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Monkey tail trees&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Home"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many huge Norfolk pine trees form this small cluster? Numeral required.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cd5a89e-c7ad-43bf-8598-c35d8417b0fd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are two car park rest areas along Mounts Bay Road and both have large trees visible from a distance. At this particular location there is a bench seat shaded by a cluster of very large trees. They dropped foliage which are often affectionately known as monkey tails.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c63dde21-8349-4b09-931a-400ae9f36155" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Inner Perth spans some picturesque river locations. This adventure lab is best done by bike. 

Since the final physical cache is hidden west of all the virtual locations, the order has been fixed to east to west. In addition, this will allow for those travelling by car to park since all parking is on the river side of Mounts Bay Road.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Time to move on.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.953083" lon="115.851117">
  <ele>45.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-03</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu03</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S03 Court by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909865" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S03 Court</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Court&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What age was Sir Charles Court when he died (see nearby plaque)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/24c90592-6036-4979-9a46-b2fddcc60872" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The statue commemorates West Australian Premier Sir Charles Court (1911-2007). The statue was unveiled on the 29th September 2011 on the occasion of Sir Charles Court's 100th birthday. The statue is symbolically located on St Georges Terrace near Parliament House and the Woodside Building to highlight Sir Charles' role in the development of the North-West Shelf and Western Australia.

Sir Charles Court served as a Member of the WA Parliament for 29 years from 1974 until 1982 and is famed for his hand in WA's mining boom. Sir Charles was a strong advocate for WA and a man who defended the right of the State to manage its own affairs.

Sir Charles was also known for his divisive decisions including the close of the Perth-Fremantle railway service and his role in the Noonkanbah land-rights dispute which led to a low point between indigenous and government relations. Sir Charles Court died in 2007. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.963417" lon="115.8404">
  <ele>54.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-02</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve02</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S02 Mt Eliza Lookout by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909835" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S02 Mt Eliza Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mt Eliza Lookout&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was the black &amp; white photo on the left taken?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0efbb25c-f6be-4dd4-b9e0-102a2db025d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mt Eliza Lookout is situated within the Western Australian Botanic Garden. Officially opening in 1965, it is an 18 hectare site within the Kings Park displaying a collection of around 3000 species of Western Australian flora.

The lookout offers spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Scarp and can be enjoyed whilst also visiting the Boab Tree, Pioneer Women’s Memorial and the Federation Walkway.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/051761bb-2ce4-44f7-a436-d7777b0d9bd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.943717" lon="115.862583">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVSGR-05</name>
  <desc>ColonialNort904</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662</url>
  <urlname>Colonial Northbridge : S5 1904 Perth Mosque by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909915" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Colonial Northbridge : S5 1904 Perth Mosque</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Colonial Northbridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1904 Perth Mosque&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the colour of the domes?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/62f339cf-54b4-438b-987d-8259a104eb85" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Perth Mosque was designed and built by Din Mohammed in 1904, with funds raised by Muslim camel drivers who were originally from Pakistan. 

These men drove the camel teams, which carried supplies from Perth to the Goldfields and were vital to the survival of the mining communities in the early days of the boom.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/057569b0-a820-4f10-b853-2d1380813011" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This series takes you around some of the important historical sites in the colonial settlement of Perth. 

From the early market gardens tended by Chinese migrants to locations that became important in the gold rush of the 1890's. 

The five locations are an easy 1.6 km walk that can be completed in about half an hour but give yourself time to enjoy the area’s attractions. 


For more information about the history of the area:
https://www.vincent.wa.gov.au/Profiles/vincent/Assets/ClientData/Document-Centre/A_Brief_History/Perth.pdf&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d3232251-28ca-48a7-b718-a5fea5ca2974" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.949267" lon="115.855067">
  <ele>18.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-01</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu01</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S01 Koorden by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909861" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S01 Koorden</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Koorden&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Koorden is the Noongar word for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/35c5383a-0330-43e7-94e3-7a5436df1fab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Composed of six dramatic figures rendered in cast bronze, these powerful skeletal male figures face east, striding across the grass of Wellington Gardens. The designs originally stem from painted ceremonial markings found in historical photographs and have been reinterpreted by Rod Garlett in extensive consultation with elders in the local Aboriginal community.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.966317" lon="115.837267">
  <ele>59.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-04</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve04</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S04 Roe Gardens by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915735" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S04 Roe Gardens</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Roe Gardens&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year did J.S Roe prepare the original plan for Perth?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5c2356f5-fa61-48cb-9489-e54a7d5892ae" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Roe Gardens remains a hidden treasure at the end of the Western Australian Botanic Gardens. It offers spectacular views and is where the Place of Reflection can also be found.

A tranquil and shady setting, it features the Roe memorial which bears a bronze plate depicting one of the earliest maps of Perth. Built in 1956 and constructed of hewn granite in hexagonal form, it was named after John Septimus Roe, who was responsible for surveying and apportioning the land in Perth.



&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0aa62021-da54-48c7-8e2a-9ab790326d04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.952217" lon="115.849033">
  <ele>35.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-05</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander25</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S05 Barracks Arch by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000453" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S05 Barracks Arch</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Barracks Arch&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In the right hand is a plate with some text.  What is the word, in capitals, on the left and raised part of this plate?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/131b8cc3-8bd2-42a2-9c5f-6a245a2bfab9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built in 1866, Barracks Arch was originally the entrance to the Pensioner Barracks which housed the Pensioner Guards. These were the guards who came to Australia on convict ships that transported nearly 10,000 prisoners to Western Australia from 1850.

Originally based in Fremantle, most convict work later moved to Perth. This created a need for accommodation in Perth for the Pensioner Guards and their families. Architect Richard Roach Jewell was appointed to design an appropriate building. 

Today the Arch is one of Perth’s most iconic historic buildings, located at the top of St George’s Terrace, next to the Mitchell Freeway and in front of Parliament House. It not only represents Perth’s convict era, but shows how Western Australia slowly came to feel a need to protect its heritage buildings.

Find the sculpture in front of the arch.  There are some metal plates between the two hands.  You are interested only in the plate in the sculptures right hand.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.952217" lon="115.849067">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXeL5-02</name>
  <desc>PerthsSculptu02</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab</url>
  <urlname>Perth's Sculptures : S02 Photographer by SpatialRiq</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909863" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth's Sculptures : S02 Photographer</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SpatialRiq</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SpatialRiq</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth's Sculptures&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Photographer&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aspatiallab&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In the photographers hands is a quotation, what is the surname of person the quote is from?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/703155ce-adba-4db9-8e93-6601703387a9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The 'Unidentified Photographer' was created by WA artists Greg James and Anne Neil sits at the top end of St. George's Terrace just across from the Barracks Arch.

"The camera does not obscure
Only with hindsight can the future be lucidly inhabited; present
reality was once prophetic imagination.
Each site, each place is seen through all senses.
The impartial lens commits structure to the archives, but recognise
the power of sensitivity to reveal essential memory."

Another easy to find sculpture at the given coordinates.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c9e4a474-ff9e-4510-a2cb-0ea70769e8a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1989 the Western Australian state government established a "Percent for Art" policy. The scheme, administered by the Department of Culture and the Arts, requires that up to one percent of the construction budget for new public works over $2,000,000, be expended on public artwork.

Over 700 works have been installed under the state government scheme since 1989.

This tour will take you to only 10 scattered around the Perth CBD.  All should be easy to locate along with the necessary information to complete each of the sites.

Good luck&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you're underway.  On to the next stage!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9719" lon="115.830417">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-05</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve05</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S05 Dryandra Lookout by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915737" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S05 Dryandra Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dryandra Lookout&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many points on the leaf above your head?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3c81fb85-16b5-4998-be11-fb881b6d0015" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Dryandra Lookout was constructed in 2003 along the Law Walk on the edge of the Mt Eliza Escarpment.

The roof's rich patina and shape was designed to resemble the leaf of Dryandra sessilis, commonly known as Parrot Bush. The species has since been incorporated into the Banksia genus but the lookout has retained the name.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/969fec48-2c73-4522-bf02-f1189463057c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.952217" lon="115.848883">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-09</name>
  <desc>RedCatS09Barrac</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S09 Barracks Arch by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000423" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S09 Barracks Arch</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Barracks Arch&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the plaque under the archway, what is the last year mentioned?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5f9f75c0-4a29-4bb7-996b-daa53ea11e23" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Red Cat Stop 14 - Qv1

From stop 14, walk through the QV1 Plaza and up St georges tce.

Built in 1866 Barracks Arch was originally the entrance to a large building called the Pensioner Barracks.
The Barracks were gradually converted to offices for the Public Works Department between 1900 and 1904, becoming its headquarters in 1904. 
Notable occupants included C. Y. O'Connor, whose office was immediately above the arch.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here walk to the Parliament house location&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.964283" lon="115.836233">
  <ele>67.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-03</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve03</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S03 DNA Tower by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909837" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S03 DNA Tower</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;DNA Tower&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the plaque at the top, what feature is directly south of the Tower? (First word starts with M, Second word starts with W) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6b28462-6942-4055-a674-57e005e1398c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built on the highest point of the park in 1966, the DNA Tower is a white 15m high double helix staircase that has 101 steps and was inspired by a double staircase in the Château de Blois in France. Its design resembles the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) molecule. The paving below the DNA Tower is made with stones sent from 11 towns and 80 shires in Western Australia.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c1084de2-7c99-4cc7-85d7-76ea20dedb9b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9726" lon="115.8292">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBoayD-05</name>
  <desc>LIflhS5Eliza</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Home</url>
  <urlname>[L] It feels like home : S5 Eliza by .K.B.</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909811" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] It feels like home : S5 Eliza</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>.K.B.</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">.K.B.</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;It feels like home&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Eliza&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Home"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Year which this ceased to be Perth’s prime competition and recreational swimming venue.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f56d26b2-b58b-43a2-9598-793ed5ed1ca0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Well known across Perth for her frequent costume changes, Eliza stands ready to dive into the river. Look for a plaque which commemorates the Crawley Baths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c63dde21-8349-4b09-931a-400ae9f36155" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Inner Perth spans some picturesque river locations. This adventure lab is best done by bike. 

Since the final physical cache is hidden west of all the virtual locations, the order has been fixed to east to west. In addition, this will allow for those travelling by car to park since all parking is on the river side of Mounts Bay Road.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4e00e00a-b53b-450b-8c02-80d4045b1e33" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Head to the iconic blue boat house instead to log the final; a bison against a square wooden stake.at
S31 58.380 E115 49.602.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9418" lon="115.8607">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVSGR-04</name>
  <desc>ColonialNort897</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662</url>
  <urlname>Colonial Northbridge : S4 1897 Hotel Northbridge and surrounding shops by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909913" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Colonial Northbridge : S4 1897 Hotel Northbridge and surrounding shops</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Colonial Northbridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1897 Hotel Northbridge and surrounding shops&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Looking at the mosaics on the pillars of the building, which profession (job) was located at 201?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e41e0a1e-a4a5-448c-90c4-c71d1b227f23" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You are standing opposite the Hotel Northbridge.

The Hotel was built around 1897-1898 at the time of the early Gold Boom and originally known as the Royal Standard Hotel, which was an important local landmark. 

On this corner there are clues to the types of businesses that existed in the building on this side of the road, (197 - 205 Brisbane street). These can be seen on the mosaic murals on the pillars - such as a Chemist, Butcher, Barber, Fruiterer and Grocer.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/057569b0-a820-4f10-b853-2d1380813011" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This series takes you around some of the important historical sites in the colonial settlement of Perth. 

From the early market gardens tended by Chinese migrants to locations that became important in the gold rush of the 1890's. 

The five locations are an easy 1.6 km walk that can be completed in about half an hour but give yourself time to enjoy the area’s attractions. 


For more information about the history of the area:
https://www.vincent.wa.gov.au/Profiles/vincent/Assets/ClientData/Document-Centre/A_Brief_History/Perth.pdf&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ec3b5102-52da-4e12-b02a-87449bcc903a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!

Take the time to read the mosaic tiles for more history of the owners and types of shops.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0189" lon="115.806183">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2020-05-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrNtN-04</name>
  <desc>CaringForWater4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard</url>
  <urlname>Caring for Waterbirds : S4 Migratory Waders by fairwaydrivers</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909789" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Caring for Waterbirds : S4 Migratory Waders</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Caring for Waterbirds&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Migratory Waders&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What nutritious food makes the chicks grow rapidly?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/89355061-e2cb-4a12-b059-e7389c8445a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here is an interesting hands on learning tool. Spin it around and see what the migratory waders are doing now.These migratory waterbirds are amazing creatures to travel thousands of kilometers in their migration.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6292440-19d6-4fcb-a1bb-d28838755318" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan Estuary and its foreshore provide habitats for a diversity of waterbirds. The sedges, samphires and trees around this area are essential for maintaining the health of the estuary and the waterbirds.The seagrass meadows provide a nursery habitat for fish and crustaceans. Dead and decaying seagrass provides food for invertebrates that are food for waterbirds. The shallow mudflats are exposed during low tide which may only be for a few hours each day. These mudflats hide mussel deposits and are home to worms, aquatic snails, insects and other minute crustaceans sought after by waterbirds.Other waterbirds trap small fish in the open water.
This adventure is best done on a lovely bike ride which will give you beautiful views of the Swan River and the many waterbirds. See how many different species you can count!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;What a beautiful river. We need to look after it well.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9168" lon="115.910733">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2020-03-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBshQu-03</name>
  <desc>LocalHeroesThe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes</url>
  <urlname>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S3 The Rose Garden by Deefa71</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-113" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S3 The Rose Garden</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Deefa71</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Deefa71</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Rose Garden &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In who's memory did the residents of Bayswater erect the memorial? (2 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/93a7c068-8723-4a68-9b27-d792c82d2fda" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Rose Garden in Bayswater was very popular around Mothers Day. Set in Halliday Park, which includes a Lacrosse field, it is one of the best known features in the area.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8adb3a14-83ff-43a6-b9fa-7dc5c0472b8d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Having grown up in Western Australia, I have always been interested in the stories behind the people listed on the suburban war memorials. 
These are ordinary people, who stood up to be counted in extraordinary times and made the ultimate sacrifice.
This series is a roughly 10 km in length and encompasses a small number of memorials, large and small, old and new that were commissioned either by the people of the community in the area or the local branch of the R.S.L.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bbe53f11-8282-45ee-9029-2d78b2f336d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.951683" lon="115.847533">
  <ele>45.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX6JW-10</name>
  <desc>RedCatS10Parlia</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour</url>
  <urlname>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S10 Parliament House by Bristag</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000425" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Red Cat Adventure : S10 Parliament House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Bristag</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Bristag</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Red Cat Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Parliament House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RedTour&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the righthand side of the entrance steps is the foundation stone, what is the last year mentioned on it?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/60197333-43e1-4075-8d74-b0b6dd5ba7da" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; Red Cat stop 15, Hay St Parliament

The first stage of Parliament House was completed in 1904. The eastern wing was constructed between 1958 and 1964. The staged construction of the building reflects two architectural styles - the Federation style of the early 1900s and more classical lines and columns of the 1960s.

The grounds of Parliament house has evolved with changes to the City and Parliament house building works. In recent times many native plants from all over the state have been incorporated.

Parliament House has a self-guided tour of the external architectural features of the building, and the Parliament House gardens and formal tours are available Mondays 10.30 am Thursdays 10.30 am and Fridays 2.00 pm. Except public holidays.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b086e18-1940-4fa8-ae54-650e6f68b64d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure takes you on a tour in the Perth CBD.

This tour takes advantage of the free to ride Red Cat Bus route, there are historic buildings, side tours, museums and parkland to checkout along the way.

If you are driving to the CBD, the adventure can be completed in any order, so just park up and hop on the bus anywhere along the route.
However I recommend catching the train into Perth CBD and commencing the adventure at the Red Cat Bus stop 24 - Wellington St Perth Station. From this stop the first location is St Mary's Cathedral.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;From here walk to the Red Cat stop 15 and ride the Cat back to the beginning, thank you very much and I hope you enjoyed my tour.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95845" lon="115.839683">
  <ele>70.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-09</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve09</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S09 Rio Tinto Naturescape by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-266" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S09 Rio Tinto Naturescape</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Rio Tinto Naturescape&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What month was the Rio Tinto Naturescape opened?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/121ae12c-238b-4578-b9aa-520c005106ca" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Rio Tinto Naturescape is a dedicated nature space that encourages learning and discovery givings kids a  ‘bush experience’ in the heart of Perth.

Surrounded by natural bushland and featuring large aerial walkways, a creek and waterhole for wading in, tunnels, rocks and a web of climbing ropes.  Covering six hectares it can be explored on accessible paths, bridges and boardwalks.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/211292e0-ba61-4f7d-b15f-8854ac50e3b0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95245" lon="115.844417">
  <ele>59.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-04</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander24</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S04 Old Perth Observatory by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000451" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S04 Old Perth Observatory</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Old Perth Observatory&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last 4 digits on the bottom line that has an arrow pointing to the left?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/10773ee5-ce8c-42e8-ae32-0f3ed1a7d75c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This 1896 building, designed by government architect George Temple-Poole is all that remains of Western Australia's first Government Observatory which also included offices and meridian and equatorial telescopes.  Several smaller out-buildings housed equipment to measure the weather and the one o'clock gun allowed locals to standardise their clocks.

Light pollution caused the relocation of the telescopes to Bickley in the 1960s.  The site continued to be used as government offices and has been the central offices of the National Trust of Western Australia since 1985.

The building is currently under renovation.  Near the coordinates and behind a construction fence is an entrance sign pointing right.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.940767" lon="115.858583">
  <ele>28.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVSGR-02</name>
  <desc>ColonialNort885</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662</url>
  <urlname>Colonial Northbridge : S2 1885 - The Moreton Bay Figs by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909909" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Colonial Northbridge : S2 1885 - The Moreton Bay Figs</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Colonial Northbridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1885 - The Moreton Bay Figs&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What building was originally constructed here c1880? ___________ House. (The plaques beneath your feet will also tell you)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b8440030-9015-482a-b5a9-4fb9632f2e3d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From Lee Hops cottage, heading north-east across the park towards Palmerston Street you will see three large Moreton Bay Figs.  

These sat behind one of the finest heritage buildings in the area that was demolished during refurbishment of the park in 1965. A paved footprint showing the position of the outer walls confirms that this was the largest residence in the area when it was built in 1885. 

At the time of its construction, it had its own fountain and tennis court. Later it became a private school and then a nursing home before eventually being demolished to open Robertson Park to Palmerston Street. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/057569b0-a820-4f10-b853-2d1380813011" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This series takes you around some of the important historical sites in the colonial settlement of Perth. 

From the early market gardens tended by Chinese migrants to locations that became important in the gold rush of the 1890's. 

The five locations are an easy 1.6 km walk that can be completed in about half an hour but give yourself time to enjoy the area’s attractions. 


For more information about the history of the area:
https://www.vincent.wa.gov.au/Profiles/vincent/Assets/ClientData/Document-Centre/A_Brief_History/Perth.pdf&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/719473b4-ef47-4a60-ad7e-e83bae070c68" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!

If you look closely at the paved perimeter you will see additional metal plaques that outline the history of the house!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.938283" lon="115.860383">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVSGR-03</name>
  <desc>ColonialNorthb3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662</url>
  <urlname>Colonial Northbridge : S3 Third Swamp- Hyde Park by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909911" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Colonial Northbridge : S3 Third Swamp- Hyde Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Colonial Northbridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Third Swamp- Hyde Park &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many lakes are in Hyde park? (digit) *If you are unsure, read the Adventure Lab location description!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3735d2d6-f5fc-4ba9-a431-bba8fd19092b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Near to Robertson park is an Aboriginal hunting and settlement area originally known as Third swamp. 

Later a camping ground was established for the bullock waggoners and men on their way to the Goldfields. 

In 1898 the site was redesigned as a park by Lyall Hall, becoming Hyde Park, which included two lakes with island bird sanctuaries in the middle. 

In subsequent years, shelter sheds, playgrounds, a rotunda, picnic and a barbeque area were all added and it remains a very popular local park.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/057569b0-a820-4f10-b853-2d1380813011" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This series takes you around some of the important historical sites in the colonial settlement of Perth. 

From the early market gardens tended by Chinese migrants to locations that became important in the gold rush of the 1890's. 

The five locations are an easy 1.6 km walk that can be completed in about half an hour but give yourself time to enjoy the area’s attractions. 


For more information about the history of the area:
https://www.vincent.wa.gov.au/Profiles/vincent/Assets/ClientData/Document-Centre/A_Brief_History/Perth.pdf&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c6cead01-b2a4-405b-b4b8-ec750797dba7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!

If you have time, it is beautiful to walk around and also pick up some park caches...&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.941083" lon="115.855617">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVSGR-01</name>
  <desc>ColonialNort852</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662</url>
  <urlname>Colonial Northbridge : S1 1852 Lake Henderson- now Robertson Park by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909907" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Colonial Northbridge : S1 1852 Lake Henderson- now Robertson Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Colonial Northbridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1852 Lake Henderson- now Robertson Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e92d6ec-b958-41cf-ae88-ecde36fdd662&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;As you stand by the front gate, look above you on the crossbeam. What month did the plaque commemorate the conservation of Lee Hop's Cottage?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b935dd1-af2f-4c36-bde6-1d6d3735dfa6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A natural wetland known originally as Lake Henderson, it was first drained in 1852 for market gardening. James Fox, an ex-convict, worked the land here and by 1893, when it was sold to John Chipper, several farm buildings had been erected. 

Later, the land was sold to Dr Daniel Kenny who built a four roomed brick and iron cottage in 1903 on the south-western corner. This has become known as Lee Hop’s Cottage, named after one of the Chinese men who lived there and worked on the 18-acre market garden. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/057569b0-a820-4f10-b853-2d1380813011" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This series takes you around some of the important historical sites in the colonial settlement of Perth. 

From the early market gardens tended by Chinese migrants to locations that became important in the gold rush of the 1890's. 

The five locations are an easy 1.6 km walk that can be completed in about half an hour but give yourself time to enjoy the area’s attractions. 


For more information about the history of the area:
https://www.vincent.wa.gov.au/Profiles/vincent/Assets/ClientData/Document-Centre/A_Brief_History/Perth.pdf&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/56a8ccbd-1390-4189-acbd-ddea59ecd723" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!

To the left is Halvorsen Hall, which used to be home of the Perth Marching Band and to the right is an AID's memorial. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.930217" lon="115.8708">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2020-03-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBshQu-05</name>
  <desc>LocalHeroesThe5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes</url>
  <urlname>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S5 Mount Lawley by Deefa71</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-115" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S5 Mount Lawley</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Deefa71</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Deefa71</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mount Lawley &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the missing words on the plaques on the nearby trees? The R.S.L. BICENTENNIAL ----- --- TREE (2 Words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/61a5d6ca-8f6c-4870-a62b-813ad819ee68" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a beautiful memorial in one of Perth's premier locations. Mt Lawley has some of the best Art Deco buildings in WA. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8adb3a14-83ff-43a6-b9fa-7dc5c0472b8d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Having grown up in Western Australia, I have always been interested in the stories behind the people listed on the suburban war memorials. 
These are ordinary people, who stood up to be counted in extraordinary times and made the ultimate sacrifice.
This series is a roughly 10 km in length and encompasses a small number of memorials, large and small, old and new that were commissioned either by the people of the community in the area or the local branch of the R.S.L.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a2cf3e28-1e66-425a-ae62-091336f6a413" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95375" lon="115.838117">
  <ele>57.0</ele>
  <time>2020-01-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN7tp-10</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve10</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S10 Lotterywest Family Area by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-267" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S10 Lotterywest Family Area</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lotterywest Family Area&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did the 460 Squadron carry out their first operation?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/60b7657f-1b6c-44d6-a120-06915e3ac3b0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Lotterywest Family Area is located on the northern boundary of the Kings Park and is most suited as a family and recreation space. 

Situated within the precinct is Stickybeaks Cafe and the historic Hale Oval which has been in use since 1897.   The Ivey Watson Playground is also located here and developed especially for children aged six and under.

The co-ordinates will take you to a plaque dedicated to the RAAF 460 Heavy Bomber Squadron.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8002ba9b-d9a1-4ee9-8481-1b9d161b7b9c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.017617" lon="115.7992">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-05-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrNtN-05</name>
  <desc>CaringForWater5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard</url>
  <urlname>Caring for Waterbirds : S5 Wetland Bush Walk by fairwaydrivers</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909791" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Caring for Waterbirds : S5 Wetland Bush Walk</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fairwaydrivers</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Caring for Waterbirds&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wetland Bush Walk&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cherryorchard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There are three waterbirds shown. Name the one on the left.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cab8173c-5203-4ce1-88ce-77c1d381386b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a detour through this area of natural wetland bush. You will need to take care you do not take dieback spores in with you, so clean your shoes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6292440-19d6-4fcb-a1bb-d28838755318" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan Estuary and its foreshore provide habitats for a diversity of waterbirds. The sedges, samphires and trees around this area are essential for maintaining the health of the estuary and the waterbirds.The seagrass meadows provide a nursery habitat for fish and crustaceans. Dead and decaying seagrass provides food for invertebrates that are food for waterbirds. The shallow mudflats are exposed during low tide which may only be for a few hours each day. These mudflats hide mussel deposits and are home to worms, aquatic snails, insects and other minute crustaceans sought after by waterbirds.Other waterbirds trap small fish in the open water.
This adventure is best done on a lovely bike ride which will give you beautiful views of the Swan River and the many waterbirds. See how many different species you can count!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Good job. You have completed all the tasks on your adventure. We hope it was fun and you learned about our wonderful river and its wildlife.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
