  <name>LBN7tp-08</name>
  <desc>KingsParkAdve08</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark</url>
  <urlname>Kings Park Adventure : S08 May Circle by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915743" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kings Park Adventure : S08 May Circle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;May Circle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many memorial plaques were placed in 1919?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/956f2520-040e-4452-acf5-be56275330aa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Honour Avenue plaques sit poignantly against a backdrop of eucalypt trees. Each bears details of service personnel who died during war service and were either buried overseas or have no known graves.

The first Honour Avenue plaques were dedicated to 404 fallen soldiers in August 1919, on the fifth anniversary of the outbreak of World War I. There are currently now around 1800 plaques along the Honour Avenues in Kings Park. 

May Circle is the site of the initial dedication, and where plaques are added to the Honour Avenues.  The most recent dedication service was held in 2019.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/15e2205a-a7c4-4b3b-88ed-390b04ef7e2c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.962117" lon="115.824717">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
