   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kings Park Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;May Drive Parkland&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/kingspark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many millimetres per year do the Stromatolites grow?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/397e9ded-f4e2-415c-ac4f-b2d5240788b0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland is one of the most popular precincts in Kings Park and Botanic Garden. With rolling lawns, great play areas, free barbecues, a café and beautiful gardens, it is perfect for gatherings of families and friends.

Situated with the parkland is the Vietnam War Memorial, Outdoor Cinema and the Zamia café.  Have fun following dinosaur footprints, discovering bird species and exploring Lycopod Island.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c4b5e2d1-4e09-477b-b926-93ab6865c238" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kings Park is one of the most popular visitor destinations in Western Australia, with spectacular views of the Perth Waterways and Darling Range. Spread over 990 acres, the park is a mixture of grassed parkland, botanical gardens and natural bushland on Mount Eliza. Two-thirds of the grounds are conserved as native bushland. 
Previously known as Perth Park, it officially opened in 1895.  Its name was changed to King's Park in 1901 (dropping the apostrophe in later years).  


This adventure lab cache will highlight some of the special places that Kings Park is famous for.  We hope you enjoy visiting the locations chosen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/36cdb428-e925-46b3-b763-a1ef11e8878e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.898517" lon="115.971317">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2020-03-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBshQu-01</name>
  <desc>LocalHeroesThe1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes</url>
  <urlname>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S1 Stirling Square by Deefa71</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-111" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac  : S1 Stirling Square</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Deefa71</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Deefa71</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Local Heroes - The Legends of Anzac &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stirling Square &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/LocalHeroes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last word of the psalm on the plaque? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f1858fb9-6d5a-482b-902e-a6357c648b6c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Surrounded by the beautiful old buildings that are remains of the Guildford Town Centre, Stirling Square contains a memorial that is framed by the silent guns. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8adb3a14-83ff-43a6-b9fa-7dc5c0472b8d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Having grown up in Western Australia, I have always been interested in the stories behind the people listed on the suburban war memorials. 
These are ordinary people, who stood up to be counted in extraordinary times and made the ultimate sacrifice.
This series is a roughly 10 km in length and encompasses a small number of memorials, large and small, old and new that were commissioned either by the people of the community in the area or the local branch of the R.S.L.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a3c49ded-85fe-4366-a124-3583059000c2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done. Don't forget that Alfred's Burgers is just the other side of the rail tracks. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.897833" lon="115.971133">
  <ele>14.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-04</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo04</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S04 Guildford by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-194" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S04 Guildford</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Guildford&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did the rainfall observations start?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/66f28e2b-2598-4d25-bc5f-c30e402bd0e4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;13.4km by road from Perth

The history of the suburb of Guildford had its beginnings in 1827 when Captain James Stirling was despatched by Governor Darling of New South Wales to explore part of the West Coast of Australia. Whilst exploring the Swan River, Stirling was favourably impressed by the fertile alluvial flats around the area now known as Guildford, and replenished his water supplies from Success Spring at Success Hill on the west bank of the Swan River at Bassendean. The junction of the Swan and Helena Rivers was also chosen as the site for a town, and the first surveys were carried out in the Town of Guildford by HC Sutherland in late 1829. The name was chosen after the town of Guildford, in Surrey, England, where James and Ellen were married.  Their property was named “Woodbridge”, which was probably the choice of Ellen Stirling, as she came from 'Woodbridge' in Surrey.

At the coordinates you will find a plaque in the ground within the square of bricks.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.897733" lon="115.97195">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF84Z-02</name>
  <desc>SSVS2GuildfordG</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan</url>
  <urlname>Settling in the Swan Valley : S2 Guildford Goal by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-107" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Settling in the Swan Valley : S2 Guildford Goal</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Settling in the Swan Valley&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Guildford Goal&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Nearby you will see a sign for the Curfew Bell, what year was it stolen? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0fbc12d7-928b-4270-814e-1e493659e2f1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Guildford is situated on a peninsular formed by the meeting of the Swan and Helena Rivers.  In Colonial times, its strategic importance was as a river port, servicing agricultural expansion in the Colony.  The layout of the town copied English designs of a central village square and an Anglican church.
 In 1841 the Guildford colonial gaol, was largely built by convict labour and is significant as an example of a public building dating from the early decades of colonial settlement in the State. The building has since been renovated  and is now part of a museum. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc1cff31-a979-47e8-849a-54b605eddcf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Swan Valley, known for its Viticulture, Breweries and its Tourism.  Follow Captain James Stirling's journey as he made his way along the Swan River in 1827 to settle a colony.  
Along the way there is plenty to see, get your taste buds working and sample some produce from well known establishments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4316c626-5daf-4f5a-a84d-5e2d5c2b3e89" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Thats another one done - Good job!!  back into your mode of transport. Dont forget to check out all of the old houses along the way.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.897367" lon="115.960517">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF84Z-03</name>
  <desc>SSVS3FishMarket</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan</url>
  <urlname>Settling in the Swan Valley : S3 Fish Market Square by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-108" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Settling in the Swan Valley : S3 Fish Market Square</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Settling in the Swan Valley&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fish Market Square&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a plaque on the limestone wall what is the last word of the first paragraph?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19a0101a-f290-4395-bb67-bb9e04ed47a7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Planned in 1829 by assistant surveyor Henry Charles Sutherland as a town market for cattle, sheep, poultry and pigs but only ever used as a fish market, so the name stayed. It's also a great area for a picnic our to go kayaking.
On the other side of the river is Success Hill where captain James Stirling in 1827 found signs of fresh water.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc1cff31-a979-47e8-849a-54b605eddcf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Swan Valley, known for its Viticulture, Breweries and its Tourism.  Follow Captain James Stirling's journey as he made his way along the Swan River in 1827 to settle a colony.  
Along the way there is plenty to see, get your taste buds working and sample some produce from well known establishments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2defb382-0728-4585-af7f-335e3dd4683c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;You did it!
As you travel to your next destination you could look out for fresh produce from road side stalls ( seasonal of course)&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.89735" lon="115.973267">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRBas-04</name>
  <desc>ParanormalPert4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth</url>
  <urlname>Paranormal Perth : S4 Rose and Crown by Barrikon</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909759" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Paranormal Perth : S4 Rose and Crown</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barrikon</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barrikon</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Paranormal Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Rose and Crown&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Located in a plaque outside the hotel, Thomas who built the Rose and Crown?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6cd4dd9-c5cc-4874-b1c9-89cd9a85c1d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1841 the Rose and Crown became WA’s the first operating hotel. Linked to the Swan River via an underground tunnel, alcohol and supplies were delivered direct from boats through the tunnel to the hotel. Whilst history can’t explain the reasons, staff and owners experience unexplained noises. 

Tales of a legless ghost wandering the establishment and a poltergeist playing havoc in the owner’s quarters have spawned. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8dc1ada0-08ea-4e7e-964c-57a7b3bf56ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stories of lost souls, destitute apparitions, shadowy figures with unfinished business and ethereal beings providing protection, fill history books and live within myths across the world. Perth is no different. 

Tales of ghostly encounters can be found across the city and by partaking on this paranormal hunt you may experience your very own unexplained event.

Your adventure covers approximately 53kms and is available anytime however to fully experience possibilities of the afterlife, it is suggested you wait until darkness falls. A good torch will be needed if venturing out after dark.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cd08b189-30cb-43e1-b71e-e49ad1b6cb67" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.93625" lon="115.846167">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-02</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo02</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S02 Leederville by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-192" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S02 Leederville</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Leederville&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the stock code on the plate affixed to the pole?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9ebbc98e-9d21-41d2-98e4-c11a18bd5bac" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3.8km by road from Perth

Leederville

Leederville is named after William Leeder, the first landowner in the area. He arrived in the colony on the 'Rockingham' in 1830, and in 1833 bought land near Lake Monger. Private subdivision began about 1890 and the expanding district was already known as Leederville in April 1895 when it was declared as a Roads Board District. It was gazetted as a municipality on 3 May 1896.

You are looking for a pole on the edge of the park SE from the sculpture shown in the image.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9364" lon="115.840833">
