 <wpt lat="-32.012" lon="115.78695">
  <ele>16.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLwUn-02</name>
  <desc>LMelvilleHisto2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S2 Point Walter Recreation Reserve by Chwiliwr</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002501" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S2 Point Walter Recreation Reserve</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Historical Places of Interest.&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Point Walter Recreation Reserve&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Date stone was laid to mark the commencement of the Point Water Redevelopment Project (As written)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d1837f01-fa22-4d13-8fba-2dd8fb3f88dc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In  1827, Captain James Stirling in his exploration of the Swan River to identify a suitable site for settlement,  named Point Walter after his brother, Walter. Land was acquired in 1830 by the first European settlers Lionel Lukin and Alfred Waylen.  Waylen built a villa at Point Walter in 1830, and acquired a further 700 acres in 1831 extending his grant from  Point Walter as far east along the Swan River frontage to Alfred Cove. Popular  access  by  the  community saw  the  area  used  for  camping, swimming, picnicking and crabbing.   A  jetty was subsequently constructed since access  was  solely by water; ferries and yachting parties frequented the Point.

In 1907 (1895?) the State Government purchased Point Walter, declaring the site as an  A-Class  Reserve. In 1912, the Melville Roads Board was appointed to manage the Reserve.  The Reserve was officially  opened in 1914 on 30 November. 

Today, Point Walter Reserve is a popular well-maintained public recreational area.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e5c1644-f91d-452f-826e-26100c031ecd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Melville has had a long association with the Swan River that borders its northern boundaries.

This adventure takes you on a trip along the Swan River visiting some historical places of interest along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/af27dbe0-dc10-41ed-9dcd-de70ad704aed" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.
Enjoy the park and amenities nearby.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
