   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Midland Workshops&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Workers Wall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d20f283a-60dc-489d-8931-e0d5ceced025"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d20f283a-60dc-489d-8931-e0d5ceced025&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On a nearby plaque ( look low) Who was the Project Manager?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/561ce9b6-0f47-40ff-9bb1-ae5d56b1ef5c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This wall is made up of many brick that have been dedicated to some of those who worked at the Railway Workshops&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/202280b3-e399-46f4-a269-0b5a164c92e6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Midland Workshops.   Between 1902 and 1904 the Western Australian Government Railway Workshops were relocated to Midland.
The Midland Railway Workshops ran for close to a century on the Eastern fringe of Perth, Western Australia.

Large and multifaceted, the Workshops were vital to the development and maintenance of the West Australian rail system, and a crucial training ground for skilled tradespeople. Their closure in 1994 brought to an end not only a specific industrial complex but also a complex social community.

Despite the harsh, dirty and often dangerous working conditions, many thousands of former workers regretted the loss of the traditions, camaraderie and pride in workmanship that characterised their Midland working lives.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a906d183-b12c-4c6f-8a9e-26dd5ae0cd92" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Take your time here, you never know you may have a relative that worked here.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.893383" lon="116.004117">
  <ele>16.0</ele>
  <time>2021-07-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYcC6-02</name>
  <desc>LMidlandWorksh2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d20f283a-60dc-489d-8931-e0d5ceced025</url>
  <urlname>[L] Midland Workshops : S2 Peace Statue by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002511" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Midland Workshops : S2 Peace Statue</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Midland Workshops&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Peace Statue&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d20f283a-60dc-489d-8931-e0d5ceced025"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d20f283a-60dc-489d-8931-e0d5ceced025&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The peace Memorial commemorates how many mens lives?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bfcdf44b-1ba2-4dc4-90ab-e77268c9c9a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; During World War I a large number of the Workshops' men joined the armed forces and they are commemorated by the Peace Statue on the site.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/202280b3-e399-46f4-a269-0b5a164c92e6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Midland Workshops.   Between 1902 and 1904 the Western Australian Government Railway Workshops were relocated to Midland.
The Midland Railway Workshops ran for close to a century on the Eastern fringe of Perth, Western Australia.

Large and multifaceted, the Workshops were vital to the development and maintenance of the West Australian rail system, and a crucial training ground for skilled tradespeople. Their closure in 1994 brought to an end not only a specific industrial complex but also a complex social community.

Despite the harsh, dirty and often dangerous working conditions, many thousands of former workers regretted the loss of the traditions, camaraderie and pride in workmanship that characterised their Midland working lives.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02d6f9f6-34d5-4470-9fde-cb55461d7b3a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done!  Its only a short stroll to the next one, get going!!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9302" lon="115.841767">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2021-01-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEMaj-03</name>
  <desc>OxfordStripS3Ki</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/369b029f-0e2d-4f72-8ffd-2de06a2a60c4</url>
  <urlname>The Oxford Strip : S3 Kids at play by .K.B.</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909817" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Oxford Strip : S3 Kids at play</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>.K.B.</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">.K.B.</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Oxford Strip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Kids at play&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/369b029f-0e2d-4f72-8ffd-2de06a2a60c4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/369b029f-0e2d-4f72-8ffd-2de06a2a60c4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What animal appears on the artwork? General name only.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8bc51e94-5715-43db-9e26-2dff626fff74" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Plenty of private residences off strip have their own art on display. Tennyson Street is one such street. Children in the street contributed to this fantastic wall art. The next street south, Gawley, has some retro work too.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/483ff958-72c2-43a0-bd81-da6995831721" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Oxford Street runs from Mount Hawthorn at the northern end to Leederville at the southern end. The strip is most famous for its cafes. Quite literally, each block has at least one. As is customary for areas with cafe atmosphere, street art abounds. I’ve picked out 5 locations but there are many more to enjoy. A bike lane does run down the length of the strip on both the eastern and western sides; not the best of designs so use at your own peril.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/adb2df62-0fbe-484c-b354-7710e65c3387" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Time to head south.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.891817" lon="116.001683">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBxh8r-02</name>
  <desc>EotLS2MidlandSt</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL</url>
  <urlname>End of the Line : S2 Midland Station by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909853" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>End of the Line : S2 Midland Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;End of the Line&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Midland Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What transcontinental railway, named for the oceans it joins, shares the line between East Perth &amp; Midland? ( _ _ _ _ _ _ - _ _ _ _ _ _ _ )&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/77e899d7-7e62-4aa0-8fea-a1a520799864" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Midland Station is the terminus of the Midland line for the Perth Rail Network, however the rails continue much further to the east.

It is located 16.0km from Perth and was opened on October 8th 1968&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b725a41a-cce1-431d-909c-cab48dd07c51" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Please be aware the locations for this Adventure Lab are quite a distance from the map marker, make sure you check your nearest Lab point before commencing.

The metropolitan Perth rail network is comprised of five major lines connecting the four Metropolitan Development Corridors and Fremantle to the Perth CBD.

Over the coming years the network is set to expand even further to accomade travelers from Ellenbrook, Forrestfield and extending lines past Armadale to Byford and continuing north to Yanchep.

This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the various lines of the Perth Rail Network. You are more than welcome to catch the trains to the stops (it can be done in a single day) if you wish but this is not a requirement, in fact all information can be gained and answered without entering the stations themselves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58d96b3f-b572-440d-9225-bebce0377076" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The clue you need for the bonus final is:
 H&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.889033" lon="115.987083">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF84Z-01</name>
  <desc>SSVS1Woodbridge</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan</url>
  <urlname>Settling in the Swan Valley : S1 Woodbridge House by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-106" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Settling in the Swan Valley : S1 Woodbridge House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Settling in the Swan Valley&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Woodbridge House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is an item nearby that was used in bygone days.  What is it?  ( dont over think the name!  starts with R)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8d50a736-87c8-42ee-bdc4-6eb6a5aeecb2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1827 Captain James Stirling identified this land as desirable and built a small cottage for his family. 
 The farmland was later leased.
Woodbridge House was built in 1885 by Charles Harper and Fanny de burgh. The house has been used for many purposes and was later listed as a Heritage building in 1968 by the National Trust.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc1cff31-a979-47e8-849a-54b605eddcf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Swan Valley, known for its Viticulture, Breweries and its Tourism.  Follow Captain James Stirling's journey as he made his way along the Swan River in 1827 to settle a colony.  
Along the way there is plenty to see, get your taste buds working and sample some produce from well known establishments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7504fef5-6792-4d85-b1e1-b61a2f2f2c00" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done!  
Back into your choice of transport and make your way to Guildford. Don't forget to check out the coffee strip along the 'way&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.91845" lon="116.07835">
  <ele>150.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrZEi-04</name>
  <desc>RailwayReserve4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020</url>
  <urlname>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S4 Darlington Station by TeamBarritts</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909751" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S4 Darlington Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>TeamBarritts</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">TeamBarritts</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Railway Reserves Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Darlington Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the given coordinates you will see 4 small green signs, what is written on the first one closest to the station sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6ba58573-4da0-40be-ab83-f9f022a21417" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Darlington developed as a locality from the establishment of the Darlington Winery in the late nineteenth century.  Unlike Glen Forrest and Greenmount, Darlington was unplanned. 

From 1890 to 1954, Darlington was served by the Mundaring Loop on the Eastern Railway, which bisected the town.  Darlington was included in "picnic" and "excursion" train itineraries in the 1930s and 1940s, also making it a popular holiday destination.  In 1966, the line was officially closed by an act of parliament and the tracks removed. 

There is an information board located near the given coordinates which will add a lot more explanation to this location and its history.  Please take the time to read it as it is well worth the effort.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84cc0cf3-8d28-4e6d-88c4-bbebe35387d5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Railway Reserves Heritage Trail follows the route of the old Eastern Railway and is unique in providing a 41 kilometer loop entirely on railway formations. This extensive trail passes through quaint hills communities including Darlington, Parkerville and Mundaring, and covers a wide range of terrains with scenic views and numerous points of historical and natural significance.

This adventure will take you to five different points along the trail and should only take a few hours to complete.  Most can be reached by driving to nearby parking areas with a short stroll to the required locations.  The longest walk is approximately 2 klm round trip between locations 1 and 2.  These trails are well kept and easily accessible to all ages and levels of fitness.

Mobile reception can be patchy along the trail in certain spots, so we have increased the geofence to ensure all relevant information and data is available to you.  Always wear appropriate footwear, hat, sunscreen and carry enough water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1f860c8a-091a-4105-ac11-621b77b4aa1a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great work, now to the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.968633" lon="115.801567">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBmb5i-02</name>
  <desc>DeadPolitician2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians</url>
  <urlname>The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta : S2 John Curtin by SVKR_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909795" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta : S2 John Curtin</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SVKR_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SVKR_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;John Curtin&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On one side of the Curtin memorial is a stone commemorating Stan Macleod.  What is the last word on this stone?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bf0c9078-73ae-4af8-8afb-1566f8370333" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John Curtin (8 January 1885 – 5 July 1945) was an Australian politician who served as the 14th Prime Minister of Australia from 1941 until his death in 1945. He led the country for the majority of World War II, including all but the last few weeks of the war in the Pacific. He was the leader of the Australian Labor Party (ALP) from 1935 to 1945, and its longest serving leader until Gough Whitlam. Curtin's leadership skills and personal character were acclaimed by his political contemporaries. He is frequently cited as one of Australia's greatest prime ministers.

Curtin joined the Labor Party at a young age and was also involved with the Victorian Socialist Party. He became state secretary of the Timberworkers' Union in 1911 and federal president in 1914.

Curtin was a leader of the "No" campaign during the 1916 referendum on overseas conscription, and was briefly gaoled for refusing to attend a compulsory medical examination. He moved to Perth in 1917 to become the editor of the Westralian Worker,&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b9e0ea1a-1329-4b63-a108-35203c6c0594" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Karrakatta Cemetery first opened for burials in 1899. This historic cemetery is the final resting place of many thousands of Western Australians. Monuments and headstones bear many famous names including those of university founders, writers and historians, state premiers and other historic individuals.

The cemetery is centrally located just seven kilometres west of Perth city centre and is easily accessible by car or train.

Stately stands of cypress trees – a hallmark of Karrakatta – flank the main driveway off Railway Road and preside over beautiful garden areas in the historic heart of the cemetery.

There are two walk trails that will lead you some of the resting places of people in our history that now call Karrakatta Cemetery home.  They can be located at this website:
https://www.mcb.wa.gov.au/our-cemeteries/karrakatta-cemetery/historical-walk-trails

This Adventure Lab will take you to the final resting place of 5 politicians who have left their mark on the State of Western Australia
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fcda993e-546c-4ca1-8e3f-a6ecd7106667" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Did you know that on 14 August 2005 a bronze statue of John Curtin was unveiled in front of Fremantle Town Hall by Premier Geoff Gallop?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.028133" lon="115.777867">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLwUn-01</name>
  <desc>LMelvilleHisto1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S1 Bicton Baths and Bicton Quarantine Station by Chwiliwr</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002499" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Historical Places of Interest. : S1 Bicton Baths and Bicton Quarantine Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Historical Places of Interest.&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bicton Baths and Bicton Quarantine Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CHWILIWR1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the topmost word to the left of the Barge Shipwreck information sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/874503f4-5ac3-409c-a515-babddea1f0f0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the late nineteenth century and early twentieth century, the Bicton foreshore was a popular public recreation place, a use which continues today.  The land at the Bicton foreshore was granted to John Hole Duffield in 1830.  Duffield named the place Bicton after Bicton near Exeter in England. The Fremantle Race Club operated from the nearby Bicton Race Course from c.1904 to 1917. The Bicton Tearooms were erected near the jetty overlooking Blackwall  Reach to service visitors to the site in c.1917 and run by the Stam Family until demolition in 1964. The Fremantle Quarantine Station was introduced on the sloping site east of the foreshore in c. 1916 until removal to Byford in1983.  It was a Commonwealth Holding Station and all animals, including bees, were obliged to pass through it.  That site and its remnant landscape were then converted to public parkland.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e5c1644-f91d-452f-826e-26100c031ecd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Melville has had a long association with the Swan River that borders its northern boundaries.

This adventure takes you on a trip along the Swan River visiting some historical places of interest along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b9452f59-f4cf-4a4f-9af3-06c375f3f2d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.
Have a swim or paddle whilst you are here.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.969533" lon="115.799833">
