 <wpt lat="-31.967417" lon="115.799517">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBmb5i-04</name>
  <desc>DeadPolitician4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians</url>
  <urlname>The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta : S4 Sir David Brand by SVKR_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909839" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta : S4 Sir David Brand</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SVKR_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SVKR_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Dead Politicians of Karrakatta&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Sir David Brand&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/TheDeadPoliticians&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;To the right of the Brand family memorial plaque is one for Glenn Adams.  What is the last word on the second last line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d318390-5d80-45f8-9c88-120048c03128" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sir David Brand KCMG (1 August 1912 – 15 April 1979) was an Australian politician. 

A member of the Liberal Party, he was a Member of the Legislative Assembly of Western Australia from 1945 to 1975, and also the 19th and longest-serving Premier of Western Australia, serving four terms from the 1959 to the 1971 election. 

The mining-pastoral boom of the 1960s played a big part in ensuring for Brand's government four successive electoral victories over the ALP opposition . However, the rapid growth of the Perth metropolitan area, and the strain this put on essential services, eroded the government's popularity, especially after 1969. In addition, Brand's relations with the federal Liberal Party worsened after the retirement of Sir Robert Menzies in 1966. 

In 1971 Brand's Coalition lost the election by 1 seat. 

Brand led the Liberals in opposition until his resignation in 1973; Sir Charles Court succeeded him as the party's leader. He died of heart disease in Carnamah, Western Australia in 1979.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b9e0ea1a-1329-4b63-a108-35203c6c0594" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Karrakatta Cemetery first opened for burials in 1899. This historic cemetery is the final resting place of many thousands of Western Australians. Monuments and headstones bear many famous names including those of university founders, writers and historians, state premiers and other historic individuals.

The cemetery is centrally located just seven kilometres west of Perth city centre and is easily accessible by car or train.

Stately stands of cypress trees – a hallmark of Karrakatta – flank the main driveway off Railway Road and preside over beautiful garden areas in the historic heart of the cemetery.

There are two walk trails that will lead you some of the resting places of people in our history that now call Karrakatta Cemetery home.  They can be located at this website:
https://www.mcb.wa.gov.au/our-cemeteries/karrakatta-cemetery/historical-walk-trails

This Adventure Lab will take you to the final resting place of 5 politicians who have left their mark on the State of Western Australia
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7cc1884d-0e02-4930-88a4-51f96771599e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The federal electoral Division of Brand in Western Australia is named after him, as is the Brand Highway.  Can you think of anything else?&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9238" lon="115.841383">
