 <wpt lat="-32.0525" lon="115.756917">
  <ele>40.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-05</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo05</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S05 Fremantle by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-195" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S05 Fremantle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the plaques unveiled?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/60f41db4-a088-4d5d-ae37-9102e41d0cfa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;21.7km by road from Perth

Named after Captain Charles Howe Fremantle who arrived at the Swan River Colony on 2 May 1829, on the HMS 'Challenger'. Fremantle had been despatched from the Cape of Good Hope on the 20 March of that year, by Commodore Schomberg of the Indian Squadron, and after anchoring off the mouth of the Swan River, hoisted the British flag on the South Head, and took formal possession in the name of His Majesty King George IV, of "all that part of New Holland which is not included within the territory of New South Wales". The name Fremantle has, at times, and in various records been incorrectly spelt as 'Freemantle'.

Locate the plaques dedicated to the Merchant Mariners on the north side of the Fremantle War Memorial.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.062033" lon="115.757117">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVXMN-02</name>
  <desc>SouthOfFremant2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo</url>
  <urlname>South of Fremantle History : S2 The Beacon Theatre by ChrisRG</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-378" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>South of Fremantle History : S2 The Beacon Theatre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ChrisRG</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ChrisRG</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;South of Fremantle History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Beacon Theatre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Near the corner where you are standing is a power pole. What is the 3 digit number on that pole?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3008db1b-c333-407f-97cd-ffb2ac4a1c35" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The building across the road to the west was opened in 1937 as a cinema. The name of the cinema “Beacon Theatre” can still be seen on the façade at the top. It also boasted the Gardens, which was an outdoor cinema at the rear of the building.
It remained a cinema until 1961 when it was converted to Stammers Supermarket and the Gardens became the supermarket carpark.
Stammers moved out around 1995 and it has had a variety of different rolls since then.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6f200df-1948-449f-8636-7e1ccace4548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you to some of the historic sites South of Fremantle that have been recycled and now have other functions.
If you wish to walk, WPs 1 to 4 will take you through the heritage filled streets of South Fremantle. WP 5 is a bit further south and you will need to drive or cycle.
Parking near WP 1 can be hard to find.
Successfully completing each stage will provide you with some letters that in the end will form a code word to get the final coordinates for a bonus cache (GC8XH3E) that is also in keeping with the theme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations you have found the correct pole.  The letters you need at this point are O and R.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0659" lon="115.757417">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVXMN-03</name>
  <desc>SouthOfFremant3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo</url>
  <urlname>South of Fremantle History : S3 The South Fremantle Post Office. by ChrisRG</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-379" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>South of Fremantle History : S3 The South Fremantle Post Office.</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ChrisRG</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ChrisRG</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;South of Fremantle History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The South Fremantle Post Office.&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On Monday to Friday, what is the last time the post box is cleared? (Just the 3 digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f595357e-3671-4097-94f7-b56431ba79d3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built as the Beaconsfield Post Office in 1896 it was renamed the South Fremantle Post and Telegraph Office in 1904. It served the community as a post office until 1984. It was classified by the National Trust in 1985.

To find the answer to the question you will need to find the remains of the original post box which is one the wall in between the 2 windows&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6f200df-1948-449f-8636-7e1ccace4548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you to some of the historic sites South of Fremantle that have been recycled and now have other functions.
If you wish to walk, WPs 1 to 4 will take you through the heritage filled streets of South Fremantle. WP 5 is a bit further south and you will need to drive or cycle.
Parking near WP 1 can be hard to find.
Successfully completing each stage will provide you with some letters that in the end will form a code word to get the final coordinates for a bonus cache (GC8XH3E) that is also in keeping with the theme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;That's it, a bit late isn't it. Here are 2 more letters for your good work. D and U&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.100117" lon="115.765567">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYDw4-05</name>
  <desc>LCoogeeTrailS5T</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail</url>
  <urlname>[L] Coogee Trail  : S5 The Lookout by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-386" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Coogee Trail  : S5 The Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Lookout &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;how many concrete seats are available at the top of the lookout?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/60f144b9-e8e2-4424-b179-b60c5f4f586a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Derbal Nara is the Whadjuk Nyungar name for Cockburn Sound. 

The Nyungar Tourist Trail is part of the city's commitment to reconciliation. 
The trail features seagull beach, the lookout and artworks along the way. the trail is located within an important traditional gathering place for the Nyungar people along our coastline. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6941e632-d2fa-4859-9303-d9a032681801" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coogee Trail features both underwater and land-based activities. The trail is centred around the Omeo, Perth’s premier shore-based shipwreck. The dive and snorkel trail features an underwater reef and artworks, which is a first of its kind in WA.

On this Adventure you won't need snorkel equipment however if you would like to stay awhile pleas bring these along and enjoy the area. Only 25 metres from the shore, the dive and snorkel trail begins at the Omeo and includes an artificial reef, underwater sculptures and educational signage. 

There is a walk trail highlighting the Nyungar cultural significance to the area also. 

Be sure to collect the clues at each stage to assist with the Bonus box to find once you have completed the adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;A&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.88715" lon="116.060333">
  <ele>86.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrZEi-01</name>
  <desc>RailwayReserve1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020</url>
  <urlname>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S1 Swan View Station by TeamBarritts</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909745" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S1 Swan View Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>TeamBarritts</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">TeamBarritts</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Railway Reserves Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Swan View Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the given coordinates you will find a plaque, what is the date shown on it?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/860bfb4a-708c-4b5c-ac54-865b3b20b503" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The second section of the Eastern Railway from Guildford to Chidlow was opened in March 1884.  This line passed through Smiths Mill (Glen Forrest) and headed eastwards through Sawyers Valley and Lion Mill (Mt Helena).  However, it was found that the trains were unable to haul economic loads up the steep inclines and also experienced excessive wear &amp; tear from the tight curves in the line.

This resulted in the construction of a second line from Bellevue running roughly parallel to the original line.  Swan View Station was the second station on this line before the rise up the escarpment towards Lion Mill (Mt Helena). The station under went renovations in the late 1990's and is an ideal spot to start the adventure.

There is an information board located near the given coordinates which will add a lot more explanation to this location and its history.  Please take the time to read it as it is well worth the effort.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84cc0cf3-8d28-4e6d-88c4-bbebe35387d5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Railway Reserves Heritage Trail follows the route of the old Eastern Railway and is unique in providing a 41 kilometer loop entirely on railway formations. This extensive trail passes through quaint hills communities including Darlington, Parkerville and Mundaring, and covers a wide range of terrains with scenic views and numerous points of historical and natural significance.

This adventure will take you to five different points along the trail and should only take a few hours to complete.  Most can be reached by driving to nearby parking areas with a short stroll to the required locations.  The longest walk is approximately 2 klm round trip between locations 1 and 2.  These trails are well kept and easily accessible to all ages and levels of fitness.

Mobile reception can be patchy along the trail in certain spots, so we have increased the geofence to ensure all relevant information and data is available to you.  Always wear appropriate footwear, hat, sunscreen and carry enough water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14bf98af-b399-4d61-8b27-f3418007b8f0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great work, now to the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8686" lon="115.98355">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF84Z-04</name>
  <desc>SSVS4Sandalford</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan</url>
  <urlname>Settling in the Swan Valley : S4 Sandalford Winery by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-109" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Settling in the Swan Valley : S4 Sandalford Winery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Settling in the Swan Valley&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Sandalford Winery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many animals can you see on the front entrance?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cf9b9be1-e58f-4c81-bb06-5c75f941a50d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The history of the Swan Valley’s grape industry dates back nearly 200 years to the early colonial settlement in Guildford.

In 1829, the very first vines were planted at Olive Farm in South Guildford by botanist Thomas Waters. He recognised that the region’s warm climate and dry summers were the perfect recipe for grape growing.

In 1840, the first vines were planted at Sandalford. While Sandalford is best known for its wines today, the Roe family were initially significant table grape producers for the region.

The 1850s saw the emergence of a new generation of land owners in the Swan Valley. During this time, the demand for dried fruit, fresh table grapes, export table grapes and colonial wine saw an abundance of new plantings.

The coordinates will take you to  the western side of the West Swan Road.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc1cff31-a979-47e8-849a-54b605eddcf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Swan Valley, known for its Viticulture, Breweries and its Tourism.  Follow Captain James Stirling's journey as he made his way along the Swan River in 1827 to settle a colony.  
Along the way there is plenty to see, get your taste buds working and sample some produce from well known establishments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b9486b84-3ffb-4b0e-b0bb-f31df6a42132" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great Job!
If your thirsty or hungry, keep a look out!!!!  I'm sure you will find something to quench your thirst.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.045233" lon="115.754383">
  <ele>18.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBinUo-03</name>
  <desc>DefenceOfPerth3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence</url>
  <urlname>The Defence of Perth : S3 Army Museum of WA by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-360" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Defence of Perth : S3 Army Museum of WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Defence of Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Army Museum of WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the Museum entrance, there are two buildings. What are the two letter initials on the wall of the building on the right? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b2a8ac1-de80-490b-980b-4ec3ea034457" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Army Museum of Western Australia forms part of the Australian Army History Unit network of museums that document, conserve and promote the history of the Australian Army. 

Housed in the heritage listed, Artillery Barracks built between 1910 and 1913 was the home for artillerymen tasked to defend the Port of Fremantle and is now one of the largest military museums in Australia. 

The museum reflects the history of the Australian Army in Western Australia and the service of Western Australians’ service within the army from the colonial period through to the present day.

For more information about visiting and photographs of the displays go to
https://juleswings.wordpress.com/2020/01/26/the-army-museum-of-western-australia-part-1/
and part 2.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58814ab7-66e9-4885-b1d4-1399d838349f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has some significant military locations, which have played an important role in the country’s wartime history. There are many military museums from Albany in the South, Broome in the North, and Kalgoorlie in the East. 

This series looks at some of the local military sites in the Perth area, which includes World War II Tunnels, local Telecommunications, Spitfires, Tanks and Artillery. 

Most museums have restricted opening hours, however the WWII Tunnels and tours of HMAS Ovens are temporarily suspended due to Covid-19. 

Important: 
The WWII Tunnels location in Mosman Park will take some physical effort to reach the site, however there is a path for wheelchairs and people with walking aides. Please follow the Directional Signs from the Boundary Road Carpark to the Heritage Site - approximately 350 metres.




https://scoop.com.au/military-museums-to-visit-across-wa


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3b0ff517-6157-4c32-a531-0c534385ad81" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations! 
Even if the gates are locked, walk around the perimeter to see the artillery.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0885" lon="115.7597">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVXMN-05</name>
  <desc>SouthOfFremant5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo</url>
  <urlname>South of Fremantle History : S5 The Chimney by ChrisRG</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-381" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>South of Fremantle History : S5 The Chimney</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ChrisRG</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ChrisRG</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;South of Fremantle History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Chimney&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;you should be standing near an electrical box. What are the last 3 digits of the ID number&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/da98c6db-096f-48ce-8725-84053ce6845d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; 
This chimney is all that remains of the Fremantle Freezing Works. The freezing and cooling works were built in 1921 to support the Robb's Jetty Abattoir. Cattle  and sheep would arrive by boat or train. Before the long jetty was built the cattle would be off loaded off the beach and made to swim to shore. 
The area was also home to skin drying sheds and tanneries. Driving down Cockburn Rd in those days was not a pleasant experience.
The abattoir was shut down in 1992 and all the equipment was removed by 1994 and the building demolished.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6f200df-1948-449f-8636-7e1ccace4548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you to some of the historic sites South of Fremantle that have been recycled and now have other functions.
If you wish to walk, WPs 1 to 4 will take you through the heritage filled streets of South Fremantle. WP 5 is a bit further south and you will need to drive or cycle.
Parking near WP 1 can be hard to find.
Successfully completing each stage will provide you with some letters that in the end will form a code word to get the final coordinates for a bonus cache (GC8XH3E) that is also in keeping with the theme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;WooHoo! that is the final one, if you did them in order, if not then keep going. The final letters you need are H and O&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0176" lon="115.7567">
  <ele>39.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBinUo-01</name>
  <desc>DefenceOfPerth1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence</url>
  <urlname>The Defence of Perth : S1 WWII Tunnels by Meme28</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-358" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Defence of Perth : S1 WWII Tunnels</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Meme28</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Meme28</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Defence of Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WWII Tunnels&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Defence&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the southern side of the Observation post, what is the word directly under the crown on the information panel?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/afc7fb48-32ca-4c9f-8dcd-adf4a78d799e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Leighton Battery at Buckland Hill, Mosman Park, Western Australia, was part of the Coastal defences of Australia during World War II and the Fremantle Fortress, protecting Fremantle Harbour. 

The battery initially consisted of two 6-inch Mk VII naval guns, operational from February 1943. The two guns were removed in 1945 and replaced by three 5.25 inch dual purpose coastal artillery/antiaircraft guns. 

While it was envisioned to have all major ports in Australia protected by these type guns, the Leighton Battery became the only one ever operational. The battery was dismantled and the equipment disposed of for scrap in 1963.


Please follow the Directional Signs from the Boundary Road Carpark to the Heritage Site - approximately 350 metres. 

Ref: http://www.artillerywa.org.au/raahs/leighton.htm
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58814ab7-66e9-4885-b1d4-1399d838349f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has some significant military locations, which have played an important role in the country’s wartime history. There are many military museums from Albany in the South, Broome in the North, and Kalgoorlie in the East. 

This series looks at some of the local military sites in the Perth area, which includes World War II Tunnels, local Telecommunications, Spitfires, Tanks and Artillery. 

Most museums have restricted opening hours, however the WWII Tunnels and tours of HMAS Ovens are temporarily suspended due to Covid-19. 

Important: 
The WWII Tunnels location in Mosman Park will take some physical effort to reach the site, however there is a path for wheelchairs and people with walking aides. Please follow the Directional Signs from the Boundary Road Carpark to the Heritage Site - approximately 350 metres.




https://scoop.com.au/military-museums-to-visit-across-wa


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/331bf8ce-c4b7-47c7-b8e1-e76889f407d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations! 
Don't forget to check out the 6 inch Mk VII Coast Gun at Buckland Hill, part of the Leighton Battery.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0474" lon="115.7536">
  <ele>16.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRBas-02</name>
  <desc>ParanormalPert2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth</url>
  <urlname>Paranormal Perth : S2 Fremantle Arts Centre by Barrikon</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909755" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Paranormal Perth : S2 Fremantle Arts Centre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barrikon</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barrikon</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Paranormal Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle Arts Centre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many wooden crosses are attached to the 3 main halls of the building? (digit)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1f1ce0d8-9baf-435a-b1fc-f821c652afb0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Fremantle Arts Centre is Western Australia’s most haunted location and is in Australia’s most haunted lists. Built in the 1860’s, the chequered history of the building begins with convict hands laying the all the stone in Gothic architecture. 
It was the Swan Colony’s lunatic asylum and dumping ground for the states most disturbed and those deemed invalid. Over the years overcrowding lead to the suspicious death of a patient, Ms Clifford.

In later years the building became a women’s home with stories of mistreatment, escapes and suicide. 

Lingering souls roam the halls and have been known to touch visitors, drop temperatures and even making appearances whilst whispering to those unwilling to listen.  
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8dc1ada0-08ea-4e7e-964c-57a7b3bf56ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stories of lost souls, destitute apparitions, shadowy figures with unfinished business and ethereal beings providing protection, fill history books and live within myths across the world. Perth is no different. 

Tales of ghostly encounters can be found across the city and by partaking on this paranormal hunt you may experience your very own unexplained event.

Your adventure covers approximately 53kms and is available anytime however to fully experience possibilities of the afterlife, it is suggested you wait until darkness falls. A good torch will be needed if venturing out after dark.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8fc26a18-e108-42af-8d6c-69851d0d1ccf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.105133" lon="115.764267">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYDw4-04</name>
  <desc>LCoogeeTrailS4B</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail</url>
  <urlname>[L] Coogee Trail  : S4 Beeliar Boogjar by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-385" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Coogee Trail  : S4 Beeliar Boogjar</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Beeliar Boogjar &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Artwork is by the ..... Artists Team &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e09a138c-e815-4c47-ab99-c3b20143ba8c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Derbal Nara is the Whadjuk Nyungar name for Cockburn Sound. 

The Nyungar Tourist Trail is part of the city's commitment to reconciliation. 
The trail features seagull beach, the lookout and artworks along the way. The trail is located within an important traditional gathering place for the Nyungar people along our coastline. 

Keep a lookout for the artwork on the ground as you walk through the area. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6941e632-d2fa-4859-9303-d9a032681801" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coogee Trail features both underwater and land-based activities. The trail is centred around the Omeo, Perth’s premier shore-based shipwreck. The dive and snorkel trail features an underwater reef and artworks, which is a first of its kind in WA.

On this Adventure you won't need snorkel equipment however if you would like to stay awhile pleas bring these along and enjoy the area. Only 25 metres from the shore, the dive and snorkel trail begins at the Omeo and includes an artificial reef, underwater sculptures and educational signage. 

There is a walk trail highlighting the Nyungar cultural significance to the area also. 

Be sure to collect the clues at each stage to assist with the Bonus box to find once you have completed the adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;R&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.059067" lon="115.753283">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVXMN-01</name>
  <desc>SouthOfFremant1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo</url>
  <urlname>South of Fremantle History : S1 The First Fremantle Cemetery by ChrisRG</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909763" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>South of Fremantle History : S1 The First Fremantle Cemetery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ChrisRG</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ChrisRG</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;South of Fremantle History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The First Fremantle Cemetery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the oldest headstone here which is for the wife of Captain Henderson. What year did she die?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e07da4a4-2e0f-489f-883c-52397e61c662" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You are now standing in what was Fremantle’s first cemetery. The first recorded burial here was on the 3rd April 1831. It remained in service until 1855 when the cemetery in Skinner St was opened. After that date the only people buried at this site were those that had close relatives here.
This cemetery was closed in 1895 and some of the bodies were exhumed and taken to the current Fremantle cemetery or Karrakatta.
Early Recycling.
When the cemetery was closed not all of the headstones were moved to the new cemeteries. Some were used as slabs for footpaths and one was later discovered as the hearthstone in an old house. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6f200df-1948-449f-8636-7e1ccace4548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you to some of the historic sites South of Fremantle that have been recycled and now have other functions.
If you wish to walk, WPs 1 to 4 will take you through the heritage filled streets of South Fremantle. WP 5 is a bit further south and you will need to drive or cycle.
Parking near WP 1 can be hard to find.
Successfully completing each stage will provide you with some letters that in the end will form a code word to get the final coordinates for a bonus cache (GC8XH3E) that is also in keeping with the theme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!  This Waypoint is bought to you by the letters N and E&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9289" lon="115.804983">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2021-04-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBByQh-05</name>
  <desc>HerdsmanLakePe5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98</url>
  <urlname>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S5 Herdsman Lake Wildlife Centre by J2V Travel</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909787" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S5 Herdsman Lake Wildlife Centre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>J2V Travel</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">J2V Travel</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Herdsman Lake, Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Herdsman Lake Wildlife Centre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the WA Gould League established?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ccfc631c-4838-4f2e-ba10-9565c19da50e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Herdsman Lake Wildlife Centre is a place for schools and the community to reconnect with nature. It is on the edge of the southern end of Herdsman Lake.

The WA Gould League is a not-for-profit organisation committed to nurturing a passion for nature through education, innovation and communication.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f31219a6-70d9-4285-8fe6-6a1f720a71e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you on a 7km loop around Herdsman Lake. It is best enjoyed by cycling, running or walking on the paths around the lake. Collect answers at each location to calculate the final coordinates for a bonus traditional geocache.

The Herdsman Lake Regional Park consists of native bush, parklands, an inner wetland and a dredged outer moat consisting of four deep permanent water bodies joined by small channels. 

It is a popular spot for birdwatchers. The lake is a breeding site for at least 20 species of birds including black swans. Please take care around the grassy areas as there may be tiger snakes.

The adventure is designed to start and finish at the car park on the south-west side of the lake, and to go clockwise around the lake. However, you can visit the locations in any order.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/193ef1fe-8566-401e-b80a-561a49ae136f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;E=6. Use all the numbers collected on the Herdsman Lake adventure lab to solve the coordinates for the bonus traditional geocache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.055033" lon="115.7526">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRBas-01</name>
  <desc>ParanormalPert1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth</url>
  <urlname>Paranormal Perth : S1 Fremantle Prison by Barrikon</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910083" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Paranormal Perth : S1 Fremantle Prison</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barrikon</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barrikon</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Paranormal Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle Prison&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ParanormalPerth&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;To your left, is an Australian Convict Site monument. What is the start date at the base of the Fremantle Prison WA post?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e8743ea-255b-4a0e-a488-d77c4fd21dc5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1855 when Fremantle Prison opened, it was Western Australia’s maximum-security prison and the only lawfully place of execution. Over its 136 year operation, 44 people had their lives ended in the gallows of the prison. 

With such a grim history, Fremantle Prison is said to be the second most haunted location in WA. After hours, past convicts are said to roam the cold corridors looking for redemption, retribution or just to plead innocence. 

Martha Rendall, convicted of the murders of her own three children, was only female sentenced to death at the prison. Legend has it, she remains and has been sighted watching in the chapel window.

Stories of a ghost named Jack exist after he breathed on visitors with tainted breath.

Oddly, owls have been noted landing by the gallows on the anniversaries of those whose finished this world here.

To the left of the entrance, is an Australian Convict Site monument. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8dc1ada0-08ea-4e7e-964c-57a7b3bf56ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stories of lost souls, destitute apparitions, shadowy figures with unfinished business and ethereal beings providing protection, fill history books and live within myths across the world. Perth is no different. 

Tales of ghostly encounters can be found across the city and by partaking on this paranormal hunt you may experience your very own unexplained event.

Your adventure covers approximately 53kms and is available anytime however to fully experience possibilities of the afterlife, it is suggested you wait until darkness falls. A good torch will be needed if venturing out after dark.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a46018b7-bd3f-4532-a7e5-7f2b74e90a26" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.054967" lon="115.7526">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-03</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander23</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S03 Fremantle Prison by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000449" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S03 Fremantle Prison</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle Prison&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last word on the last line of the information plaque displayed for number 12?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c186f7c0-ef8d-449f-a94d-2c1c9e3dc2db" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Fremantle Prison was built as a convict barracks in the 19th century and remained in continual use until 1991. The Prison was a place of hangings, floggings, dramatic convict escapes and prisoner riots.

The Convict Establishment, as the prison was first known, was built by convict labour between 1852 and 1859 using limestone quarried on the site. The first prisoners moved into the main cell block in 1855.

It was renamed Fremantle Prison in 1867.

Fremantle Prison was decommissioned on 8 November 1991 and its prisoners transferred to Casuarina Prison, replacing Fremantle Prison as the state's main maximum-security prison.

After its closure the WA state government embarked on a long-term conservation plan to ensure the Prison’s preservation for future generations. Fremantle Prison is one of the largest surviving convict prisons in the world today.

Find address number 12 on the outside of the prison.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.069217" lon="115.753667">
  <ele>10.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVXMN-04</name>
  <desc>SouthOfFremant4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo</url>
  <urlname>South of Fremantle History : S4 The Biscuit Factory by ChrisRG</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-380" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>South of Fremantle History : S4 The Biscuit Factory</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ChrisRG</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ChrisRG</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;South of Fremantle History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Biscuit Factory&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/southfreo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Over how many different types of biscuits and cakes were produced by the 1930's &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fa23b680-239a-4b03-be02-c1cc0376ac28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mills and Ware was the major producer of biscuits and cakes in WA and it moved to this site from Cottesloe in 1899. It was a largest industrial employer of women in Western Australia.
Arnott’s Biscuits became a shareholder in 1953 and the majority shareholder in 1973.
The factory closed in 1992
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6f200df-1948-449f-8636-7e1ccace4548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you to some of the historic sites South of Fremantle that have been recycled and now have other functions.
If you wish to walk, WPs 1 to 4 will take you through the heritage filled streets of South Fremantle. WP 5 is a bit further south and you will need to drive or cycle.
Parking near WP 1 can be hard to find.
Successfully completing each stage will provide you with some letters that in the end will form a code word to get the final coordinates for a bonus cache (GC8XH3E) that is also in keeping with the theme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Yes! Another one down, maybe time to stop for some tea and bickies.  Here are 2 more letters that may come in handy U and S&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.10555" lon="115.762533">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYDw4-03</name>
  <desc>LCoogeeTrailS3D</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail</url>
  <urlname>[L] Coogee Trail  : S3 Derbal Nara by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-384" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Coogee Trail  : S3 Derbal Nara</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Derbal Nara &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is Nyungar name for (egg)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b7a9586-9a50-4683-824f-715942ca5469" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Derbal Nara is the Whadjuk Nyungar name for Cockburn Sound. 

The Nyungar Tourist Trail is part of the city's commitment to reconciliation. 
The trail features seagull beach, the lookout and artworks along the way. the trail is located within an important traditional gathering place for the Nyungar people along our coastline. 

Keep a lookout for the artwork on the ground as you walk through the area. 

The start of the trail is here at Socrates Park.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6941e632-d2fa-4859-9303-d9a032681801" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coogee Trail features both underwater and land-based activities. The trail is centred around the Omeo, Perth’s premier shore-based shipwreck. The dive and snorkel trail features an underwater reef and artworks, which is a first of its kind in WA.

On this Adventure you won't need snorkel equipment however if you would like to stay awhile pleas bring these along and enjoy the area. Only 25 metres from the shore, the dive and snorkel trail begins at the Omeo and includes an artificial reef, underwater sculptures and educational signage. 

There is a walk trail highlighting the Nyungar cultural significance to the area also. 

Be sure to collect the clues at each stage to assist with the Bonus box to find once you have completed the adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;T&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.104967" lon="115.7618">
  <time>2020-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYDw4-01</name>
  <desc>LCoogeeTrailS1T</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail</url>
  <urlname>[L] Coogee Trail  : S1 The Omeo by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-382" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Coogee Trail  : S1 The Omeo</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Omeo &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Omeo entered the Northern Territory's Roper River in what year? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e9675843-2f72-4768-81e0-6ba0ef0836c3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The 605-ton barque-rigged iron screw steamer was built at Newcastle, England in 1858. Part of its cargo on its maiden voyage to Australia were the materials and equipment for laying the Bass Strait submarine cable. After many years carrying passengers and cargo between Melbourne and Adelaide and then  New Zealand Omeo was sent to Port Darwin in 1870 on charter to the  contractors  completing the northern section of the Overland Telegraph line linking Australia to Britain via Singapore.  She then played a major part in the construction of the overland telegraph line from Darwin, carrying telegraph poles and cargo 160 km up the Roper River. After making over 100 round trips to New Zealand her engines were removed in 1880 and her funnel replaced with a new mast. As a 4-masted ‘jackass barque’ ( Two square and two barque rigged masts). In that distinctive livery Omeo voyaged between Melbourne and Newcastle and  it traded for many years in the Pacific and Indian Oceans carrying passengers and cargo.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6941e632-d2fa-4859-9303-d9a032681801" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coogee Trail features both underwater and land-based activities. The trail is centred around the Omeo, Perth’s premier shore-based shipwreck. The dive and snorkel trail features an underwater reef and artworks, which is a first of its kind in WA.

On this Adventure you won't need snorkel equipment however if you would like to stay awhile pleas bring these along and enjoy the area. Only 25 metres from the shore, the dive and snorkel trail begins at the Omeo and includes an artificial reef, underwater sculptures and educational signage. 

There is a walk trail highlighting the Nyungar cultural significance to the area also. 

Be sure to collect the clues at each stage to assist with the Bonus box to find once you have completed the adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;E&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.105683" lon="115.762083">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYDw4-02</name>
  <desc>LCoogeeTrailS2C</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail</url>
  <urlname>[L] Coogee Trail  : S2 Coogee dive and snorkel trail by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-383" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Coogee Trail  : S2 Coogee dive and snorkel trail</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Coogee dive and snorkel trail &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CoogeeMaritimeTrail&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Omeo's anchors were recovered in what year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/aaa41287-5508-45bb-add4-dd779b87d40f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The trail begins in shallow water while its westernmost extent reaches a maximum depth of 7 metres.

The underwater art gallery includes a giant sea star which divers can swim through, an old railway wheel, anchor and Dutch inspired replica cannon and a sea lion near the shore. You can experience a large variety a marine life on the trail including a variety of fish, starfish, cuttlefish, nudibranchs, sea squirts and corals.

The purpose-built reef consists ofover 55 reef modules, ranging in height from 1m to 5m tall. The reef has been designed by marine engineers and scientists to support a diverse range of aquatic flora and fauna. The unique sections create a complex reef that includes lots of cave-like spaces to support fish, crays and molluscs and are great for snorkelling and scuba diving.  Larger species will be attracted to the tall reef temple which includes a swim through.
The reef will weigh over 30 tonnes in total, and all reef modules are fabricated from fibre-reinforced concrete.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6941e632-d2fa-4859-9303-d9a032681801" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coogee Trail features both underwater and land-based activities. The trail is centred around the Omeo, Perth’s premier shore-based shipwreck. The dive and snorkel trail features an underwater reef and artworks, which is a first of its kind in WA.

On this Adventure you won't need snorkel equipment however if you would like to stay awhile pleas bring these along and enjoy the area. Only 25 metres from the shore, the dive and snorkel trail begins at the Omeo and includes an artificial reef, underwater sculptures and educational signage. 

There is a walk trail highlighting the Nyungar cultural significance to the area also. 

Be sure to collect the clues at each stage to assist with the Bonus box to find once you have completed the adventure. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;X&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.928567" lon="115.800483">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2021-04-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBByQh-01</name>
  <desc>HerdsmanLakePe1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98</url>
  <urlname>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S1 Wetlands bird sign by J2V Travel</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-273" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S1 Wetlands bird sign</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>J2V Travel</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">J2V Travel</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Herdsman Lake, Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wetlands bird sign&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What colour features twice in the names of the water birds?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df6e4405-9d30-4278-92a5-6a5b973931ea" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The adventure lab starts at a children's playground with an interpretative sign of common birds sighted in Herdsman Lake Regional Park.

On the way to the next location, you can take a detour to see the settler's cottage built around 1930. Beware, there are signs warning of tiger snakes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f31219a6-70d9-4285-8fe6-6a1f720a71e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you on a 7km loop around Herdsman Lake. It is best enjoyed by cycling, running or walking on the paths around the lake. Collect answers at each location to calculate the final coordinates for a bonus traditional geocache.

The Herdsman Lake Regional Park consists of native bush, parklands, an inner wetland and a dredged outer moat consisting of four deep permanent water bodies joined by small channels. 

It is a popular spot for birdwatchers. The lake is a breeding site for at least 20 species of birds including black swans. Please take care around the grassy areas as there may be tiger snakes.

The adventure is designed to start and finish at the car park on the south-west side of the lake, and to go clockwise around the lake. However, you can visit the locations in any order.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/abbf11e7-d125-4a33-bc2a-1c9fb15e4c5f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Collect coordinates for the bonus traditional cache. A = 8.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.916567" lon="115.81285">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2021-04-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBByQh-04</name>
  <desc>HerdsmanLakePe4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98</url>
  <urlname>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S4 Balgay bird hide by J2V Travel</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909785" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S4 Balgay bird hide</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>J2V Travel</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">J2V Travel</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Herdsman Lake, Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Balgay bird hide&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What colour are the walls of the bird hide?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/acc1431d-c8d8-45e4-a60d-b10de5b9d0bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On the east side of the lake, take the limestone path through the woodlands. Pass the Baumea bird hide and arrive at the Balgay bird hide.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f31219a6-70d9-4285-8fe6-6a1f720a71e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you on a 7km loop around Herdsman Lake. It is best enjoyed by cycling, running or walking on the paths around the lake. Collect answers at each location to calculate the final coordinates for a bonus traditional geocache.

The Herdsman Lake Regional Park consists of native bush, parklands, an inner wetland and a dredged outer moat consisting of four deep permanent water bodies joined by small channels. 

It is a popular spot for birdwatchers. The lake is a breeding site for at least 20 species of birds including black swans. Please take care around the grassy areas as there may be tiger snakes.

The adventure is designed to start and finish at the car park on the south-west side of the lake, and to go clockwise around the lake. However, you can visit the locations in any order.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceb44874-ca5d-4075-906b-63c6d74fd3a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;D=9&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051817" lon="115.745433">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBxh8r-05</name>
  <desc>EotLS5Fremantle</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL</url>
  <urlname>End of the Line : S5 Fremantle Station by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909859" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>End of the Line : S5 Fremantle Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;End of the Line&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/EOTL&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many arches make up the main entrance to the platform? ( _ )&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/85f0ed7e-cac6-4f56-8906-1f58761299eb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Fremantle Station is the oldest terminus station on the Perth Rail Network.

Located 18.7km from Perth. The current station was opened on July 1st 1907 and is situated 300m north east of the original station that opened March 1st 1881.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b725a41a-cce1-431d-909c-cab48dd07c51" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Please be aware the locations for this Adventure Lab are quite a distance from the map marker, make sure you check your nearest Lab point before commencing.

The metropolitan Perth rail network is comprised of five major lines connecting the four Metropolitan Development Corridors and Fremantle to the Perth CBD.

Over the coming years the network is set to expand even further to accomade travelers from Ellenbrook, Forrestfield and extending lines past Armadale to Byford and continuing north to Yanchep.

This Adventure Lab will take you on a tour of the various lines of the Perth Rail Network. You are more than welcome to catch the trains to the stops (it can be done in a single day) if you wish but this is not a requirement, in fact all information can be gained and answered without entering the stations themselves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/96f4d0a3-7137-430e-abd4-5a9bb172eaab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The clue you need for the bonus final is:
 R&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.882217" lon="116.071">
  <ele>131.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrZEi-02</name>
  <desc>RailwayReserve2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020</url>
  <urlname>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S2 Swan View Tunnel by TeamBarritts</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909747" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Railway Reserves Heritage Trail : S2 Swan View Tunnel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>TeamBarritts</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">TeamBarritts</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Railway Reserves Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Swan View Tunnel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/RRHT2020&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the given coordinates you will see a number engraved / written high on the brickwork (look up), what is that number?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d947cf2-0fd0-45a1-ab81-8a055b2e43d4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan View Tunnel is located further up the line from the Swan View Station, was built in 1895 and is the earliest railway tunnel in West Australian's history.  Engineer Charles O'Connor designed the 340m Swan View Tunnel to overcome a granite barrier formed by a fold in the Darling Range.

A deviation was built around the tunnel in 1945.  This was due to a number of fatalities caused by the slow upward progress of the trains in the tunnel coupled with poor ventilation.  The deviation was then used by the "up trains", while the "down trains" continued to use the tunnel as they could coast through at greater speeds.  The tunnel ceased to be used after the new Avon Valley rail route was opened in 1966.

Make sure you bring a torch to check out some of the interesting features inside the tunnel, such as the masonry-lined face &amp; roof which was carried out to prevent rock falls.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84cc0cf3-8d28-4e6d-88c4-bbebe35387d5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Railway Reserves Heritage Trail follows the route of the old Eastern Railway and is unique in providing a 41 kilometer loop entirely on railway formations. This extensive trail passes through quaint hills communities including Darlington, Parkerville and Mundaring, and covers a wide range of terrains with scenic views and numerous points of historical and natural significance.

This adventure will take you to five different points along the trail and should only take a few hours to complete.  Most can be reached by driving to nearby parking areas with a short stroll to the required locations.  The longest walk is approximately 2 klm round trip between locations 1 and 2.  These trails are well kept and easily accessible to all ages and levels of fitness.

Mobile reception can be patchy along the trail in certain spots, so we have increased the geofence to ensure all relevant information and data is available to you.  Always wear appropriate footwear, hat, sunscreen and carry enough water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d8d5768-2805-4da8-890e-ed7b76948264" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great work, now to the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.977467" lon="115.7603">
  <ele>31.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-03</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo03</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S03 Swanbourne by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-193" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S03 Swanbourne</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Swanbourne&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the surname mentioned in the brackets on the last line of text on the plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ed23b3f-b46e-448b-a9cb-706f8273b0da" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;10.7km from Perth by road.

The name Swanbourne commemorates the home of Thomas Francis Fremantle, 1st Baron Cottesloe.. He was the brother of Captain Charles Howe Fremantle RN. after whom Fremantle is named. Swanbourne was at first referred to as Cottesloe Beach, and the first homes in the area were built around 1890.  To some the nearby beach is better known as where clothing is optional.

What a nice view of the coast.  Locate the plaque at the coordinates and find the name in brackets on the last line of text of the plaque.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.057333" lon="115.7426">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRXKy-02</name>
  <desc>LWestOzWander20</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan</url>
  <urlname>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S02 WA Shipwrecks Museum by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000447" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] West Oz Wander #2 : S02 WA Shipwrecks Museum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;West Oz Wander #2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WA Shipwrecks Museum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZBlackSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Minus the '-' at the begining of the line under Architects what is the exact text of the first line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e3aff185-9969-4e17-81d6-3f4c45b420a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The WA Shipwrecks Museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the southern hemisphere.

The Museum is housed in 1850s-era Commissariat building and has since been restored to its historic glory.

Steeped in history, the galleries house hundreds of relics from ships wrecked along WA’s treacherous coastline, including the original timbers from the Batavia (wrecked in 1629), the de Vlamingh Dish, and also countless artifacts from the Dutch shipwrecks Zuytdorp, Zeewijk and Vergulde Draeck.

On the wall to the left of the main entrance in front of the anchors is a plaque.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ba2105b-269b-4f75-9d77-8a158e9cdf1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a trip visiting the early European history of the Western Australia colony.

Indigenous Australians have been occupying Western Australia for around 40,000 to 60,000 years.

The first recorded European contact was in 1616 when Dutch explorer Dirk Hartog landed on the west coast but the first permanent settlement was not made until 1826 when a settlement was established at Albany to formally take possession of the western third of the continent of Australia for the British Crown.  The Swan River Colony was established in 1829 starting the official history of European settlement of Western Australia.

This set of lab caches explores the early buildings and places of the Swan River Colony.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.955633" lon="116.16295">
  <ele>116.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-15T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqEvG-03</name>
  <desc>MundaringWeir31</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring</url>
  <urlname>Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab : S3 No 1 Pump Station by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910065" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab : S3 No 1 Pump Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No 1 Pump Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What date was the restoration of the old building officially opened?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c87472f3-120d-4e79-a048-197d5376f349" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Completed in 1902, the No 1 Pump Station was the first of 8 similar stations situated along the Goldfields water supply pipeline.

The pump station is now managed by the National Trust &amp; open to the public on weekends.  

Hope you enjoy checking out this lovely old building!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8fd2b6fd-31f2-4a79-8e94-15d370f26e67" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located in the Perth Hills, the Mundaring Weir is one of the world's greatest engineering projects.

Completed in 1903, the weir is the start of the water pipeline that pumps water to the Kalgoorlie goldfields some 700km away.

We have created this adventure lab to highlight some of the history &amp; beauty of the precinct.  

Mundaring Weir is a great spot to take the family &amp; the little ones will enjoy feeding the birds, enjoy!

**  Once you complete the adventure, use your journal entries to work out the Bonus Cache (GC8M58V). **&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84f6c650-26e6-45bc-8aff-aa2d9a615933" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;NO 1 PUMP STATION:
Take note of this year.  This is EFGH.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.958283" lon="116.1654">
  <ele>146.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-15T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqEvG-02</name>
  <desc>MundaringWeirA2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring</url>
  <urlname>Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab : S2 Weir Wall by jasenjanine</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910063" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab : S2 Weir Wall</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jasenjanine</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jasenjanine</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mundaring Weir Adventure Lab&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Weir Wall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Mundaring&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Take a look at your surroundings; 21 JOHN IRON ????&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1e972450-56a3-4e6e-8a94-0043e4519627" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The dam overflowed regularly and as demand increased the wall was raised a further 32 feet shortly after World War II.  Although the wall was raised, overflows still occurred until the 1960's.  With a growing population &amp; a drying climate, the Lower Helena Dam was created to pump water back to the weir.

At this location is an another iconic viewing point of the weir &amp; also the highly photographed valves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8fd2b6fd-31f2-4a79-8e94-15d370f26e67" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located in the Perth Hills, the Mundaring Weir is one of the world's greatest engineering projects.

Completed in 1903, the weir is the start of the water pipeline that pumps water to the Kalgoorlie goldfields some 700km away.

We have created this adventure lab to highlight some of the history &amp; beauty of the precinct.  

Mundaring Weir is a great spot to take the family &amp; the little ones will enjoy feeding the birds, enjoy!

**  Once you complete the adventure, use your journal entries to work out the Bonus Cache (GC8M58V). **&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/097b3ab8-c219-421f-b3b3-fef791937e5a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;WEIR WALL:
Take note of this year.  This number is ABCD!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
