  <urlname>Be bold : Reabold Hill at Bold Park : S1 1: Welcome to Bold Park by m4yb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915795" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Be bold : Reabold Hill at Bold Park : S1 1: Welcome to Bold Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>m4yb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">m4yb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Be bold : Reabold Hill at Bold Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1: Welcome to Bold Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ReaboldHill"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ReaboldHill&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the season at the top of the artwork, with a pink background.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d234daa2-2444-4a21-890f-ebe711af906f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this spot you will find a map of the Bold Park trails, suggested walks and useful phone numbers. There is also a sign showing The Six Nyoongar Seasons. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5277b505-cf56-43e9-9cf9-16b463375042" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bold Park close to the Coast, North of the River, covers 437 hectares and is one of the largest remaining bushland remnants in the urban area ofthe Swan Coastal Plain.  It was declared an A Class Reserve in 1998, and has an impressive biodiversity despite its proximity to the CBD.
Reabold Hill took the names of local Council figures  of the early 1900’s, Frank Rea and William Bold. It is the highest natural point on the Coastal Plain, the path you will be on will reward you with amazing views of the City and the Indian Ocean.
Please note the carpark hours: 5.30 am to 7pm from 1 April to 31 October, and 5.30am to 8pm 1 November to 31 March.
There is a Bonus Box associated with this Adventure, GC8Z9C2.  Make sure to collect the clues . And have fun!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Correct! 
 A in the Bonus Box is the number of letters in the answer, divided by 2&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.951933" lon="116.16725">
  <ele>176.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHWnu-03</name>
  <desc>MundaringLGAHe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph</url>
  <urlname>Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl : S3 Mundaring Weir Hotel by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000321" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl : S3 Mundaring Weir Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mundaring Weir Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the Mundaring Weir Tavern sign, the Hotel is  located at Lot ??? Hall Road?  (digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2f07d5c6-49e5-4487-a5c0-11c0e6da72d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Mundaring Weir Hotel is located adjacent to the formation of the former branch line to the weir - the Mundaring Weir Railway on a hill to the north, and above Mundaring Weir.

It was previously known as the Reservoir Hotel, then the Goldfields Weir Hotel and has been in the area since 1898. It was a crucial location where C Y O'Connor stayed regularly during the construction of the weir.

MHI site 50 - The Mundaring Weir Hotel has very high social, historic and aesthetic significance, both locally and State, for its associations with the Weir’s construction, operation and tourist activities together with its two storey architectural style of which there are only several examples in the Shire.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5ed890c9-75a3-435c-b521-bf5e55a0aecf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;

We invite you to visit some local Heritage Listed establishments within the Mundaring LGA - this adventure should take between 1 - 2 hours.  The required information is able to be accessed 24/7.



Municipal Heritage Inventory -
It is mandatory under the Heritage of Western Australia Act 1990 for each local government
to maintain a Municipal Heritage Inventory (MHI). The Shire of Mundaring MHI lists many
places that contribute to the history and character of the local area and classifies them
according to the degree of significance. Modification of places on the MHI may not always
require planning approval, although sympathetic design is still recommended to maintain the
character and heritage values. 


&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.909767" lon="115.801567">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2021-04-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBByQh-03</name>
  <desc>HerdsmanLakePe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98</url>
  <urlname>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S3 Popeye Lake by J2V Travel</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-275" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Herdsman Lake, Perth : S3 Popeye Lake</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>J2V Travel</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">J2V Travel</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Herdsman Lake, Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Popeye Lake&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3f03b2b5-e8c7-4523-a788-f89320921b98&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What does the indigenous word ngurghboro mean? Six words.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5221d8cf-c440-4849-b6f9-930873270cd5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You have arrived at Popeye Lake a popular children's playground with a large car park. The interpretative sign gives some interesting history about the area. 

The first people to use this site were the local Noongar people, the Yellagonga, who call the area Ngurghboro and used it as a food resource. In the 1930s the once-thick bush around the lake was transformed into pastures for horse stables and five-acre market gardens. In the early 1990s the north west section, known as Floreat Lakes, was rezoned for residential development.

After this location you will pass by the Perth Horse and Pony Club grounds. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f31219a6-70d9-4285-8fe6-6a1f720a71e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab will take you on a 7km loop around Herdsman Lake. It is best enjoyed by cycling, running or walking on the paths around the lake. Collect answers at each location to calculate the final coordinates for a bonus traditional geocache.

The Herdsman Lake Regional Park consists of native bush, parklands, an inner wetland and a dredged outer moat consisting of four deep permanent water bodies joined by small channels. 

It is a popular spot for birdwatchers. The lake is a breeding site for at least 20 species of birds including black swans. Please take care around the grassy areas as there may be tiger snakes.

The adventure is designed to start and finish at the car park on the south-west side of the lake, and to go clockwise around the lake. However, you can visit the locations in any order.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4786ba20-794d-481f-9ed1-789d2c7be1ac" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;C=9&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.84465" lon="115.9459">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBTUz7-05</name>
  <desc>LWPS5MusselPool</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA</url>
  <urlname>[L] Whiteman Park : S5 Mussel Pool by sevabo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919032" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Whiteman Park : S5 Mussel Pool</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sevabo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sevabo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Whiteman Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mussel Pool&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;From left to right what birds do you see in the picture in the wall?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9b0dbf31-f38b-4020-bf43-15491eac4ae1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Whiteman Park’s namesake, Lew Whiteman, was instrumental in the development of the Mussel Pool picnic area and the body of water for which the area is named.

Back in the 1960s, Lew constructed a dam across the southern end of an ephemeral lake along Bennett Brook, creating a pool which now remains year-round. When water levels are low, you can see the weir wall construction well.

Freshwater mussels which were once prolific in the area are found in the waterbody, and so it was named Mussel Pool. Now, it is a scenic picnic area favoured by Park visitors endeavouring to find a quiet spot amongst the shady trees.

Mussel Pool is an important wetland habitat, as the lake holds water almost year-round. Classed as a Conservation category lake, the area is home to healthy population of brushtail possums (Trichosurus vulpecula), nine amphibian species, numerous birdlife and the long-necked turtle (Chelodina oblonga).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0823e411-d9ef-4ac9-901d-a0a757367e16" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
Whiteman Park is a 4,000-hectare bushland area located 22 km north of Perth, WA. The park is in the suburb of Whiteman, in the Swan Valley in the upper reaches of the Swan River.
It encompasses the source of Bennett Brook - an important place of the Nyoongar people, and a source of mythology of the Wagyl and stories about Aboriginal occupancy of the area.
Whiteman Park is known for its biodiversity, including more than 450 endemic plants and more than 120 vertebrate animals (some of which are rare and endangered). More than 17% of Western Australian bird species occur in Whiteman Park, including migratory birds attracted to the habitat provided by Bennett Brook and associated wetlands including Grogan's Swamp, a Conservation Category Wetland.
The state government purchased the land from a number of private owners in 1978 to protect the underlying aquifer as a drinking water source for Perth. The major owner was Lew Whiteman (1903–1994), after whom the park is named.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/67721a08-b0f5-4ffa-bd9b-e4a2a00ded59" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this lab cache. You can find the bonus cache at 

S31 50.236 E115 57.460

good luck :-) &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
