 <wpt lat="-31.938383" lon="115.755167">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7Yq4-01</name>
  <desc>SLSCCityBeachT1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367</url>
  <urlname>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S1 City of Perth SLSC by papillon367</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-343" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S1 City of Perth SLSC</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>papillon367</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">papillon367</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;City of Perth SLSC&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the nineteen metal objects at the Western end of the SLSC? (1 word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/83939530-26da-442c-bb98-0f421d5aaf4a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In December 1924, members of the City of Perth Amateur Swimming Club started informal life saving operations at City Beach. Early in 1925, Western Australia’s third Surf Life Saving Club was formed.
In 1938 more substantial accommodation was built, and 30 years later those clubrooms were replaced by today’s facility in time for the club to host the 1971 Australian Surf Life Saving Championships. In 2014 the club underwent a major transformation as a part of the redevelopment of City Beach by the Town of Cambridge.
There are now well over 1200 members ranging from the very young to the very old, and facilities including clubrooms with a magnificent view overlooking the beach, exhaustive lifesaving and competition equipment, a well-equipped gymnasium, a bar and social facilities.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1b3833c7-e4ba-4779-a7a1-798e221e747d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab is a tribute to all the volunteers who keep our beaches safe. It will take you to the Surf Life Saving Clubs between City Beach and Sorrento. It can be done in any order but the Bonus cache is at the Northern end of the Adventure Lab locations.
Please enter all answers in lowercase.
Hope you enjoy this AL, and don't forget to collect the clues for the Bonus which can be found at:
South 31 47.ABC East 115 44.DBE
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c81f1128-9e4e-4e78-97e7-68b685acdac9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you have entered the right answer!
The clue for the Bonus cache is: A = 3&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.834467" lon="115.944333">
  <ele>32.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBTUz7-02</name>
  <desc>LWPS2AllAboard</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA</url>
  <urlname>[L] Whiteman Park : S2 All Aboard by sevabo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919026" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Whiteman Park : S2 All Aboard</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sevabo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sevabo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Whiteman Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;All Aboard&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Bennett Brook Railway and Central Station were opened by who?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ad02d4e0-168e-4280-bb5f-8733f1b8fe3c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bennett Book Railway is proudly owned and operated by members of the Western Australian Light Railway Preservation Association Inc.  The Association was formed on the 26th of April 1976 by a group of friends who shared an interest in the lesser known narrow gauge railways of Western Australia. Construction of the Bennett Brook Railway was started in the early 1980’s.

Starting from small beginnings in a member’s backyard, the railway has evolved to a fully fledged railway operation based in Whiteman Park. Today it encompasses relocated heritage railway infrastructure using heritage locomotives and carriages from all over Australia and the world.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0823e411-d9ef-4ac9-901d-a0a757367e16" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
Whiteman Park is a 4,000-hectare bushland area located 22 km north of Perth, WA. The park is in the suburb of Whiteman, in the Swan Valley in the upper reaches of the Swan River.
It encompasses the source of Bennett Brook - an important place of the Nyoongar people, and a source of mythology of the Wagyl and stories about Aboriginal occupancy of the area.
Whiteman Park is known for its biodiversity, including more than 450 endemic plants and more than 120 vertebrate animals (some of which are rare and endangered). More than 17% of Western Australian bird species occur in Whiteman Park, including migratory birds attracted to the habitat provided by Bennett Brook and associated wetlands including Grogan's Swamp, a Conservation Category Wetland.
The state government purchased the land from a number of private owners in 1978 to protect the underlying aquifer as a drinking water source for Perth. The major owner was Lew Whiteman (1903–1994), after whom the park is named.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/efa25850-de4c-471c-801f-9478745e577d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9292" lon="115.755733">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7Yq4-02</name>
  <desc>SLSCCityBeachT2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367</url>
  <urlname>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S2 Floreat SLSC by papillon367</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-344" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S2 Floreat SLSC</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>papillon367</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">papillon367</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Floreat SLSC&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the word in brackets on the green beach access sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c0f1fcc5-f08b-42e5-89d8-9467ceb467a7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Floreat Surf Life Saving Club was first formed in 1947 under the name of North City Surf Life Saving Club. The first general meeting that called for the forming of an official and fully functional committee was in 1957. In 1958, it was voted to change the club's name from North City to Floreat. Between 1947 and 1962, the clubrooms were wooden change rooms located just in front of the site. In 1962, new and larger clubrooms were build on the hill, closer to the main and popular area near the groyne. This location enabled easy patrolling of all the Floreat Beach, and a good position to keep watch for potential dangers. Those clubrooms were extended on two occasions (1966 &amp; 1972).
In April 1978 the coast was lashed by cyclone Alby and the clubrooms could not be saved from the eroded coastline. A small group of supporters and members successfully lobbied to maintain the Floreat SLSC, threatened of being amalgamated with the nearby City of Perth SLSC. The present day facilities were built in 1981.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1b3833c7-e4ba-4779-a7a1-798e221e747d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab is a tribute to all the volunteers who keep our beaches safe. It will take you to the Surf Life Saving Clubs between City Beach and Sorrento. It can be done in any order but the Bonus cache is at the Northern end of the Adventure Lab locations.
Please enter all answers in lowercase.
Hope you enjoy this AL, and don't forget to collect the clues for the Bonus which can be found at:
South 31 47.ABC East 115 44.DBE
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/802e783d-35a2-4117-8ff1-a33ee4477d3c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you have entered the right answer!
The clue for the Bonus cache is: B = 1&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.834033" lon="115.943983">
  <ele>32.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBTUz7-03</name>
  <desc>LWPS3TheMetropo</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA</url>
  <urlname>[L] Whiteman Park : S3 The Metropolitan Omnibus by sevabo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919028" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Whiteman Park : S3 The Metropolitan Omnibus</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sevabo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sevabo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Whiteman Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Metropolitan Omnibus&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WPWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Metropolitan Omnibus Co. was the first bus company to hire female fare collectors during the?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc13bf16-22a4-42e2-8a8c-b6464d45b59f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Metro 22 was delivered new to the Metropolitan Omnibus Company (Metro) of Fremantle on 28th June 1929 and remained in service until December 1953 when it was sold to the Central Norseman Gold Corporation. 
Metro operated an intensive bus service between Perth and Fremantle which was the route this bus would have been used on for many years. It was acquired for preservation from a property in Southern Cross in 1974 and has been fully restored by the members of the Bus Preservation Society of W.A. 
Parts from sister bus Metro 24 including its petrol engine were used to complete the restoration.  Restoration was completed in 2005.  The bus is registered for road use and is regularly used around Whiteman Park and at community events around Perth and Fremantle. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0823e411-d9ef-4ac9-901d-a0a757367e16" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
Whiteman Park is a 4,000-hectare bushland area located 22 km north of Perth, WA. The park is in the suburb of Whiteman, in the Swan Valley in the upper reaches of the Swan River.
It encompasses the source of Bennett Brook - an important place of the Nyoongar people, and a source of mythology of the Wagyl and stories about Aboriginal occupancy of the area.
Whiteman Park is known for its biodiversity, including more than 450 endemic plants and more than 120 vertebrate animals (some of which are rare and endangered). More than 17% of Western Australian bird species occur in Whiteman Park, including migratory birds attracted to the habitat provided by Bennett Brook and associated wetlands including Grogan's Swamp, a Conservation Category Wetland.
The state government purchased the land from a number of private owners in 1978 to protect the underlying aquifer as a drinking water source for Perth. The major owner was Lew Whiteman (1903–1994), after whom the park is named.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/be69324b-3027-4b2a-8389-62aec31c3374" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage
Don't forget check the last question for a bonus cache FINAL Coordinates&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.904217" lon="116.165017">
