 <wpt lat="-31.904217" lon="116.166133">
  <ele>305.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-15T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBhyXS-05</name>
  <desc>LetsExploreMun5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExploreMundaring</url>
  <urlname>Let's Explore Mundaring : S5 Mundaring Sculpture Park by arnott.family</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909979" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Let's Explore Mundaring : S5 Mundaring Sculpture Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>arnott.family</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">arnott.family</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Let's Explore Mundaring&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mundaring Sculpture Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExploreMundaring"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExploreMundaring&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many gum nuts are on the blue shirt? (in words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/04bfd8bf-2077-4402-9137-40e662fcd9ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located in the heart of Mundaring, the Park underwent a major redevelopment in 2016.
The redevelopment was supported by Lotterywest and the Department of Parks and Wildlife and incorporates a playzone where children will be able to enjoy a range of new equipment and a nature play area. One of the key attractions is the Ornate Crevice Dragon located in the sandpit, where children can dig for her eggs and insects.
There is also the boardwalk tunnel onto the equipment and rocks to climb. With ramps, soft fall and many accessible equipment pieces, this is an inclusive playground for the whole community.
Shaded areas are also available, with free gas BBQs, picnic tables, public toilets, information boards and a series of specially commissioned sculptural works featuring some of Western Australia's best known artists.
The Railway Reserves Heritage Trail passes through the park, which is also the&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e819f454-c863-4344-ba64-c0dc289f2626" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Mundaring.  Mundaring is a pleasant town on the eastern edge of the Perth Hills. It's famous in Western Australia for the Mundaring Weir which, through the remarkable engineering achievements of Charles Yelverton O'Connor, has supplied water to towns in the wheatbelt and the gold towns of Kalgoorlie and Coolgardie. Since the dam was built people have come from Perth to marvel at this watery saviour of the Eastern Goldfields. 
In this adventure we'll explore a few locations of interest in the Mundaring townsite.  All waypoints are located in walking distance and can easily be completed in under an hour.  But be sure to take some time to enjoy all that this charming little town has to offer.
Please note that any answers requiring numbers shall be in written form.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great one.  You've completed another stage&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.878283" lon="116.135933">
  <ele>210.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHWnu-01</name>
  <desc>MundaringLGAHe1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph</url>
  <urlname>Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl : S1 Parkerville Tavern by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000317" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl : S1 Parkerville Tavern</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mundaring LGA Heritage Pub Crawl&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Parkerville Tavern&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/hph&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many concrete steps lead up to the front verandah? (digit)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d1b19f2a-3b52-4f60-a05a-c9444cf13193" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Parkerville Tavern was opened in 1902 in Parkerville a hills suburb of Perth, Western Australia. It was originally called The Railway Hotel and later The Parkerville Hotel before adopting its current name in the 1970s.

In 2016 the Parkerville Tavern was listed on Western Australia’s Heritage Register for the contribution made by the place to Western Australia’s cultural heritage.

The MHI site 71 - The Parkerville Tavern has very high aesthetic significance for the style, scale and landmark value the building has which are rare both in the Townsite and the Shire generally.  The Tavern also has high social significance for the role and focus the place has provided, and should continue to provide for Parkerville’s community. 

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5ed890c9-75a3-435c-b521-bf5e55a0aecf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;

We invite you to visit some local Heritage Listed establishments within the Mundaring LGA - this adventure should take between 1 - 2 hours.  The required information is able to be accessed 24/7.



Municipal Heritage Inventory -
It is mandatory under the Heritage of Western Australia Act 1990 for each local government
to maintain a Municipal Heritage Inventory (MHI). The Shire of Mundaring MHI lists many
places that contribute to the history and character of the local area and classifies them
according to the degree of significance. Modification of places on the MHI may not always
require planning approval, although sympathetic design is still recommended to maintain the
character and heritage values. 


&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.903683" lon="116.16585">
