  <desc>SSVS5AllSaintsC</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan</url>
  <urlname>Settling in the Swan Valley : S5 All Saints Church by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-110" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Settling in the Swan Valley : S5 All Saints Church</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Settling in the Swan Valley&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;All Saints Church&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SettlingalontheSwan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Lich gate restored?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/866de299-9aa6-41f2-a16f-1eb7b399700b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Little hidden gem is Western Australia's oldest church.  It's also the farthest point Captain Stirling  reached during his examination of the Swan River.  Oh did you check to see if there was any geocaches nearby?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc1cff31-a979-47e8-849a-54b605eddcf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Swan Valley, known for its Viticulture, Breweries and its Tourism.  Follow Captain James Stirling's journey as he made his way along the Swan River in 1827 to settle a colony.  
Along the way there is plenty to see, get your taste buds working and sample some produce from well known establishments along the way.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/108b9492-e9c2-42bb-8edc-6a1c822f8141" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Excellent you have completed the lab  cache.  Whilst your here check out the cemetery, its full of history. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.868" lon="115.752517">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBx2SP-04</name>
  <desc>WESTCOASTWANDE4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WCW</url>
  <urlname>A WEST COAST WANDER : S4 Mettam's Pool by m4yb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-331" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>A WEST COAST WANDER : S4 Mettam's Pool</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>m4yb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">m4yb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;A WEST COAST WANDER&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mettam's Pool&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WCW"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WCW&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Near the coordinates at the street level are some benches, how many?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6dee7783-c890-4f59-b490-2d3a5f0e6da5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mettam's Pool is a very popular swimming spot in the northern beaches.  It is protected by a 50 to 100m wide platform reef and except on days of big swell or onshore winds, is a safer  place for families and swimmers of all ages, as well as a good snorkelling spot.
The Mettam family had a holiday house at nearby Trigg, one of the first to be built on what would come to be called Mettam Street, and lived there for 34 years.  In the 1940's, Frank Mettam and his family patiently removed the reef that bisected what was then called Lennards Pool piece by piece, using nothing but muscle and grit, and a few pickaxes. Lennards Pool was renamed Mettams Pool and promoted as a safe ocean swimming pool for children and the elderly. (West Australian, 10/9/2016)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/48498caf-36fd-4cf3-a819-120b5c5f1b75" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A little wander along some of the beachside suburbs of Perth North of the River, checking out landmarks and historic spots. The distance from start to finish is 4.7 km, a pleasant drive, cycle or walk enjoying the ocean outlook.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.857567" lon="115.759667">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLYBF-05</name>
  <desc>LSSBS5Sanitatio</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland</url>
  <urlname>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S5 Sanitation Disposal Site by iluvtrekking</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909957" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S5 Sanitation Disposal Site</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>iluvtrekking</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">iluvtrekking</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Star Swamp Bushland&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Sanitation Disposal Site&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When was the Great Depression?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86f55a97-a7b3-40d0-873a-e3b5088bf7a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Before septic tanks were introduced in the 1940s a contractor for the Road Board buried rubbish and toilet waste at this site for the people who lived nearby.

These included many unfortunate people ‘on hard times’ who lived in a tent village south of North Beach Road during the Great Depression.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6848cb18-a6c8-41dd-bdb0-612e81a8caf9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp Bushland is a 96-hectare Class-A reserve, vested in, and managed by the City of Stirling. It is located 15 km north of Perth and comprises four hectares of semi-permanent freshwater lake with paperbark trees and 92 hectares of banksia, tuart, jarrah and marri woodlands, and areas of low heath.

Star Swamp provides a breeding site and sanctuary for both waterbirds and other bushland bird species with over 65 species of birds have been recorded. The bushland is also an important habitat for species of reptiles, insects, and spiders.

The main flowering period is between August and October, although there are plants in flower all year round. Over a dozen species of orchids occur in the bushland and several hundred flora species have been listed.

Ref: https://www.bushlandperth.org.au/treasures/star-swamp-bushland-reserve/&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;E=7
Congratulations, you now have all the clues you need to find the Star Swamp Bushland Bonus Cache at
S 31 51.ABC  E 115 45.DEC
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
