   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;A WEST COAST WANDER&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Centaur Wreck Memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WCW"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WCW&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Near the given coordinates is a plaque.  In what year was the plaque unveiled?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d9ddd554-31f8-4602-8d54-96ae253fed55" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Centaur Wreck Memorial features a simple concrete bow and a plaque marking the direction of the Centaur wreck which lies approximately 2 kilometres directly out to sea in the Marmion Marine Park.
The Centaur was sunk after striking a reef (now called Centaur Reef) on 9 December 1874 while travelling from Champion Bay to Fremantle. All nine crew and four passengers, including noted politician and lawyer Septimus Burt, survived.
The 30 metre long brig, built in Aberdeen, Scotland in 1849, was carrying a cargo of 180 tonne of galena (lead ore) when it struck the reef.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/48498caf-36fd-4cf3-a819-120b5c5f1b75" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A little wander along some of the beachside suburbs of Perth North of the River, checking out landmarks and historic spots. The distance from start to finish is 4.7 km, a pleasant drive, cycle or walk enjoying the ocean outlook.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.856583" lon="115.759017">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLYBF-02</name>
  <desc>LSSBS2OrchardsA</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland</url>
  <urlname>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S2 Orchards and Pastoral Leases by iluvtrekking</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910077" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S2 Orchards and Pastoral Leases</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>iluvtrekking</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">iluvtrekking</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Star Swamp Bushland&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Orchards and Pastoral Leases&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did the orchard commence operation?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6cea7e32-f7db-4ce8-968d-a3e26608cbcc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
The North Beach Watermans Bay area was subdivided into pastoral leases in 1869-70, with the bulk of the leases taken up by the Hamersley family.

Evidence of clearing is still visible on the swamps southern side. All that remains of the old orchard is the large olive tree which you passed on your way in.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6848cb18-a6c8-41dd-bdb0-612e81a8caf9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp Bushland is a 96-hectare Class-A reserve, vested in, and managed by the City of Stirling. It is located 15 km north of Perth and comprises four hectares of semi-permanent freshwater lake with paperbark trees and 92 hectares of banksia, tuart, jarrah and marri woodlands, and areas of low heath.

Star Swamp provides a breeding site and sanctuary for both waterbirds and other bushland bird species with over 65 species of birds have been recorded. The bushland is also an important habitat for species of reptiles, insects, and spiders.

The main flowering period is between August and October, although there are plants in flower all year round. Over a dozen species of orchids occur in the bushland and several hundred flora species have been listed.

Ref: https://www.bushlandperth.org.au/treasures/star-swamp-bushland-reserve/&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;It's hard to believe that this was once an orchard, did you see the large olive tree down the track?
B=9&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.85615" lon="115.759483">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
