  <desc>LSSBS1QuendaHab</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland</url>
  <urlname>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S1 Quenda Habitat by iluvtrekking</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910075" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Star Swamp Bushland : S1 Quenda Habitat</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>iluvtrekking</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">iluvtrekking</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Star Swamp Bushland&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Quenda Habitat&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/StarSwampBushland&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who or what, should be kept under control?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7daeaaa5-77ee-43d0-8939-1a34522921d6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp bushland is habitat for the Quenda or Southern Brown Bandicoot. 

As the Quenda is a species in decline, every effort is being made to protect it from threats and disturbances. These measures include Dieback quarantine, animal pest control, weed management, revegetation and protective fencing. 

If you visit this area early morning or late afternoon, keep your eyes peeled and noise to a minimum and you may be lucky enough to spot one or two of these shy little creatures.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6848cb18-a6c8-41dd-bdb0-612e81a8caf9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp Bushland is a 96-hectare Class-A reserve, vested in, and managed by the City of Stirling. It is located 15 km north of Perth and comprises four hectares of semi-permanent freshwater lake with paperbark trees and 92 hectares of banksia, tuart, jarrah and marri woodlands, and areas of low heath.

Star Swamp provides a breeding site and sanctuary for both waterbirds and other bushland bird species with over 65 species of birds have been recorded. The bushland is also an important habitat for species of reptiles, insects, and spiders.

The main flowering period is between August and October, although there are plants in flower all year round. Over a dozen species of orchids occur in the bushland and several hundred flora species have been listed.

Ref: https://www.bushlandperth.org.au/treasures/star-swamp-bushland-reserve/&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Were you nice and quiet? Did you see a Quenda? They live under those Paperbark trees.

You will need this clue for the Bonus Geocache: A=2&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
