 <wpt lat="-31.797417" lon="115.81405">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbhsw-01</name>
  <desc>WannerooPionee1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers</url>
  <urlname>Wanneroo Pioneers : S1 Della's Dairy Farm Site by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-238" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wanneroo Pioneers : S1 Della's Dairy Farm Site</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wanneroo Pioneers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Della's Dairy Farm Site&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Between the shed and the cow pens is a square concrete foundation with 4 metal prongs. What are the first two letters etched into the concrete?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f28b4adb-4233-4352-87c0-7a33a377d9a0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Enrico Della-Marta arrived in Australia in 1924 aged 19. He initially worked in a gold mine near Gwalia (~233km north of Kalgoorlie) where he learnt to speak English. By 1925 he was working in Hotham felling trees before he took up dairy farming in Osborne Park. It was here that he met and married Ida. After 2-3 years he moved the cattle to Wanneroo where he leased the land for the dairy and subsequently purchased 500 acres. The dairy had about 100 heads of cattle but a TB outbreak reduced numbers by 20-30 over five years. 
Between 1931 - 1945 the site also operated as a second unofficial post office and telephone exchange.
The dairy was subsequently leased by a Mr Duffy before being purchased by Giovanni Gava in the 1950s.

Little of the dairy still exists with only a few cow pens and small shed still standing but the recent addition of a shared pathway makes accessibility to the site straightforward.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79813de7-ecfc-42a0-a886-bdf5617f2329" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between 1834-41 explorers &amp; surveyors John Butler, George Grey, Thomas Watson &amp; John Septimus Roe visited the area now known as Wanneroo. The success of the Swan colony depended upon its ability to grow food so finding good arable land was vital. The first farms were set up around the shores of Lake Joondalup in the mid 1840s, with the Wesleyan Missionary Society setting up an experimental farm to be lived &amp; worked on by Aboriginal people under the guidance of the church. Running at a loss with poor soil, failed crops &amp; floods, the departure of overseer Frederick Waldeck led to the demise of the farm which then moved out to York.

In 1852 Wanneroo finally became home to its first permanent settlers James and Mary-Ann Cockman. This adventure cache explores some of Wanneroo's pioneering history.

The start of this Lab Cache points to the Wanneroo Museum which is usually open 10am to 4pm Monday to Friday, 12pm to 4pm Saturday and 11am to 3pm on Sundays.

https://tinyurl.com/y9nekakx - bike friendly route.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bf51fdbd-5ad9-4849-9664-b9fdd700bad2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done! It's a bit hard to read isn't it?

Remember the first letter to your answer in order to find the finale cache&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
