 <wpt lat="-31.83045" lon="115.746033">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJffw-01</name>
  <desc>KayaWandjooS1Ma</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Kaya</url>
  <urlname>Kaya Wandjoo : S1 Mamang (whale) by thornies</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002537" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kaya Wandjoo : S1 Mamang (whale)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>thornies</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">thornies</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kaya Wandjoo&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mamang (whale)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Kaya"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Kaya&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many people depicted in the sculpture are surveying the location with binoculars?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e2bfa36-2ba6-45d6-85ff-abc59fdf971d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This beach is a great spot for a dip in the ocean or to watch a spectacular sunset. The public art work Odyssey designed by Albie Herbert remembers the site of a Whaling station.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dc7ad8d6-de30-4c45-8925-6dde0736d902" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kaya Wandjoo. These words are Hello and Welcome in Noongar language, the language of the Noongar Whadjuk  people. This beautiful coast is part of Whadjuk boodjar (country) and as you explore this adventure lab you will learn some other Noongar words and names.

This adventure lab is ideal to do on foot or bike and fills in the section of coastline between two other adventure labs. It can be started at any of the 5 locations.

I would like to acknowledge the Noongar Whadjuk  people, the Traditional Custodians of this land and pay my respects to their Elders past, present and emerging. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f7b151ac-e057-467f-95c6-ba337b5d7600" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.829167" lon="115.745183">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7Yq4-05</name>
  <desc>SLSCCityBeachT5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367</url>
  <urlname>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S5 Sorrento SLSC by papillon367</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910131" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento : S5 Sorrento SLSC</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>papillon367</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">papillon367</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;SLSC - City Beach to Sorrento&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Sorrento SLSC&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/SLSCpapillon367&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What shouldn't patrons and members climb over?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6f8ca08d-80f0-40f4-909c-eadec2e7f2e9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Sorrento Surf Life Saving Club was founded in 1958. Initially there was no club rooms so the members had to store the patrol equipment in their homes or garages. In 1960, the first clubrooms were built in front of the intersection of West Coast Drive and Padbury Circle to the south of the present clubrooms. It survived erosion during a major storm in 1962 but not the widening of West Coast Hwy. On 16 July 1969, a new set of clubrooms was built on the current site. In the late 70's, growing membership led to larger clubrooms being required. The clubrooms now comprise the old and the new building next to each other and were officially opened on 30th September 1978.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1b3833c7-e4ba-4779-a7a1-798e221e747d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab is a tribute to all the volunteers who keep our beaches safe. It will take you to the Surf Life Saving Clubs between City Beach and Sorrento. It can be done in any order but the Bonus cache is at the Northern end of the Adventure Lab locations.
Please enter all answers in lowercase.
Hope you enjoy this AL, and don't forget to collect the clues for the Bonus which can be found at:
South 31 47.ABC East 115 44.DBE
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b2d39e00-502c-4175-8c08-b9f079e2c9d7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, you have entered the right answer!
The clue for the Bonus cache is: E = 6&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8255" lon="115.740717">
  <ele>10.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZ5P9-03</name>
  <desc>LHillarysBoatH3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HBH</url>
  <urlname>[L] Hillarys Boat Harbour  : S3 Iron by the Sea by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-335" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Hillarys Boat Harbour  : S3 Iron by the Sea</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Hillarys Boat Harbour &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Iron by the Sea&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HBH"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HBH&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who designed and manufactured the boulder? (3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b1bd981-0d23-4a7e-b742-3cd7ff9cb037" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At the listed coordinates you will find a statue to Langley George Hancock, the man credited with discovering Western Australia's great Iron deposits in the Pilbara. The Statue itself is a large boulder of Banded Iron Formation (BIF).
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5d4ea447-030f-4b64-8021-d67e3cddab1a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hillarys was named after an early settler, Bertram John Hillary (1895-1957), who had become blind in his right eye while fighting in Gallipoli during the First World War. He came to the area during the Great Depression while fishing for a living with his brother Harry, and built a boatshed on the beach in 1930. Subsequently, he expanded it to accommodate himself, his wife and four children. For many years, they were the only residents of what is now Hillarys, and the nearest store, at North Beach to the south, was a 5 km walk away. During the Second World War, the Australian Army used the area, and named it "Hillary's Beach" on their maps. Bert Hillarys died in 1957 and the shack and 14 others nearby were destroyed in a fierce storm in 1964.
There will be a Bonus Cache - GC8JXYB
Good Luck
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1bc323ff-8659-4f14-ae22-193527f836e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
