 <wpt lat="-31.784233" lon="115.805717">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbhsw-02</name>
  <desc>WannerooPionee2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers</url>
  <urlname>Wanneroo Pioneers : S2 Cockman House by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-239" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wanneroo Pioneers : S2 Cockman House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wanneroo Pioneers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cockman House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Next to the Cockman Information sign is a lamp post - what is the stock code on this post?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/47be8170-02d9-4b6f-b9ae-27aabcfaf705" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;James Cockman (1809-1877) arrived in Australia in August 1829 from Gloucestershire, England - just 3 months after the colony was proclaimed. He married Mary-Anne Roper (1812-1901) on 15th March 1830 in the first Anglican wedding in the colony. In 1852 they moved to the area to set up a 45 acre dairy farm and market garden at the edge of Walluburnup Swamp.

The building we see today is their second home built approx 1860 by James and his son John. The first house was a wattle and daub construction known as the little house. It was burnt down after becoming vermin infested. James and John quarried, by hand, the limestone from outcrops on the eastern side of the lake. The timber was prepared in one of the Shenton saw mills.

James and Mary had 7 children and 4 generations lived here until it was purchased and restored by the City of Wanneroo in 1988.

The house is normally open Sundays 2pm-5pm (closed February) but you do not need to enter to answer the question.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79813de7-ecfc-42a0-a886-bdf5617f2329" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between 1834-41 explorers &amp; surveyors John Butler, George Grey, Thomas Watson &amp; John Septimus Roe visited the area now known as Wanneroo. The success of the Swan colony depended upon its ability to grow food so finding good arable land was vital. The first farms were set up around the shores of Lake Joondalup in the mid 1840s, with the Wesleyan Missionary Society setting up an experimental farm to be lived &amp; worked on by Aboriginal people under the guidance of the church. Running at a loss with poor soil, failed crops &amp; floods, the departure of overseer Frederick Waldeck led to the demise of the farm which then moved out to York.

In 1852 Wanneroo finally became home to its first permanent settlers James and Mary-Ann Cockman. This adventure cache explores some of Wanneroo's pioneering history.

The start of this Lab Cache points to the Wanneroo Museum which is usually open 10am to 4pm Monday to Friday, 12pm to 4pm Saturday and 11am to 3pm on Sundays.

https://tinyurl.com/y9nekakx - bike friendly route.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/855571a4-02d8-448c-ad98-db0617fa84d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Pictured is John Cockman, son of James and Mary-Anne.

Remember the first letter to your answer in order to find the finale cache&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.33445" lon="116.057333">
