 <wpt lat="-31.78105" lon="115.799917">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbhsw-03</name>
  <desc>WannerooPionee3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers</url>
  <urlname>Wanneroo Pioneers : S3 Perry's Paddock by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-240" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wanneroo Pioneers : S3 Perry's Paddock</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wanneroo Pioneers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perry's Paddock&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the sign board - what is the 4th word on the 6th line in the main body of text&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/08bc4c78-ab47-4250-b0a4-d85f1d668bfe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally known as Shenton Park, Perry's Paddock was renamed by John (Jack) Perry who purchased 1300 acres from the Shenton Estate in 1912. The Perry family operated a horse breaking and stock dealing business here from 1912 to 1929.

Between 1915 and 1930, Wanneroo Picnic Race Days were held here annually. This community event was one of the highlights of the year and consisted of foot races in the morning and horse racing in the afternoon. Lunch was served under the shade of the large trees to the north. The last race day occurred in 1930 after which the WA Turf Club refused permission for racing and betting at Perry's Paddock.

From the 1930s the paddock was used as a market garden by the Hastings and then Duffy families.

Race days were re-introduced between 1992 and 2001.

Perry's cottage dates from around 1850 and was known as Linhay. The stables are reduced to a pile of rubble. The second building, the bunkhouse, was reconstructed in the 1990s but its origin is unknown.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79813de7-ecfc-42a0-a886-bdf5617f2329" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between 1834-41 explorers &amp; surveyors John Butler, George Grey, Thomas Watson &amp; John Septimus Roe visited the area now known as Wanneroo. The success of the Swan colony depended upon its ability to grow food so finding good arable land was vital. The first farms were set up around the shores of Lake Joondalup in the mid 1840s, with the Wesleyan Missionary Society setting up an experimental farm to be lived &amp; worked on by Aboriginal people under the guidance of the church. Running at a loss with poor soil, failed crops &amp; floods, the departure of overseer Frederick Waldeck led to the demise of the farm which then moved out to York.

In 1852 Wanneroo finally became home to its first permanent settlers James and Mary-Ann Cockman. This adventure cache explores some of Wanneroo's pioneering history.

The start of this Lab Cache points to the Wanneroo Museum which is usually open 10am to 4pm Monday to Friday, 12pm to 4pm Saturday and 11am to 3pm on Sundays.

https://tinyurl.com/y9nekakx - bike friendly route.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b928fa7-3e16-4db7-aee4-128632fac7a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Nice one! Great place to let the geo-kids &amp; geo-animals run around.

Remember the first letter to your answer in order to find the finale &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
