  <desc>JRS4ThePit</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Jarrahdale</url>
  <urlname>Jarrahdale Ramble : S4 The Pit by OOH YEH</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910007" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Jarrahdale Ramble : S4 The Pit</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>OOH YEH</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">OOH YEH</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Jarrahdale Ramble&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Pit &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Jarrahdale"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Jarrahdale&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The sawyer standing on top of the log is called&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c415b35-743f-40d2-834e-eb903bb6e231" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This will require a short walk from the main road . Take in the scenery as the area is all re-growth . You are looking for a plaque &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/82ad2925-f839-4e39-b927-7576c26b2604" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One of Western Australia’s oldest settlements, Jarrahdale is set in the Darling Range and surrounded by Jarrah forest. The town is only an hour south of Perth and thirty minutes from Armadale or Rockingham.
HISTORICALLY 
In 1871, the Rockingham Jarrah Company was granted a lease of 250,000 acres of jarrah forest in the Darling Range. In 1872, the company built a sawmill in Jarrahdale, a jetty at Rockingham for loading the timber onto ships for export, and a railway line to connect them. The Jarrahdale to Rockingham train line was the second rail line in the colony and initially had wooden rails and a locomotive that averaged 20km/hour. The wooden rails were replaced with iron from 1878 - 1882, and they lasted till 1950. Jarrahdale was  the state's first major timber milling operation, and it played a key role in the development of W.A. through the exportation of jarrah around the world. In 1872, 8220 people lived in Perth, and over 300 lived and worked in Jarrahdale. Between 1893 and 1899.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7403f833-ba02-4d96-95f4-cab4a805e9ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
