  <desc>BaldivisDogPar2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BDP</url>
  <urlname>Baldivis Dog Park : S2 Buds by Kyzabra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001485" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Baldivis Dog Park : S2 Buds</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kyzabra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kyzabra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Baldivis Dog Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Buds&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BDP"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/BDP&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many Tuart Buds or fruit (in total including all stages) can you see on the ground here? (Write the number as a word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ea34465-5402-425e-9a96-c03f6d36c898" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Tuart trees in Baldivis drop buds everywhere. There are a few on the ground right here (albeit, these ones are a bit bigger)
You will see them in various stages here.

Tuarts grow up to 40 metres high. Its rough, fibrous grey bark flakes into small pieces. The leaves are often curved, 90 to 160 millimetres long, and are a shiny light green above and paler below. The almost stalkless buds cluster in groups of seven. Tuart buds are very distinctive; they have swollen bud caps and are shaped like small ice cream cones. Each bud cap is eight to 10 millimetres long and the species name refers to this feature - ‘gompho’ (club) and ‘cephala’ (head). &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/604a7b92-e3c2-46b2-b593-9bcb4266a413" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a short stroll from the Baldivis Dog Park, and down along the creek.
There are some interesting artworks along this short adventure lab series.
Park at the dog park, start your wander, then turn around and come back to give your 4 legged friends a real run.

At each location, there will be a clue once you answer the question. Grab the 5 letters (one in each answer), which will reveal a key word. You will need this keyword for a nearby mystery cache.

Keep a note of the clues for the bonus at https://coord.info/GC8PQ4H 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cade99f3-a09a-463f-8986-aea2483ad975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done. You obviosuly got the extra one by itself.

The letter you need here is "E"&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
