 <wpt lat="-32.268317" lon="115.68975">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdVvS-02</name>
  <desc>ExplorePointPe2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron</url>
  <urlname>Explore Point Peron  : S2 North Beach by Kapelula</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002547" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Explore Point Peron  : S2 North Beach</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kapelula</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kapelula</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Explore Point Peron &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;North Beach&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many pylons can you count on the Causeway Bridge?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df132b9f-9fb7-4195-bc11-fa614d5c3719" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With its protection from the sea breeze and its north facing location looking towards HMAS Stirling Naval Base, this a great place for a swim or a snorkel.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/72fff342-9c75-423e-8579-8fec3b1e60b5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Cape Peron, or as we know it locally, Point Peron.

Point Peron holds great significance for the Whadjuk people, the traditional owners of the area, whose family groups would travel through the region utilising seasonal resources.  It is still a special place for them to preserve their Aboriginal ties and traditions.

Point Peron was named after French Zoologist and Naturalist, François Péron who accompanied Commodore Nicholas Baudin on his expedition along the western coast of Australia in 1801.
From 1942-1945 (during WWII) Point Peron was the site of the "K" Battery complex .  This battery complex was one of a chain of coastal gun placements defending the Perth region from Swanbourne, in the north to Cape Peron in the south.

The area is well known for its protected beaches, limestone cliffs, great snorkelling, diving, fishing and scenic views.  

We hope you enjoy this Adventure Lab along this beautiful coastline.  
 
Don't forget to take note of the clues for the bonus cache GC8Z7VQ!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3e07d7a9-72ba-43ab-b187-e59254f16e67" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;e
Write this down for a bonus cache!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.265417" lon="115.686383">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdVvS-03</name>
  <desc>ExplorePointPe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron</url>
  <urlname>Explore Point Peron  : S3 John Point by Kapelula</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002549" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Explore Point Peron  : S3 John Point</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kapelula</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kapelula</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Explore Point Peron &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;John Point&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the length span of the northern approach anti-submarine boom defence?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87d571de-fcdb-425a-9cb1-49c52537fe8e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;"In the Spotlight"
Two Coastal Artillery Searchlight (CASLs) were established once the Peron Battery was commissioned.  They were controlled by a Director Electric Control which enabled them to be operated from John Point and Mushroom Rock.  Whilst dark the searchlights were manned from dawn to dusk and were the Battery's 'eyes'.
An anti-submarine boom fence began construction in 1943 as a part of the strategy to prevent passage of enemy ships or midget submarines.  This was also constructed across the southern entrances in the approximate area where the Causeway Bridge now links Point Peron to Garden Island.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/72fff342-9c75-423e-8579-8fec3b1e60b5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Cape Peron, or as we know it locally, Point Peron.

Point Peron holds great significance for the Whadjuk people, the traditional owners of the area, whose family groups would travel through the region utilising seasonal resources.  It is still a special place for them to preserve their Aboriginal ties and traditions.

Point Peron was named after French Zoologist and Naturalist, François Péron who accompanied Commodore Nicholas Baudin on his expedition along the western coast of Australia in 1801.
From 1942-1945 (during WWII) Point Peron was the site of the "K" Battery complex .  This battery complex was one of a chain of coastal gun placements defending the Perth region from Swanbourne, in the north to Cape Peron in the south.

The area is well known for its protected beaches, limestone cliffs, great snorkelling, diving, fishing and scenic views.  

We hope you enjoy this Adventure Lab along this beautiful coastline.  
 
Don't forget to take note of the clues for the bonus cache GC8Z7VQ!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fe00ca18-c16f-4b18-84ef-bb1bacf10cd9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;f
Write this down for a bonus cache!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
