 <wpt lat="-32.269883" lon="115.688867">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdVvS-01</name>
  <desc>ExplorePointPe1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron</url>
  <urlname>Explore Point Peron  : S1 Peron Battery Observation Post by Kapelula</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-417" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Explore Point Peron  : S1 Peron Battery Observation Post</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kapelula</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kapelula</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Explore Point Peron &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Peron Battery Observation Post&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Peron Battery built?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/13b2a428-8b14-4377-8db4-7306985ef328" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the highest point on the Cape and offers spectacular views of the coastline and the Shoalwater Marine Park.
Point Peron was once an integral part of the coastal defence or Western Australia during WWII.  Once the Japanese attacked Darwin Harbour there was an urgent need to strengthen the defences of Australia.  This battery was last in one of a string of heavy coastal batteries up to Swanbourne which also included Garden and Rottnest Islands.  Thankfully none of this artillery was used in battle.  The battery was decommissioned in December 1944.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/72fff342-9c75-423e-8579-8fec3b1e60b5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Cape Peron, or as we know it locally, Point Peron.

Point Peron holds great significance for the Whadjuk people, the traditional owners of the area, whose family groups would travel through the region utilising seasonal resources.  It is still a special place for them to preserve their Aboriginal ties and traditions.

Point Peron was named after French Zoologist and Naturalist, François Péron who accompanied Commodore Nicholas Baudin on his expedition along the western coast of Australia in 1801.
From 1942-1945 (during WWII) Point Peron was the site of the "K" Battery complex .  This battery complex was one of a chain of coastal gun placements defending the Perth region from Swanbourne, in the north to Cape Peron in the south.

The area is well known for its protected beaches, limestone cliffs, great snorkelling, diving, fishing and scenic views.  

We hope you enjoy this Adventure Lab along this beautiful coastline.  
 
Don't forget to take note of the clues for the bonus cache GC8Z7VQ!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c361f17f-14cc-471c-8a19-9e31b99c1a20" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;r 
Write this down for a bonus cache!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
