   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Explore Point Peron &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Skywalk&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ExplorePointPeron&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Cape Peron is a part of which Marine Park?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b8f29cfd-3081-49b5-a62c-284cc0f8d723" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Peron has a diverse coastal environment comprising of rocky islands, submarine banks, reefs, dunes and wetlands.  There are many species of plants and animals, some of which are rare outside the area.
Enjoy the views from the skywalk!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/72fff342-9c75-423e-8579-8fec3b1e60b5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Cape Peron, or as we know it locally, Point Peron.

Point Peron holds great significance for the Whadjuk people, the traditional owners of the area, whose family groups would travel through the region utilising seasonal resources.  It is still a special place for them to preserve their Aboriginal ties and traditions.

Point Peron was named after French Zoologist and Naturalist, François Péron who accompanied Commodore Nicholas Baudin on his expedition along the western coast of Australia in 1801.
From 1942-1945 (during WWII) Point Peron was the site of the "K" Battery complex .  This battery complex was one of a chain of coastal gun placements defending the Perth region from Swanbourne, in the north to Cape Peron in the south.

The area is well known for its protected beaches, limestone cliffs, great snorkelling, diving, fishing and scenic views.  

We hope you enjoy this Adventure Lab along this beautiful coastline.  
 
