 <wpt lat="-31.750667" lon="115.8072">
  <ele>71.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbhsw-05</name>
  <desc>WannerooPionee5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers</url>
  <urlname>Wanneroo Pioneers : S5 Bortolo (Bert) Togno by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-242" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wanneroo Pioneers : S5 Bortolo (Bert) Togno</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wanneroo Pioneers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bortolo (Bert) Togno&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the second most northern post, which is the 3rd surname on the 2nd from bottom plate?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7d1dbf1d-e997-49d3-a93b-bb85529c7720" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bert Togno Park in Wanneroo is named after Bortolo (Bert) Togno (1894 - 1987). Bert arrived in Australia in 1911 and originally settled in Cranbrook and worked as a shearer for Synott and Dunbar, later becoming a dairy farmer in Wanneroo. After returning to Italy and marrying Maria Pollette, he returned to WA. They had 4 children - Erica, Zelia, Frank and Alex.

Local history notes that he lived at Buckingham House and sometime between the 1940s and 50s they added windows to the south side of the property and the verandah.

Bert Togno park contains a path with wall of honour commemorating the settler families of Wanneroo which show many familiar names later attributed to roads, parks and suburbs in the Wanneroo surrounds.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79813de7-ecfc-42a0-a886-bdf5617f2329" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between 1834-41 explorers &amp; surveyors John Butler, George Grey, Thomas Watson &amp; John Septimus Roe visited the area now known as Wanneroo. The success of the Swan colony depended upon its ability to grow food so finding good arable land was vital. The first farms were set up around the shores of Lake Joondalup in the mid 1840s, with the Wesleyan Missionary Society setting up an experimental farm to be lived &amp; worked on by Aboriginal people under the guidance of the church. Running at a loss with poor soil, failed crops &amp; floods, the departure of overseer Frederick Waldeck led to the demise of the farm which then moved out to York.

In 1852 Wanneroo finally became home to its first permanent settlers James and Mary-Ann Cockman. This adventure cache explores some of Wanneroo's pioneering history.

The start of this Lab Cache points to the Wanneroo Museum which is usually open 10am to 4pm Monday to Friday, 12pm to 4pm Saturday and 11am to 3pm on Sundays.

https://tinyurl.com/y9nekakx - bike friendly route.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cfa4bffd-dbee-422f-8eac-def9472e4786" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;So many famous surnames!

Remember the first letter to your answer in order to find the finale cache&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.75105" lon="115.802533">
  <ele>51.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-06</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo06</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S06 Wanneroo by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-196" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S06 Wanneroo</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wanneroo&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the sum of the numbers to the right of '=' in the right hand column of the blackboard?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf2f909-5dec-4c39-9a2c-7df954cbc792" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;31.3km by road from Perth

In the early days of the Swan River Colony, the district of Wanneroo represented an "outer" region and it is not until 1834, that an excursion into the area by any explorers or settlers is recorded. In that year, a party of four led by John Butler, travelled about 35 miles north from Perth to search for lost cattle and passed through the area just to the east of Lake Joondalup. Butler stated that the aborigines of the area were "those wanneroo men who frequent Perth in company with the Yellowgonga tribe." It is not known when early settlers started using the name "Wanneroo", but, as early as 1842, a Surveyor recorded "road to Wanneroo" and in 1844, James Dobbins gave his address as Wanneroo. A government townsite was gazetted as "Wanneru", an Aboriginal word referring to the sticks used by women for digging roots, in August 1907 and it was not until May 1953 that the spelling was amended to "Wanneroo".&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.743817" lon="115.795633">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2020-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbhsw-04</name>
  <desc>WannerooPionee4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers</url>
  <urlname>Wanneroo Pioneers : S4 Buckingham House and Old School Room by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-241" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wanneroo Pioneers : S4 Buckingham House and Old School Room</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wanneroo Pioneers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Buckingham House and Old School Room&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Pioneers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the sign board seen behind the fence what is the 2nd word on the 6th line of the body of text?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/00b9fa7f-b558-4a05-9f98-0ab04f2067b7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Buckingham House 
John Buckingham arrived in the Swan River Colony in 1850 aboard "Sophia" with his wife and two children. John Buckingham Jnr married Emily Amelia Okley and had 5 children. He purchased 100 acres of land in 1876 around Lake Joondalup and built the house sometime in the 1880s. The house was leased by dairy farmers including William Tapping (1846-1935) and his wife Mary in 1899.  The Shire of Wanneroo purchased the house in 1975 and today it runs as a heritage education centre for local primary school children. 

Old Wanneroo Schoolroom - is a combination of two school rooms. Wanneroo school was originally located on the corner of Dundebar &amp; Wanneroo Rds. The larger section dates from 1899 and the smaller section was added in 1928 due to increased numbers of children at the East Wanneroo school (relocated from Jandabup). It has been moved 3 further times including in 1992 to Perry's Paddock as part of the city's collection of heritage buildings. In 2009 it was moved to the current location.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79813de7-ecfc-42a0-a886-bdf5617f2329" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between 1834-41 explorers &amp; surveyors John Butler, George Grey, Thomas Watson &amp; John Septimus Roe visited the area now known as Wanneroo. The success of the Swan colony depended upon its ability to grow food so finding good arable land was vital. The first farms were set up around the shores of Lake Joondalup in the mid 1840s, with the Wesleyan Missionary Society setting up an experimental farm to be lived &amp; worked on by Aboriginal people under the guidance of the church. Running at a loss with poor soil, failed crops &amp; floods, the departure of overseer Frederick Waldeck led to the demise of the farm which then moved out to York.

In 1852 Wanneroo finally became home to its first permanent settlers James and Mary-Ann Cockman. This adventure cache explores some of Wanneroo's pioneering history.

The start of this Lab Cache points to the Wanneroo Museum which is usually open 10am to 4pm Monday to Friday, 12pm to 4pm Saturday and 11am to 3pm on Sundays.

https://tinyurl.com/y9nekakx - bike friendly route.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84795de8-d2f4-4771-9c20-6ba02dbfb80e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Lots of local names listed in that school photo!

Remember the first letter to your answer in order to find the finale cache&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
