 <wpt lat="-31.749" lon="115.775283">
  <ele>52.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfYWR-05</name>
  <desc>JoondalupArtWa5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk</url>
  <urlname>Joondalup Art Walk : S5 The Graduate by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-272" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Joondalup Art Walk : S5 The Graduate</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Joondalup Art Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Graduate&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many parts of the shoelace can you see on the statue's left shoe? (write the word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e4c7e36-ff09-480f-a3e3-9b4855239570" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Not technically part of the Joondalup public art collection, this bronze sculpture at the entrance to the WA Police training academy is worth checking out anyway.  The life-size figure was originally modelled in clay before being cast in bronze, the whole process taking around 12 months.

'The Graduate' was created by father and son duo Tony Jones and Ben Jones, who, individually and together are the artists responsible for some of WA's interesting and iconic public art, the most notable being the bronze sculpture 'Eliza' in the Swan River.  Others include the bronze sculpture life guards at Scarborough beach, and C. Y. O'Connor and his horse in the surf at Coogee.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e099d6f-908b-4eaa-a996-33ee351892e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This art trail explores some of the early public art installed in Joondalup when the suburb was being developed.  Most of the art has been specifically commissioned by the City of Joondalup, but many of the institutions in the area have also installed public art worth visiting.

If you have time, a walk or cycle is the best way to explore the area.  You could visit the locations in either a clockwise or anticlockwise direction, and enjoy the art, scenery and bushland on the way.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0340b413-fa3f-4e3a-9ddb-9fb845ada53b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;'Arbor Vitae' nearby is an abstract representation of police shields inlaid with braille expressions relating to the Police recruit oath.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.3555" lon="115.7858">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2020-04-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEh6J-03</name>
  <desc>CraftBeerWineT3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CBAWT</url>
  <urlname>Craft Beer &amp; Wine Trail : S3 Whitelakes Brewing by pommieparkin2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-285" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Craft Beer &amp; Wine Trail : S3 Whitelakes Brewing</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pommieparkin2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pommieparkin2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Craft Beer &amp; Wine Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Whitelakes Brewing&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CBAWT"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CBAWT&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many lights are set into the wall? Enter the number, not the word.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19833d73-55e3-4222-a9b3-f54eb7b93240" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take an easy 40-minute drive south from the Perth CBD and you’ll find Whitelakes Brewing, created via a substantial makeover to the Old Barnyard function centre in Baldivis and launched in July 2016. 
Located on a vast corner block, the building's modern glass, steel and wood stylings hold a small bar area within the brewhouse. The alfresco deck looks across a large lawn – typically filled with families on the weekends – to Lake Walyungup, the inspiration for the brewery’s name and artwork.
Despite the saline appearance of the protected wetlands, the "white lakes" are actually fresh water, with the illusion the result of a limestone cap beginning at the lakes and running south more than 200 kilometres to Yallingup. It’s a vista that provides a unique backdrop – often complete with stunning sunsets &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5f5ebda9-0d65-4e22-ba61-6bcbb26516e4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If discovering quality, unique craft beers and wine is at the top of your geocaching 'to do' list, the Peel Region Craft Beer and Wine Trail makes it easy for you. We have included five of the locations in this Adventure. The trail provides an authentically local experience, to be enjoyed at your own pace. You will need to hop into the geomobile and go for a drive through some attractive countryside in the Peel Region. You do not have to enter the establishment......but then again.....Why not?
 VIEW ALL JOURNAL ENTRIES FOR DETAILS ABOUT A BONUS CACHE. CHEERS, Have fun.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ccb8d24a-5dba-4a04-8d92-4a2ec6076ecf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;You will need to make a note of the letter R for the keyword in the checker for the bonus cache&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.743017" lon="115.778317">
  <ele>32.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfYWR-01</name>
  <desc>JoondalupArtWa1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk</url>
  <urlname>Joondalup Art Walk : S1 Bibulman Yorga by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-268" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Joondalup Art Walk : S1 Bibulman Yorga</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Joondalup Art Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bibulman Yorga&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;"..........." is a songline for a country.  Looking at the plaque, what is the missing word?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fad0e101-db26-4c67-a06d-9f68321ec9e4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ron Corbett is an Aboriginal artist born in 1956.  He created the bronze artwork 'Bibulman Yorga', which was installed at Neil Hawkins Park in 1998.

The piece is a life-size sculpture of an Aboriginal woman and her dog, who represent the traditional owners of the Joondalup region.

Ron Corbett's other public art includes the Indigenous inspired images at the eastern end of the Northbridge Tunnel. 

Take the time to enjoy Neil Hawkins park while you are here.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e099d6f-908b-4eaa-a996-33ee351892e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This art trail explores some of the early public art installed in Joondalup when the suburb was being developed.  Most of the art has been specifically commissioned by the City of Joondalup, but many of the institutions in the area have also installed public art worth visiting.

If you have time, a walk or cycle is the best way to explore the area.  You could visit the locations in either a clockwise or anticlockwise direction, and enjoy the art, scenery and bushland on the way.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2af6b028-4e9c-43be-afd7-50c686b60eea" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Check out the bollards around Joondalup. The sculptor Peter Dailey drew inspiration from the 1920’s German
expressionist film 'Metropolis'.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.746133" lon="115.771033">
  <ele>54.0</ele>
