  <desc>JoondalupArtWa4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk</url>
  <urlname>Joondalup Art Walk : S4 Metal Trees by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-271" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Joondalup Art Walk : S4 Metal Trees</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Joondalup Art Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Metal Trees&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Have a look at either of the two bronze plaques.  What was the surname of the Mayor at the time?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2cd62c9a-bc33-4809-8df2-6ba00d098de4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Metal Trees was created as part of a 'National Metal Industry’ public art project in 1994. 

In Western Australia, Kevin Draper was selected to work from the Transfield workshops at Garden Island to create the artwork.  The sculptures were originally located at Joondalup Arena, but a major redevelopment of the sporting complex resulted in them being relocated to Joondalup's Central Park in 2002.

Albany artist Kevin Draper has a number of art pieces throughout Perth and the South West, which are often made of steel and worked metal.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e099d6f-908b-4eaa-a996-33ee351892e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This art trail explores some of the early public art installed in Joondalup when the suburb was being developed.  Most of the art has been specifically commissioned by the City of Joondalup, but many of the institutions in the area have also installed public art worth visiting.

If you have time, a walk or cycle is the best way to explore the area.  You could visit the locations in either a clockwise or anticlockwise direction, and enjoy the art, scenery and bushland on the way.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84710197-5b4d-44eb-b6df-08ae6e629560" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Following relocation, artist Indra Geidans worked with people from Disabled and Disadvantaged Arts Aust. to create the surrounding mosaics.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.744967" lon="115.771433">
  <ele>56.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfYWR-03</name>
  <desc>JoondalupArtWa3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk</url>
  <urlname>Joondalup Art Walk : S3 War Memorial by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-270" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Joondalup Art Walk : S3 War Memorial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Joondalup Art Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;War Memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name on the boys belt?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a730daf3-24e1-4808-a988-649750e0a9af" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The war memorial in Joondalup was created by Joan Walsh-Smith and Charles Smith on behalf the 
Wanneroo – Joondalup Returned and Services, and the 10 metre high memorial and bronze statue here was unveiled on ANZAC day in 1996.

The top of the memorial is a semi-circular window through which the rising sun shines.  It is inscribed with the words, ‘Those only deserve freedom who are prepared to defend it’. The sculptors describe the boy with the poppy as "a symbol of the innocence of children and the desire for world peace".

Originally from Ireland, Smith Sculptors are a husband and wife team who, along with their daughter, specialise in large public art projects in Western Australia, Australia, and internationally. The team is responsible for some of the iconic memorials and public art in WA, such as the Kangaroos on St Georges Terrace, Percy Button (the street entertainer doing a handstand) in the Hay Street Mall, and the HMAS Sydney memorial in Geraldton.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e099d6f-908b-4eaa-a996-33ee351892e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This art trail explores some of the early public art installed in Joondalup when the suburb was being developed.  Most of the art has been specifically commissioned by the City of Joondalup, but many of the institutions in the area have also installed public art worth visiting.

If you have time, a walk or cycle is the best way to explore the area.  You could visit the locations in either a clockwise or anticlockwise direction, and enjoy the art, scenery and bushland on the way.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d356d950-b7f0-410b-92a9-2f6801ea916b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Keep an eye on the kerbs in Joondalup. 1,000 ceramic medallions were painted by the community and were installed around the City in 1994. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.743567" lon="115.773367">
  <ele>58.0</ele>
  <time>2020-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfYWR-02</name>
  <desc>JoondalupArtWa2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk</url>
  <urlname>Joondalup Art Walk : S2 Bronze Plaques by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-269" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Joondalup Art Walk : S2 Bronze Plaques</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Joondalup Art Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bronze Plaques&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/JoondalupArtWalk&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;One pair of plaques don't match.  What living creatures are found on these two plaques?   &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/26908612-0170-458e-ac95-35165433a2ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Steve Tepper created this series of 15 pairs of bronze plaques, which were installed here in 1998.  You will find the plaques incorporated in the columns along the Joondalup Library and Civic Centre underpass.

Each pair of plaques can be found installed opposite each other.  All but one pair is the positive and negative cast of each other, which represent early domestic life in the Joondalup area.

Steve Tepper has many other public art installations all around Perth, a favourite of which is the octopus at Heathcote playground. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e099d6f-908b-4eaa-a996-33ee351892e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This art trail explores some of the early public art installed in Joondalup when the suburb was being developed.  Most of the art has been specifically commissioned by the City of Joondalup, but many of the institutions in the area have also installed public art worth visiting.

If you have time, a walk or cycle is the best way to explore the area.  You could visit the locations in either a clockwise or anticlockwise direction, and enjoy the art, scenery and bushland on the way.
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7faf475d-ae9f-42ac-a15c-65a26445ae85" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Nearby is a floor mosaic created in 1997 by Mark Cox.
The stainless-steel and stone inlay takes inspiration from Lake Joondalup.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.99555" lon="115.541733">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-30T08:00:00Z</time>
