   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Welcome to Rottnest&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Welcome to Country&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a20eeaee-4f8d-4a77-9351-3ce8f4f7d0a3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a20eeaee-4f8d-4a77-9351-3ce8f4f7d0a3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the first two words of the main text on the Welcome to Country sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/635bcc4a-d48d-4782-9c0a-0a7d04af659b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;However you got here I hope you enjoyed your journey over to Rottnest.

In this area you will find the main tourist information office and 4 large information boards along the edge of the quay. These detail just a few of the many activities to be enjoyed on Rotto, and provide maps of the island and the main settlement. With so much to see &amp; do (and so little time!) it can be helpful to plan your visit to gain maximum enjoyment.

You will quickly realise that the pace of life is a little slower over here - helped in part by the fact the only road traffic allowed is a small fleet of essential vehicles. There are no private cars on the island. The majority of visitors to Rotto hire a bicycle (or bring their own over with them) - the bike hire facility on Rottnest is the largest in the southern hemisphere!

Also here you will find an interactive Welcome to Country greeting from an Elder of the local Aboriginal people and the traditional owners of the land on which you stand.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6be0f959-3fcc-482b-823b-da42db56d643" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Situated 18kms offshore in the sparkling Indian Ocean, Rottnest Island is Perth's playground - with over 500,000 visitors each year coming to enjoy this A Class protected reserve.

Known as Wadjemup to the local Nyoongar people, 'Rotto' is absolutely packed with incredible experiences, breathtaking scenery, fantastic beaches, amazing wildlife and a huge array of things to see &amp; do. Whether you flew here, sailed over, caught a ferry or even swam here (yes, people do!), you'll quickly discover why Rotto is much-loved by locals and visitors alike.

It's a destination like no other. This Adventure will highlight a few locations to compliment your visit and can easily be completed on foot during a day trip - giving you heaps of time to explore and enjoy the rest of the island!

Note: All visitors are required to pay an admission fee to the island (Government levy to help fund conservation). Accordingly, this Adventure has been approved for publication by Geocaching HQ.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d7e35283-3c93-4668-aa60-92af871e93b7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done - now your Rotto experience can begin :)

Don't forget to grab a free map of the island from the tourist information office.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.996017" lon="115.540683">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBB6HK-02</name>
  <desc>WelcomeToRottn2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a20eeaee-4f8d-4a77-9351-3ce8f4f7d0a3</url>
  <urlname>Welcome to Rottnest : S2 Thomson Bay Settlement by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919014" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Welcome to Rottnest : S2 Thomson Bay Settlement</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Welcome to Rottnest&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Thomson Bay Settlement&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a20eeaee-4f8d-4a77-9351-3ce8f4f7d0a3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a20eeaee-4f8d-4a77-9351-3ce8f4f7d0a3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was a tramway built to carry visitors from the (Army) Jetty to the Settlement?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/90143a0b-682e-4e50-a40d-f0c27d0af3bc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Thompson Bay is the main hub of the island. Various facilities are located here to keep you happy &amp; safe, including places to eat &amp; drink, shop, essential services (fire, police &amp; medical), a variety of holiday accomodation units and holiday fun for all the family (fun park, playgrounds, mini golf, even a cinema). It is also the start / end point for many of the island tours (fees payable).

The Settlement is also one of the places you are guaranteed to see Rottnest's most famous resident - the quokka. Famed for always looking happy, this little marsupial (similar to kangaroos and wallabies), can be seen hopping all over the island. And with no natural predators it's estimated there are between 12,000 &amp; 15,000 of them!

The quokka is also how Rottnest got its name: early Dutch explorers mistook them for giant rats and named the island Rotte Nest (meaning rat's nest).

An information board nearby provides some details of the history behind the main shopping area and its origins as a stable yard.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6be0f959-3fcc-482b-823b-da42db56d643" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Situated 18kms offshore in the sparkling Indian Ocean, Rottnest Island is Perth's playground - with over 500,000 visitors each year coming to enjoy this A Class protected reserve.

Known as Wadjemup to the local Nyoongar people, 'Rotto' is absolutely packed with incredible experiences, breathtaking scenery, fantastic beaches, amazing wildlife and a huge array of things to see &amp; do. Whether you flew here, sailed over, caught a ferry or even swam here (yes, people do!), you'll quickly discover why Rotto is much-loved by locals and visitors alike.

It's a destination like no other. This Adventure will highlight a few locations to compliment your visit and can easily be completed on foot during a day trip - giving you heaps of time to explore and enjoy the rest of the island!

Note: All visitors are required to pay an admission fee to the island (Government levy to help fund conservation). Accordingly, this Adventure has been approved for publication by Geocaching HQ.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/42ddb7c0-1990-4e3d-abf9-0ac16091c191" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done.

I hope you got your 'quokka selfie'. Just remember, they are wild animals so look but don't touch (and definitely don't feed).&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.994967" lon="115.539733">
