  <name>LBkaWL-03</name>
  <desc>YaberooBudjara3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/YBHT</url>
  <urlname>Yaberoo Budjara Heritage Trail : S3 Directional signs by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-340" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Yaberoo Budjara Heritage Trail : S3 Directional signs</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Yaberoo Budjara Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Directional signs&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/YBHT"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/YBHT&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many triangles can you see on the banksia tree?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8530532a-c235-4112-a286-e6393a4d2146" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On all trails you will find small triangles acting as an arrow to keep you on the right path and heading in the correct direction.  Quite often with a specific pattern or design, lots of these were destroyed in the last bushfire, the design on this trail is the Xanthorrhoea australis or commonly known as a "black boy" or the more politically correct title of a grass tree.  However I am advised that many indigenous locals have always referred to them as black boys!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c2f2c5b7-befb-464d-a211-5baf96d6e98f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This 28 kilometre one way trail is based on the movement of Yellagonga and his people between Lake Joondalup and Yanchep.

Welcome to a small section of this trail.  This section runs approximately 1.5 Kilometres South from Yanchep Beach Road and follows about 8 of my Traditional caches towards the end of this section of the trail at Lacey Rd.  You can start anywhere along the trail but worth doing with the traditional's along that route, if you haven't already done it!

There is a "Bonus Cache" so you will need to take notes of the clues as you progress.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6e8cd5db-867d-46ef-9874-0d533233645c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Deduct 1 from your answer for this clue and you will have completed the southings co-ordinates.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
