 <wpt lat="-31.5508" lon="115.625467">
  <ele>18.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnWWY-02</name>
  <desc>PerthNorthernB2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM</url>
  <urlname>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S2 Fisherman's Hollow by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-368" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S2 Fisherman's Hollow</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Northern Beaches Meander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fisherman's Hollow&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last name of the artist who created the Mosaic?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7cb69570-c14f-4290-a14c-4d8f60b9ef6a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At GZ you will find an outstanding mosaic mural, which is quite stunning.

Fisherman's Hollow together with Yanchep Lagoon have been frequented by holiday makers and fishermen since the early 1900s. In 1926 Mary Lindsay purchased approximately 23,000 acres of land along the coast in the approximate location of Two Rocks. This became known as Yanchep Estate. A timber framed
homestead was constructed in the area north of Brazier Road and west of Two Rocks Road. This
has been totally renovated and is now an art studio/gallery and is your next visit on this adventure.

The hollow is still used as a popular picnic spot by locals and tourists and it has BBQ facilities and is an ideal spot to relax or kick a ball of walk your dog.  An ideal spot for Geocaching events and I have held 2 here.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c216064-ccd7-4b93-bb4c-d9523e5b22a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A meander from the Wreck of the Alkimos to Leeman's Landing in Two Rocks, with some scenic outlooks and plenty of spots of historical significance on the way. 

This can easily be completed within a day or you could rush and do it in a few hours, however I recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Western Australia's early history.  Some parts might have you thinking about the early European passengers who thought they were heading to Batavia (Jakarta) but ended up being shipwrecked on what is now the beautiful northern beaches of Perth.

It is best completed from south to north, however it can be completed in any order you like, parking is readily available at all points or for the fitter geocachers a bicycle ride is quite doable.

Keep a close eye on your answers at each stage, because there might be a "Bonus Box" (GC8ANQJ) published.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.544883" lon="115.623067">
  <ele>14.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnWWY-03</name>
  <desc>PerthNorthernB3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM</url>
  <urlname>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S3 Mary Lindsey's Homestead by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-369" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S3 Mary Lindsey's Homestead</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Northern Beaches Meander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mary Lindsey's Homestead&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the nickname of the overseer of Wydgee for a decade.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/205f49c9-8b43-4b30-af1f-61de6d4a074d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this location, you will be standing outside the entrance to the newly renovated Mary Lindsey Cottage, in front of the plaque where you will find the answer to allow you to move on.  BUT before you do I suggest it is well worth a read of the history of this extraordinary woman and if you time you visit well, have a visit to the gallery and a chat with some of the volunteers who look after the place now it has been redeveloped.

FRIDAY is OPEN STUDIO 10am - 3pm Everyone is welcome to visit the studio, bring a project to work on or drop in for a coffee and a chat or look at the art work.
SUNDAY, the Gallery is OPEN to the public from 11am - 3pm. ALL WELCOME!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c216064-ccd7-4b93-bb4c-d9523e5b22a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A meander from the Wreck of the Alkimos to Leeman's Landing in Two Rocks, with some scenic outlooks and plenty of spots of historical significance on the way. 

This can easily be completed within a day or you could rush and do it in a few hours, however I recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Western Australia's early history.  Some parts might have you thinking about the early European passengers who thought they were heading to Batavia (Jakarta) but ended up being shipwrecked on what is now the beautiful northern beaches of Perth.

It is best completed from south to north, however it can be completed in any order you like, parking is readily available at all points or for the fitter geocachers a bicycle ride is quite doable.

Keep a close eye on your answers at each stage, because there might be a "Bonus Box" (GC8ANQJ) published.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1900b804-17c4-4399-b8de-fd45db170bc6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;View from the Lookout just up the path on a blustery day. There is a geocache there as well!  Actually 2 in close vicinity to the homestead.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.627683" lon="115.877017">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2021-07-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHURU-01</name>
  <desc>LSixSeasonsS1Bi</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc</url>
  <urlname>[L] Six Seasons : S1 Birak &amp; Bunuru by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001493" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Six Seasons : S1 Birak &amp; Bunuru</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Birak &amp; Bunuru&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What season falls in August-September&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/32b04c35-4be6-4c1d-ad4c-0fdb626592ba" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These are the two hottest seasons of the year and are known as the First Summer (Birak) &amp; Second Summer (Bunuru)

Running roughly from December to March the winds tend to be from the east with afternoon sea breezes giving some respite if you are close to the coast.

The cooler Birak season was a time of year for burning the land to help with fuel reduction, aid seed germination, clear grazing lands and ease travel across the land.

The hotter Bunuru and the respite of the cooler sea breeze had many people living nearer to coastal areas, rivers and estuaries and as such diets of seafoods became a staple for the people.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5831b488-1fdd-4ff3-8850-33840cedc500" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The South West of Western Australia is the land of the Noongar people.
Here the year is separated into six season, the signs of the land, animals and weather dictate the changing of the seasons.
This short Adventure Lab will give you some knowledge of each of these seasons.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/119a0682-ebc1-43d7-8c0a-2f8cab417e38" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done you will need the following for the bonus cache:
AB&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.627717" lon="115.877067">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2021-07-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHURU-03</name>
  <desc>LSixSeasonsS3Ma</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc</url>
  <urlname>[L] Six Seasons : S3 Makuru by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910103" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Six Seasons : S3 Makuru</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Makuru&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What season has turtles on the tiles at the base of the totem?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9426aec5-8217-4f58-8f54-2452965ef207" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Makuru runs through June &amp; July.

It is the coldest and wettest part of the year, populations moves inland away from the coast and as such the diet changed from seafood to grazing animals such as the yongar (kangaroo).
These animals not only provided food, every part of the animal was used, the skins were made into coverings to protect through the cold and bones were fashioned into tools and weapons.

In nature you will see birds starting to pair up for breeding and the flowers changing from purples to whites.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5831b488-1fdd-4ff3-8850-33840cedc500" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The South West of Western Australia is the land of the Noongar people.
Here the year is separated into six season, the signs of the land, animals and weather dictate the changing of the seasons.
This short Adventure Lab will give you some knowledge of each of these seasons.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dff2b2e2-3c5d-4d6c-a20f-b1729094a505" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done you will need the following for the bonus cache:
MR&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.627717" lon="115.877">
