  <urlname>[L] Six Seasons : S4 Djilba by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910105" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Six Seasons : S4 Djilba</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Djilba&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d139f461-a4db-4c71-9e62-8aa0e170c4fc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What seasons totem depicts emu, kangaroo and bird?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1ab4dafb-4d97-447c-8b1d-d149148c13ce" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Djilba is a season of growth and occurs from August to September.

The weather starts to transition from the cold and wet of Makuru, there can be clear and cold nights and warmer rainy days.

The birds that were pairing up in the previous season now have chicks and parents will protect their nests, just like the koolbardi (magpie) and djidi djidi (willy wag tail).

Diet continued to be based around grazing animals such as the yongar (kangaroo), waitj (emu) and the koomal (possum).

Towards the end of the season the flower stalks of the balga (grass tree) start to grow in preparation for blooming in the new season.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5831b488-1fdd-4ff3-8850-33840cedc500" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The South West of Western Australia is the land of the Noongar people.
Here the year is separated into six season, the signs of the land, animals and weather dictate the changing of the seasons.
This short Adventure Lab will give you some knowledge of each of these seasons.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d264decd-a352-4327-811a-79f0c34d8e18" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done you will need the following for the bonus cache:
AK&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
