   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Northern Beaches Meander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Alex T Brown&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the word on the start of the 9th line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/950ac1e0-b09a-4d3b-a34b-68d5d05ed610" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here you will find a nice new plaque with good description of the wreck, I had thought this might require a much further walk out to the point where the original plaque is.  It is still there but starting to corrode rather badly. 

The cover photo for this was taken last year after the winds and storms had uncovered the wreck and this shows the old plaque in the distance.  Do not go down to the wreck site, as there is now a Permanent Conservation Order on the wreck now, however you can get great views form the hill not too far of a walk from GZ, or from the old plaque.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c216064-ccd7-4b93-bb4c-d9523e5b22a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A meander from the Wreck of the Alkimos to Leeman's Landing in Two Rocks, with some scenic outlooks and plenty of spots of historical significance on the way. 

This can easily be completed within a day or you could rush and do it in a few hours, however I recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Western Australia's early history.  Some parts might have you thinking about the early European passengers who thought they were heading to Batavia (Jakarta) but ended up being shipwrecked on what is now the beautiful northern beaches of Perth.

It is best completed from south to north, however it can be completed in any order you like, parking is readily available at all points or for the fitter geocachers a bicycle ride is quite doable.

Keep a close eye on your answers at each stage, because there might be a "Bonus Box" (GC8ANQJ) published.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8535054e-6659-4c23-b4ea-afa092034966" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The wreck as we found it after I was alerted to it by a local friend, its nearly fully covered up again now.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.50195" lon="115.586583">
  <ele>10.0</ele>
  <time>2019-07-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnWWY-05</name>
  <desc>PerthNorthernB5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM</url>
  <urlname>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S5 Vergulde Draeck by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910081" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Perth Northern Beaches Meander : S5 Vergulde Draeck</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Perth Northern Beaches Meander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Vergulde Draeck&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/PNBM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the dates on the coins found?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3805c0f2-17a7-423c-ad13-744155bf136f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this point you will find a plaque with a story of one of the first shipwrecks on the WA coast.  I think this raises some interesting questions or thoughts as to what happened to the survivors or the original wreck!

Some great rock formations here, and if you wonder down to the beach you can see where the name Two Rocks comes from.

Worth a visit up to the shopping centre at Two Rock and the old car park for Atlantis the old water theme park and the statue of King Neptune.  Also full of interesting history, and could be another Adventure Lab Cache, if more get given out.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c216064-ccd7-4b93-bb4c-d9523e5b22a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A meander from the Wreck of the Alkimos to Leeman's Landing in Two Rocks, with some scenic outlooks and plenty of spots of historical significance on the way. 

This can easily be completed within a day or you could rush and do it in a few hours, however I recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Western Australia's early history.  Some parts might have you thinking about the early European passengers who thought they were heading to Batavia (Jakarta) but ended up being shipwrecked on what is now the beautiful northern beaches of Perth.

It is best completed from south to north, however it can be completed in any order you like, parking is readily available at all points or for the fitter geocachers a bicycle ride is quite doable.

Keep a close eye on your answers at each stage, because there might be a "Bonus Box" (GC8ANQJ) published.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.550167" lon="116.467167">
  <ele>137.0</ele>
  <time>2019-04-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBdqJo-07</name>
  <desc>GreatWestOzRo07</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019</url>
  <urlname>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S07 Toodyay by West Oz Geocaching</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-197" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Great West Oz Road Trip : S07 Toodyay</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Great West Oz Road Trip&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Toodyay&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WOZ2019&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Nearby is a plaque dedicating his service to Toodyay district of a mail contractor.  What is his surname?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5c611a8f-3f87-434f-b08d-c59c8954dcb9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;88.3km by road from Perth

The townsite of Toodyay is located on the Avon River in the central agricultural region. It is one of the earliest inland towns in the state, and was surveyed in 1833 following the settlement of the Avon region. However, the townsite's location on the Avon was subject to flooding, and in 1860 an alternative site about 5 km higher up the Avon was gazetted as a townsite and named Newcastle (probably named after the Duke of Newcastle, Secretary of State 1853). 

In 1909 the Prime Minister wrote to the state government requesting consideration be given to changing the name of Newcastle because of duplication with Newcastle in New South Wales. The Prime Minister suggested the name Toodyay be considered. The Newcastle Municipality at first opposed the change, but following a referendum of residents the name change to Toodyay was supported. The change was gazetted in May 1910, and at the same time the Toodyay townsite was renamed West Toodyay.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/02bfc544-156d-4cad-a499-2bcc61ab7736" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the West Oz Geocaching Road Trip adventure taking you to some interesting locations in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.71265" lon="116.062833">
  <ele>265.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBb6k9-01</name>
  <desc>LDwellingupTra1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce</url>
  <urlname>[L] Dwellingup Trails : S1 First Surveyor's Peg by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909843" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Dwellingup Trails : S1 First Surveyor's Peg</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Dwellingup Trails&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;First Surveyor's Peg&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last name of the person who made the plaque in 2009?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1981367a-b1ef-4953-a35b-730411ce0bc2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Prior to European settlement, the Dwellingup area was recognised as important an important area for the local Aboriginal groups for foraging and tool making.

Different tribes used to travel through the Dwellingup forest area to access watering holes and use caves as stop over for shelter, before moving down onto the open coastal plain. The Darling Scarp and forest environment was therefore an important area for hunting, gathering, tool making and movement of Aboriginal tribes before the colonisation.

Primarily a timber mill town when gazetted in 1910, Dwellingup became the administrative centre for a number of neighbouring mill towns which popped up after the opening of the railway from Pinjarra to Dwellingup.

This marks the spot where the first peg was driven into the ground by Surveyor William F Rudell on 19 October 1909.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b61a24aa-07de-4f97-90fa-3a81d9a0e69e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure will take you on a small tour of the town of Dwellingup.  

At the end of the Adventure, you will receive the coordinates for a Bonus Cache located a short drive away: Dwellingup Trails AL Bonus Box (GC940X8).

Seeing as you have driven all the way here, I recommend you park at the visitor centre and take the opportunity to stretch the legs and walk to the various locations. It won't take longer than half an hour.

It is worth taking the time to check out the visitor centre either before or after your walk.  And if you have even more time to spend in the area, there is a lot to explore.  Take a ride on the Hotham Valley Railway, go camping or adventuring at Lane Pool Reserve, there are heaps of trails to explore by foot, bike, horse or 4WD - and this is is one of the few places the Bibbulmun and the Munda Biddi tracks intersect.

Or if too much adventure is not your thing, just spend time in town checking out some of the unique attractions.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.7133" lon="116.061217">
