   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Dwellingup Trails&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Heritage Garden Precinct&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did Mick join the army?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/552406bb-7a5a-41f1-ab6c-95365bd33697" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here you will find the Mick French Memorial Garden.  Mick French lived in Dwellingup for 80 years, and loved spending time in his garden.  

There is now a Mick French Award at the annual Dwellingup Rose Show each autumn.

If roses aren't your thing, how about pumpkins?  Dwellingup hosts an annual Pumpkin Festival each autumn which includes a giant pumpkin weigh-off.

Or check out the Dwellingup Country Markets hosted by the Dwellingup Country Womens Association volunteers every third Sunday of the month from March to December in the garden at the CWA Hall.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b61a24aa-07de-4f97-90fa-3a81d9a0e69e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure will take you on a small tour of the town of Dwellingup.  

At the end of the Adventure, you will receive the coordinates for a Bonus Cache located a short drive away: Dwellingup Trails AL Bonus Box (GC940X8).

Seeing as you have driven all the way here, I recommend you park at the visitor centre and take the opportunity to stretch the legs and walk to the various locations. It won't take longer than half an hour.

It is worth taking the time to check out the visitor centre either before or after your walk.  And if you have even more time to spend in the area, there is a lot to explore.  Take a ride on the Hotham Valley Railway, go camping or adventuring at Lane Pool Reserve, there are heaps of trails to explore by foot, bike, horse or 4WD - and this is is one of the few places the Bibbulmun and the Munda Biddi tracks intersect.

Or if too much adventure is not your thing, just spend time in town checking out some of the unique attractions.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
