   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Dwellingup Trails&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;RSL Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the front of the building is a sign that says "DWELLINGUP R.S.L. SUB : ........".  What is the missing word?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/304209e4-7c27-4a77-b7be-2d9ea8d47465" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This building opened in 1911 and was originally the Marrinup School, and in 1947 the building was relocated to Dwellingup to become the RSL Hall.

A major bushfire devastated Dwellingup and surrounding towns in January 1961.  People gathered at the school, football oval and the hotel to escape the fire. 

The RSL Hall managed to survive, and was used to house people who lost their homes.

Dwellingup was the only town to be rebuilt.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b61a24aa-07de-4f97-90fa-3a81d9a0e69e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure will take you on a small tour of the town of Dwellingup.  

At the end of the Adventure, you will receive the coordinates for a Bonus Cache located a short drive away: Dwellingup Trails AL Bonus Box (GC940X8).

Seeing as you have driven all the way here, I recommend you park at the visitor centre and take the opportunity to stretch the legs and walk to the various locations. It won't take longer than half an hour.

It is worth taking the time to check out the visitor centre either before or after your walk.  And if you have even more time to spend in the area, there is a lot to explore.  Take a ride on the Hotham Valley Railway, go camping or adventuring at Lane Pool Reserve, there are heaps of trails to explore by foot, bike, horse or 4WD - and this is is one of the few places the Bibbulmun and the Munda Biddi tracks intersect.

Or if too much adventure is not your thing, just spend time in town checking out some of the unique attractions.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.715983" lon="116.063667">
  <ele>284.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBb6k9-04</name>
  <desc>LDwellingupTra4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce</url>
  <urlname>[L] Dwellingup Trails : S4 Whims by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-301" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Dwellingup Trails : S4 Whims</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Dwellingup Trails&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Whims&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/10bd7e37-07f9-4517-acdf-c44b86bcbfce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;By 1885, how many 'loads' of timber were exported from WA?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2f48252e-d062-43c5-9bea-a309ce0af9fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dewllingup was built around the timber industry.

WA's first recorded export of Jarrah, known as Swan River Mahogany by the first settlers, was in February 1831.  The industry grew, with increasing demand for Jarrah in the form of sleepers, paving, piles and pales.

Before steam locomotives took over their role, Clydesdale horses were used for transporting logs from the bush to the mill and were used extensively in the timber industry during the late 1800’s to early 1900’s.

A whim was used to allow a team of horses to pull logs from the bush to the mill’s bush landing, where the logs were loaded onto rail trolleys and hauled by another team of horses to the mill. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b61a24aa-07de-4f97-90fa-3a81d9a0e69e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure will take you on a small tour of the town of Dwellingup.  

At the end of the Adventure, you will receive the coordinates for a Bonus Cache located a short drive away: Dwellingup Trails AL Bonus Box (GC940X8).

Seeing as you have driven all the way here, I recommend you park at the visitor centre and take the opportunity to stretch the legs and walk to the various locations. It won't take longer than half an hour.

It is worth taking the time to check out the visitor centre either before or after your walk.  And if you have even more time to spend in the area, there is a lot to explore.  Take a ride on the Hotham Valley Railway, go camping or adventuring at Lane Pool Reserve, there are heaps of trails to explore by foot, bike, horse or 4WD - and this is is one of the few places the Bibbulmun and the Munda Biddi tracks intersect.

Or if too much adventure is not your thing, just spend time in town checking out some of the unique attractions.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8868" lon="116.770183">
  <ele>181.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZXAY-01</name>
  <desc>WalkAroundYork1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY</url>
  <urlname>Walk Around York : S1 York Swing Bridge by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-46" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Walk Around York : S1 York Swing Bridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walk Around York&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;York Swing Bridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In which month was the old bridge on this site officially closed?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/17fdad29-7b28-4e7e-9af8-bb5b1dafb115" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first bridge over the Avon River was built by convicts in 1853 but the bridge has been rebuilt several times over the ensuing years. This existing suspension bridge was built in 1988 to a modified design that addresses relevant safety concerns.  There is a plaque near the western side of the bridge that will provide you with more information. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c452bfad-b838-47c2-add9-bdb16cad50e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to York, the first and therefore the oldest inland town in Western Australia.  This delightful country town is situated on the Avon River on Ballardong Nyoongar land.  Within an hour's drive from Perth, York provides an escape to a place bursting with natural, cultural and historical hidden gems.
The Adventure Lab will take you for a casual stroll on an easy circuit to places of significant historical interest including locations with links to the town's convict past.
Although the locations can be visited in any order, a good place to start would be the Swing Bridge where there is ample parking and conveniences,
Please note that several of the places of interest are privately owned and the required information can be found on plaques adjacent to the footpaths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a76cf5c8-1d51-4b84-a0c4-d627e60962b2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;You got it!  And if you are thinking about the bonus cache, keep track of the kangaroos.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.65625" lon="116.652233">
  <ele>231.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsxjg-04</name>
  <desc>NSS4MtOmmanney</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights</url>
  <urlname>Northam Sights : S4 Mt Ommanney by geotrakka</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-54" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Northam Sights : S4 Mt Ommanney</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>geotrakka</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">geotrakka</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Northam Sights&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mt Ommanney&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many discs at the base of the tower? (use digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/155d01d7-5875-4b84-b250-7e7313ad0690" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Northams lookout. A great view over the valley and Northam townsite. This is especially beautiful during winter or in spring with the wildflowers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/74f2cf0c-62b8-46b9-bc98-7d9c3780b3b8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Northam the heart of the picturesque Avon Valley. Only an hour from Perth, Northam is home to a diverse range of tourist and heritage attractions. Come relax by the Avon River, take in some of the town’s rich history, or walk across the suspension bridge to view WAs only colony of white swans. Other attractions include, walking trails, historic buildings, the Avon Decent and much more. Seasonal canola splashing fields of yellow, rolling hills and bright coloured hot air balloons are just some of the visual delights to be enjoyed.
There is a bonus cache after collecting the clues. Saving Mount Ommanney till last will stop you having to backtrack.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cfb2b2d1-7220-4785-95a9-9766be694d55" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!! You just had to get the right angle to see them all !
You are close to the final 
E=9&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.886517" lon="116.772533">
  <ele>186.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZXAY-02</name>
  <desc>WalkAroundYork2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY</url>
  <urlname>Walk Around York : S2 Holy Trinity Church by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-47" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Walk Around York : S2 Holy Trinity Church</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walk Around York&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Holy Trinity Church&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the surname of the person who laid this foundation stone in 1893?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/25515ab5-7216-4432-a729-ee998dd34d22" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built in 1854, and consecrated in 1858, the Holy Trinity Church was the second Anglican Church to be built in York.  The building was designed by architect Richard Jewell in the style of 11th century Victorian Romanesque. A replica of the original foundation stone can be found on the eastern side of the building.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c452bfad-b838-47c2-add9-bdb16cad50e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to York, the first and therefore the oldest inland town in Western Australia.  This delightful country town is situated on the Avon River on Ballardong Nyoongar land.  Within an hour's drive from Perth, York provides an escape to a place bursting with natural, cultural and historical hidden gems.
The Adventure Lab will take you for a casual stroll on an easy circuit to places of significant historical interest including locations with links to the town's convict past.
Although the locations can be visited in any order, a good place to start would be the Swing Bridge where there is ample parking and conveniences,
Please note that several of the places of interest are privately owned and the required information can be found on plaques adjacent to the footpaths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cd3e8225-083e-453e-aae2-13a9204f0e3d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Another correct answer. Well done!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8928" lon="116.774533">
  <ele>183.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZXAY-04</name>
  <desc>WalkAroundYork4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY</url>
  <urlname>Walk Around York : S4 The Old Hospital by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-49" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Walk Around York : S4 The Old Hospital</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walk Around York&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Old Hospital&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was the maternity block added?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/de124c4c-866d-4c9f-bd88-dc329bd41f90" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Old York Hospital was built on the site of the York Convict Depot in 1894 ( replacing the first York Hospital) from the designs of architect George Temple-Poole. The building is said to be haunted complete with terrifying stories of paranormal encounters from very 'credible witnesses'.  Fortunately, you can get all the information you need from the York Heritage Trail plaque in front of the building.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c452bfad-b838-47c2-add9-bdb16cad50e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to York, the first and therefore the oldest inland town in Western Australia.  This delightful country town is situated on the Avon River on Ballardong Nyoongar land.  Within an hour's drive from Perth, York provides an escape to a place bursting with natural, cultural and historical hidden gems.
The Adventure Lab will take you for a casual stroll on an easy circuit to places of significant historical interest including locations with links to the town's convict past.
Although the locations can be visited in any order, a good place to start would be the Swing Bridge where there is ample parking and conveniences,
Please note that several of the places of interest are privately owned and the required information can be found on plaques adjacent to the footpaths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c272a954-c148-4c99-99f9-9f875c3011fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Correct! That plaque makes for interesting reading.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.885083" lon="116.772967">
  <ele>181.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZXAY-03</name>
  <desc>WalkAroundYork3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY</url>
  <urlname>Walk Around York : S3 Kairey Cottage by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-48" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Walk Around York : S3 Kairey Cottage</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walk Around York&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Kairey Cottage&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What natural material was used for the original roof shingles?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ef5acf38-cf60-4891-b009-ecf83cbb60f5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Colonial mudbrick cottage was built in 1859 and is York's last surviving Pensioner Cottage. Pensioner guards were granted land in York for services guarding convicts on the voyage to Australia. This cottage is typical of the era and boasts well kept gardens and outbuildings. A York Heritage Trail plaque on the verge in front of the cottage provides more detailed information.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c452bfad-b838-47c2-add9-bdb16cad50e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to York, the first and therefore the oldest inland town in Western Australia.  This delightful country town is situated on the Avon River on Ballardong Nyoongar land.  Within an hour's drive from Perth, York provides an escape to a place bursting with natural, cultural and historical hidden gems.
The Adventure Lab will take you for a casual stroll on an easy circuit to places of significant historical interest including locations with links to the town's convict past.
Although the locations can be visited in any order, a good place to start would be the Swing Bridge where there is ample parking and conveniences,
Please note that several of the places of interest are privately owned and the required information can be found on plaques adjacent to the footpaths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79073e40-d96a-4abb-aa36-757678762c1c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Earned another tick here and a few more roos to count!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8927" lon="116.775433">
  <ele>187.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZXAY-05</name>
  <desc>WalkAroundYork5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY</url>
  <urlname>Walk Around York : S5 The Residency Museum by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-50" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Walk Around York : S5 The Residency Museum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walk Around York&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Residency Museum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/WAY&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the first name of Nicholas Hasluck's paternal grandmother?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/616c8b4e-be20-4b04-842a-2f830f737a7c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The museum was originally part of York's Convict Hiring Depot and was built in the 1850's.  It was built as the Depot Superintendent's Quarters and is the last remaining part of the York Convict Depot.  As a museum, it now provides a fascinating insight into the history of the region. Even if it is closed, there are information panels and old relics of bygone eras on display outside. On the front wall, there is also a brass plaque sponsored by Nicholas Hasluck, a retired judge and poet who has a home in York.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c452bfad-b838-47c2-add9-bdb16cad50e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to York, the first and therefore the oldest inland town in Western Australia.  This delightful country town is situated on the Avon River on Ballardong Nyoongar land.  Within an hour's drive from Perth, York provides an escape to a place bursting with natural, cultural and historical hidden gems.
The Adventure Lab will take you for a casual stroll on an easy circuit to places of significant historical interest including locations with links to the town's convict past.
Although the locations can be visited in any order, a good place to start would be the Swing Bridge where there is ample parking and conveniences,
Please note that several of the places of interest are privately owned and the required information can be found on plaques adjacent to the footpaths.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5b26a619-48a1-4046-b928-4b566205abd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;All done, unless you are after the bonus cache. If so, you will need to know the number of kangaroos that have made an appearance in the lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.651967" lon="116.6701">
  <ele>153.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBsxjg-01</name>
  <desc>NSS1SwingingBri</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights</url>
  <urlname>Northam Sights : S1 Swinging Bridge by geotrakka</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-51" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Northam Sights : S1 Swinging Bridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>geotrakka</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">geotrakka</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Northam Sights&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Swinging Bridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/NorthamSights&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt; What is the live load capacity of this bridge? (In digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ceca9d3a-8f6c-4bee-b784-5c129cbf9c0b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built in 1975 at 117m in length makes this Australia's longest pedestrian suspension bridge. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/74f2cf0c-62b8-46b9-bc98-7d9c3780b3b8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Northam the heart of the picturesque Avon Valley. Only an hour from Perth, Northam is home to a diverse range of tourist and heritage attractions. Come relax by the Avon River, take in some of the town’s rich history, or walk across the suspension bridge to view WAs only colony of white swans. Other attractions include, walking trails, historic buildings, the Avon Decent and much more. Seasonal canola splashing fields of yellow, rolling hills and bright coloured hot air balloons are just some of the visual delights to be enjoyed.
There is a bonus cache after collecting the clues. Saving Mount Ommanney till last will stop you having to backtrack.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/beebbc67-c229-4d7d-b50e-48c721db699d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Welldone.
Dont forget to note down the clue
A=4&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
