  <name>LBD9ce-04</name>
  <desc>BoddingtonS4Tul</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e</url>
  <urlname>Boddington : S4 Tullis Bridge by SirFancy</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-89" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Boddington : S4 Tullis Bridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SirFancy</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SirFancy</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Boddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tullis Bridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the crossing on the Tullis Walk Trail sign? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/35b04874-9fba-4769-b0e4-934af1815f55" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The bridge was built in 1912 and was used to link the railway line from Pinjarra to Narrogin for use by the timber industry up until 1968. Tullis Bridge now remains as two separate structures at either side of the riverbank as it was set alight in the early 1990’s by some campers in the area. 

At Tullis Bridge there are picnic tables and a public toilet. 
A 3km walk trail has been created out at Tullis Bridge that takes you around the bridge and surrounding river.  

There is limited signal at Tullis bridge, it is recommened that you keep the app open during the drive to this location.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71563ee4-6257-4c3c-a3cf-253849e6da04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;History
The town owes its name to an early settler, Henry Boddington, who was a farmer and shepherd in the 1860s and 1870s and leased land in the area in 1875.

The area declined slowly over time, and the 1961 Western Australian bushfires devastated the local timber industry. Boddington became a typical small service area for the surrounding district. However, the establishment of bauxite mining in 1979 to service Western Australia's alumina production at Worsley, Kwinana and Wagerup, and the opening of the Boddington Gold Mine in 1987 created a thriving mining town.

Present day
Boddington is located 11 kilometres (7 mi) off Albany Highway and 123 kilometres (76 mi) southeast of Perth in the Darling Scarp. Boddington each year on the first weekend of November hosts a 3-day rodeo.

A Royal Historical Society plaque near the town marks the grave of a local Aboriginal named Quency Dilyan who helped explorers Alexander Forrest and H. S. Ranford during their expeditions in the area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.799333" lon="116.4722">
  <ele>210.0</ele>
