   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Boddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Hotham River Foreshore&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the outside Toilet wall there is some tile art, what number is the Tortoise tile art piece?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e17c769f-76c4-42bb-9562-38f79e644b84" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here is a nice place to park and a new playground for children and toilets and a café.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71563ee4-6257-4c3c-a3cf-253849e6da04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;History
The town owes its name to an early settler, Henry Boddington, who was a farmer and shepherd in the 1860s and 1870s and leased land in the area in 1875.

The area declined slowly over time, and the 1961 Western Australian bushfires devastated the local timber industry. Boddington became a typical small service area for the surrounding district. However, the establishment of bauxite mining in 1979 to service Western Australia's alumina production at Worsley, Kwinana and Wagerup, and the opening of the Boddington Gold Mine in 1987 created a thriving mining town.

Present day
Boddington is located 11 kilometres (7 mi) off Albany Highway and 123 kilometres (76 mi) southeast of Perth in the Darling Scarp. Boddington each year on the first weekend of November hosts a 3-day rodeo.

A Royal Historical Society plaque near the town marks the grave of a local Aboriginal named Quency Dilyan who helped explorers Alexander Forrest and H. S. Ranford during their expeditions in the area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.799183" lon="116.476483">
  <ele>217.0</ele>
  <time>2021-03-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBD9ce-01</name>
  <desc>BoddingtonS1Mem</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e</url>
  <urlname>Boddington : S1 Memorial Park by SirFancy</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-86" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Boddington : S1 Memorial Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SirFancy</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SirFancy</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Boddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Memorial Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was this site rededicated? (numbers only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b1696d25-06b7-4dae-8fff-c818986c9c0b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The creation of the Boddington War Memorial was instigated by the Returned Services League Boddington sub branch in 1990. It has been utilized every year since for the dawn service ceremony.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71563ee4-6257-4c3c-a3cf-253849e6da04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;History
The town owes its name to an early settler, Henry Boddington, who was a farmer and shepherd in the 1860s and 1870s and leased land in the area in 1875.

The area declined slowly over time, and the 1961 Western Australian bushfires devastated the local timber industry. Boddington became a typical small service area for the surrounding district. However, the establishment of bauxite mining in 1979 to service Western Australia's alumina production at Worsley, Kwinana and Wagerup, and the opening of the Boddington Gold Mine in 1987 created a thriving mining town.

Present day
Boddington is located 11 kilometres (7 mi) off Albany Highway and 123 kilometres (76 mi) southeast of Perth in the Darling Scarp. Boddington each year on the first weekend of November hosts a 3-day rodeo.

A Royal Historical Society plaque near the town marks the grave of a local Aboriginal named Quency Dilyan who helped explorers Alexander Forrest and H. S. Ranford during their expeditions in the area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.800617" lon="116.474433">
  <ele>217.0</ele>
  <time>2021-03-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBD9ce-05</name>
  <desc>BoddingtonS5Tow</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e</url>
  <urlname>Boddington : S5 Town Hall by SirFancy</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-90" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Boddington : S5 Town Hall</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SirFancy</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SirFancy</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Boddington&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Town Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35054fa4-6b44-4886-a547-86eead8a4c6e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;A new town hall was proposed in 1954 for what cost? (numbers only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d8bf8d3e-db01-4cb7-808d-2337dbd29331" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The initial Town Hall was built in 1923 on the same site of the Boddington Town Hall. The Settlers' Association were responsible for building and managing the Hall. It was made of timber weatherboard and corrugated iron. 
In 1957, the new Hall was built despite community opposition. 
Internal features within the Hall were considered significantly progressive for the 1950's. These features included a projector room, a cloak room, a proscenium arch stage with rear access, adjacent dressing rooms, internal toilets, and an established kitchen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71563ee4-6257-4c3c-a3cf-253849e6da04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;History
The town owes its name to an early settler, Henry Boddington, who was a farmer and shepherd in the 1860s and 1870s and leased land in the area in 1875.

The area declined slowly over time, and the 1961 Western Australian bushfires devastated the local timber industry. Boddington became a typical small service area for the surrounding district. However, the establishment of bauxite mining in 1979 to service Western Australia's alumina production at Worsley, Kwinana and Wagerup, and the opening of the Boddington Gold Mine in 1987 created a thriving mining town.

Present day
Boddington is located 11 kilometres (7 mi) off Albany Highway and 123 kilometres (76 mi) southeast of Perth in the Darling Scarp. Boddington each year on the first weekend of November hosts a 3-day rodeo.

A Royal Historical Society plaque near the town marks the grave of a local Aboriginal named Quency Dilyan who helped explorers Alexander Forrest and H. S. Ranford during their expeditions in the area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.971933" lon="116.21205">
  <ele>225.0</ele>
  <time>2020-10-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKWv5-02</name>
  <desc>LMNNS2NewNorcia</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/newnorcia</url>
  <urlname>[L] Monkeying about in New Norcia : S2 New Norcia Hostel by atCatpacity</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000074" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Monkeying about in New Norcia : S2 New Norcia Hostel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>atCatpacity</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">atCatpacity</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Monkeying about in New Norcia&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;New Norcia Hostel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/newnorcia"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/newnorcia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Walk to the top of the steps. What word is on the floor? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/04d1ea4b-e69b-49fc-9238-dd39163dcc44" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The hostel was originally built to accommodate parents visiting their children at St Gertrude's and St Ildephonsus' colleges. In 1955 the building was renovated and reopened as a hotel. It operated until January 2020, when the Board of the Benedictine Community decided, with regret, to permanently close the hotel. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2fa8714d-7bbf-457d-a958-153ce7f66b93" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;New Norcia is Australia's only monastic town. Founded in 1847 by Spanish Benedictine Monks, the catholic mission quickly grew into a small self-sufficient town. The town’s focus has changed over the years. From missionary work, to monastic and pastoral duties, to education, and now tourism.

A gem in the bush and a place of deep serenity, New Norcia is a place of beautiful historic buildings and spectacular landscapes. 

We would like to take you on a quick tour of the township. There’s much more to see than the five locations we have chosen. The route will take you past some other places where you might like to stop and explore. 

The waypoints will need to be collected in sequential order. This is so that we can deliver coordinates for our “Bonus Box” (GC91G24) when you have visited the final waypoint.

So, take your time and enjoy the peace and beauty of this truly unique place.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e91455a9-75ef-47e2-a0b5-d33b42d0f95f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Our main source of historical information has been 'New Norcia's Buildings &amp; Their Stories' by Larrie Strautmanis.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.971883" lon="116.213833">
