 <wpt lat="-32.333633" lon="117.878">
  <ele>304.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgmKQ-02</name>
  <desc>WheatbeltMuseu2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275</url>
  <urlname>Wheatbelt Museums : S2 Wheatbelt Museums - Pioneer Museum by burns345</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000064" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wheatbelt Museums : S2 Wheatbelt Museums - Pioneer Museum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>burns345</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">burns345</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wheatbelt Museums&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wheatbelt Museums - Pioneer Museum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the gardens shown on a building just inside?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/864b074f-30eb-4185-b3d3-5566cc73a1af" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Pioneer Museum in Corrigin

Housing the history of our days gone by, a worthwhile visit whilst in Corrigin. View the collection of tools and restored farm machinery, including tractors in working order, see the blacksmiths shop, the one room school, the shearing shed, old district photographs, clothing and other pioneer memorabilia. The Pioneer Memorial Wall at the entrance to the Museum commemorates early settlers. Please call in for a visit, you will be surprised!  

This collection of tools and restored farm machinery includes tractors in working order. See the blacksmith’s shop, the old one room school, the shearing shed and a collection of district photographs and other memorabilia, that commemorates early settlers. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8d9e6215-e093-4b60-b38b-ca79e7ab1af1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the start of 5 wheatbelt museums around Kulin and surrounding Shires

You can do them in any order and whenever you are in the area. However you do not have to go into the museums to answer any of the questions. Check on the internet as to opening times as they may vary month to month. 

Volunteers play an important roll in keeping the museums open for the public to enjoy so respect what they are doing.

The museums are:-
Alberts Facey's homestead – Wickepin
Pioneer Museum – Corrigin
Butlers Museum – Kulin
Australiam Inland Mission Hospital Museum – Lake Grace
Bluebird Discovery Centre – Dumbleyung

Now start on your journey into some of Western Australias history in this area.

CONGRATULATIONS ebenie for being the first to complete this Adventure Lab.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ed58c38-6eb3-44c5-967a-a344b79e71d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations you have completed the Pioneer Museum part of Adventure Lab Wheatbelt Museums.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.6445" lon="115.344833">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB8aBw-01</name>
  <desc>ByTheBayS1TheJe</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay</url>
  <urlname>By The Bay : S1 The Jetty by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-471" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>By The Bay : S1 The Jetty</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;By The Bay&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Jetty&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the statue?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14efcb8c-f1c1-4f87-beab-946fbd904155" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The original section of the Busselton Jetty was constructed in 1865, it is culturally significant as an early West Australian timber structure and a valuable relic of Busselton's early days, with the connection to the bustling timber industry.
It is the longest Wooden Jetty constructed in the Southern Hemisphere.
Cylcone Alby wreaked a trail of devastation in April 1978, completely destroying the Promenade.  
After a subsequent fire at the end of the jetty, damage closed the far end.
A Jetty Preservation Committee was set up and restoration projects have been carried out to what it is today. 
The Underwater Observatory Showcases the stunning underwater environment, the corals, the fish and as an added bonus, you maybe privileged to see a Mermaid swimming by.
There are more development plans in action and should be finished by the end of 2020.
A stroll out on the Jetty to see the locals and tourists fishing and jetty jumping, enjoying the experience is wonderful. 

There is a plaque at GZ.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/902ea194-af46-4e38-93cb-054668520576" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A Meander from the Iconic Busselton Jetty, along the scenic Foreshore, down through the Cultural Centre of town towards some Local Historical Buildings.

Geographe Bay was named in May 1801 by the French Explorer Nicolas Baudin, he named the Bay after his ship, the 'Geographe'.
Busselton was settled in the early 1830's by the Bussell family, George Layman and the Chapman Brothers.

This Adventure Lab can easily be completed within a day, however I would recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Busselton's Cultural and Early Historical Locations. 

There are plenty of stories to read along the way, depicting the Early Farming Settlements, the Pioneers and their Livelihoods from the Pioneering Days.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d0da3e5-e791-47b0-9614-762064e0cf31" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
