   <groundspeak:owner id="">Falcon351</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Lets not Forget&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Oldest stone church&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Notforget"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Notforget&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What rank was John Molloy in Her Majestys Army&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0bdfb826-9c69-49a0-921c-2ac7e83e9e46" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;St Marys is the oldest stone church in the state. It was built by the early settlers in the 1840s the surrounding area has some of the oldest headstones in Busselton and represents some of the oldest family names of Busselton. It still a place of worship for the Busselton community to this day.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9465ec12-45bd-49d8-b0a1-78eab4aa0fcb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My adventure is unique because it gives you time to reflect and give thanks for what we have today because of the sacrifice of our ancestors. This adventure will have you wandering thru cemetaries,a memorial and a cemotaph. Please be respectful of your surrounding.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congrates hope you enjoyed some history of our pioneers.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.652683" lon="115.345667">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2020-02-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB8aBw-04</name>
  <desc>ByTheBayS4StMar</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay</url>
  <urlname>By The Bay : S4 St Mary's by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-474" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>By The Bay : S4 St Mary's</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;By The Bay&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;St Mary's&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ByTheBay&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the second name of John and Charlotte Bussell's third daughter?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8ed71e41-873c-47a3-ae95-0f273ecc8452" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Heritage Precinct of St Mary's Anglican Church 
which is the Oldest Stone Church in Western Australia.
This Church was built in 1844-1845 guided by John Molloy and John Bussell, work began on the church whose design was based on the Winchester College Chapel, in England.  
It was named after St Mary's in Portsea where John's father had been curate.  
Local settlers quarried limestone for the walls and hand cut jarrah for the rafters, arches, pews, and timber shingles.
On 4th March, 1844, the Foundation Stone was laid by Bussell's mother, Frances. 
The original building was consecrated in 1848, the Rectory was built in 1897.
A bell tower and bell were installed in 1902.
Between 1958 and 1970s the plain glass windows were replaced with the beautiful stained glass window you see today.
The closed graveyard, also heritage listed, contains the graves of many of the original settles of the district.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/902ea194-af46-4e38-93cb-054668520576" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A Meander from the Iconic Busselton Jetty, along the scenic Foreshore, down through the Cultural Centre of town towards some Local Historical Buildings.

Geographe Bay was named in May 1801 by the French Explorer Nicolas Baudin, he named the Bay after his ship, the 'Geographe'.
Busselton was settled in the early 1830's by the Bussell family, George Layman and the Chapman Brothers.

This Adventure Lab can easily be completed within a day, however I would recommend you take your time and explore the area and take in some of Busselton's Cultural and Early Historical Locations. 

There are plenty of stories to read along the way, depicting the Early Farming Settlements, the Pioneers and their Livelihoods from the Pioneering Days.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b0b0ef2-038f-4720-8a95-9682e370e5d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.537417" lon="115.018767">
  <ele>111.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF71d-02</name>
  <desc>CoastalLightin2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA</url>
  <urlname>Coastal Lighting WA  : S2 Cape Naturaliste Lighthouse by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000006" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coastal Lighting WA  : S2 Cape Naturaliste Lighthouse</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coastal Lighting WA &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cape Naturaliste Lighthouse&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is written on the first two lines of the plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/381496f6-2835-4a71-9a92-995579d47c8e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Cape Naturaliste tower is positioned on a 100m high bluff overlooking Geographe Bay, it was constructed in 1903 from locally quarried limestone. The steps leading up the tower were made from wooden teak blocks dowelled together and placed grain-end up for long wearing. These step are still in good order and in use today.

The three keepers’ quarters, of stone construction, were built in 1904. The light was exhibited for the first time in 1904. The apparatus was originally powered by an incandescent vapourised kerosene lamp. The power was increased to 1,213,000 candles in 1924. The light was converted to automatic operation in 1978 and the keepers were reduced to one.

At least 12 ships have come to grief in the strong currents and dangerous reefs which lie off Cape Naturaliste’s sharp point.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9a1f8750-e3dc-47b8-b391-e919b7d37e5d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has the longest coastline of any state or territory in Australia, at 12,889 km (20,781 km including islands). It is a significant portion of the coastline of Australia, which is 35,877 km (59,736 km including islands). 

The earliest full charting of the coastline occurred during exploration in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.

The coastline has some features or organisms that are found on the entire length, while some others are specific to particular coastal regions. 

Western Australia has some of the most remote and harsh coastline in the world.  The coast sweeps in a great semicircle, facing south on the Southern Ocean, west and northwest on the Indian Ocean, and north on the Timor Sea. It was one of the last states to be lit, with most lights dating from the turn of the 20th Century. 

This lab cache highlights some of the lighthouses along Western Australia’s coastline, the history and the importance of navigating our dangerous coastline.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8064a2ef-64da-4239-9aee-1c8387817d2d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage, we hope you enjoy Coastal Lighting WA.

You will need to keep note of this...... YOUR&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
