   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bridgetown Heritage Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bridgetown Train Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91d23bcf-074c-446a-9f3c-7db52232c85a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91d23bcf-074c-446a-9f3c-7db52232c85a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who officially opened the Bridgetown Railway Station? (Surname only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/43dbbe97-4b24-4ab0-88f5-0d11d1594910" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bridgetown Train Station was opened on 7th October 1898. The station closed on 2nd June 1988 while the train service continued until 12th March 2005. The opening of this train station allowed for town growth and increased the local economy by transporting local produce namely timber and fruit to Perth and the Goldfields.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b75b5e86-e468-46d3-adfd-677451772790" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Bridgetown, I C Nothing’s second home located on the Blackwood River.
The town was surveyed in April 1868 by Thomas Carey who proposed the name Bridgetown for two reasons.
1. As it is at a bridge
2. Bridgetown was the first ship that transported wool from this district.

Bridgetown is a timber and agricultural  town producing sheep, cattle, dairy products, fruit , nuts and timber.

Bridgetown has many heritage buildings from when timber was in high demand during the building boom of 1890’s. This lab will take you on a walk to explore some of these heritage buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
