 <wpt lat="-33.314733" lon="117.7398">
  <ele>277.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgmKQ-05</name>
  <desc>WheatbeltMuseu5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275</url>
  <urlname>Wheatbelt Museums : S5 Wheatbelt Museums - Bluebird Discovery Centre by burns345</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000070" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wheatbelt Museums : S5 Wheatbelt Museums - Bluebird Discovery Centre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>burns345</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">burns345</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wheatbelt Museums&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wheatbelt Museums - Bluebird Discovery Centre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/98bb97f2-6ba9-4cd3-b363-4a194e301275&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;If you check on the bluebird replica you will see which number after the K?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/aba1c003-1ad3-49e7-b60c-ed3c8c79d070" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located at the Dumbleyung CRC opposite the bluebird replica of the vessel Donald Campbell made world record history at Lake Dumbleyung in 1964 by breaking both land and water speed records in 1 year.

The bluebird replica can be seen 24/7.

The Dumbleyung CRC  is open Monday 9.30am to 4.30pm and Tuesday to Friday 9am to 4.30pm.
Contains all the information about Donald Campbell and events leading up to his record breaking events.

There is also a web site that is worth reading.
www.dumbleyungbluebird.com.au

A cache has been placed out near Lake Dumbleyung - Pussy Cat Hill. - GC97R1Y.
Go out and find the cache and also drive up Pussy Cat Hill - more information and a view to remember.
Sorry no dogs allowed and no caravans as you can't turn around anywhere on the track.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8d9e6215-e093-4b60-b38b-ca79e7ab1af1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the start of 5 wheatbelt museums around Kulin and surrounding Shires

You can do them in any order and whenever you are in the area. However you do not have to go into the museums to answer any of the questions. Check on the internet as to opening times as they may vary month to month. 

Volunteers play an important roll in keeping the museums open for the public to enjoy so respect what they are doing.

The museums are:-
Alberts Facey's homestead – Wickepin
Pioneer Museum – Corrigin
Butlers Museum – Kulin
Australiam Inland Mission Hospital Museum – Lake Grace
Bluebird Discovery Centre – Dumbleyung

Now start on your journey into some of Western Australias history in this area.

CONGRATULATIONS ebenie for being the first to complete this Adventure Lab.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations you have completed the  Bluebird Discovery Centre part of Adventure Lab Wheatbelt Museums&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.486483" lon="118.251283">
  <ele>313.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgKg8-04</name>
  <desc>MerredinS4Merre</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d96b4b9c-0cc2-4033-8a39-641e7d60871b</url>
  <urlname>Merredin : S4 Merredin Wheat Silo Art Trail by geotrakka</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-9" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Merredin : S4 Merredin Wheat Silo Art Trail</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>geotrakka</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">geotrakka</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Merredin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Merredin Wheat Silo Art Trail&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d96b4b9c-0cc2-4033-8a39-641e7d60871b"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d96b4b9c-0cc2-4033-8a39-641e7d60871b&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many days painting to complete this silo art piece? (use digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/10368bce-138e-4f23-8433-8a2c0fb9c471" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Merredin CBH Group silos are the largest grain storage bins in the southern hemisphere being 610 meters long and 59 meters high.
In August 2017, four of the eight silos cells were transformed by artist Kyle Hughes-Odgers, with artwork inspired by the local landscape and community.
The Merredin Silo Art was the 3rd Silo to join WAs Public Silo Art.
The artwork was rather controversial in the eyes of some the locals. The geometric style this artist is known for were not well received by all. What do you think? 

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3abdc620-6d7d-4535-914b-0d3179db3fb4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Merredin 'The Heart of The Wheatbelt'. Place of my birth and childhood memories. I hope you enjoy a small insight to the place I call home.

 The town’s name is derived from the Aboriginal word “merrit-in” the place of the merrit tree, a eucalypt used to make spears.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8828f587-a70b-42e8-a4d2-4aa2f6bc0b71" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.483133" lon="118.27925">
