 <wpt lat="-34.959533" lon="117.354067">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2020-11-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMCVE-02</name>
  <desc>DTreesS2Millars</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054</url>
  <urlname>Denmark - It's all about the trees! : S2 Millars Sawmill by richlink</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919038" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Denmark - It's all about the trees! : S2 Millars Sawmill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Denmark - It's all about the trees!&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Millars Sawmill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What circa is the photo of the axeman?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d7267ef8-504c-44e6-96ad-c046126732ad" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This was the original main site of Millars Sawmill.
In 1895 Edwin and Charles Millar secured leases for 20,000 acres of old growth karri forest and built a number of mills on the banks of the Denmark River to process the trees which were felled inland. 
Millars exported the timber to Britain, China, India, Africa and South America from the port of Albany.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1690ca58-73d6-4ff8-8e9b-64d4a0a08411" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dr Thomas Braidwood Wilson ventured to Denmark in 1829 and named the area after his colleague - Alexander Denmark.
In 1895 the first settlers arrived, including the Millar Brothers who had obtained leases on the timber in the area. Denmark was created to house the families of workers at the sawmills. Timber was used initially to build the workers cottages, several shops and the town bridge.
There was high demand for timber from Europe, UK and WA gold rush towns. Denmark at that time had a population of over 1000. However by 1903, all easily accessed millable trees had been cut down, leaving workers short of a livelihood. The Millar Brothers wanted to dismantle the town and move onto 'greener pastures' but the WA Government purchased the town 'lock, stock and barrel' from them and made it possible for workers to return and settle in Denmark.
Today Denmark is a beautiful, peaceful community whose economy is sustained by a combination of tourism, farming, fishing, vineyards and the arts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Clue: IS&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
