   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Denmark - It's all about the trees!&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tall trees - recovery!&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the first traffic bridge built near Peace Street?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a8d18f1c-180a-4cc7-ab82-85dfd246a8b6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Today between Denmark and Walpole there are tall stands of karri, jarrah and tingle plus over 700 native species of trees, making the region one of the top ten areas of terrestrial biodiversity on the planet.

The location where you are now standing is called the Traffic Bridge which allows traffic to pass through town.

Our community is a a colourful mix of different characters, individualists, followers of natural care, spiritualists, naturalists, artisans, artists and retired people of all ages from all parts of the world. 

We hope you enjoy your jaunt around Denmark!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1690ca58-73d6-4ff8-8e9b-64d4a0a08411" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dr Thomas Braidwood Wilson ventured to Denmark in 1829 and named the area after his colleague - Alexander Denmark.
In 1895 the first settlers arrived, including the Millar Brothers who had obtained leases on the timber in the area. Denmark was created to house the families of workers at the sawmills. Timber was used initially to build the workers cottages, several shops and the town bridge.
There was high demand for timber from Europe, UK and WA gold rush towns. Denmark at that time had a population of over 1000. However by 1903, all easily accessed millable trees had been cut down, leaving workers short of a livelihood. The Millar Brothers wanted to dismantle the town and move onto 'greener pastures' but the WA Government purchased the town 'lock, stock and barrel' from them and made it possible for workers to return and settle in Denmark.
Today Denmark is a beautiful, peaceful community whose economy is sustained by a combination of tourism, farming, fishing, vineyards and the arts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Code: R&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
