   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Denmark - It's all about the trees!&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Felling the trees&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d602db28-5115-4419-92b4-9c4eca947054&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Observing the wheel, what is the name of the manufacturer? (first 2 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/55d8c435-d33d-44f8-acb9-ae1a61142aa3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The karri trees were felled by hand, usually two workers using axes and a saw to cut the giants down.
At this location you will find a 'jinker' which was used to move the cut trunks to the mill.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1690ca58-73d6-4ff8-8e9b-64d4a0a08411" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dr Thomas Braidwood Wilson ventured to Denmark in 1829 and named the area after his colleague - Alexander Denmark.
In 1895 the first settlers arrived, including the Millar Brothers who had obtained leases on the timber in the area. Denmark was created to house the families of workers at the sawmills. Timber was used initially to build the workers cottages, several shops and the town bridge.
There was high demand for timber from Europe, UK and WA gold rush towns. Denmark at that time had a population of over 1000. However by 1903, all easily accessed millable trees had been cut down, leaving workers short of a livelihood. The Millar Brothers wanted to dismantle the town and move onto 'greener pastures' but the WA Government purchased the town 'lock, stock and barrel' from them and made it possible for workers to return and settle in Denmark.
Today Denmark is a beautiful, peaceful community whose economy is sustained by a combination of tourism, farming, fishing, vineyards and the arts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Code: CO&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
