 <wpt lat="-35.02955" lon="117.915683">
  <ele>118.0</ele>
  <time>2020-05-21T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvKeK-04</name>
  <desc>LANZACS4Princes</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/AAH</url>
  <urlname>[L] Albany ANZAC History : S4 Princess Royal Fortress by allinaday</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001519" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Albany ANZAC History : S4 Princess Royal Fortress</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>allinaday</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">allinaday</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Albany ANZAC History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Princess Royal Fortress&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/AAH"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/AAH&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the plaque, locate A26.  What is the name of that ship?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/989cf5d0-a894-4ee8-8350-7dd791eaf6ce" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;During the 19th century the loss of the strategic port of Albany was seen as a potential threat to the security of Australia.  All the Australian States contributed to pay for the construction of a fort and the British Government supplied the guns.  The fort was opened in 1893 and was the first federal defense of Australia.  The site occupies an area of approximately 4 hectares.  Two gun batteries were dug into the hillside of Mount Adelaide. 
After many years of abandonment, the fortress was extensively restored commencing in 1987.  Eventually, the site became a museum.  Within the grounds there are many restored buildings, military equipment, batteries, armories and barracks.
There is so much to explore in this area, so take your time, and then make your way up to the Convoy Lookout.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/380447a7-65e2-438a-a9e2-a791f67c8161" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On the 1st of November 1914, Albany was the final departure point for the first and second convoys of ANZAC troops on their way to the battlefields of the First World War.  For many, Albany was the last image Australian soldiers had of their homeland.
Most thought they were on a great adventure.  Instead, they were fighting a conflict that many would not return from.
This Adventure will take you to a number of locations around our beautiful city which pay tribute to those who fought for our freedom. 'As the sun rises and goes down, we will remember them'.  Take your time to enjoy and explore the various locations you will visit.  This can take anything from a few hours to a day.

At each location, there will be a clue once you answer the question. The 5 letters (one in each answer will reveal a key word that you will require for a bonus mystery cache.

Please Note:  No full stops are required when you enter your answers.


&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;A&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-35.030033" lon="117.915217">
