 <wpt lat="-32.352333" lon="123.6173">
  <ele>181.0</ele>
  <time>2020-09-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBY64B-02</name>
  <desc>NullarborLinks2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Nullarbor</url>
  <urlname>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way : S2 Balladonia Links by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918620" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way : S2 Balladonia Links</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Balladonia Links&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Nullarbor"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Nullarbor&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In the picture on the sign, what animals were puling the wagons?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f9632e28-0d93-4946-9c1f-7e0731b0a15c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Balladonia Station, encompassing the original hotel site became pastoral land in 1879 when 2 pioneers, Steve Ponton and John Sharp settled in the vicinity with a spirit to match their tough remote selection. 
In the same year, their first wool clip reached the coast, 130kms south.  
Wagons pulled by teams of horses made the arduous journey to Israelite Bay, where the bales were loaded on to ships for export.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2a334634-b43a-447d-8767-e1cd05cd766f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Nullarbor Plain
As you travel across it’s windblown, barren surface you might feel more like you have left earth and are traversing the surface of another planet. 
Crossing this huge plain is a quintessential Australian road trip experience and the incredible vast landscapes will make you feel in awe of the wonderful Country we are traveling across.
The name Nullarbor comes from the Latin word “nullus” meaning “nothing” and the word “arbor” meaning “tree”.
Wombats, emus and kangaroos call the Nullarbor home.
Can you imagine driving for 146.6km in a completely straight line, without a single bend or turn in the road? The Eyre Highway is where you will find this curve-free road, stretching between Caiguna and Balladonia.
As you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you could play 18 holes of golf along the world’s longest golf course – the Nullarbor Links.
Now as you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you can add Australia's longest Adventure Lab the 'Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way', to your journey.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3f400c5d-d0b9-4180-96c8-fab5b2da6137" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations - you have wandered through the Pioneering Days of Balladonia Station.
Balladonia Telegraph Station&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-25.481917" lon="112.971917">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF71d-05</name>
  <desc>CoastalLightin5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA</url>
  <urlname>Coastal Lighting WA  : S5 Cape Inscription Lighthouse by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000012" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coastal Lighting WA  : S5 Cape Inscription Lighthouse</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state></groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coastal Lighting WA &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cape Inscription Lighthouse&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is written on the plaque above the doors? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a28c54fb-c3fb-4145-b692-f52bcc0ef40b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Enjoy stunning views of the beach and rocky headland at the most northerly point of Dirk Hartog Island National Park, which is also arguably the most historically significant site in Australia.

This was the site of the first recorded landing by Europeans on Australian soil, when Dutchman Dirk Hartog came ashore in 1616 on the northern end of the island which now bears his name. He left a record of his landing inscribed on a pewter plate nailed to a post in a cleft of rock on the high cliff. Today, more modern posts mark this spot.

The Cape Inscription lighthouse - together with the keepers' quarters - was built between 1908 and 1910. It still operates, but has been automated since 1917. In 2012, the Shire of Shark Bay renovated the lighthouse keepers' quarters, which had fallen into disrepair over the years since lighthouse automation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9a1f8750-e3dc-47b8-b391-e919b7d37e5d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has the longest coastline of any state or territory in Australia, at 12,889 km (20,781 km including islands). It is a significant portion of the coastline of Australia, which is 35,877 km (59,736 km including islands). 

The earliest full charting of the coastline occurred during exploration in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.

The coastline has some features or organisms that are found on the entire length, while some others are specific to particular coastal regions. 

Western Australia has some of the most remote and harsh coastline in the world.  The coast sweeps in a great semicircle, facing south on the Southern Ocean, west and northwest on the Indian Ocean, and north on the Timor Sea. It was one of the last states to be lit, with most lights dating from the turn of the 20th Century. 

This lab cache highlights some of the lighthouses along Western Australia’s coastline, the history and the importance of navigating our dangerous coastline.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d867f012-7d0a-4585-b2b6-3bd757b7e021" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage, we hope you enjoy Coastal Lighting WA.

You will need to keep note of this...... BRIGHT&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-21.808333" lon="114.1103">
  <ele>60.0</ele>
  <time>2020-12-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBF71d-01</name>
  <desc>CoastalLightin1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA</url>
  <urlname>Coastal Lighting WA  : S1 Vlaming Head Lighthouse by parkacruz</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000002" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coastal Lighting WA  : S1 Vlaming Head Lighthouse</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>parkacruz</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">parkacruz</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coastal Lighting WA &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Vlaming Head Lighthouse&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/CLWA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;You will find a plaque with the following script "oh yes, plenty of fish - got plenty of fish...." Who is this text's author?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/032a680e-f86c-4c9f-8089-09ad6c0a097a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first recorded sighting of this part of Australia was in 1618, Willem de Vlamingh then charted the headland in 1696. 

The wreck of the SS Mildura off the North West Cape in 1907 led to Vlamingh Head, a site proposed by a number of ships' masters to the Board of Enquiry.

Vlamingh Head Lighthouse is situated 17 km to the north of the settlement of Exmouth, overlooking Lighthouse Bay. The lighthouse is notable as being one of the few locations in Australia where both the sunrise and the sunset can be observed.

The lighthouse started operations on 10 November 1912 with a light that could be seen for up to 22 nautical miles (41 kilometres). The light took two lighthouse keepers to keep its light burning. The light used kerosene and flashed twice every 7.5 seconds. The lighthouse operated for sixty years up to 1969.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9a1f8750-e3dc-47b8-b391-e919b7d37e5d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has the longest coastline of any state or territory in Australia, at 12,889 km (20,781 km including islands). It is a significant portion of the coastline of Australia, which is 35,877 km (59,736 km including islands). 

The earliest full charting of the coastline occurred during exploration in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.

The coastline has some features or organisms that are found on the entire length, while some others are specific to particular coastal regions. 

Western Australia has some of the most remote and harsh coastline in the world.  The coast sweeps in a great semicircle, facing south on the Southern Ocean, west and northwest on the Indian Ocean, and north on the Timor Sea. It was one of the last states to be lit, with most lights dating from the turn of the 20th Century. 

This lab cache highlights some of the lighthouses along Western Australia’s coastline, the history and the importance of navigating our dangerous coastline.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f989d899-f943-4624-a9bf-2809a4bf2697" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage, we hope you enjoy Coastal Lighting WA.

You will need to keep note of this...... LET

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
