   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>South Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Penong Links&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Nullarbor"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Nullarbor&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many vanes are there on the largest Comet Windmill? number, not word.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c1320d86-beb4-462b-b6ea-ce67dd07f101" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Penong is a wonderful little town on the eastern end of the Nullarbor.
The township of Penong is a small wheatbelt settlement on the western edge of South Australia's wheatbelt which is characterised by dozens of windmills which pump the local water supply from a large underground basin, the Anjutabie water Basin. 
Around the back of the town on the northern side, the historical society has been busy restoring an amazing number of old windmills, some with their history is documented on the information boards. 
The Large Comet Windmill has a colourful history and is now well preserved.
The town and the surrounding district is so dry that the underground water is supplemented by rain water and water which is carted by truck from a reticulated water supply connected to the Tod River pipeline some 15 kilometres east of Penong.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2a334634-b43a-447d-8767-e1cd05cd766f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Nullarbor Plain
As you travel across it’s windblown, barren surface you might feel more like you have left earth and are traversing the surface of another planet. 
Crossing this huge plain is a quintessential Australian road trip experience and the incredible vast landscapes will make you feel in awe of the wonderful Country we are traveling across.
The name Nullarbor comes from the Latin word “nullus” meaning “nothing” and the word “arbor” meaning “tree”.
Wombats, emus and kangaroos call the Nullarbor home.
Can you imagine driving for 146.6km in a completely straight line, without a single bend or turn in the road? The Eyre Highway is where you will find this curve-free road, stretching between Caiguna and Balladonia.
As you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you could play 18 holes of golf along the world’s longest golf course – the Nullarbor Links.
Now as you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you can add Australia's longest Adventure Lab the 'Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way', to your journey.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/50183116-e022-471c-8d09-5faccd858172" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations  - enjoy the journey across the Nullarbor&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
