 <wpt lat="-17.957633" lon="122.223267">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2020-07-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLPny-01</name>
  <desc>BroomeItsAPear1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome</url>
  <urlname>Broome - It's a pearler! : S1 Japanese Cemetery by richlink</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000014" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome - It's a pearler! : S1 Japanese Cemetery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome - It's a pearler!&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Japanese Cemetery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the three digit number on the lower right hand corner of the obelisk?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14222dd4-8535-4b80-a16a-04c6d5bb942c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the latter part of the 19th and the early years of the 20th centuries, Japanese divers were recruited as pearl divers. They wore cumbersome full dive suits made of canvas with copper helmets and lead weighted boots which enabled them to  dive in very deep waters.
At the cemetery, obelisks mark the graves of over 900 Japanese pearlers. Most died due to decompression sickness or other illnesses related to diving.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d2ad1127-ffe2-4a9c-a2f7-84653d30f0bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first recorded European to land on Broome shores was explorer William Dampier aboard his ship the HMS Roebuck.
The town was founded in 1883 as a port for the pearling industry and was named after the then Governor of WA – Sir Frederick Broome. 
Although today, Broome is famous for it's beautiful pearls, originally Broome made it's name from the shells - not the pearls. From the 1880's, mother of pearl (the shell) was used to make buttons, cutlery handles, inlays and other ornate objects but with the introduction of plastic in the 1900's, mother of pearl became too expensive to use. The local economy collapsed and it would take until the mid 1950's before the pearl industry was revitalised and since has developed into the booming industry it has become.
This historic pearling town in now one of the most favourite holiday destinations in the country.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations you have correctly answered this question!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-17.955167" lon="122.244617">
