  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome</url>
  <urlname>Broome - It's a pearler! : S2 Pearl Lugger by richlink</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000016" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome - It's a pearler! : S2 Pearl Lugger</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome - It's a pearler!&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pearl Lugger&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Broome&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who is the licensee of this property? (4 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/40ccf5a4-cbbb-4ff0-9478-c78e80d4fc9d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the early 1950's the first cultured pearl farm began in Kuri Bay, north of Broome. These days there are many pearl farms in the unpolluted waters near Broome. A number of manufacturers and retail outlets in town specialise in transforming these unique gems into beautiful jewellery items.
In the courtyard at this location, you will see an original pearl lugger and a display of old time diving gear.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d2ad1127-ffe2-4a9c-a2f7-84653d30f0bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first recorded European to land on Broome shores was explorer William Dampier aboard his ship the HMS Roebuck.
The town was founded in 1883 as a port for the pearling industry and was named after the then Governor of WA – Sir Frederick Broome. 
Although today, Broome is famous for it's beautiful pearls, originally Broome made it's name from the shells - not the pearls. From the 1880's, mother of pearl (the shell) was used to make buttons, cutlery handles, inlays and other ornate objects but with the introduction of plastic in the 1900's, mother of pearl became too expensive to use. The local economy collapsed and it would take until the mid 1950's before the pearl industry was revitalised and since has developed into the booming industry it has become.
This historic pearling town in now one of the most favourite holiday destinations in the country.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations you have answered this question correctly!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
