  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000104" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S2 The Centre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Centre &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Jurabi Turtle Centre opened? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/de2812b8-fcf5-4eeb-98f4-e94650d8648b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jurabi is right beside a popular rookery for green, loggerhead and hawksbill turtles, and you can minimise your impact on the nesting turtles by following the Turtle Watchers' Code of Conduct presented in the displays and signage at Jurabi, or by joining a guided turtle viewing experience.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c30090c-7e2c-4bd2-af02-392ff7bb25e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre is open year round. Take a walk through the interpretive displays and discover the marine turtles found along the Ningaloo Coast. Learn about their lifecycle, the threats they face and how to view mating and nesting turtles and turtle hatchlings in the natural environment.

From December through to early March join a guided Turtle Eco-Education tour and scout for female turtles making their way on to the beach to lay their eggs. Towards the end of the season you may even be lucky enough to encounter turtle hatchlings as they emerge from their nest, making their way to the ocean to start their long journey to adulthood.

Guided Turtle Eco-Education tours increase the chance of finding and observing nesting female turtles in the natural environment. Viewing nesting female turtles and hatchlings with a guide minimises your chance of disturbing them which will increase the chance of female turtles successfully laying eggs.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-21.806567" lon="114.101483">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2021-09-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBckN8-04</name>
  <desc>JurabiTurtleCe4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae</url>
  <urlname>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S4 Boy or Girl? by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000108" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S4 Boy or Girl?</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Boy or Girl?&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the temperature pictured + or - for a girl or a boy? (numerical numbers only) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/211768e9-6983-481b-bef1-dc6ab4c92052" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;When the sand is warm only girls are born. If the sand is cool it is boys as a rule. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c30090c-7e2c-4bd2-af02-392ff7bb25e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre is open year round. Take a walk through the interpretive displays and discover the marine turtles found along the Ningaloo Coast. Learn about their lifecycle, the threats they face and how to view mating and nesting turtles and turtle hatchlings in the natural environment.

From December through to early March join a guided Turtle Eco-Education tour and scout for female turtles making their way on to the beach to lay their eggs. Towards the end of the season you may even be lucky enough to encounter turtle hatchlings as they emerge from their nest, making their way to the ocean to start their long journey to adulthood.

Guided Turtle Eco-Education tours increase the chance of finding and observing nesting female turtles in the natural environment. Viewing nesting female turtles and hatchlings with a guide minimises your chance of disturbing them which will increase the chance of female turtles successfully laying eggs.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-21.806517" lon="114.101433">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2021-09-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBckN8-03</name>
  <desc>JurabiTurtleCe3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae</url>
  <urlname>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S3 Do Turtles use a GPS too? by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000106" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S3 Do Turtles use a GPS too?</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Do Turtles use a GPS too? &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;More than how many years later do turtles start heading for home? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e45d9691-4041-4ffd-a590-a4621542dcf1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Turtles navigate the ocean using the earths magnetic field. Locating specific coastlines based on a magnetic signature stored in the brain. Hatchings "turn on" their GPS, uploading the magnetic signatures as they make their way to the ocean. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c30090c-7e2c-4bd2-af02-392ff7bb25e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre is open year round. Take a walk through the interpretive displays and discover the marine turtles found along the Ningaloo Coast. Learn about their lifecycle, the threats they face and how to view mating and nesting turtles and turtle hatchlings in the natural environment.

From December through to early March join a guided Turtle Eco-Education tour and scout for female turtles making their way on to the beach to lay their eggs. Towards the end of the season you may even be lucky enough to encounter turtle hatchlings as they emerge from their nest, making their way to the ocean to start their long journey to adulthood.

Guided Turtle Eco-Education tours increase the chance of finding and observing nesting female turtles in the natural environment. Viewing nesting female turtles and hatchlings with a guide minimises your chance of disturbing them which will increase the chance of female turtles successfully laying eggs.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-21.806217" lon="114.10145">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2021-09-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBckN8-05</name>
  <desc>JurabiTurtleCe5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae</url>
  <urlname>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S5 Let Then Flow by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000110" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Jurabi Turtle Centre  : S5 Let Then Flow</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Let Then Flow &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4a0e544a-60a4-4211-979e-ceb9e67fe3ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are mating marine turtles extremely vulnerable to?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/acc0e19c-7a4e-4b18-b3dc-0d3635698b77" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A guided turtle viewing experience at Jurabi Turtle Centre is the best way to see some of the world's most threatened species of marine turtle, as they make their nests on the white-sand beaches of World Heritage listed Ningaloo Reef. 

Remember when searching not as part of a group to pay attention to the signs, hatchlings usually appear between dusk and dawn. Remember No glow and Stay low and Move Slow! &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c30090c-7e2c-4bd2-af02-392ff7bb25e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jurabi Turtle Centre is open year round. Take a walk through the interpretive displays and discover the marine turtles found along the Ningaloo Coast. Learn about their lifecycle, the threats they face and how to view mating and nesting turtles and turtle hatchlings in the natural environment.

From December through to early March join a guided Turtle Eco-Education tour and scout for female turtles making their way on to the beach to lay their eggs. Towards the end of the season you may even be lucky enough to encounter turtle hatchlings as they emerge from their nest, making their way to the ocean to start their long journey to adulthood.

Guided Turtle Eco-Education tours increase the chance of finding and observing nesting female turtles in the natural environment. Viewing nesting female turtles and hatchlings with a guide minimises your chance of disturbing them which will increase the chance of female turtles successfully laying eggs.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
