  <urlname>European settlement in Margaret River : S3 The Old Settlement by papillon367</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000461" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>European settlement in Margaret River : S3 The Old Settlement</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>papillon367</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">papillon367</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;European settlement in Margaret River&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Old Settlement&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aa57e288-e927-4359-addb-d35433e3c51e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aa57e288-e927-4359-addb-d35433e3c51e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Bramley School building transported to its current Old Settlement location?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea677b63-3d59-4b5c-8749-7bca9bd77573" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Established in 1977 by members of the local Rotary Club, the site is a Group Settlement Scheme-style village that also depicts the beginnings of the Augusta-Margaret River dairy industry in the 1920's. There are a number of relocated buildings on site.
The Bramley School contains historical school paraphernalia, many local photographs and changing displays. The Group House operates as a small museum and holds a collection of artefacts, dating from between 1921-1935.  The grounds are surrounded by farming implements, tools, renovated spring carts, an old dairy, a "dunny" and milk shed. These buildings offer an insight into the history of early life in the district during the early 1920s and the families that once lived and worked the land.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e6fef59-0fbc-4337-9fb8-20eeee93f9ed" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Wadandi Noongar people are the traditional landowners of this part of the South West region of WA. A group of white settlers established the town of Augusta near Cape Leeuwin in the 1830s and the Margaret River was named in the late 1830s after early settler John Bussell’s step-second-cousin Margaret Whicher.
It wasn’t until 1912 that Margaret River started to be built, with three houses constructed in what is today regarded as the town site. 
This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the European settlement sites in and around Margaret River. Most of the information for this AL was sourced from the website of the Margaret River &amp; Districts Historical Society. 
There is more to discover in the area so please visit their website for further information: https://mrdhs.com.au/locations-margaret-river
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ab53409a-276a-48f1-bdd2-e2b2359168bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, now onto the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
