 <wpt lat="-33.95645" lon="115.053367">
  <ele>85.0</ele>
  <time>2021-10-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRDhz-02</name>
  <desc>MargS2Basildene</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aa57e288-e927-4359-addb-d35433e3c51e</url>
  <urlname>European settlement in Margaret River : S2 Basildene Farmhouse by papillon367</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000459" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>European settlement in Margaret River : S2 Basildene Farmhouse</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>papillon367</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">papillon367</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;European settlement in Margaret River&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Basildene Farmhouse&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aa57e288-e927-4359-addb-d35433e3c51e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aa57e288-e927-4359-addb-d35433e3c51e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What name is located above the year 1912 on the plaque? (8 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ebef98f7-5809-4239-b112-fa56e0200856" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Basildene Farmhouse was built in 1912 by William Henry Percival Willmott.
Willmott became the first Head Lightkeeper of Cape Leeuwin Lighthouse when it was completed in 1895 and spent fourteen years in that capacity, residing with his wife and growing family in one of the lighthouse cottages, before taking up land at Margaret River.
Willmott engaged Yallingup builder Robert Donald to construct his house, a two-storey edifice with four rooms upstairs and four downstairs. It was built of local stone and locally milled jarrah and karri timber with a jarrah staircase leading to the upper rooms. The large central hallway occupying a two-storey space in the centre of the building was a distinctive feature of the design.
The house was classified by the National Trust in 1978. In 1997 the place was added into the HCWA Register of Heritage Places. Today Basildene Farmhouse operates as a hotel under the name Basildene Manor.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e6fef59-0fbc-4337-9fb8-20eeee93f9ed" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Wadandi Noongar people are the traditional landowners of this part of the South West region of WA. A group of white settlers established the town of Augusta near Cape Leeuwin in the 1830s and the Margaret River was named in the late 1830s after early settler John Bussell’s step-second-cousin Margaret Whicher.
It wasn’t until 1912 that Margaret River started to be built, with three houses constructed in what is today regarded as the town site. 
This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the European settlement sites in and around Margaret River. Most of the information for this AL was sourced from the website of the Margaret River &amp; Districts Historical Society. 
There is more to discover in the area so please visit their website for further information: https://mrdhs.com.au/locations-margaret-river
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/544ccd23-57c5-44f3-af72-53027dc7ae0c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, now onto the next location!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
