 <wpt lat="-33.948733" lon="115.074067">
  <ele>77.0</ele>
  <time>2021-10-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvB7g-02</name>
  <desc>MargaretRiverA2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9aeac2a1-be3c-4a3f-aa80-fdf1eb57926d</url>
  <urlname>Margaret River Adventure : S2 Prehistoric Marsupials - Zygomaturus trilobus by PilotJen</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-532" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Margaret River Adventure : S2 Prehistoric Marsupials - Zygomaturus trilobus</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PilotJen</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PilotJen</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Margaret River Adventure&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Prehistoric Marsupials - Zygomaturus trilobus&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9aeac2a1-be3c-4a3f-aa80-fdf1eb57926d"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9aeac2a1-be3c-4a3f-aa80-fdf1eb57926d&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the FIRST NAME of the sculptor at the bottom of the plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bc311734-aa2e-4307-88f4-71f00d9e044f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;An Australian megafauna species, Zygomaturus trilobus, a relative of today's wombats, was one of the largest marsupials to have ever existed and would have weighed up to 500 kg. 

It was once thought that Zygomaturus became extinct about 45,000 years ago until a surprisingly late date between 33.3 ±3.7 Kya and 36.7 ±5.1 Kya is known from the Willandra Lakes Region in New South Wales, the latest known date for any Australian Megafauna. This is well after aboriginal arrival in Australia around 50 Kya

Since its bones have only been found in former swamp-forests, it may have been semi-aquatic like a hippopotamus, scooping vegetation into its mouth with shovel-like incisors.

Zygomaturus trilobus belonged to the now extinct family Diprotodontidae, which in turn falls within the Infraorder Vombatomorphia, a group also including wombats and the marsupial lions (Thylacoleonidae). &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cf9f9c76-bdd7-4cbc-9cf6-1dafe9694e41" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This lab will take you to well known areas of Margaret River and areas that tourists rarely notice or visit. You will see kangaroos and a small waterfall at the end of this lab if you start at the visitor centre. 

This lab is not a long one, but it will be fun. Enjoy! &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
