  <name>LBNma9-05</name>
  <desc>LHiddenTreasur5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Goldenpipeline</url>
  <urlname>[L] Hidden Treasures of Perth : S5 Forrest House by dezzabills</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000446" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Hidden Treasures of Perth : S5 Forrest House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>dezzabills</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">dezzabills</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Hidden Treasures of Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Forrest House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Goldenpipeline"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/Goldenpipeline&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did Lord Alexander Forrest start residing in the residence?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a6f47e4d-b07b-4d67-800a-86b409384162" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Alexander Forrest was a great explorer, surveyor, politician and investor. Born 22 September 1849, the fourth son of William Forrest and Margaret Guthrie, he worked at his father’s mill until 1868 when he advertised himself as a surveyor. In 1870, he served as second-in-command for his first transcontinental expedition along the edge of the great Australian Bight. On 1 January 1871 he was appointed as surveyor-in-charge of the Albany district and later became an independent licensed surveyor working by contract.  Alexander married Amy Eliza Barrett Lennard on 15 January 1880. Their first born was daughter Sylvia, then four sons, Kimberley, Anthony, John and William. Alexander held the office of Mayor of Perth for six years (1892-95) and was unopposed for a second term 1897-1900. Alexander Forrest died 20 June 1901  aged 51 years old. He had accomplished a great deal including the provision of parks, reserves, tram ways, roads and footpaths and as such had modernized the City of Perth during his lifetime.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e7d9b778-67c6-4a21-a9fb-ddb8b59f71d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The city of Perth has its origins in 1829 when the Swan River Colony was established by the British Government. The first colonial Governor, Capt James Stirling, named the new settlement after the Scottish birthplace and parliamentary constituency of the then British Secretary of State for the Colonies, Sir George Murray. When surveying the area previously, Captain Stirling was said to have been stunned by the beauty of the Swan River and the fertile land around it.

Since water transport was vital to communications in the new colony before roads were built, the meanderings of the Swan River determined the site of the first towns. Governor Stirling decided that the site for the colony’s capital would be sited on the river 18km from the sea port of Fremantle. On 12 August 1829 Mrs Helen Dance, wife of the commander of HMS Sulphur, drove an axe into a tree to mark the colony’s foundation.

The city site was mid-way between the sea and the farming areas of the Upper Swan.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8be2f6e3-8b05-4b70-9e71-22760872e880" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations, hope you enjoyed this hidden treasure.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
