   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Hamilton Hill Memorial Hall&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Hall Facade&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/97b81221-05fe-445d-8561-f7a22dbe3ee3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/97b81221-05fe-445d-8561-f7a22dbe3ee3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year is mentioned on the top of the building &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/adae64da-b800-4a0f-b5b9-469808f3bfde" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This location will take you to the facade of the hall. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86ff71f5-e2da-4200-9929-9cdcd34ffb91" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hamilton Hill Memorial Hall was completed and officially opened on 19th July 1925. 

At the end of the 1914-1918 war memorial centographs were being built throughout the state, but the newly formed Hamilton Hill Association looked to built a hall in memory of those who lost their lives. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/98a840fd-f4cc-4e9d-a56d-d50097f41084" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.405583" lon="116.026933">
  <ele>130.0</ele>
  <time>2021-11-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBCfmg-05</name>
  <desc>DiscoverPember5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9</url>
  <urlname>Discover Pemberton : S5 Big Brook Dam by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-530" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Discover Pemberton : S5 Big Brook Dam</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Discover Pemberton&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Big Brook Dam&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the 3 initials on the sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1ceb5707-7e80-48b0-8c72-74324701b92b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Big Brook Dam was built in 1986 to provide water for Pemberton and for the aquaculture in the area. It is a great place for recreation - you can you for a swim, paddle a canoe, walk around the dam on the footpath.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4595632c-5318-4829-ae8b-01b558120734" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pemberton is known for it's tall karri trees. There are a couple of fire lookout trees in the region you can still climb - Gloucester Tree and Dave Evans Bicentennial Tree. The area has some beautiful landscapes and popular for photographers. Having been a regular visitor to the town for 40 years - I encourage you to discover it for yourself.

Please note: There is paid entry to Gloucester Tree.
$15 National Parks Day Pass - per car
$8 Concession (or Motorbike) National Park Day Pass&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;I hope there is no emergency.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.443433" lon="116.030233">
  <ele>108.0</ele>
  <time>2021-11-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBCfmg-04</name>
  <desc>DiscoverPember4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9</url>
  <urlname>Discover Pemberton : S4 Pemberton Swimming Pool by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-529" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Discover Pemberton : S4 Pemberton Swimming Pool</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Discover Pemberton&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pemberton Swimming Pool&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is at this location?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b7b50c9-b367-43d1-a7d1-5960931d00fb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A historic swimming pool constructed in the late 1920's for the families of the timber workers, the Pemberton Pool has been an iconic destination for generations of locals and visitors. Nestled on the Lefroy Brook next to the Pemberton Mountain Bike Park.
The pool once had rail up the steep slope which you rode down in the pool. It also had a 5m platform (and a diving board) to jump off, and a small diving board on the eastern side of the pool.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4595632c-5318-4829-ae8b-01b558120734" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pemberton is known for it's tall karri trees. There are a couple of fire lookout trees in the region you can still climb - Gloucester Tree and Dave Evans Bicentennial Tree. The area has some beautiful landscapes and popular for photographers. Having been a regular visitor to the town for 40 years - I encourage you to discover it for yourself.

Please note: There is paid entry to Gloucester Tree.
$15 National Parks Day Pass - per car
$8 Concession (or Motorbike) National Park Day Pass&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;I hope you made a wish.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.444233" lon="116.035833">
