   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Discover Pemberton&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gloucester Tree&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91b9475f-dbd0-439d-b7bd-883bc8bfa5a9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look for a small sign. To learn about the natural wonders around you - which App can you use?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4d26853b-fa80-4b92-a2aa-ae331c870172" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Gloucester Tree is a giant karri tree in the Gloucester National Park of Western Australia. At 53 metres in height, it is one the world's tallest fire-lookout tree, and visitors can climb up to a platform in its upper branches for views of the surrounding karri forest. It is owned by the Shire of Manjimup. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4595632c-5318-4829-ae8b-01b558120734" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pemberton is known for it's tall karri trees. There are a couple of fire lookout trees in the region you can still climb - Gloucester Tree and Dave Evans Bicentennial Tree. The area has some beautiful landscapes and popular for photographers. Having been a regular visitor to the town for 40 years - I encourage you to discover it for yourself.

Please note: There is paid entry to Gloucester Tree.
$15 National Parks Day Pass - per car
$8 Concession (or Motorbike) National Park Day Pass&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.74905" lon="121.47055">
  <ele>380.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjckR-01</name>
  <desc>HeartOfTheGold1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73</url>
  <urlname>Heart of the Goldfields : S1 Paddy Hannan by sandyofthesea</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001454" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Heart of the Goldfields : S1 Paddy Hannan</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sandyofthesea</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sandyofthesea</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Heart of the Goldfields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Paddy Hannan&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is Paddy's first name? (see plaque on statue)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7c63b810-e14e-4afc-9857-682044261ba2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Paddy Hannan. Imagine what Paddy would think now with a bustling street, a suburb in north-western Kalgoorlie and many mines named after him.  Kalgoorlie is wealthy town with a lot of the early money going into the many buildings that adorn this street. The Town Hall, the amazing pubs and the gold domed Court House to name a few.  This journey is all about Paddy and his mates.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6d8d2e3-72f2-4ffe-a9b7-4ba99bca1adb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1893, when Paddy Hannan and his mates happened upon alluvial gold near Mt Charlotte, no one could have predicted that, just a decade later, the twin boom towns of Kalgoorlie and Boulder would be home to the fabled ‘Golden Mile’, reputedly the richest square mile on earth and one of the greatest concentrations of underground mines ever established.

This adventure lab will take you on Paddy's journey around Kalgoorlie, the heart of the Goldfields.

There is a bonus box at the end of the journey too. Enjoy!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1d5e0e81-50b2-4e67-a05a-350083f05271" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done.  Now walk up the street and go to the York Hotel. Walk the stairs and run your hand along the bannister.  It's a beauty.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
