   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Heart of the Goldfields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;St Barbara&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a red plaque at the foot of the statue of St Barbara.  When was St Barbara's Square officially opened?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2320bccf-9e38-4615-b830-945a2e2daa5c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Kalgoorlie, as patron saint of miners St Barbara is venerated in the annual St. Barbara's Day parade typically in early December each year.  It is an amazing spectacle with floats, underground loaders and most years, 793 Caterpillar dump truck.  Paddy would roll in his grave seeing such a big machine compared the pick, shovel and wheel barrow he had in 1893.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6d8d2e3-72f2-4ffe-a9b7-4ba99bca1adb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1893, when Paddy Hannan and his mates happened upon alluvial gold near Mt Charlotte, no one could have predicted that, just a decade later, the twin boom towns of Kalgoorlie and Boulder would be home to the fabled ‘Golden Mile’, reputedly the richest square mile on earth and one of the greatest concentrations of underground mines ever established.

This adventure lab will take you on Paddy's journey around Kalgoorlie, the heart of the Goldfields.

There is a bonus box at the end of the journey too. Enjoy!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/468ce82f-c201-41bf-9823-af5d2da50489" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done on completing another stage.  Be on the lookout for the one keyword for Bonus Box cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.744967" lon="121.47845">
  <ele>392.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjckR-03</name>
  <desc>HeartOfTheGold3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73</url>
  <urlname>Heart of the Goldfields : S3 Paddy Hannan's Tree by sandyofthesea</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001470" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Heart of the Goldfields : S3 Paddy Hannan's Tree</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sandyofthesea</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sandyofthesea</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Heart of the Goldfields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Paddy Hannan's Tree&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;It is an amazing gold field! How much gold was produced here in the first 90 years (in ounces)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/88b4f9cc-4b47-49d0-bd44-dcacf4534df2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hannan, Flanagan and Shea found colours of gold on the 10th June, 1893.  The first tree to mark this spot was planted on the 3rd August 1897. A plaque and new tree was planted here in June 1993 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the find.  There are a number of other plaques here which make interesting reading.

The photo is how I remember the site, but it recently has been cleaned up with new turf and a paint job.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6d8d2e3-72f2-4ffe-a9b7-4ba99bca1adb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1893, when Paddy Hannan and his mates happened upon alluvial gold near Mt Charlotte, no one could have predicted that, just a decade later, the twin boom towns of Kalgoorlie and Boulder would be home to the fabled ‘Golden Mile’, reputedly the richest square mile on earth and one of the greatest concentrations of underground mines ever established.

This adventure lab will take you on Paddy's journey around Kalgoorlie, the heart of the Goldfields.

There is a bonus box at the end of the journey too. Enjoy!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4942d1d5-2b2b-4db9-a147-f993e8ad7bbc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Awesome work! If you get a chance visit Hammond Park and see the peacocks.  The clue for the bonus box isn't on this stage.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.7458" lon="121.479167">
  <ele>392.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjckR-04</name>
  <desc>HeartOfTheGold4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73</url>
  <urlname>Heart of the Goldfields : S4 Reward Claim by sandyofthesea</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001472" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Heart of the Goldfields : S4 Reward Claim</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sandyofthesea</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sandyofthesea</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Heart of the Goldfields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Reward Claim&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This obelisk was moved from the most westerly corner of the Reward Claim due to ....  (two words).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8eae7148-a1b7-4fc9-a6be-e189d99f4617" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the site of the Reward Claim, the original lease pegged by Thomas Flanagan, Patrick Hannan and Daniel Shea.  The claim was pegged at this site and registered in Coolgardie on the 17th June, 1893.  Many prospectors and mining companies have held this ground continuously and now forms part of the Golden Mile.  Who would have thought this would form the richest square mile in the world!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6d8d2e3-72f2-4ffe-a9b7-4ba99bca1adb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1893, when Paddy Hannan and his mates happened upon alluvial gold near Mt Charlotte, no one could have predicted that, just a decade later, the twin boom towns of Kalgoorlie and Boulder would be home to the fabled ‘Golden Mile’, reputedly the richest square mile on earth and one of the greatest concentrations of underground mines ever established.

This adventure lab will take you on Paddy's journey around Kalgoorlie, the heart of the Goldfields.

There is a bonus box at the end of the journey too. Enjoy!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/67f43e3c-f262-4e13-92b7-8936931ff5c4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;The Mt Charlotte shaft and headframe are situated on the original claim.  Another stage complete and the bonus box clue is NUGGET&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.78895" lon="121.503167">
  <ele>358.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjckR-05</name>
  <desc>HeartOfTheGold5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73</url>
  <urlname>Heart of the Goldfields : S5 Super Pit by sandyofthesea</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001474" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Heart of the Goldfields : S5 Super Pit</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sandyofthesea</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sandyofthesea</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Heart of the Goldfields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Super Pit&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0e87dcc6-71a3-496e-8640-f5fe8ecbcc73&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the DID YOU KNOW? fact finder on the sign. How many million ounces has been produced since the first lease was pegged?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8bb972d8-4b63-4a76-bc51-c4c0c9d6e451" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ten years after the discovery of gold, the Golden Mile had 49 operating mines, 100 headframes and 3,000 km of underground workings.  It wasn't until Alan Bond started buying individual leases in 1980's to create one company and one big pit. It wasn't until 1989, the area was combined into one set of leases and KCGM (Kalgoorlie Consolidated Gold Mines) was formed. Take time to read the signs especially the one on Golden Mile History.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6d8d2e3-72f2-4ffe-a9b7-4ba99bca1adb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1893, when Paddy Hannan and his mates happened upon alluvial gold near Mt Charlotte, no one could have predicted that, just a decade later, the twin boom towns of Kalgoorlie and Boulder would be home to the fabled ‘Golden Mile’, reputedly the richest square mile on earth and one of the greatest concentrations of underground mines ever established.

This adventure lab will take you on Paddy's journey around Kalgoorlie, the heart of the Goldfields.

There is a bonus box at the end of the journey too. Enjoy!!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7f49d65b-7ad9-4a36-a800-eac2a2e0f0e6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Awesome! No clue for the bonus box here. Keep looking.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.012683" lon="115.7883">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpMmQ-01</name>
  <desc>PointWalterRes1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680</url>
  <urlname>Point Walter Reserve  : S1 Entry Statement by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001476" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Point Walter Reserve  : S1 Entry Statement</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Point Walter Reserve &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Entry Statement &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Steamboats and Crabbing mosaic, what years it representing (numerical numbers only) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/af42a111-cbda-47a5-aec4-629d02bf7769" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Commissioned by the City Of Melville this artwork celebrates some of the important historical contemporary and topographical features of point walter known to the Aboriginal Beeliar tribe as "Dyundalup"

Special thanks to the Applecross Senior High School students who designed  and made the twelve mosaics. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c64c838-c2d3-4291-b34f-aa94c24fc812" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Reserve was named after the principal feature on the Swan River, Point Walter, which was originally named by Captain James Stirling in 1827 after a favourite uncle.

When the tide is out, you can walk from the foreshore out into the river along the Point Walter Sandbar. Many people take advantage of this natural phenomenon to snap an interesting photo!

Ample parking is available on Honour Avenue and at the end of Burke Drive.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.012483" lon="115.788083">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpMmQ-02</name>
  <desc>PointWalterRes2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680</url>
  <urlname>Point Walter Reserve  : S2 Shower time. by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001478" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Point Walter Reserve  : S2 Shower time.</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Point Walter Reserve &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Shower time. &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many outdoor showers are located here. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d45c4ae-9d37-44b4-9276-bfd70a634e4d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter's size, natural beauty and abundance of facilities makes it the perfect location for any occasion, from a casual family outing to a large wedding or a quick Adventure lab cache. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c64c838-c2d3-4291-b34f-aa94c24fc812" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Reserve was named after the principal feature on the Swan River, Point Walter, which was originally named by Captain James Stirling in 1827 after a favourite uncle.

When the tide is out, you can walk from the foreshore out into the river along the Point Walter Sandbar. Many people take advantage of this natural phenomenon to snap an interesting photo!

Ample parking is available on Honour Avenue and at the end of Burke Drive.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.010833" lon="115.787467">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpMmQ-03</name>
  <desc>PointWalterRes3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680</url>
  <urlname>Point Walter Reserve  : S3 The Jetty by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001480" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Point Walter Reserve  : S3 The Jetty</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Point Walter Reserve &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Jetty &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Whats the maximum time allowed for a vessel to be secured along side the jetty. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/746341ca-7a94-4226-9467-aff9c3001fc6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The jetty is a very popular fishing destination and can at times be very productive.
Species likely to be encountered here include, black bream, flathead, flounder, tailor, herring, pilchard, mulloway, silver Bream, yellowfin whiting, crabs, river prawns and king prawns.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c64c838-c2d3-4291-b34f-aa94c24fc812" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Reserve was named after the principal feature on the Swan River, Point Walter, which was originally named by Captain James Stirling in 1827 after a favourite uncle.

When the tide is out, you can walk from the foreshore out into the river along the Point Walter Sandbar. Many people take advantage of this natural phenomenon to snap an interesting photo!

Ample parking is available on Honour Avenue and at the end of Burke Drive.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0111" lon="115.786733">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpMmQ-04</name>
  <desc>PointWalterRes4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680</url>
  <urlname>Point Walter Reserve  : S4 History by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001482" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Point Walter Reserve  : S4 History</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Point Walter Reserve &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;History &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who was this place (Point Walter) named after? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/09f2d7fe-8047-4dd8-8877-8be21ec22c5a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1830, following the original swan river colony settlers, the second wave of colonists received land grants, only 2 chose to stay and develop property here. 
one grant was for 36 acres here and the plan was to build a tavern and establishing a ferry service. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c64c838-c2d3-4291-b34f-aa94c24fc812" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Reserve was named after the principal feature on the Swan River, Point Walter, which was originally named by Captain James Stirling in 1827 after a favourite uncle.

When the tide is out, you can walk from the foreshore out into the river along the Point Walter Sandbar. Many people take advantage of this natural phenomenon to snap an interesting photo!

Ample parking is available on Honour Avenue and at the end of Burke Drive.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.010267" lon="115.78485">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpMmQ-05</name>
  <desc>PointWalterRes5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680</url>
  <urlname>Point Walter Reserve  : S5 The Spit by cravo10</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001484" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Point Walter Reserve  : S5 The Spit</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>cravo10</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">cravo10</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Point Walter Reserve &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Spit &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2842d6fd-be70-40d3-8a4b-62e28ca0c680&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Depending on tides and time of year the Spit can extend how far from land? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5adece75-4b7d-47e9-bcf5-cf2da47e969c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Spit is one of the Swan Canning Riverpark’s most popular and scenic recreational locations.
The spit is also an important site for waterbirds using the Swan River. It is a feeding ground, a resting place and during spring and summer it may be a nesting site for the Black Swan, Pied Oystercatcher, Red-capped Plover and Fairy Tern.
These birds are easily disturbed and frightened off by people walking, fishing, kitesurfing and paddle boarding on and near the spit.

For this reason you don't need to walk to the end!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c64c838-c2d3-4291-b34f-aa94c24fc812" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Walter Reserve was named after the principal feature on the Swan River, Point Walter, which was originally named by Captain James Stirling in 1827 after a favourite uncle.

When the tide is out, you can walk from the foreshore out into the river along the Point Walter Sandbar. Many people take advantage of this natural phenomenon to snap an interesting photo!

Ample parking is available on Honour Avenue and at the end of Burke Drive.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.006567" lon="115.751717">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2021-12-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtL9j-05</name>
  <desc>SculptureByThe5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0cc4a3da-595a-41a3-be90-0042addb0066</url>
  <urlname>Sculpture by the Sea : S5 Pause by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99001494" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Sculpture by the Sea : S5 Pause</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Sculpture by the Sea&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pause&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0cc4a3da-595a-41a3-be90-0042addb0066"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0cc4a3da-595a-41a3-be90-0042addb0066&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the artist's full name?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0c1b40af-93ce-4f11-b170-c58aa07fc5be" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Statues of people looking endlessly out to sea are always evocative and this surfer is no exception.

Donated by the family of Tim Browne-Cooper, 22/4/69 - 22/5/92. Gone but not forgotten.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/abb4eb2a-8ff8-4016-883e-b279e2019ff2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sculpture by the Sea started in Bondi in 1997 and quickly grew to become one of Sydney's most popular annual events. The popularity of the Bondi event led to the creation of a Cottesloe event in 2005 - which is now enjoyed by around 250,000 visitors annually.

The event is free and each event showcases around 70 sculptures. The exhibition is featured from the sea wall all the way along the sand towards North Cottesloe and on the surrounding grassed areas creating a beautiful sculpture park.

The 18th Annual Cottesloe Exhibition is scheduled for 4-21 March 2022.

The public can commission and buy sculptures, and since the first Cottesloe exhibition the Town of Cottesloe has also regularly purchased sculptures for ongoing public display.

This Adventure Lab takes you to a few of the permanent sculptures along a 2km stretch of coast - perfect for a nice walk, cycle, drive, skateboard, scooter or hoverboard.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done.

The private residence on the corner of Salvado Street opposite also has 3 sculptures on display around their verge.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
