 <wpt lat="-28.7717" lon="114.609417">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-01-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBDXnS-04</name>
  <desc>GERALDTONFORES4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fc29b1a0-e5af-4d53-94aa-2463ff375afc</url>
  <urlname>GERALDTON FORESHORE WANDERING  : S4 Emu eggs by OOH YEH</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-583" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>GERALDTON FORESHORE WANDERING  : S4 Emu eggs</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>OOH YEH</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">OOH YEH</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;GERALDTON FORESHORE WANDERING &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Emu eggs &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fc29b1a0-e5af-4d53-94aa-2463ff375afc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fc29b1a0-e5af-4d53-94aa-2463ff375afc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the first word on this plaque ??&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/269a7c77-9df1-42d8-bd9f-f35e2c3e3db5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Geraldton Foreshore when local Yamaji artists’ two-dimensional artwork drawn from the night skies is transformed into three dimensional sculpted emu eggs. The inspiration draws from indigenous astronomy. Indigenous Australians saw different shapes and designs in the night sky, such as the Emu and the Seven Sisters and the Hunter, passing knowledge through the centuries.
Yamaji Arts has partnered with the City to produce and install the sculpture. The sculpture evolved from the interpretation of two paintings - The Emu in the Sky by Margaret Whitehurst (Wajarri) and The Seven Sisters and the Hunter by Barbara Merritt (Badimaya), selected for their aesthetic, cultural and historical merits.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b1ae1a8-e434-452a-a142-a01c2a4c5571" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Geraldton foreshore.
Here you can find many activities for the young and old . I recommend you start at one end and work your way to the other . There is plenty of parking along the way . 
In 2007, the Geraldton foreshore area, previously an abandoned railway marshalling yard, was redeveloped and today hosts a playground with water activities, public green spaces, public beaches, picnic areas, basketball court and shaded play areas. The project was completed in 2008.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9666ecca-a0b8-45e6-b4b0-8428fc1da08a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-28.77155" lon="114.608183">
