   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walking Busselton Jetty&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stop for a drink?&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9ff0b9a3-5e97-4816-aaed-59191a057716"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9ff0b9a3-5e97-4816-aaed-59191a057716&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who was the author of the verse written Titled “the extravagance of Water?"&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/017e6d6b-6ad8-435c-8fa8-07256e0374d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here the jetty widens, there is a water fountain if you are a little thirsty.  

Find the item pictured and answer the question......&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/414b92a0-1c04-466f-97d0-642f5c5840e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Busselton Jetty is the longest timber piled jetty in the Southern Hemisphere at 1841m long.  Construction Commenced in 1864 and the first section opened in 1865,  The jetty has been extended numerous times until the 1960s when it reached its current length.

The jetty has survived Cyclone Alby in 1978, insect borers, several fires, extreme weather and the threat of demolition, to now become a major tourist attraction.

The jetty features a railway line along its length and an underwater observatory at the end.

Walking the Jetty has become a "right of passage" for locals, and tourists alike.  There are many fishing spots and places to jump into the water on a hot day. 

There is a small cost to enter the jetty.  Pop into the visitors centre first to book a ticket on the train, or for the observatory.  

You will need to walk at least one way to complete this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c41cd1bb-5218-45e4-8cc4-8831178aeec9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Have you seen the train yet??&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
