   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Walking Busselton Jetty&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Saving Busselton Jetty&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9ff0b9a3-5e97-4816-aaed-59191a057716"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9ff0b9a3-5e97-4816-aaed-59191a057716&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the plaque "Saving Busselton Jetty" which Australian Prime Minister is named"? (First Name and Surname) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/615dc035-700c-4452-b270-9639a115f841" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Busselton Jetty has survived numerous "attacks".  From cyclone to fire to threats of demolition, the jetty remains standing today through the determined efforts of the local community.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/414b92a0-1c04-466f-97d0-642f5c5840e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Busselton Jetty is the longest timber piled jetty in the Southern Hemisphere at 1841m long.  Construction Commenced in 1864 and the first section opened in 1865,  The jetty has been extended numerous times until the 1960s when it reached its current length.

The jetty has survived Cyclone Alby in 1978, insect borers, several fires, extreme weather and the threat of demolition, to now become a major tourist attraction.

The jetty features a railway line along its length and an underwater observatory at the end.

Walking the Jetty has become a "right of passage" for locals, and tourists alike.  There are many fishing spots and places to jump into the water on a hot day. 

There is a small cost to enter the jetty.  Pop into the visitors centre first to book a ticket on the train, or for the observatory.  

You will need to walk at least one way to complete this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/143b95b1-c25d-47e4-871f-4a2247150628" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
