  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002523" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Australind Bunbury Tourist Drive 260 : S09 Fenian John Boyle O'Reilly</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">West Oz Geocaching</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Australind Bunbury Tourist Drive 260&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fenian John Boyle O'Reilly&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0b629cdd-df49-49a1-b896-4727eef727e4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0b629cdd-df49-49a1-b896-4727eef727e4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the date the monument was unveiled. (Exactly as written on the plaque minus comma and full stop)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e041a61c-fb5c-4d29-908a-d8ddcc6a9c1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John Boyle O'Reilly (1844 – 1890) was an Irish poet, journalist, author and activist. As a youth in Ireland, he was a member of the Irish Republican Brotherhood, or Fenians, for which he was transported to Western Australia. After escaping to the United States, he became a prominent spokesperson for the Irish community and culture through his editorship of the Boston newspaper The Pilot, his prolific writing and his lecture tours.

In 1864 he joined the Irish Republican Brotherhood under an assumed name and was part of the group for two years until he and many others were arrested by authorities in early 1866. After a trial the same year he was sentenced to death which was  later commuted to 20 years' penal servitude. In 1867 O'Reilly was transported to Western Australia and moved to the town of Bunbury where he escaped two years later.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9efba563-718e-4429-b417-f748ede82572" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Australind Bunbury Tourist Drive, designated as Tourist Drive 260, is a 14-kilometre-long  historic route from Bunbury to Leschenault via Australind.

There are numerous places of interest along the way and this Adventure Lab will take you to some of them.

Note the numeric answers to some of the questions as they will allow you to find a bonus geocache after completing all stages of this Adventure Lab.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df7d0ae6-b372-407e-98c6-d521e40db018" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congratulations on completing this stage of the adventure lab.

For the bonus cache record the day and year (QR STUV)&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
