 <wpt lat="-26.409967" lon="114.462783">
  <ele>67.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQbz2-01</name>
  <desc>SharkBayWorldH1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1</url>
  <urlname>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S1 Shark Bay Entry Statement by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919045" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S1 Shark Bay Entry Statement</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Shark Bay World Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Shark Bay Entry Statement&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was the road graded and surfaced with clay?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8ea97682-5d5f-4713-b6fd-32d144103f09" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you are a little bit like me and just love the road north, then this spot known as Overlander will be very familiar with you, however my favourite spot is just 50 odd Kms south at Billabong, great free camp and a great meal and always has had the cheapest fuel.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c34043f4-fb9a-4369-83b5-e2b0549c3d21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This self-drive trail takes in all the significant features that give Shark Bay its World Heritage Listing. Unique features include the largest sea grass beds in the world - providing food for the dugong (sea cow), the tiny cockleshells of Shell Beach, the stromatolites of Hamelin Bay and the dolphins at Monkey Mia.

Interpretive signage exists at the sites along the drive that relate to the World Heritage listing.

Visit the Shark Bay World Heritage Discovery Centre for information on the trail, whales, manta rays, turtles, sharks, fish species, walk trails and the World Heritage features throughout the Shark Bay area.

Specific interests of the drive include the stories of the early Dutch, French and English explorers of the area, the endangered species reintroduced with Project Eden, the views of Eagle Bluff and Shell Beach and the beautiful Ocean Park, Nanga Bay, Hamelin Bay and Peron Peninsula.

I had Telstra coverage at Stromatolites and Shell Beach but can’t vouch for other Telcos.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5ddaad8d-2840-436e-a105-f106badfe489" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Yay, correct, but sadly there's no bonus box for this Adventure!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-26.401" lon="114.160467">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQbz2-02</name>
  <desc>SharkBayWorldH2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1</url>
  <urlname>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S2 Stromatolites by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919047" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S2 Stromatolites</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Shark Bay World Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stromatolites&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Local Aboriginal people refer to these stromatolites as"???", meaning our ancestors.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/31373234-ac90-45a9-a95a-bf7c0dbffcb6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sadly the boardwalk was severely damaged by cyclone Seroja in April 2021 so it is not possible to view them up close, however you can get all the information off these panels about these amazing things.  There are also good examples further down south near Cervantes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c34043f4-fb9a-4369-83b5-e2b0549c3d21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This self-drive trail takes in all the significant features that give Shark Bay its World Heritage Listing. Unique features include the largest sea grass beds in the world - providing food for the dugong (sea cow), the tiny cockleshells of Shell Beach, the stromatolites of Hamelin Bay and the dolphins at Monkey Mia.

Interpretive signage exists at the sites along the drive that relate to the World Heritage listing.

Visit the Shark Bay World Heritage Discovery Centre for information on the trail, whales, manta rays, turtles, sharks, fish species, walk trails and the World Heritage features throughout the Shark Bay area.

Specific interests of the drive include the stories of the early Dutch, French and English explorers of the area, the endangered species reintroduced with Project Eden, the views of Eagle Bluff and Shell Beach and the beautiful Ocean Park, Nanga Bay, Hamelin Bay and Peron Peninsula.

I had Telstra coverage at Stromatolites and Shell Beach but can’t vouch for other Telcos.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congrats but again, sadly no bonus box!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-26.216333" lon="113.777517">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQbz2-03</name>
  <desc>SharkBayWorldH3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1</url>
  <urlname>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S3 Shell Beach by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919049" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Shark Bay World Heritage Trail : S3 Shell Beach</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Shark Bay World Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Shell Beach&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ea51de7f-d5c0-4ec5-8498-3aa41e5bacd1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the deeper layers of limestone called?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dbb6a4a3-7770-49d3-a15c-0d90aad9616c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This beach is made entirely of tiny white coquina bivalve shells (Fragum erugatum) .This strange beach stretches for about 60 Kms and the shells range from around 7 meters to 10 meters deep in places.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c34043f4-fb9a-4369-83b5-e2b0549c3d21" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This self-drive trail takes in all the significant features that give Shark Bay its World Heritage Listing. Unique features include the largest sea grass beds in the world - providing food for the dugong (sea cow), the tiny cockleshells of Shell Beach, the stromatolites of Hamelin Bay and the dolphins at Monkey Mia.

Interpretive signage exists at the sites along the drive that relate to the World Heritage listing.

Visit the Shark Bay World Heritage Discovery Centre for information on the trail, whales, manta rays, turtles, sharks, fish species, walk trails and the World Heritage features throughout the Shark Bay area.

Specific interests of the drive include the stories of the early Dutch, French and English explorers of the area, the endangered species reintroduced with Project Eden, the views of Eagle Bluff and Shell Beach and the beautiful Ocean Park, Nanga Bay, Hamelin Bay and Peron Peninsula.

I had Telstra coverage at Stromatolites and Shell Beach but can’t vouch for other Telcos.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Congrats now you can move closer to the beach if you like, or complete the earth cache near by.  Hope you enjoyed "Rex"!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-25.930083" lon="113.5379">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-06T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBFbh6-04</name>
  <desc>DENHAMDISCOVER4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/24dcde1e-9304-47a6-83d2-f3ed31191e10</url>
  <urlname>DENHAM DISCOVERY TRAIL : S4 A NATURAL TREASURE by m4yb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002474" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>DENHAM DISCOVERY TRAIL : S4 A NATURAL TREASURE</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>m4yb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">m4yb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;DENHAM DISCOVERY TRAIL&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A NATURAL TREASURE&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/24dcde1e-9304-47a6-83d2-f3ed31191e10"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/24dcde1e-9304-47a6-83d2-f3ed31191e10&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name (3 words) of the small mammal pictured on the sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c9040bf-7d1d-4aaf-af64-d450278830e6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Shark Bay was added to UNESCO’s World Heritage list in 1991 because of its outstanding natural values . Its islands and shores are home to rich flora and fauna including rare and critically endangered mammals.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9c278d7b-88d6-493b-bb72-fcbd399c9e1e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a short, walkable Adventure through the town of Denham in Shark Bay. 
Shark Bay is traditional country of the Malgana, Nhamda and Yingkarta people who have inhabited the area as early as 30,000 years ago. The Malgana name for Shark Bay is Gutharraguda, which means “two bays” or “ “two waters “
Denham is situated 8.5 hours’ drive north of Perth in the UNESCO Shark Bay  World Heritage Area. Captain Henry Mangles Denham charted the area in 1858 and gave the town his name. It was once a pearling port but now gives visitors a wide range of experiences from boating, diving, and swimming, as well as natural wonders like the dolphins of Monkey Mia, the stromatolites of Hamelin Pool, Shell Beach, and further afield François Peron National Park and Dirk Hartog Island.
This Adventure Lab can be done on foot in a short time, all being accessed from  Denham’s main street, Knight Street.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Nearly there!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
