  <desc>SwingThroughSw2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12123491-dfc0-4b3d-ad8c-6285380e0ef5</url>
  <urlname>A Swing through Swan : S2 Edgecombe Brothers by SirFancy</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002492" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>A Swing through Swan : S2 Edgecombe Brothers</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SirFancy</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SirFancy</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;A Swing through Swan&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Edgecombe Brothers&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12123491-dfc0-4b3d-ad8c-6285380e0ef5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12123491-dfc0-4b3d-ad8c-6285380e0ef5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a infomation board near by. The West Swamp Tortious is the solesurviding member or the genus ______&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4d1a4e84-d292-4b4e-b3f7-8ec013a87707" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Founded by the Edgecombe brothers Don and Frank in 1925, Their vintage vines are family-owned to this very day.
Don and Frank immigrated to Western Australia from Worthing in the UK.
They purchased fifty-acres of Swan Valley property that would soon become celebrated as a historic wine region in WA.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/adc4fa20-01c9-48bb-856c-e9afac1e098f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab features a few favourite places to be with friends when i use to live near by.


The Cover image and starting location is of my Favorite ice cream place but is now sadly closed.
Oggies Ice Cream Cafe.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2b6a2aeb-1a78-47d4-bf28-b8d060a7b6fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.652667" lon="117.518367">
  <ele>356.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBNUdU-02</name>
  <desc>LLostKatanning2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c</url>
  <urlname>[L] Lost Katanning : S2 The Railway by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909767" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Lost Katanning : S2 The Railway</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Lost Katanning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Railway&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the date on the back of the cairn? (Numeric with hyphen and stops)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/06b0687b-0eeb-4ead-a260-1797c448cb59" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1880 the only railway operating in the State was, apart from short lengths privately owned in the timber concessions, the Geraldton-Northampton line. A railway to connect Albany with the terminal of the Eastern railway was mooted in 1880, but it was not until 1884 that a concrete proposal, put forward by Anthony Horden, of Sydney, to construct the railway on the land grant system, was accepted.
Mr. Horden was returning to this State to organise operations, but died on the voyage out. His control was transferred to the Western Australian Land Company, the work of construction being in the hands of Millar Brothers, of Melbourne, who commenced at both ends and proceeded so rapidly that the line was opened to traffic on June 1, 1889.
The actual junction of construction was at Katanning, which thereby received its first impetus. The township was declared shortly after the completion of the line, as were those of Mt. Barker, Cranbrook, Broomehill, Wagin, Narrogin and Pingelly.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/31f0a8f3-8c80-4869-ba26-d196fce31e3a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Katanning, situated 362km from Perth and 185km from Albany. Katanning is thought to come from an Aboriginal word ‘Kartannin’ meaning ‘big meeting place’ and it still holds true to this meaning in the 21st century, as the largest town in the Upper Great Southern servicing many surrounding towns. 
Midway between Eticup and Yowangup was a clear pool of sweet water, named by the local peoples  “Kartanup”, and from this pool, which is in the bed of the creek passing through the Katanning Park, now filled with sand and serving as a playground in the summer for the children of the present generation, did the town of Katanning receive its name.
Official recognition was given to the settlement to the east of the Perth-Albany road in 1861, when Sergeant McKenna, with his wife and family, was appointed the first trooper to the district. The police camp was set up at the waterhole now known as the Police Pools.
Katanning was declared a town on June 10, 1898.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/386dff9e-7548-4d12-9c37-d4c41dce1548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Great work, on to the next stop!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.681583" lon="117.566867">
  <ele>328.0</ele>
