 <wpt lat="-33.691383" lon="117.555683">
  <ele>317.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBNUdU-05</name>
  <desc>LLostKatanning5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c</url>
  <urlname>[L] Lost Katanning : S5 Electricity by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909773" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Lost Katanning : S5 Electricity</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Lost Katanning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Electricity&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What can be supplied with electricity at this point? (Two words, caps)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a77c366f-4d47-4ef2-b992-12c4799077d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Katanning was one of the first inland country towns to have electricity supplied to homes and businesses in Western Australia. It was certainly the first to have electric street lighting installed.
The Mill’s founder, F H Piesse, recognised early on the commercial advantage of using electricity to power the Mill and began to gradually convert more and more of the Mill’s operations over to electrical power. The Mill was lit by incandescent bulbs from 1891 and by 1904 it was able to generate more electricity than it could use.
Starting with his private residence, Piesse embarked on an ambitious project to power the entire town of Katanning. Piesse saw the sale of power to the townsfolk as a lucrative business and had the WA Parliament pass the Katanning Electric Light And Power (Private) Act, 1904, granting them the ability to do so.

In December of 1903, a bill was passed in State Parliament to allow F. &amp; C. Piesse &amp; Co to provide electricity for lighting to homes and businesses in Katanning until 1964.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/31f0a8f3-8c80-4869-ba26-d196fce31e3a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Katanning, situated 362km from Perth and 185km from Albany. Katanning is thought to come from an Aboriginal word ‘Kartannin’ meaning ‘big meeting place’ and it still holds true to this meaning in the 21st century, as the largest town in the Upper Great Southern servicing many surrounding towns. 
Midway between Eticup and Yowangup was a clear pool of sweet water, named by the local peoples  “Kartanup”, and from this pool, which is in the bed of the creek passing through the Katanning Park, now filled with sand and serving as a playground in the summer for the children of the present generation, did the town of Katanning receive its name.
Official recognition was given to the settlement to the east of the Perth-Albany road in 1861, when Sergeant McKenna, with his wife and family, was appointed the first trooper to the district. The police camp was set up at the waterhole now known as the Police Pools.
Katanning was declared a town on June 10, 1898.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d3110c69-8bcb-4a76-99cb-5a27b076ebde" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Thank you for completing the Lost Katanning Adventure Lab; if you are interested please refer to: https://lostkatanning.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.717417" lon="117.588583">
  <ele>296.0</ele>
  <time>2022-03-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBNUdU-01</name>
  <desc>LLostKatanning1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c</url>
  <urlname>[L] Lost Katanning : S1 Soldier Settlers by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909765" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Lost Katanning : S1 Soldier Settlers</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Lost Katanning&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Soldier Settlers&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/161e044d-28db-4eca-b062-3e56529ce60c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which years was Trooper Grover a Pensioner Guard?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c0ca581e-cd21-4d13-a40d-e462f362fd31" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Following the survey of the route in 1848 and with the arrival of convicts from England, the direct road from Perth to Albany was cleared, as were roads from Perth to Northam and York, and from York to Williams, thus giving access between the then only settled portions of the whole State.
To guard the convicts on roadwork, military depots were established at Kojonup, Arthur, Williams, Bannister and elsewhere, the soldiers being recruited from time-expired men of the British army, mostly from India. These pensioners were made grants of land to induce them to stay in the colony, and thus became pioneer settlers. In the Kojonup district today, such well known families as those of Norrish, Elverd. Riley, Bagg, Newstead, Forsythe, Tunney, Noonan, Jones and others were established, descendants of their soldier forbears.
Twonkwillingup was an important meeting place and water source for local Aboriginals (Noongars). It came to be known as Police Pools after the area was used as an outpost/campsite from 1865-70.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/31f0a8f3-8c80-4869-ba26-d196fce31e3a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Katanning, situated 362km from Perth and 185km from Albany. Katanning is thought to come from an Aboriginal word ‘Kartannin’ meaning ‘big meeting place’ and it still holds true to this meaning in the 21st century, as the largest town in the Upper Great Southern servicing many surrounding towns. 
Midway between Eticup and Yowangup was a clear pool of sweet water, named by the local peoples  “Kartanup”, and from this pool, which is in the bed of the creek passing through the Katanning Park, now filled with sand and serving as a playground in the summer for the children of the present generation, did the town of Katanning receive its name.
Official recognition was given to the settlement to the east of the Perth-Albany road in 1861, when Sergeant McKenna, with his wife and family, was appointed the first trooper to the district. The police camp was set up at the waterhole now known as the Police Pools.
Katanning was declared a town on June 10, 1898.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ab5f4aec-0c42-4717-9b68-06ad92132465" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well done, for more information refer to https://lostkatanning.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.745483" lon="115.655667">
