   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wandering around Wandering&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Old school Wandering&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/808ebf1f-61da-4499-9de2-3f1e116fa6cb"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/808ebf1f-61da-4499-9de2-3f1e116fa6cb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;what was Mrs Ken Cornish (nee Dowsett) relation to the Old School? (Two Words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ccfcd3fc-2c50-426f-afb3-1d64ec30d55e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Old Wandering School Site 1870 - 1924. Erected to Commemorate Former Teachers and Students. Unveiled by Mrs Ken Cornish (nee Dowsett), 8th April 1990. Erected by the Shire of Wandering.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6dc6050d-a8df-49ba-8a19-a2540647fc01" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The area's name appears to come from a local Aboriginal word, "wandooin", after the wandoo or white gum tree that is prevalent in the area, although some sources suggest it was named to recall the first sighting of wandering stock, and was originally applied to Wandering Brook, first recorded in 1866.

