   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wagin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Old Wagin Well&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e9339261-b5e0-4d77-8ae1-b118dcf1261a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e9339261-b5e0-4d77-8ae1-b118dcf1261a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many vertical posts around the well?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d7a6928-e3ad-4d0e-a7d4-544e21348236" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Build around 1886 by the Arthur River road board for the use of carters and sandalwood teams. This well was a water supply for early pioneer of Wagin.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a530fa34-ca3e-4af9-a540-53a6a466367e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The name of the town is derived from Wagin Lake, a usually dry salt lake south of the town. The lake's name is of Noongar origin, and was first recorded by a surveyor in 1869–72. It means "place of emus", or "site of the foot tracks from when the emu sat down".
The town itself came into existence after the construction of the Great Southern Railway which was completed in 1889 with the town originally called Wagin Lake.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Award Information&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e440d3c1-8d9f-4552-ac5b-fdc72e478759" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Award Message:&lt;br /&gt;Well Done! 

What is the Giant Ram's name (4 letters) for GC9VXJE Bonus

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
