  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909778" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Welcome to Perth : S2 RAAF Pearce - Bullsbrook</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Welcome to Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;RAAF Pearce - Bullsbrook&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/80c1d3e9-402f-4f2e-b66d-305b908dbdb8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/80c1d3e9-402f-4f2e-b66d-305b908dbdb8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;RAAF Pearce's mockup Boeing 747 used for counter-terrorism training is painted in what fictional airline livery? (two words)? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4b1001d4-4588-4316-8c2b-a7068ea829f0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;RAAF Base Pearce is the main Royal Australian Air Force (RAAF) military air base in Western Australia. The base is located in Bullsbrook, north of Perth. It is used for training by the RAAF and the Republic of Singapore Air Force. 

The base also serves as an anti-hijacking training aid for Special Air Service Regiment counter-terrorism squadron, also known as Tactical Assault Group (West). It is used to practise airliner entry and hostage rescue drills. The base is home to a mockup of a Boeing 747 used for this counter-terrorism training. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dc289c8f-ab76-4013-9afa-872188ca6ddc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Perth Airport is not the world's largest transfer hub but it (in normal times) does handle approx 1.3 million passengers per month! That's a lot of passengers twiddling their thumbs with not much to do. So here is a small AdLab to pass 5 minutes or more.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.937217" lon="115.97135">
  <ele>18.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBXuVM-03</name>
  <desc>LWelcomeToPert3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/80c1d3e9-402f-4f2e-b66d-305b908dbdb8</url>
  <urlname>[L] Welcome to Perth : S3 Fly to the king of selfies by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909780" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Welcome to Perth : S3 Fly to the king of selfies</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Welcome to Perth&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fly to the king of selfies&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/80c1d3e9-402f-4f2e-b66d-305b908dbdb8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/80c1d3e9-402f-4f2e-b66d-305b908dbdb8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What animal is famous for the Rottnest Selfie craze (also the 'Rat' that Vlamingh documented)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/38b4f9e0-919f-4efc-9d4a-1344145e4ea8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Rottnest Island (Nyungar: Wadjemup), often colloquially referred to as "Rotto", is a 19-square-kilometre island off the coast of Western Australia, located 18 kilometres west of Fremantle. The island was first documented by Willem de Vlamingh in 1696, who called it 't Eylandt 't Rottenest ("Rats' Nest Island") after the famous marsupial population. 

At one stage, the 32 km (20 mi) Perth to Rottnest flight was the world's shortest scheduled air route. The Jimmy Woods Air Terminal on the island is named after the pioneer aviator who flew scheduled flights to the island from 1948 to 1961.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dc289c8f-ab76-4013-9afa-872188ca6ddc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Perth Airport is not the world's largest transfer hub but it (in normal times) does handle approx 1.3 million passengers per month! That's a lot of passengers twiddling their thumbs with not much to do. So here is a small AdLab to pass 5 minutes or more.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
