   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Northbridge Step Back in Time&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Irish Pub- An Sibin&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f6d45c18-5aa4-4bd4-8d1e-55260ce64c18"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f6d45c18-5aa4-4bd4-8d1e-55260ce64c18&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What two colours are on the sculpture at the roundabout?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8210f467-59dd-439d-9282-cbfe100c68a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There have been many restaurants in the area over the years, however this building was built in the Art Deco style. 

Once La Tavernetta Winehouse and Restaurant, the building changed hands many times over the years. Currently, it is known as An Sibin with Air and Mint nightclubs upstairs. 

If you look carefully on the Lake Street side of the building (referencing the old photo that will pop up after your answer the question)- the top round windows at the back are a tell-tale sign of the original building, including the brick feature wall at the front. 

https://purl.slwa.wa.gov.au/slwa_b3800004_22

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d7d0fc34-4849-4953-b6c3-03e1c73d9263" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like my last AdLab - Colonial Northbridge, I love the history of the area including how the buildings all have a story. Once you answer the question an older photo will pop up.
It was hard to cull my list, but I hope you enjoy wandering around the area...

30 minutes should be enough time for this walk...&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
