 <wpt lat="-32.031" lon="115.827633">
  <ele>49.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMsxB-03</name>
  <desc>LSamuelsRamble3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e</url>
  <urlname>[L] Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill : S3 Original contractors for Wireless Hill station by .tbh</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909935" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill : S3 Original contractors for Wireless Hill station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>.tbh</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">.tbh</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Original contractors for Wireless Hill station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What word do you translate from the Morse Code on the seat?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86e6913f-7d1a-481c-9aa3-2afadd3376df" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In April 1910 the Federal Government announces Telefunken/ Australasian Wireless Limited as the successful contractors for the two wireless stations.

As there were no adequate roads for transportation of materials to the site.  Ferries were still the main source of public and commercial transport to and from Perth so the construction materials were landed at 'German Jetty' on the foreshore below Wireless Hill.  (German Jetty is a location on the Melville Historical Places Adventure Lab.)

On 30th September, 1912 Applecross Wireless Station (Radio Perth POP) officially opened.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3450fb1f-8d22-4efe-9c6e-36fa300036ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At a recent geocaching event at Wireless Hill it was noticed that various tables and seats had symbols embedded on them that once translated detail information relating to the original European purpose of Wireless Hill.

This Adventure takes you around 5 of the seats.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.030517" lon="115.8271">
  <ele>46.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-04T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBMsxB-01</name>
  <desc>LSamuelsRamble1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e</url>
  <urlname>[L] Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill : S1 The Code by .tbh</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909931" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill : S1 The Code</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>.tbh</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">.tbh</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Code&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many 'dits' are there in the code on this seat? (2 digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87cd4a97-fbae-4f20-8c3f-830981754291" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Morse code

Morse code is formatted as a series of on-off tones, lights, or clicks which can be easily decoded by a skilled 'listener' without the use of any additional special equipment. 

Each of these characters is represented as the standardized sequences of short and long signals (often called "dots" and "dashes", or "dits" and "dahs"), with a duration of a long signal being three times longer than the short one. 

The separation between each character is maintained with a small period of silence that is equal to the length of the short sound (or dot). Not only that Morse code is very easy to be used, but it is also very efficient. 

The original code created by the Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail in early 1840s was the predecessor to the modern International Morse Code.

(Source http://www.samuelmorse.net/samuel-morse-facts/morse-code/)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3450fb1f-8d22-4efe-9c6e-36fa300036ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At a recent geocaching event at Wireless Hill it was noticed that various tables and seats had symbols embedded on them that once translated detail information relating to the original European purpose of Wireless Hill.

This Adventure takes you around 5 of the seats.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
