   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Samuel's Ramble Around Wireless Hill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Man&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5949f316-1cb7-4105-b294-ca15e07aff7e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the translation of the first word of the code?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/50dcc781-478a-49b0-b258-9d488fc04a95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Samuel Finley Breese Morse (April 27, 1791 – April 2, 1872) was an American inventor and painter. After having established his reputation as a portrait painter, in his middle age Morse contributed to the invention of a single-wire telegraph system based on European telegraphs. He was a co-developer of Morse code and helped to develop the commercial use of telegraphy. 

(Source: Wikipedia)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3450fb1f-8d22-4efe-9c6e-36fa300036ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At a recent geocaching event at Wireless Hill it was noticed that various tables and seats had symbols embedded on them that once translated detail information relating to the original European purpose of Wireless Hill.

This Adventure takes you around 5 of the seats.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-28.914333" lon="121.328767">
  <ele>380.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB5drg-03</name>
  <desc>GoldenOutbackL3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8970664b-70ca-4022-a11c-773049a87aca</url>
  <urlname>The Golden Outback - Leonora and Gwalia : S3 The State Hotel by Kyzabra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004017" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Golden Outback - Leonora and Gwalia : S3 The State Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kyzabra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kyzabra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Golden Outback - Leonora and Gwalia&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The State Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8970664b-70ca-4022-a11c-773049a87aca"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8970664b-70ca-4022-a11c-773049a87aca&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The building has been leased to Sons of Gwalia since ??? (as written on the sign)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f480018d-0407-4088-b141-5174c6632726" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Gwalia State Hotel was built in 1903 and the first in a range of 'State Hotels' constructed throughout Western Australia. The hotel is an impressive two-story brick building with 10ft. balcony wrapped around the Station and Kane Street façade. 

The Gwalia State Hotel no longer operates as a functioning hotel and is privately owned by St Barbara Ltd and not accessible to the public.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/84dc6c09-2214-4641-9907-8b60fa210332" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Leonora and Gwalia are a two hours’ drive north of Kalgoorlie in Western Australia, on Goldfields Highway. Gwalia is located 2.5km south of Leonora.
These towns form part of the Golden Quest Discovery trail, spanning a distance of 965km through the Goldfields of Western Australia, the trail provides travellers with a unique insight into the heritage of the region.
Check out the sites along the trail. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
