 <wpt lat="-31.942633" lon="115.910583">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB59RE-01</name>
  <desc>MaylandsHistor1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222</url>
  <urlname>Maylands Historical Buildings : S1 Peninsula Farm by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909830" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Maylands Historical Buildings : S1 Peninsula Farm</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Maylands Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Peninsula Farm&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the Corporation involved in the Farm's acquisition and restoration? (4 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d1ac49f1-b4dc-44f7-a5ee-dbb95cc93041" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Peninsula Farm was one of the first farms in the Swan River Colony and is one of the earliest Perth metropolitan residences still standing. The entire peninsula was granted to colonists arriving aboard the Tranby in 1830 and became known as the ‘Peninsula Farm’. Joseph Hardey was allocated a share of this land and he with his wife Ann built this house in 1839, after losing the previous two houses to flooding. Peninsula Farm remained in the Hardey family until 1913. Joseph Hardey and his son Richard, who took over management of the property in the late 1860s, were highly influential in the religious, business and political activities of the colony. Peninsula Farm was purchased by Harold Cheshire in 1951, to keep and train racehorses. The house and remnant land survived major subdivision as Perth became more urbanised and the National Trust took over management of the place in 1977.

The coords will take you to a plaque at the entrance to the house.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/939f9691-68c6-4270-9e7c-e4f5b846c975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Maylands Peninsula is on the northern bank of the Swan River about 5 kilometres east of Perth's city centre, and has a rich and varied history. This Adventure Lab will take you to just a few of the interesting historical buildings and locations in the area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9565" lon="115.879867">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB18gL-02</name>
  <desc>EastPerthCemet2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6</url>
  <urlname>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S2 Gravesite: W.J. Huggins by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909842" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S2 Gravesite: W.J. Huggins</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gravesite: W.J. Huggins&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Finish this: "A voice we loved is...."&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf8d130-5658-4ae3-bbfc-f4d44b448255" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this location you will see the headstone of William John (W.J.) Huggins, placed in the Church of England section of the Cemeteries.

William died in 1895 at the young age of only 34 years - recorded cause of death: heart failure. 

Look closely at the headstone inscription.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/957e74de-66e9-479e-81ab-7b42c23a772e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The East Perth Cemeteries is a highly significant heritage place. From tuberculosis (brought to the colony from the Old World) to typhoid (a fever that struck Perth at the same time as gold fever), the graves of East Perth Cemeteries are a record of the first 70 years of European migration to what is now Perth. It is a remnant of what was seven independently owned and managed cemeteries covering seven religious/cultural groups, in addition to an eighth burial ground for felons.

As the area is fragile, entrance to the East Perth Cemeteries (now maintained by the National Trust of Western Australia) is only on Sundays between 2pm and 4pm. There is an entrance fee of $10 for adults. However, it is well worth a visit as it is one of a rare surviving group of colonial cemeteries that have retained a high degree of integrity and authenticity within the CBD of an Australian capital city.

The locations of this Adventure Lab are all outside the cemetery fence perimeter, accessible 24/7.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.956717" lon="115.87865">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
