  <name>LB18gL-04</name>
  <desc>EastPerthCemet4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6</url>
  <urlname>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S4 Gravesite: Grigson Family by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909846" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S4 Gravesite: Grigson Family</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gravesite: Grigson Family&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Besides Thomas, how many other Grigson family members are listed on the headstone? (Numeral)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/07ab1817-4d75-40e6-8387-f3f07b42e390" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this location you can see the headstone of Thomas Grigson and family, placed in the Church of England section of the cemeteries. 

Thomas was born in England in 1819, and arrived in WA aboard the Eleanor in 1837. He married Margaret Nairn in 1847, and together they had 14 children. Thomas worked as a builder in Perth in the 1840s before moving to York in the 1950s where he farmed.  Thomas built a school on his property opposite Grigson's Well, presumably to educate his large family.

Thomas died in 1890, aged 71 years. His cause of death was not recorded.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/957e74de-66e9-479e-81ab-7b42c23a772e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The East Perth Cemeteries is a highly significant heritage place. From tuberculosis (brought to the colony from the Old World) to typhoid (a fever that struck Perth at the same time as gold fever), the graves of East Perth Cemeteries are a record of the first 70 years of European migration to what is now Perth. It is a remnant of what was seven independently owned and managed cemeteries covering seven religious/cultural groups, in addition to an eighth burial ground for felons.

As the area is fragile, entrance to the East Perth Cemeteries (now maintained by the National Trust of Western Australia) is only on Sundays between 2pm and 4pm. There is an entrance fee of $10 for adults. However, it is well worth a visit as it is one of a rare surviving group of colonial cemeteries that have retained a high degree of integrity and authenticity within the CBD of an Australian capital city.

The locations of this Adventure Lab are all outside the cemetery fence perimeter, accessible 24/7.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9566" lon="115.878417">
