   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gravesite: Thomas Angove&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the age difference (in years) between Thomas and Mary, at the time of their respective deaths?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/80f3b6d3-c3c0-4571-91d8-cfb0b3c69974" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this location, you will see the headstone of Thomas and Mary Angove, and Annie Maria Brown, placed in the Wesleyan section of the Cemeteries. 

Thomas was born in 1824, in Cornwall England. He married Mary in 1850 and they went on to have four children. 

Tragically, Thomas and Mary, as well as their youngest child Annie all died within months of each other in 1899. Their causes of death were not recorded. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/957e74de-66e9-479e-81ab-7b42c23a772e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The East Perth Cemeteries is a highly significant heritage place. From tuberculosis (brought to the colony from the Old World) to typhoid (a fever that struck Perth at the same time as gold fever), the graves of East Perth Cemeteries are a record of the first 70 years of European migration to what is now Perth. It is a remnant of what was seven independently owned and managed cemeteries covering seven religious/cultural groups, in addition to an eighth burial ground for felons.

As the area is fragile, entrance to the East Perth Cemeteries (now maintained by the National Trust of Western Australia) is only on Sundays between 2pm and 4pm. There is an entrance fee of $10 for adults. However, it is well worth a visit as it is one of a rare surviving group of colonial cemeteries that have retained a high degree of integrity and authenticity within the CBD of an Australian capital city.

The locations of this Adventure Lab are all outside the cemetery fence perimeter, accessible 24/7.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
