  <urlname>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S5 Jewish Memorial Cemetery - Martellup by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909848" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside : S5 Jewish Memorial Cemetery - Martellup</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;East Perth Cemeteries: Standing on the Outside&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Jewish Memorial Cemetery - Martellup&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b08bc9ac-3ca2-4c3f-a035-ded70b2a27b6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the headstone of Charles Gustus Israel. How old - in years and months - was Charles when he died in 1896?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d906865a-2be5-4b76-bd7e-42fce0c9d1ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A cemetery was opened in this area in 1867, as part of the East Perth Cemeteries, to provide consecrated burial grounds for WA’s growing Jewish community. It is thought that about 30 people were buried in the Hebrew Cemetery before Karrakatta Cemetery came into use in 1899. The area fell into decline and was redeveloped with the help of the Jewish community in the 1950s.

Eight surviving headstones were eventually relocated to the Jewish Memorial Cemetery, opened in 2001, which lies on the site of the original Hebrew Cemetery. 

At this location, you will be at the front gates to the cemetery. Look through the fence towards the headstones &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/957e74de-66e9-479e-81ab-7b42c23a772e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The East Perth Cemeteries is a highly significant heritage place. From tuberculosis (brought to the colony from the Old World) to typhoid (a fever that struck Perth at the same time as gold fever), the graves of East Perth Cemeteries are a record of the first 70 years of European migration to what is now Perth. It is a remnant of what was seven independently owned and managed cemeteries covering seven religious/cultural groups, in addition to an eighth burial ground for felons.

As the area is fragile, entrance to the East Perth Cemeteries (now maintained by the National Trust of Western Australia) is only on Sundays between 2pm and 4pm. There is an entrance fee of $10 for adults. However, it is well worth a visit as it is one of a rare surviving group of colonial cemeteries that have retained a high degree of integrity and authenticity within the CBD of an Australian capital city.

The locations of this Adventure Lab are all outside the cemetery fence perimeter, accessible 24/7.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
