 <wpt lat="-31.930083" lon="115.894783">
  <ele>28.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB59RE-02</name>
  <desc>MaylandsHistor2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222</url>
  <urlname>Maylands Historical Buildings : S2 Old Police Station by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909832" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Maylands Historical Buildings : S2 Old Police Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Maylands Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Old Police Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Near the front door, what is the name of the organisation on the wall plaque below the number 196? (5 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d0deb8bb-4665-4fd1-a26d-73b454017cd4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1904, the Maylands Progress Association applied for a police constable to be stationed in the  Maylands area. The WA government purchased the land at 196 Guildford Road for 135 pounds.

The Maylands Police Station and residential quarters was built by Franklin and Finlay at a cost of 962 pounds and opened on 9 August 1908. At that time, it was common practice to accommodate both the local Police station and quarters for the Police officers under one roof. 

The original building had a charge room and two cells. Its yard had a washhouse, earth closets and also a timber stable as the district constable was a mounted constable.  For many years, the horse drinking trough on the footpath at the front entrance served as a reminder of bygone days.

In 1973, the stables were removed and replaced with a double garage. From 1999 to 2005, a single policeman staffed the Station, and the Station eventually closed in 2007.

At the coords, you will be standing at the front gate to the premises. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/939f9691-68c6-4270-9e7c-e4f5b846c975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Maylands Peninsula is on the northern bank of the Swan River about 5 kilometres east of Perth's city centre, and has a rich and varied history. This Adventure Lab will take you to just a few of the interesting historical buildings and locations in the area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.93465" lon="115.884617">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB59RE-03</name>
  <desc>MaylandsHistor3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222</url>
  <urlname>Maylands Historical Buildings : S3 Albany Bell Castle by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909834" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Maylands Historical Buildings : S3 Albany Bell Castle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Maylands Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Albany Bell Castle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many windows can you count on the facade of the Albany Bell Castle?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/eeb3de77-d1aa-470b-84f4-bbf5b70076f3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Albany Bell Castle was built in 1914 for the catering company Albany Bell Ltd. It served as a factory to manufacture cakes and confectionery for its eleven tearooms in Perth and three in Kalgoorlie/Boulder. The site chosen was 19 acres of land two miles from Perth, with natural springs that could supply 100,000 gallons of fresh water per day. The company's founder, Peter Albany Bell, used ideas derived from the Cadbury factory in Bournville, UK to incorporate superior working conditions and amenities for employees, including two weeks' paid annual leave.

After the company was closed and sold in 1928, the factory changed hands many times: becoming a chicken hatchery and, later, a reserve building for WA Newspapers Ltd during World War II. The building was heritage listed in 1991. It was eventually resold and developed into apartment accommodation, retaining the external structure and gardens.

At the coords, you'll be standing at the front gate to the complex. Look towards the building's facade.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/939f9691-68c6-4270-9e7c-e4f5b846c975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Maylands Peninsula is on the northern bank of the Swan River about 5 kilometres east of Perth's city centre, and has a rich and varied history. This Adventure Lab will take you to just a few of the interesting historical buildings and locations in the area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.930383" lon="115.889767">
  <ele>34.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB59RE-05</name>
  <desc>MaylandsHistor5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222</url>
  <urlname>Maylands Historical Buildings : S5 West Australian Institute for the Blind by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909838" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Maylands Historical Buildings : S5 West Australian Institute for the Blind</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Maylands Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;West Australian Institute for the Blind&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the Place No. on the bottom right hand corner of the plaque? (4 digits)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8cd63931-411e-4d28-b04d-b617f3833635" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1898, the Governor of Western Australia, Sir Gerard Smith, laid the foundation stone for the
Victoria Institute and Industrial School for the Blind. The Institute originally consisted of a complex of buildings including a factory, workshops and residential facilities. 

In 1932, the building was renamed the West Australian Institute and Industrial School for the Blind, and in 1937 the Lotteries Commission and the government funded the development of the “L-shaped” building, and combined the three previous buildings together.

The resultant 1937 building is a fine example of the Inter-War Stripped Classical Style, and is one the few examples of a prominent Western Australian institution that features strong art deco detailing. 

The refurbished building is now home to the West Australian Ballet Centre.

At the coords, you will find a State Register of Heritage Places plaque.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/939f9691-68c6-4270-9e7c-e4f5b846c975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Maylands Peninsula is on the northern bank of the Swan River about 5 kilometres east of Perth's city centre, and has a rich and varied history. This Adventure Lab will take you to just a few of the interesting historical buildings and locations in the area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.928" lon="115.891517">
  <ele>28.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB59RE-04</name>
  <desc>MaylandsHistor4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222</url>
  <urlname>Maylands Historical Buildings : S4 Peninsula Hotel by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909836" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Maylands Historical Buildings : S4 Peninsula Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Maylands Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Peninsula Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1bacbfaf-2492-4d7c-b3b7-6ecbbae90222&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look at the information board. What was the Breakfast Room 'complete with'? (2 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/66fb9b9f-e97f-4ccf-8e50-49dad3d3d90d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Peninsula Hotel is a prominent landmark in the Maylands town centre. The circa 1906 hotel building was originally built and owned by Wilhelm Friederich Gustave Liebe. 

Mr Liebe was a well known Perth building contractor whose other prominent projects include His Majesty’s Theatre and the Public Art Gallery. The building is a rare example of a richly detailed Edwardian Hotel in metropolitan Perth.

The local community and the National Trust were successful in their efforts to prevent the demolition of the building in the 1970’s, making it one of the earliest historic places in Western Australia to be saved through public outcry. The National Trust classified the building in 1974.

Today, the building is beautifully restored and is home to Dome Coffees Australia, who in 2013 won an award for their outstanding contribution to heritage at the Western Australian Heritage Awards. 

The listed coords will take you to an information board in the front garden. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/939f9691-68c6-4270-9e7c-e4f5b846c975" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Maylands Peninsula is on the northern bank of the Swan River about 5 kilometres east of Perth's city centre, and has a rich and varied history. This Adventure Lab will take you to just a few of the interesting historical buildings and locations in the area. Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
