  <desc>LDiscoverGeral4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2125fac9-bf70-4b8b-b775-57a9f3010a23</url>
  <urlname>[L] Discover Geraldton : S4 Radio Theatre Building by Ian&amp;Sue - Team Pathfinder</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918374" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Discover Geraldton : S4 Radio Theatre Building</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Ian&amp;Sue - Team Pathfinder</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Ian&amp;Sue - Team Pathfinder</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Discover Geraldton&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Radio Theatre Building&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2125fac9-bf70-4b8b-b775-57a9f3010a23"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2125fac9-bf70-4b8b-b775-57a9f3010a23&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Potters House.  Year mentioned on the artwork.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0a817103-fb78-4d9a-876a-3c38e49fa989" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The construction of the Art Deco Radio Theatre Building was an expression of faith in the future of Geraldton in 1937.  The theatre could seat 800 people and hosted both live theatre and films.

In addition to its entertainment role, a Radio Motors showroom and service station was constructed as part of the building.  The reflected both the growing popularity of motor vehicles and the increased ability of more people to buy them.

In the 1970’s the Radio Theatre suffered from the popularity of drive-in theatres.  At one point there were three in Geraldton.  

Radio Theatre closed in 1971, its seating removed, it became a night club for a few years.

In 2020, after being abandon for many years it suffered a large fire.  Luckily the exterior still remains.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6da70222-27e7-491d-82ab-bb449c046563" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Discover Geraldton will take you on a short stroll / cycle around town sharing with you some of Geraldtons’ history which is often overlooked.

A short stroll - approximately 30 minutes.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.351017" lon="115.904667">
  <ele>104.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBV8Wk-01</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfGingi1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72</url>
  <urlname>History of Gingin : S1 Gingin Railway Station by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909862" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Gingin : S1 Gingin Railway Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Gingin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gingin Railway Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the station built? (yyyy)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f5ef6307-1086-4026-85e2-c45619a3e397" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gingin Railway Station &amp; Quarters was one of the first railway stations constructed on the Midland Junction to Walkway line by the Midland Railway Company. It was constructed by prominent builder, innovative farmer and developer Friedrich Wilhelm Gustav Liebe, and is also associated with Edward Keane, a leading railway builder. It was part of the development of the Midland-Walkaway Railway that provided the essential transport for pastoral and agricultural development in the region.

You will find information to answer the question on the brown information sign on the front verandah of the station building.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3a69ce78-35ff-45b8-b9af-e3631375a519" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gingin is one of the oldest towns in the State, with explorers and pioneers arriving in 1831, just two years after the Swan River Colony was proclaimed. To this day Gingin retains some important remnants of its pioneering days dating back to the 1850s. Join us on a short tour of Gingin and some of its sites near Granville Park.

This Adventure Lab has bonus final cache located at another historical site in Gingin. Please refer to GC9V9CA (https://coord.info/GC9V9CA) for more information.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.3509" lon="115.904283">
  <ele>103.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBV8Wk-02</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfGingi2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72</url>
  <urlname>History of Gingin : S2 Gingin Hotel by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909864" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Gingin : S2 Gingin Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Gingin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gingin Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What day and month did the Gingin Hotel open? (dd/mm)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bd5ac2a4-642f-4369-8197-6b050d1ddcee" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gingin Hotel is the only surviving Hotel in Gingin and has operated continually since it opened in 1903. It is a fine representative example of the Federation period of architecture and of development in Gingin at that time, making a significant contribution to the townscape heritage. It is also significant for associations with various owners and managers including John Musk, wife Catherine, Tom Jones and others, and demonstrates a sense of place for the social and hospitality functions of the place.

You will find information to answer the question on the brown information sign under the front verandah of the building.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3a69ce78-35ff-45b8-b9af-e3631375a519" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gingin is one of the oldest towns in the State, with explorers and pioneers arriving in 1831, just two years after the Swan River Colony was proclaimed. To this day Gingin retains some important remnants of its pioneering days dating back to the 1850s. Join us on a short tour of Gingin and some of its sites near Granville Park.

This Adventure Lab has bonus final cache located at another historical site in Gingin. Please refer to GC9V9CA (https://coord.info/GC9V9CA) for more information.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.349683" lon="115.90695">
  <ele>104.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBV8Wk-05</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfGingi5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72</url>
  <urlname>History of Gingin : S5 Dewar's House by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909870" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Gingin : S5 Dewar's House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Gingin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dewar's House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e684a1d6-cdb0-44ff-acf5-64143cb90d72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The House was built for John Dewar in what year? (yyyy)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4677ca42-59db-4f1d-b5ff-24f972dac7cb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Railway Hotel (fmr) Dewar's House, a single-storied brick and shingle cottage (facing Weld Street) with a two-storey addition on the north side (facing Edwards Street) and a stone walled barn in the rear yard, is significant for its aesthetic values form and the colour and texture of its fabric. 

The landmark qualities arising from the elevated position overlooking the town.

Dewar's House is important for its association with Cockram and Dewar families; its contribution to the sense of place of the community; and, its role development of Gingin in its diverse roles as a home, shop and hotel.

You will find information to answer the question on the plaque on the corner of the wall encompassing the House's yard.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3a69ce78-35ff-45b8-b9af-e3631375a519" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gingin is one of the oldest towns in the State, with explorers and pioneers arriving in 1831, just two years after the Swan River Colony was proclaimed. To this day Gingin retains some important remnants of its pioneering days dating back to the 1850s. Join us on a short tour of Gingin and some of its sites near Granville Park.

This Adventure Lab has bonus final cache located at another historical site in Gingin. Please refer to GC9V9CA (https://coord.info/GC9V9CA) for more information.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.345767" lon="115.905">
  <ele>103.0</ele>
