 <wpt lat="-31.956183" lon="116.163233">
  <ele>113.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYrJb-02</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipeli121</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S2 No. 1 Pump Station - Chimney by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909854" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S2 No. 1 Pump Station - Chimney</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 1 Pump Station - Chimney&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What material was used for the original chimney? (Five letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/33605797-bd69-4ec3-aae4-86a3215b7f85" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally the pump station was to be coal fired. A branch of the train line was built running high alongside the building so the coal could be tipped out into a large bin near the boilers room.
Timber was cheaper and plentiful so the boilers were converted to wood. The smoke from the chimney burning timber caused the original steel chimney to corrode and it was replaced in 1910 with the 41.5m brick chimney you see today. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cc175bb-6ecc-4c58-86bc-a572a7753b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 1 Pump Station sits in an area known as Minderinjy to the local Whadjuk people at the foot of Mundaring Weir, which dams the Helena River, 39 km east of Perth.
The river was a traditional east-west travel route for corroborees in what is now called the suburb of Guildford. The damming of this river has changed the cultural landscape dramatically.
No 1 Pump Station was the first of eight steam pump stations constructed as part of the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme, designed by CY O’Connor to transfer water over a distance of 560 km from the Perth hills to the WA Goldfields to bring precious water to this notoriously dry part of the state.

In sweltering heat on 22 January 1903, Lady Margaret Forrest, wife of Sir John Forrest, ceremoniously started the engines at No 1 Pump Station to open the scheme. It was her husband, then a federal politician, who had obtained a loan and parliamentary approval to build the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme under his premiership of WA. 
Ref: National Trust WA&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.956333" lon="116.163367">
  <ele>111.0</ele>
