  <name>LBYrJb-05</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipeli151</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S5 No. 1 Pump Station - Water flow by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909860" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S5 No. 1 Pump Station - Water flow</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 1 Pump Station - Water flow&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How long does it take for the water to reach Kalgoorlie?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fdc395ab-53e6-437f-9b5f-69f7626d456d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mount Charlotte Reservoir, Kalgoorlie, 24 January 1903.
It is 5pm and the large crowd is sweating in the evening heat. The former Premier of Western Australia, Sir John Forrest, steps forward and with a simple twist of a valve sends a stream of water gushing into the reservoir. The longest freshwater pipeline in the world has ever seen is eventually open. 
Behind the towering concrete wall is a storage reservoir that is fed by the Helena River, direct rainfall run-off and water pumped from other sources.
Look at the digital display of water flow; each time it changes, another 1000 litres of water are pumped from Mundaring Weir to begin the long 560km journey east.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cc175bb-6ecc-4c58-86bc-a572a7753b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 1 Pump Station sits in an area known as Minderinjy to the local Whadjuk people at the foot of Mundaring Weir, which dams the Helena River, 39 km east of Perth.
The river was a traditional east-west travel route for corroborees in what is now called the suburb of Guildford. The damming of this river has changed the cultural landscape dramatically.
No 1 Pump Station was the first of eight steam pump stations constructed as part of the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme, designed by CY O’Connor to transfer water over a distance of 560 km from the Perth hills to the WA Goldfields to bring precious water to this notoriously dry part of the state.

In sweltering heat on 22 January 1903, Lady Margaret Forrest, wife of Sir John Forrest, ceremoniously started the engines at No 1 Pump Station to open the scheme. It was her husband, then a federal politician, who had obtained a loan and parliamentary approval to build the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme under his premiership of WA. 
Ref: National Trust WA&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95615" lon="116.163517">
  <ele>112.0</ele>
