  <desc>GoldenPipeli111</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S1 No. 1 Pump Station - Boilers by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909852" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S1 No. 1 Pump Station - Boilers</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 1 Pump Station - Boilers&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many boilers can you see through the large arched window?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c65bb1af-fa54-47d8-a9a4-77cc98ec8ce7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The original pump station was powered by steam. Firewood was cut from the surrounding Forrest and delivered by train, horde drawn carts and later trucks. It was fed into fire-boxes beneath enormous Babcock and Wilcox boilers. The steam generated was used to power the engines that pumped the water uphill and along the pipeline. The boilers were kept going 24 hours a day with the men working Eight hour shifts.
Today the scheme is powered by electricity. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cc175bb-6ecc-4c58-86bc-a572a7753b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 1 Pump Station sits in an area known as Minderinjy to the local Whadjuk people at the foot of Mundaring Weir, which dams the Helena River, 39 km east of Perth.
The river was a traditional east-west travel route for corroborees in what is now called the suburb of Guildford. The damming of this river has changed the cultural landscape dramatically.
No 1 Pump Station was the first of eight steam pump stations constructed as part of the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme, designed by CY O’Connor to transfer water over a distance of 560 km from the Perth hills to the WA Goldfields to bring precious water to this notoriously dry part of the state.

In sweltering heat on 22 January 1903, Lady Margaret Forrest, wife of Sir John Forrest, ceremoniously started the engines at No 1 Pump Station to open the scheme. It was her husband, then a federal politician, who had obtained a loan and parliamentary approval to build the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme under his premiership of WA. 
Ref: National Trust WA&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.956367" lon="116.164617">
  <ele>120.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYrJb-03</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipeli131</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S3 No. 1 Pump Station - Fancy shed by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909856" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S3 No. 1 Pump Station - Fancy shed</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 1 Pump Station - Fancy shed&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This shed housed?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/82ef3bb2-44bc-4e7b-8051-b52da8a6afa6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This building was constructed to house the wheels that operated valves to control the flow of water to No.1 Pump Station. It took more than 1000 turns of a wheel to open or close the valves so luckily they were for emergencies only. The main controls were in the upper valve house which is on the top of the dam wall. 
It is believed both valve houses were designed by prominent WA Government architect George Temple Poole.
The lower valve house is no longer used.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cc175bb-6ecc-4c58-86bc-a572a7753b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 1 Pump Station sits in an area known as Minderinjy to the local Whadjuk people at the foot of Mundaring Weir, which dams the Helena River, 39 km east of Perth.
The river was a traditional east-west travel route for corroborees in what is now called the suburb of Guildford. The damming of this river has changed the cultural landscape dramatically.
No 1 Pump Station was the first of eight steam pump stations constructed as part of the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme, designed by CY O’Connor to transfer water over a distance of 560 km from the Perth hills to the WA Goldfields to bring precious water to this notoriously dry part of the state.

In sweltering heat on 22 January 1903, Lady Margaret Forrest, wife of Sir John Forrest, ceremoniously started the engines at No 1 Pump Station to open the scheme. It was her husband, then a federal politician, who had obtained a loan and parliamentary approval to build the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme under his premiership of WA. 
Ref: National Trust WA&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
