 <wpt lat="-31.9558" lon="116.164467">
  <ele>126.0</ele>
  <time>2022-07-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBYrJb-04</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipeli141</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S4 No. 1 Pump Station - Rail Line by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909858" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station : S4 No. 1 Pump Station - Rail Line</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 1 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 1 Pump Station - Rail Line&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0f250bd9-576c-4bb5-9448-3290a3259482&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who created the drawing that is etched onto the info board? (First and last name)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c6cd931b-6c28-46b1-acf9-aa60156f0246" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A railway line to bring materials for building the dam and supplies for the workers and their families was built in 1898.
Because of the gradient, the train had to zigzag down to the dam site. 
The railway also brought coal and later wood for the No1. Pump Station. 
The landscaped grounds of the weir, and the lower zig-zag section of the end of the railway line (which can still be seen when the capacity of the dams falls below approximately 35%) feature prominently in postcards of numerous weekend and special picnic excursion trains that travelled to the weir from the 1910s.
After the 1920’s wood was trucked in, the tracks were covered when the height of the wall was increased between 1948-51.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5cc175bb-6ecc-4c58-86bc-a572a7753b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 1 Pump Station sits in an area known as Minderinjy to the local Whadjuk people at the foot of Mundaring Weir, which dams the Helena River, 39 km east of Perth.
The river was a traditional east-west travel route for corroborees in what is now called the suburb of Guildford. The damming of this river has changed the cultural landscape dramatically.
No 1 Pump Station was the first of eight steam pump stations constructed as part of the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme, designed by CY O’Connor to transfer water over a distance of 560 km from the Perth hills to the WA Goldfields to bring precious water to this notoriously dry part of the state.

In sweltering heat on 22 January 1903, Lady Margaret Forrest, wife of Sir John Forrest, ceremoniously started the engines at No 1 Pump Station to open the scheme. It was her husband, then a federal politician, who had obtained a loan and parliamentary approval to build the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme under his premiership of WA. 
Ref: National Trust WA&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
