   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Cunderdin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Water tower&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1e3dd37-36af-43f3-8eaf-8d0902ff5b3e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1e3dd37-36af-43f3-8eaf-8d0902ff5b3e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the sign on the tower, the tower was erected circa&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed1ab23e-6dce-493e-a497-c94e3e4efaab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This water tower was operated by Government railways. Water gravitated from the rock catchment and reservoir at Cunderdin Hill into this water tank to supply the railway steam engines&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b626109c-17b0-45bd-9a8a-eb2278c7a229" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Golden Pipeline Heritage Trail follows the water pipeline conceived by C.Y. O'Conner to supply the goldfields of Western Australia with much needed fresh water.

The Golden Pipeline Trail aims to use adventure labs to highlight some of the historic places and towns along the journey.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
