   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Cunderdin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Traditional water pump&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1e3dd37-36af-43f3-8eaf-8d0902ff5b3e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1e3dd37-36af-43f3-8eaf-8d0902ff5b3e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the sign at the base of the windmill, what was the nickname of C T Beard&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1d381079-c389-4c75-8dba-502120105e5c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the early 1900s, windmills were the original water pump, driven by wind, they would lift underground water into tanks as storage for watering stock. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b626109c-17b0-45bd-9a8a-eb2278c7a229" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Golden Pipeline Heritage Trail follows the water pipeline conceived by C.Y. O'Conner to supply the goldfields of Western Australia with much needed fresh water.

