 <wpt lat="-31.967433" lon="115.816817">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVpSB-05</name>
  <desc>QEIIMCS5Chrysal</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636</url>
  <urlname>QEIIMC : S5 Chrysalis by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909924" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>QEIIMC : S5 Chrysalis</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;QEIIMC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Chrysalis&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What kind of butterfly is the artwork an interpretation of? (two words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e34e128e-cc67-413a-b048-97b542248abd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Standing here you see the main imposing G block of SCGH. The originators of the Perth Medical Centre scheme stipulated amongst other things, 'outlook from the buildings is to be as pleasant as possible; integration with nature is to be sought after at all times'.  It succeeds with that remit in that the views from the 8th floor across Kings Park and over to Matilda Bay are beautiful. It is therefore such a shame the building itself is so ugly!

Across Hospital Avenue from G block you will find a sparkly piece of artwork.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/efa3aa84-d9c9-4abf-88de-3d6b51046d7e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Queen Elizabeth II Medical Centre, home to the largest medical centre in the southern hemisphere, hosting globally recognised major medical, research and education tenants.

Back in the 1950s the first hospital to be housed on the site was the Perth Chest Hospital (currently A Block), built to look after mainly tuberculosis patients. In 1963 the expanding hospital became known as Sir Charles Gairdner Hospital after the then current Governor of Western Australia.

Photographs taken from A Fair Sized Town, Sir Charles Gairdner Hospital and its history 1958-1988 - Carolyn Polizzotto.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.968833" lon="115.813833">
  <ele>16.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBVpSB-04</name>
  <desc>QEIIMCS4Georgef</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636</url>
  <urlname>QEIIMC : S4 Georgeff Quadrangle by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-486" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>QEIIMC : S4 Georgeff Quadrangle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;QEIIMC&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Georgeff Quadrangle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a5013315-2abd-439f-b217-41c6e1b4e636&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the artist's name who created the Carl Georgeff bust? (3 'words')&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2383a3ab-39bb-482a-b13c-3c7998f2c70d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carl Georgeff was born in Perth, Western Australia, on 14 December 1913. His father, Michael, was a farmer, and his mother was Anna Kamburova. He attended Perth Boy's School, Perth Modern School and the University of Western Australia, before studying medicine at the University of Melbourne and qualifying in 1938. He was resident medical officer at the Royal Perth Hospital from 1938 to 1940.

He was appointed outpatient surgeon to the Royal Perth Hospital from 1952 to 1960. He was a permanent member of the Raine Research Foundation of the University of Western Australia.

One of his patients, Mary Raine, died a millionairess and Georgeff was instrumental in her leaving the bulk of her fortune to the university.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/efa3aa84-d9c9-4abf-88de-3d6b51046d7e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Queen Elizabeth II Medical Centre, home to the largest medical centre in the southern hemisphere, hosting globally recognised major medical, research and education tenants.

Back in the 1950s the first hospital to be housed on the site was the Perth Chest Hospital (currently A Block), built to look after mainly tuberculosis patients. In 1963 the expanding hospital became known as Sir Charles Gairdner Hospital after the then current Governor of Western Australia.

Photographs taken from A Fair Sized Town, Sir Charles Gairdner Hospital and its history 1958-1988 - Carolyn Polizzotto.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9685" lon="115.813967">
