 <wpt lat="-24.868167" lon="113.704167">
  <ele>37.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN8m1-02</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonCatch2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S2 Sugar Scoop Antennae. by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004824" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S2 Sugar Scoop Antennae.</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Sugar Scoop Antennae.  &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many of these antennae left in the world. 2 words&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/107e688e-d3f2-4899-b96e-92fac21713a2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon now has a Space &amp; Technology Museum
Unfortunately it is run by volunteers so runs to season times.  Please check before arriving
The museum focuses on two parts.  The Tracking Station and the OTC Satellite Earth Station
The Tracking Station was located 10 kms south rom Carnarvon or the NASA Gemini, Apollo and Skylab missions.  It was commissioned in 1964 and operated for 11 years.
It was the last station to communicate with space capsules leaving the earth orbit and the in contact before splashdown.
The OTC Satellite Earth Station and now museum was opened in 1966 initially with the 12.8 metre Casshorn antennae, or  "sugar scoop" form, which played an important part in television history.  It was used to transmit the first pictures live via satellite to London from Australia  
The earth station was expanded in 1969, when a second and larger antennae was complete, with a diameter of 97 ft (almost 30mtrs).  It took over the original antennae in relaying NASA communication &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/53c062ac-ed42-407e-85e1-aa62a4b26dfd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town and I still enjoy coming home to see family and friends
 home you will enjoy your visit whilst your here too.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-24.8758" lon="113.630517">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN8m1-03</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonCatch3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S3 Carnarvon Lighthouse by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004826" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S3 Carnarvon Lighthouse</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Carnarvon Lighthouse&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many lighthouse keepers had the same surname name.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1f2b3b73-61da-423c-a096-658b6fbd44c8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A wooden tower 60 ft high  and 42 ft above sea level and visible for 15 miles in good conditioner.  It was converted from paraffin oil to acetylene  in 1909.
A new tower was erected by steel framework in the early 1960's, which was present and working in 1994.,
The original 1896 wooden tower was damaged by fire and replaced by the present steel sketal tower in 1962.  The lantern is now on display on the grounds near the tower.
Sometime between 2015 and 2020 the new metal tower was removed.   
a very short section and the stub of the lantern room remain.
The museum does open for the public if you would like a look back into the past, but you will have to check for times or enjoy an awesome cuppa and snack or ice cream at the 'One Mile Jetty Centre' which gives a few pictures of our jetty which was a prosperous life line to our town.  Also there is a museum connected to the area which is well worth a look.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/53c062ac-ed42-407e-85e1-aa62a4b26dfd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town and I still enjoy coming home to see family and friends
 home you will enjoy your visit whilst your here too.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-24.875333" lon="113.63065">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnSKC-01</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonATast1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   A taste of a good life : S1 The Water Tower Lookout by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004832" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   A taste of a good life : S1 The Water Tower Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   A taste of a good life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Water Tower Lookout&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many ships a year would berth in its hey day.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/74bde8bc-b13d-4a6d-9635-537aeea24e2e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Water Tower Lookout is no longer available for the 360* viewing from the top which showed the views of the Gascoyne River, Carnarvon townsite, OTC Dish, Nor West Seafood and the 'One Mile Jetty". The wooden float in the tank would shut the valve when full, and was ready to be delivered through a pipe on the jetty to ships, and with shut off valves along the jetty if needed in case of fire on the wooden jetty.  From 1912 - 1977 many state ships berthed along with livestock being transported by ship to Perth.
The water would also have been used for watering livestock in nearby yards.
The Koombana was one of the steam ships to berth at the beginning of the century.  Sadly in 1912 it went down during a cyclone north off Port Hedland with no survivors
There is a lot more history in the "One Mile Jetty Centre" with pictures and the information staff full of local info.  And the coffee and ice cream goes down a treat.  
There is also a museum of old relics well worth a look.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5d64ffda-7dd8-4b44-8760-a705492fea80" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town.  I still enjoy coming home to family &amp; friends
Seafood to die for especially when cooked for you
Beautiful weather (98% off the time). what more could you ask for
I hope you enjoy your stay here too
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-24.875333" lon="113.63065">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBnSKC-02</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonATast2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   A taste of a good life : S2 The Babbage Island Meatworks by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004834" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   A taste of a good life : S2 The Babbage Island Meatworks</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   A taste of a good life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Babbage Island Meatworks&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/11a58238-cd9d-47a8-92d7-346526564700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was the shark-processing factory start&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3420f2c2-2754-4a67-ab90-6bdbfd9561ea" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately all that is left here is the information sign and views of the rail trams that would transport the livestock that stand in the distance.
The idea to build a meat works was conceived in 1895 but didn't happen until 1920. The meat works was built from handmade bricks by Dunkley and a sail trolley transported supplies to Carnarvon. Machinery was installed to complete the development but was never used.Then the area was designed to butcher and market the Gascoyne's sheep and cattle.  This was a success either.  
Then used for tanning shark skins for shoes wallets and belts.  Again another failed venture.
Then a marine products in 1926 to export smoked shark fins to China.  Failed again
During the Great Depression 1930's people lived in the abandoned buildings and then finally demolished around 1980.
Handmade bricks were finally distributed locally and recycled.  Some foundations off the metalwork site remain today.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5d64ffda-7dd8-4b44-8760-a705492fea80" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town.  I still enjoy coming home to family &amp; friends
Seafood to die for especially when cooked for you
Beautiful weather (98% off the time). what more could you ask for
I hope you enjoy your stay here too
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-24.87415" lon="113.630333">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN8m1-04</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonCatch4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S4 The Lock Hospital Tragedy Memorial by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004828" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S4 The Lock Hospital Tragedy Memorial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Lock Hospital Tragedy Memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The young girl can only point in the direction of these '...... .. .....' where her parents are now interned.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b080f971-eac0-46e9-b3a6-253116b11c1d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This sculpture is the healing process between the Aboriginal community and ourselves and looking forward to a better future.
This is a departure point, in chains from the mainland to their forced medical incarceration on the Dorre and Bernier Islands off Shark Bay, between the years off 1908 to 1919.
The story on this board is very powerful.   The two islands were separated the hospital and women on Dorrie Island and men on Bernie Island.  And for the next 11 years, almost 700 Aborginal men, women and children, suspected of having general diseases, were transported form the One Mile Jetty and incarcerated on these island.
There is a lot off history at the "One Mile Jetty Centre" and 'Gwoonwardu Mia Aboriginal Cultural Centre
I had heard of a bit off this story on ABC Who Do You Think You Are.
The island had been returned to native vegetation for fauna and flora.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/53c062ac-ed42-407e-85e1-aa62a4b26dfd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town and I still enjoy coming home to see family and friends
 home you will enjoy your visit whilst your here too.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-24.826833" lon="113.768317">
  <ele>16.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBN8m1-01</name>
  <desc>CarnarvonCatch1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089</url>
  <urlname>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S1 Gascoyne Bridge by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99004822" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life : S1 Gascoyne Bridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Carnarvon   catch a taste o the great life&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gascoyne Bridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8bad94c6-4dd3-49d7-8325-b33c81a3f089&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The cost of the bridge on opening in 1931and how long was the foot span.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c7ff5097-3218-4bd3-8629-833f2e23346a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It is said the Gascoyne River is an upside-down river, as it flow for about 120 days off the year and below the dry river bed or the remainder of the year.
It rises in the northeastern Robinson Ranges west off the Gibson Desert, flowing generally westward for 475 mile (760km), through gold mining snd sheep raising country, and empties into the Indian Ocean at Carnarvon.  It is the only place in Australia where the desert reaches out to the sea.
The original bridge was built in 1931 to traffic the people from the north to the south off the country.  Before this the only crossing was stout wire mesh and staked to the wide sandy river bed. and only when the bed was dry enough for people to travel safely.  
this is the second bridge as when the floods came through the water would cover the bridge and the side rails were taken off, so debris could flow freely..  And it was only a one lane bridge at the time.  On the right side picture you can see a piece off the first bridge.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/53c062ac-ed42-407e-85e1-aa62a4b26dfd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Carnarvon is my home town and I still enjoy coming home to see family and friends
 home you will enjoy your visit whilst your here too.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.532633" lon="117.084">
  <ele>308.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBq1sN-02</name>
  <desc>PingellyWA1S212</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/65314309-0688-4605-8685-3af6495d470a</url>
  <urlname>Pingelly, WA (1) : S2 Pingelly Hotel by Everlasting</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909938" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Pingelly, WA (1) : S2 Pingelly Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Everlasting</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Everlasting</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Pingelly, WA (1)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pingelly Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/65314309-0688-4605-8685-3af6495d470a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/65314309-0688-4605-8685-3af6495d470a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year is the "Circa" on the sign? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/32b3ed62-3b28-47c8-b88a-48cc8932c091" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first hotel to be built was a one-storey structure erected for G. M. Sewell in 1889.In about 1902 added a top storey. Early license holders were Ernie Monger, George Murdock, Harold Sewell, John Elsegood, Frank Markwell Snr, John Moss, Douglas Marwell. The first Liquor License was issued on Jan 1, 1890
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6e99795e-4d4d-42d7-886e-fe143aed6193" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pingelly is a quiet, rural service centre surrounded by a rural area which produces wheat, barley, sheep and cattle. While the town is pleasant and unassuming the surrounding area is known for its remarkable remnants of the flora and fauna which existed before the district was farmed.

Location
Pingelly is located 154 km south-east of Perth and 297 m above sea level.

Origin of Name
By 1873 maps of the area were referring to Pingeculling Rocks. It is believed that "pinge" was a local Aboriginal word meaning "small gully" and "culling" was water. The small settlement became known as "Pinge Gully" or "Pinge Culling" but when the railway reached the town in the late 1880s the spelling became "Pingelly".
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
