   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Pingelly, WA (2)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pingelly - Town Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a2abc443-231a-42ff-b26b-ac1b06853f02"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a2abc443-231a-42ff-b26b-ac1b06853f02&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the date on this stone laid by The Hon James Price M.L.A Minister for Public Works on (date)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/512b3808-a44f-4b83-bc04-0b643449088a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In May 1907, Messrs Naylor, J. S. Webb, members of the Agricultural Hall Committee and Mr E. C.. Monger, chairman of the trustees, visited Perth to interview the Minister for Works and ask that a grant should be given to enable the committee to build a new hall.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/733c94eb-0eae-46e3-a288-4c2657bd1fe2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pingelly is a quiet, rural service centre surrounded by a rural area which produces wheat, barley, sheep and cattle. While the town is pleasant and unassuming the surrounding area is known for its remarkable remnants of the flora and fauna which existed before the district was farmed.

Location
Pingelly is located 154 km south-east of Perth and 297 m above sea level.

Origin of Name
By 1873 maps of the area were referring to Pingeculling Rocks. It is believed that "pinge" was a local Aboriginal word meaning "small gully" and "culling" was water. The small settlement became known as "Pinge Gully" or "Pinge Culling" but when the railway reached the town in the late 1880s the spelling became "Pingelly".
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
