 <wpt lat="-32.534283" lon="115.721833">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjjPw-01</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfPeelS1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel</url>
  <urlname>History of Peel : S1 Anglican Church by PollyMichele</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-766" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Peel : S1 Anglican Church</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PollyMichele</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PollyMichele</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Peel&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Anglican Church&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where was the barque wrecked (4 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0b5a75cb-2dd8-4d18-89ef-0ac19d024cb2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1829 church services where held in Thomas Peel’s house. In 1842 Peel turned a workman’s hit into a limestone chapel. In 1869 a fire burnt the village, hastening plans for a new church. Over 100 pounds was donated by settlers and 2 grants, each of £35 were made by the Perth diocese. Dorothy Anne Peel and her husband Henry Hastings Hall donated the land for the church. On 26 February the first service was held in the new church by Pinjarra Chaplain, Revd. James Steward Price. At this time the church had no pews or any plaster on the walls, but there was a pulpit donated by a church in Fremantle. The church was consecrated on the 25th of October 1871. This church is still in use today.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/94276232-3599-4262-a66f-87e112b844d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Region of Peel. The Peel region is known for it’s beautiful waterways, dolphins, beaches, holidays blue manner crabs and canal lifestyles. Many don’t know about the history side come with me on a journey to visit a few of these sites. Most of the destinations are reached by driving and a small amount of walking. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.53445" lon="115.720883">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjjPw-02</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfPeelS2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel</url>
  <urlname>History of Peel : S2 Migrant Barracks by PollyMichele</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-767" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Peel : S2 Migrant Barracks</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PollyMichele</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PollyMichele</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Peel&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Migrant Barracks &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is at your feet?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8de845da-9a83-4942-bbd7-953b5449bfc0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Migrant Barracks were built in 1880 when the Tuckey Brothers Peel Inlet Preserving Works was established. This canning factory was the second in operation and the largest in Mandurah. James Tuckey, with his wife and five children, sailed to the Victorian goldfields in the 1850’s and came back to Mandurah in 1862 with enough money to acquire land and purchase a small coastal vessel for trading. James’s son John jnr. and Charles became involved in the Northwest Pearling industry in the 1870s a profitable venture in which they became successful. Charles and John invested some of the pearling money into a new venture, C Tuckey &amp; Co Peel Inlet Preserving Works.
The migrant workers were housed in the barracks behind the cannery, the barracks were a long low rectangular building with shingle roof divided into six rooms. At its peak the preserving factory was producing 5000 cans of fish a day. The wall of the building is the only evidence of a sizeable Japanese community who lived in Mandurah for many years. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/94276232-3599-4262-a66f-87e112b844d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Region of Peel. The Peel region is known for it’s beautiful waterways, dolphins, beaches, holidays blue manner crabs and canal lifestyles. Many don’t know about the history side come with me on a journey to visit a few of these sites. Most of the destinations are reached by driving and a small amount of walking. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.534933" lon="115.7194">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjjPw-03</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfPeelS3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel</url>
  <urlname>History of Peel : S3 Police Station by PollyMichele</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-768" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Peel : S3 Police Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PollyMichele</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PollyMichele</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Peel&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Police Station &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is in the labour’s hands (2 item’s, with &amp; in between words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4d0e1247-9b7c-49dd-a9c9-e36048c653a0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The museum has had many changes over the years. 
1830 in the beginning the area of land on which the Museum now stands was first occupied by Military built for soldiers. 
1852 The Mandurah Police barracks were built. 
1887 - 1889 Mrs C H Cooper rented the property followed by the Tuckey family. 
1894 The barracks were converted to a school and teachers quarters. 
1900 The entire barracks were converted to teachers quarters whilst new school was built. 
Many more organisations used these premises over the years. 
In 1989 the historical society was given permission to use the original schoolroom as a museum. 
2001 saw the City of Mandurah take over management of the museum. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/94276232-3599-4262-a66f-87e112b844d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Region of Peel. The Peel region is known for it’s beautiful waterways, dolphins, beaches, holidays blue manner crabs and canal lifestyles. Many don’t know about the history side come with me on a journey to visit a few of these sites. Most of the destinations are reached by driving and a small amount of walking. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.5336" lon="115.713883">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjjPw-04</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfPeelS4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel</url>
  <urlname>History of Peel : S4 Cottage by PollyMichele</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-769" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Peel : S4 Cottage</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PollyMichele</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PollyMichele</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Peel&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cottage&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is on the veranda post?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/89ccee3a-8afa-4df3-a9e4-ce418915c497" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hall’s Cottage was built around 1832-1833 on 200-acre property Henry Hall obtained as part of his original allocation. There is no record of the construction of the cottage, though it is likely that it was built with the assistance of the tradesmen and servants originally brought out on the Protector. The five roomed cottage is a perfect example of a pioneer home built entirely from local materials and represents an early European settlers cottage. 
Over the  years several families leased or lived in the house: 
Henry Edward Hall lived in the cottage, which he called “Dedallumup” until 1836. 
It was leased to Thomas Watson, an original pioneer- surveyor, the first mail contractor and ferry keeper. 
In 1843 John and Eleanor Sutton rented a small block of Henry E Halls property and occupied the cottage. John Sutton spent money on improvements to Halls Cottage. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/94276232-3599-4262-a66f-87e112b844d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Region of Peel. The Peel region is known for it’s beautiful waterways, dolphins, beaches, holidays blue manner crabs and canal lifestyles. Many don’t know about the history side come with me on a journey to visit a few of these sites. Most of the destinations are reached by driving and a small amount of walking. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.538733" lon="115.713967">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjjPw-05</name>
  <desc>HistoryOfPeelS5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel</url>
  <urlname>History of Peel : S5 The farm by PollyMichele</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-770" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>History of Peel : S5 The farm</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>PollyMichele</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">PollyMichele</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;History of Peel&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The farm&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/HistoryofPeel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many original buildings remain on the farm site?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/32326e24-322f-49da-b8be-85222182311e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1839 the Sutton family, consisting of John Sutton, his wife Eleanor and son Henry, left Ireland and arrived on the WA coast aboard the ship the Hindoo. John Sutton came as indentured servant to James Tate and worked for him until 1843 farming the Peel region. 
John Sutton died in 1857 followed by his son Henry in 1861. This left his wife Eleanor Sutton the difficult job of managing the farm, inn and ferry service. She wrote home for help from relatives in Ireland and her nephew also called Henry Sutton was sent over in 1862. 
Eleanor died in 1868 leaving the Sutton estate to her nephew Henry who married Jane Mclarty in 1866. They went on to have 10 children and built a homestead and other buildings on the Sutton farm. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/94276232-3599-4262-a66f-87e112b844d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Region of Peel. The Peel region is known for it’s beautiful waterways, dolphins, beaches, holidays blue manner crabs and canal lifestyles. Many don’t know about the history side come with me on a journey to visit a few of these sites. Most of the destinations are reached by driving and a small amount of walking. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.661083" lon="117.121283">
  <ele>311.0</ele>
