   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Scenic Dampier&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Hearson’s Cove&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ab4fdcbc-6e03-46b4-af9e-9f0408cc77c3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ab4fdcbc-6e03-46b4-af9e-9f0408cc77c3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What product was Francis Gregory assessing the area for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f6eaed48-5573-4d9e-ad1e-0c8845ab82bd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This will bring you to the beautiful location of Hearson’s Cove, another favourite of mine ??.  The beach is made up of shells and pebbles.
You can view the famous ‘Staircase to the Moon’, which is a spectacular phenomenon in the northern region of WA, here occurring on full moon dates between March and November.

There is a plaque up near the rocks that will give you your answer.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dddf275f-9ca1-4ea2-9275-813beac4b7c1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Dampier, a small coastal port town in the Pilbara region of Western Australia.
Dampier is rich in history, Indigenous culture, mineral, marine and natural resources.
Dampier was constructed in 1965 as a port and processing centre for Hamersley Iron’s inland Pilbara iron ore mines. The port was opened when the first iron ore from Mount Tom Price was transported by Hamersley railway to Parker Point for shipment in 1966.
Five decades later Dampier Port is the second largest bulk export port in the world, shipping iron ore, liquefied gas, salt and anhydrous ammonia and also other cargo.
There are 42 islands off the coast of Dampier as well as outcrops and islets that make up the Dampier Archipelago and is home to several endangered species.
The Dampier region has over 3,700 hours of annual sunshine making it one of the sunniest places in Australia.

I ?? this town for it’s beautiful raw scenery, the sunshine, warm water and there is plenty to see and do around the area. What’s not to love
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
