   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Tomato Lake Walk Trail &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tragedies and Triumphs&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4d2d2149-79c1-455f-9e8b-7a72c1fa25b6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4d2d2149-79c1-455f-9e8b-7a72c1fa25b6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The teenage girl who unfortunately drowned here was playing in what sort of canoe?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e02d98e5-6b69-4af1-88e9-4c2922bcab94" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;All areas have a history and Tomato Lake is no exception. The interpretative signage at this location outlines various examples of some of the past tragedies and triumphs associated with this area. It makes for interesting reading! &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/93a6c83f-60dc-401c-8fbe-8dda4ed5cf10" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Tomato Lake Walk Trail winds its way among shady trees around the perimeter of this peaceful urban oasis in Kewdale. The dual-use pathway provides a safe walk-friendly environment for people of all ages as well as an opportunity to be awed by the abundance of birdlife on and around the lake.

Enjoy a short walk around (half) the lake and marvel at the breeding colony of Australian White ibises as you cross the boardwalk bridge. 

There is a mystery bonus cache for those interested in such things.







&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.967283" lon="115.81835">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2022-08-29T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEzAb-01</name>
  <desc>KidsBridgeS1Kin</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/649b8218-82d2-4dc1-a7b0-180e38d7829d</url>
  <urlname>Kids' Bridge : S1 Kings Park End by olthomas</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-513" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kids' Bridge : S1 Kings Park End</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>olthomas</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">olthomas</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kids' Bridge&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Kings Park End&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/649b8218-82d2-4dc1-a7b0-180e38d7829d"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/649b8218-82d2-4dc1-a7b0-180e38d7829d&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the sign facing you as you exit the bridge what kind of lily is shown? (one word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea936a61-3502-40f1-a512-289fc574c803" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You should find yourself at the Kings Park side of the bridge.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1d9a47a0-7f75-4a8e-a0af-b47e4401c608" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Kids' Bridge, also named Koolangka Bridge meaning 'children' in Noongar, officially opened on 4 August 2021.

The bridge is providing families and children using facilities on the Queen Elizabeth II Medical Centre campus the opportunity to escape the rigours of their hospital journey by safely visiting Kings Park. Clinicians strongly agree that providing the opportunity to visit places like Kings Park is positive for the overall well-being of unwell children and their families.??

The three metre wide, 217-metre long pedestrian bridge over Winthrop Avenue in Nedlands connects to the lawn area located between Perth Children’s Hospital (PCH) and the multi-story carpark on the Queen Elizabeth II Medical Centre grounds to Kings Park.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
