 <wpt lat="-32.690567" lon="116.026833">
  <ele>270.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LByHVg-01</name>
  <desc>MarrinupNo16161</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc</url>
  <urlname> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S1 Italian Compound by pommieparkin2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909984" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S1 Italian Compound</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pommieparkin2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pommieparkin2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt; Marrinup No 16 POW Camp&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Italian Compound&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Why didn't the prisoners have any Italian literature to read?(3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/415ebcc3-5b71-42ca-8e25-3d62ef63b6df" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Every Italian prisoner of war at Marrinup was a Catholic. Visiting Red cross Inspector Dr Morrell commented on the importance of attending to their spiritual welfare. There were no priests among the prisoners at Marrinup and a request was sent to the Apostolic Delegate for an Italian speaking priest. Mass was held in the Mess Hall on a monthly basis.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b9f0635-d917-440a-a204-9f359d8efaf6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One POW camp and 30 control centres were located in WA.  The POW Camp, Number 16 Prisoner of War Compound and Garrison, was built at Marrinup, near Dwellingup, to provide farm labour and cut firewood for the State capital, Perth.  It was approximately  1 hectare in size and some of the building foundations can still be seen today.

The  Camp  took its first  prisoners  in August 1943 and released its last in April,1946. It was built to accommodate 1,200 men, including Army  personnel,  and thousands of prisoners passed through its gates. Most were Italian and German, who were put into separate compounds in accordance with the Geneva Convention.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.691333" lon="116.027733">
  <ele>258.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LByHVg-05</name>
  <desc>MarrinupNo16165</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc</url>
  <urlname> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S5 The Guard Towers by pommieparkin2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910085" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S5 The Guard Towers</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pommieparkin2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pommieparkin2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt; Marrinup No 16 POW Camp&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Guard Towers&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the name of the Italian POW still at large 9 days after escaping from the camp? (include punctuation)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1708d633-57e9-46fe-99fc-00e969954103" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The camp was surrounded by a high wire fence with triple concertina barbed wire strategically placed outside.
Powerful floodlights lit the area and six watch towers guarded the perimeter. The watch towers were built on top of sawn off trees - the bases of some, complete with holes for the step boards, can still be seen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b9f0635-d917-440a-a204-9f359d8efaf6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One POW camp and 30 control centres were located in WA.  The POW Camp, Number 16 Prisoner of War Compound and Garrison, was built at Marrinup, near Dwellingup, to provide farm labour and cut firewood for the State capital, Perth.  It was approximately  1 hectare in size and some of the building foundations can still be seen today.

The  Camp  took its first  prisoners  in August 1943 and released its last in April,1946. It was built to accommodate 1,200 men, including Army  personnel,  and thousands of prisoners passed through its gates. Most were Italian and German, who were put into separate compounds in accordance with the Geneva Convention.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.6914" lon="116.02715">
  <ele>263.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LByHVg-02</name>
  <desc>MarrinupNo16162</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc</url>
  <urlname> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S2 Craftsmen At Work by pommieparkin2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909986" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S2 Craftsmen At Work</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pommieparkin2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pommieparkin2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt; Marrinup No 16 POW Camp&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Craftsmen At Work&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a wooden sign close by what are the 2 letters before the word MESS? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/afcb64a5-c124-4590-a883-5ec4ce02b9c8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Skilled Italian stone masonry is one of the lasting legacies remaining on site at Marrinup. Despite the passage of time, two major wildfires and reclamation of this site by the forest, the stonework remains distinct. Renowned for excellence in stone masonry and carpentry Italian prisoners worked with dedication on these projects such as a goldfish pond and shaped flower beds.
Evidence of these skills extends far beyond the perimeters of this POW camp. St Francis Xavier convent at Wandering was built by these artisans, quarrying and cutting the stone by hand.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b9f0635-d917-440a-a204-9f359d8efaf6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One POW camp and 30 control centres were located in WA.  The POW Camp, Number 16 Prisoner of War Compound and Garrison, was built at Marrinup, near Dwellingup, to provide farm labour and cut firewood for the State capital, Perth.  It was approximately  1 hectare in size and some of the building foundations can still be seen today.

The  Camp  took its first  prisoners  in August 1943 and released its last in April,1946. It was built to accommodate 1,200 men, including Army  personnel,  and thousands of prisoners passed through its gates. Most were Italian and German, who were put into separate compounds in accordance with the Geneva Convention.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
