   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt; Marrinup No 16 POW Camp&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Solitary Confinement&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;how long were the sentences in the detention Area? (exactly as on the information board)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f37d1eb7-d7e0-4c15-a65a-9ea5dbd7f33b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The worn concrete floor of the detention area indicates that many Marrinup prisoners experienced confinement here. 
Political extremists were considered unsuitable for Marrinup and serious offences were dealt with by Court Martial. Camp life was however not free from tension and discontent.
German and Italian prisoners were confined in separate barracks. Each cell had a door and a window. Escape or refusal to obey a lawful order was the main cause of long detention&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b9f0635-d917-440a-a204-9f359d8efaf6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One POW camp and 30 control centres were located in WA.  The POW Camp, Number 16 Prisoner of War Compound and Garrison, was built at Marrinup, near Dwellingup, to provide farm labour and cut firewood for the State capital, Perth.  It was approximately  1 hectare in size and some of the building foundations can still be seen today.

The  Camp  took its first  prisoners  in August 1943 and released its last in April,1946. It was built to accommodate 1,200 men, including Army  personnel,  and thousands of prisoners passed through its gates. Most were Italian and German, who were put into separate compounds in accordance with the Geneva Convention.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.693317" lon="116.027333">
  <ele>268.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LByHVg-04</name>
  <desc>MarrinupNo16164</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc</url>
  <urlname> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S4 Flower Gardens by pommieparkin2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909990" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name> Marrinup No 16 POW Camp : S4 Flower Gardens</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pommieparkin2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pommieparkin2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt; Marrinup No 16 POW Camp&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Flower Gardens&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9fcf4863-e28a-4028-8ab7-c4e50295dbcc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;what flowers were grown in the flower gardens(3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4ba00d11-be8e-4b0e-b180-3fe31d90e092" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Prisoners were grateful for any break from the monotony. They threw themselves into activities that nourished the sense of their homelands. Italian prisoners made their own vermiclli and macaroni and supplemented their diet with vegetables grown in the camp's vegetable garden. German prisoners made their own sauerkraut and borscht and spent time mending and remaking their uniforms.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b9f0635-d917-440a-a204-9f359d8efaf6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One POW camp and 30 control centres were located in WA.  The POW Camp, Number 16 Prisoner of War Compound and Garrison, was built at Marrinup, near Dwellingup, to provide farm labour and cut firewood for the State capital, Perth.  It was approximately  1 hectare in size and some of the building foundations can still be seen today.

The  Camp  took its first  prisoners  in August 1943 and released its last in April,1946. It was built to accommodate 1,200 men, including Army  personnel,  and thousands of prisoners passed through its gates. Most were Italian and German, who were put into separate compounds in accordance with the Geneva Convention.  &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.780683" lon="116.017133">
  <ele>18.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBFCkH-05</name>
  <desc>LTheStoryOfYag5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509</url>
  <urlname>[L] The Story of Yagan : S5 Return of Yagan by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-230" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] The Story of Yagan : S5 Return of Yagan</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Story of Yagan&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Return of Yagan&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Derbari Yerrigan is another name for what river&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5dc96c33-279a-42ce-a9b2-f343fc6fcbff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1990 a request by the Western Australian Nyoongah community was put to the British Government to exhume the head. Permission was finally granted.
164 years after it was sent to England Yagan's head was brought back to Australia for burial according to Nyoongah custom.
However due to the uncertainty of the whereabouts of the rest of the body, burial has not yet taken place.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a5ab75c8-83bd-4c13-bccb-2a752a4f2be4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Yagan memorial.

“I would like to acknowledge the traditional owners the Whadjuk Noongars of the lands on which we meet today and the Elders, past and present. 
This is the story of Yagan known as a Noongar leader and resistance fighter.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.780283" lon="116.017033">
  <ele>30.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBFCkH-01</name>
  <desc>LTheStoryOfYag1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509</url>
  <urlname>[L] The Story of Yagan : S1 Colours by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-226" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] The Story of Yagan : S1 Colours</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Story of Yagan&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Colours&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/775a8e5c-f81c-4941-8524-66b0d69e1509&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many yellow 1/2 circles do you see?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c0100f31-d596-4102-a544-c40fc3bb841f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1829, when Captain Stirling established the Swan River Colony, Yagan was probably in his thirties with a wife and two children.
In the early days of the settlement, Yagan was friendly with a number of the settlers. However, in1830, misunderstandings and tensions arose between the Nyoongah people and the new pioneers.
An incident concerning the shooting and death of a young Aboriginal boy who had entered a homestead near Melville caused a serious rift between the settlers and the local Aboriginal
community. Under Aboriginal law the boy's death had to be avenged&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a5ab75c8-83bd-4c13-bccb-2a752a4f2be4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Yagan memorial.

“I would like to acknowledge the traditional owners the Whadjuk Noongars of the lands on which we meet today and the Elders, past and present. 
This is the story of Yagan known as a Noongar leader and resistance fighter.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
