   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Historic Cue&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Railway Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many tall windows do you see from the roadside looking towards the building?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/07827bf6-9fce-4e1b-8ec1-8f549b1a51f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia's first Premier, Sir John Forest, promised a railway line to Cue in 1894, but it was not until 1897 that he, his wife and a 100 special guests arrived on the first train from Perth. 
The building is still being restored and looks out onto a sports oval.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8b2e090c-0e7a-4a63-8ab3-64b44f62170f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Shire of Cue is 120kms south of Meekatharra and 80kms north of Mt Magnet, right in the middle of the beautiful outback. As you make your way into town you are struck by the heritage of the gold rush town lying in every building along the main street.
Cue was named after Tom Cue, an early prospector who became a major figure in the town's development. 
There is much to explore around Cue and this Adventure Lab will only give you a small snippet into the town's very rich goldmining history.  
If you have some time, travel out to Day Dawn and Big Bell both of whom were once thriving gold mining towns close to Cue. Sadly, now only ghost towns to explore.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-27.42355" lon="117.8981">
  <ele>457.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBuDHp-05</name>
  <desc>ExploringHisto5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Historic Cue : S5 The Rotunda by allinaday</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910000" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Historic Cue : S5 The Rotunda</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>allinaday</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">allinaday</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Historic Cue&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Rotunda&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What shape is the Rotunda?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/36dafd06-08f6-4e11-869f-98969013f85f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Victorian style rotunda was built in 1904 on the site of an old well, which was reputed to have started a typhoid epidemic.  The drinking fountain in the centre was added in 1934.  
For many years the town band played here on Saturday evenings.  
The Rotunda is of considerable architectural value, as there are few structures of this type remaining in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8b2e090c-0e7a-4a63-8ab3-64b44f62170f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Shire of Cue is 120kms south of Meekatharra and 80kms north of Mt Magnet, right in the middle of the beautiful outback. As you make your way into town you are struck by the heritage of the gold rush town lying in every building along the main street.
Cue was named after Tom Cue, an early prospector who became a major figure in the town's development. 
There is much to explore around Cue and this Adventure Lab will only give you a small snippet into the town's very rich goldmining history.  
If you have some time, travel out to Day Dawn and Big Bell both of whom were once thriving gold mining towns close to Cue. Sadly, now only ghost towns to explore.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-27.423133" lon="117.8992">
  <ele>459.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBuDHp-03</name>
  <desc>ExploringHisto3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689</url>
  <urlname>Exploring Historic Cue : S3 Old Post Office by allinaday</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476909996" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Exploring Historic Cue : S3 Old Post Office</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>allinaday</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">allinaday</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Exploring Historic Cue&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Old Post Office&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/abe1abf0-4cfe-4053-9ff7-38eb89ead689&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When was this building built?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e6a5444-976b-4750-9e5a-fdac4291a762" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The town clock is a prominent feature on the tower at the front of the former Post Office.  This had been promised by Sir John Forrest when he arrived for the opening of the railway line in 1897, but was not installed until February 1898 after alterations were made to the Post Office to accommodate the clock tower.  The building now houses the Information Centre.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8b2e090c-0e7a-4a63-8ab3-64b44f62170f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Shire of Cue is 120kms south of Meekatharra and 80kms north of Mt Magnet, right in the middle of the beautiful outback. As you make your way into town you are struck by the heritage of the gold rush town lying in every building along the main street.
Cue was named after Tom Cue, an early prospector who became a major figure in the town's development. 
There is much to explore around Cue and this Adventure Lab will only give you a small snippet into the town's very rich goldmining history.  
If you have some time, travel out to Day Dawn and Big Bell both of whom were once thriving gold mining towns close to Cue. Sadly, now only ghost towns to explore.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-27.422717" lon="117.899483">
