  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010</url>
  <urlname>National Trust Trees - WA : S3 Morton Bay Fig - individual tree by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476912246" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>National Trust Trees - WA : S3 Morton Bay Fig - individual tree</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;National Trust Trees - WA&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Morton Bay Fig - individual tree&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many turned white wooden posts are on the front of Kirkman House?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b921439-3bed-4c09-880b-80dd87455d89" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scientific name: Ficus Macrophylla. This tree is 120+ years old with a 3m diameter and height of 36m.
Significance: the tree is a landmark in the street and is associated with the historical buildings of Royal Perth Hospital.
History: the tree was planted between 1890 and the early 1900’s although the exact date and person/persons are unknown it is a dominant feature and dictates the flow of traffic. It is also known as the Kissing Tree as the nurses from the nearby hospital would say goodbye to their sweethearts before entering their quarters.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c378ab2c-ff17-4c4b-b2ae-c27b615e8f45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The National Trusts of Australia are community based, non government organisations, committed to promoting and conserving Australia’s indigenous, natural and historic heritage places and objects. The National Trusts of Australia are collaborating to create a national register of significant trees, which is consistent with their mission to protect and celebrate Australia’s heritage. There are over 2,500 significant tree records compiled over thirty years across Australia. 
This adventure lab focuses on five trees 100+ years of age. 
Jarrah Tree - Armadale: Historic value; at 1500+ years, this tree reflects the great age of the species and is a significant remnant of the period prior to European colonisation.
Scientific value; the girth and age of this Eucalyptus margin at a makes it valuable as a benchmark site with potential to contribute to future research. Unfortunately it has been significantly impacted by development and vandalism. 
Ref: Register of Historic Places.wa.gov.au/Trust Trees.org.au&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
