   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;National Trust Trees - WA&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Plane Trees - avenue&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is inscribed in the central chapel style plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0d44c807-f6c7-432e-bb15-5715740bf396" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scientific name: Platanus orientalis and Platanus X acerifolia. The 24 trees are 110+ years old with a diameter of 0.9m and stand 24m high. 
Significance: the trees are in excellent condition for the species and contribut strongly to the streetscape of Mounts Bay Road where they edge the curved road and contrast strongly with the open riverside of the road. 
History: it has been estimated the trees were planted around 1910.
The trees shade an unpaved carpark with a natural spring.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c378ab2c-ff17-4c4b-b2ae-c27b615e8f45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The National Trusts of Australia are community based, non government organisations, committed to promoting and conserving Australia’s indigenous, natural and historic heritage places and objects. The National Trusts of Australia are collaborating to create a national register of significant trees, which is consistent with their mission to protect and celebrate Australia’s heritage. There are over 2,500 significant tree records compiled over thirty years across Australia. 
This adventure lab focuses on five trees 100+ years of age. 
Jarrah Tree - Armadale: Historic value; at 1500+ years, this tree reflects the great age of the species and is a significant remnant of the period prior to European colonisation.
Scientific value; the girth and age of this Eucalyptus margin at a makes it valuable as a benchmark site with potential to contribute to future research. Unfortunately it has been significantly impacted by development and vandalism. 
Ref: Register of Historic Places.wa.gov.au/Trust Trees.org.au&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
