   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;National Trust Trees - WA&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Morton Bay Fig - individual tree&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a monument nearby, what is the date of death? (as written)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f487634d-8ba9-4032-b27a-9fefce549265" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Botanical name: Ficus macrophilla; 130+ years of age with a height of 11m and 1m diameter.
Significance: Proclamation tree; commemorative tree planted in 1890 by Governor H.E. Sir William Robinson to mark granting of responsible government of the WA colony. The stone and memorial plaque associated with the tree are also significant. 
Unfortunately the plaque is missing but an information sign is in place. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c378ab2c-ff17-4c4b-b2ae-c27b615e8f45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The National Trusts of Australia are community based, non government organisations, committed to promoting and conserving Australia’s indigenous, natural and historic heritage places and objects. The National Trusts of Australia are collaborating to create a national register of significant trees, which is consistent with their mission to protect and celebrate Australia’s heritage. There are over 2,500 significant tree records compiled over thirty years across Australia. 
This adventure lab focuses on five trees 100+ years of age. 
Jarrah Tree - Armadale: Historic value; at 1500+ years, this tree reflects the great age of the species and is a significant remnant of the period prior to European colonisation.
Scientific value; the girth and age of this Eucalyptus margin at a makes it valuable as a benchmark site with potential to contribute to future research. Unfortunately it has been significantly impacted by development and vandalism. 
Ref: Register of Historic Places.wa.gov.au/Trust Trees.org.au&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
