   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;National Trust Trees - WA&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Norfolk Island Pine - avenue&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number on the lamppost at the GZ (alpha and numeric)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1267fac6-b300-452d-af81-913dd01337b2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Botanical name: Araucaria heterophylla. There are 52 trees, aged 120+ with an average girth of 0.9m.  
Significance: the area was planted by two residents of John Street, William Zimpel and John Doscas in 1905. 
The trees are of outstanding aesthetic quality and occur in a unique location or provides a significant contribution to the landscape.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c378ab2c-ff17-4c4b-b2ae-c27b615e8f45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The National Trusts of Australia are community based, non government organisations, committed to promoting and conserving Australia’s indigenous, natural and historic heritage places and objects. The National Trusts of Australia are collaborating to create a national register of significant trees, which is consistent with their mission to protect and celebrate Australia’s heritage. There are over 2,500 significant tree records compiled over thirty years across Australia. 
This adventure lab focuses on five trees 100+ years of age. 
Jarrah Tree - Armadale: Historic value; at 1500+ years, this tree reflects the great age of the species and is a significant remnant of the period prior to European colonisation.
Scientific value; the girth and age of this Eucalyptus margin at a makes it valuable as a benchmark site with potential to contribute to future research. Unfortunately it has been significantly impacted by development and vandalism. 
Ref: Register of Historic Places.wa.gov.au/Trust Trees.org.au&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
