 <wpt lat="-31.607283" lon="116.452833">
  <ele>316.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKVBr-04</name>
  <desc>NationalTrustT4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010</url>
  <urlname>National Trust Trees - WA : S4 Powderbark - individual tree by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476912248" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>National Trust Trees - WA : S4 Powderbark - individual tree</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;National Trust Trees - WA&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Powderbark - individual tree&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0da0279d-831e-4d0b-aaba-f9eea0351010&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is written on the nearby traffic sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/65557667-fe56-4ba7-be3f-c6f626d478d7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scientific name: Eucalyptus accedens. This tree is 400+ years old with a 1m diameter and height of 19m.
Significance: this tree has horticultural value due to its great age, the seeds are also important as a gene pool resource, it’s outstanding height, circumference and canopy spread. It’s colourful bark, highlighted by the rays of sunshine makes a significant aesthetic contribution to the immediate landscape of the street. 
History: it’s age at 420+ years means that it would have been 25 years old at the time the Dutch explorer Willem Janos discovered Australia in 1606. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c378ab2c-ff17-4c4b-b2ae-c27b615e8f45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The National Trusts of Australia are community based, non government organisations, committed to promoting and conserving Australia’s indigenous, natural and historic heritage places and objects. The National Trusts of Australia are collaborating to create a national register of significant trees, which is consistent with their mission to protect and celebrate Australia’s heritage. There are over 2,500 significant tree records compiled over thirty years across Australia. 
This adventure lab focuses on five trees 100+ years of age. 
Jarrah Tree - Armadale: Historic value; at 1500+ years, this tree reflects the great age of the species and is a significant remnant of the period prior to European colonisation.
Scientific value; the girth and age of this Eucalyptus margin at a makes it valuable as a benchmark site with potential to contribute to future research. Unfortunately it has been significantly impacted by development and vandalism. 
Ref: Register of Historic Places.wa.gov.au/Trust Trees.org.au&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-29.5373" lon="115.761767">
  <ele>255.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB2FzV-05</name>
  <desc>ThreeSpringsLA5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a1450509-9868-41c7-8e5d-3eb6b63a07ca</url>
  <urlname>The Three Springs LAB : S5 Three Springs Caravan Park by Team Red Roo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-741" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Three Springs LAB : S5 Three Springs Caravan Park</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Team Red Roo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Team Red Roo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Three Springs LAB&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Three Springs Caravan Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a1450509-9868-41c7-8e5d-3eb6b63a07ca"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a1450509-9868-41c7-8e5d-3eb6b63a07ca&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many letters in 'Caravan'&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/786325fc-d70e-4992-b36b-4d5c30e8b09e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;While the actual caravan sites are around / under the trees at the end of the Swimming Pool, I have used this location to point out the showers/toilet area. 

Rumor has it that the ranger only attends one day per week. There are no powered sites for your van but there are free showers and toilets attached to the Swimming Pool (see coordinates).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8443c525-557a-4048-818b-e4c4084eaee0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Lab Cache can be done in any order and has been placed to introduce you to some of the more interesting points in &amp; around Three Springs. The town has a reserve area between the railway line and the main road - and much of it allows for parking of cars &amp; caravans. 

The Midlands Road passes through the center of town and also holds most of the commercial businesses (Hotel, Post Office, Shops, Shire Offices etc). Other roads service towns to the west and east. All points of this Lab are caravan friendly - so if you're towing you can stay hitched. 

IIRC - the Midlands Road used to be called The Great Northern Highway, but lost that title when the Brand Highway was opened. Up until then it was the main road between Perth and Geraldton. 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
