   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Three Springs LAB&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Three Springs Police Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a1450509-9868-41c7-8e5d-3eb6b63a07ca"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a1450509-9868-41c7-8e5d-3eb6b63a07ca&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many letters in the word 'Police'&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/38370b95-ffe9-4fca-a6cc-6ed2fa06a2f0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;While you're in town, keep in mind that the Police are active and do their best to keep crime (of all kinds) down. The speed limit in town is 50 kmh and the Police are active with traffic as well and holding regular road blocks - checking driver and vehicle licences and often - vehicle roadworthiness.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8443c525-557a-4048-818b-e4c4084eaee0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Lab Cache can be done in any order and has been placed to introduce you to some of the more interesting points in &amp; around Three Springs. The town has a reserve area between the railway line and the main road - and much of it allows for parking of cars &amp; caravans. 

The Midlands Road passes through the center of town and also holds most of the commercial businesses (Hotel, Post Office, Shops, Shire Offices etc). Other roads service towns to the west and east. All points of this Lab are caravan friendly - so if you're towing you can stay hitched. 

IIRC - the Midlands Road used to be called The Great Northern Highway, but lost that title when the Brand Highway was opened. Up until then it was the main road between Perth and Geraldton. 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.721467" lon="116.502333">
  <ele>256.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-25T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB4sSn-01</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin11</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f3813ddb-b480-490c-838d-302b6fd57364</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail:  Clackline : S1 No 1 Bricks by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910002" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail:  Clackline : S1 No 1 Bricks</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail:  Clackline&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No 1 Bricks&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f3813ddb-b480-490c-838d-302b6fd57364"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f3813ddb-b480-490c-838d-302b6fd57364&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was clay first found?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/612794e0-ce2a-4096-8891-102986ab13a4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bricks from Clackline Refractory, now closed, were used in the factory that produced pipes for the water supply scheme and in the boiler room at No 1 Pump Station.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/04c76173-e736-468d-8b52-0d7f1b9bd58c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Clackline was an important junction of three railway lines – remains of a turntable and platforms are reminders. Bricks from Clackline Refractory, now closed, were used in the factory that produced pipes for the water supply scheme and in the boiler room at No 1 Pump Station.

A small village sprung up at Clackline during the late 1880s at the terminus of the road to Newcastle (now Toodyay) and railway. A hotel was established in about 1890 and a school and Methodist Church in 1897. Clackline Brook was dammed to provide water for the railways.

The discovery of fine quality clay for fire bricks in 1898 consolidated Clackline’s existence and a short-lived company was established to manufacture them. It was restarted in 1901 and continued operation until the 1950s. Bricks made from Clackline clay used to insulate the economizer remain in situ in No 1 Pump Station today.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
