 <wpt lat="-32.0574" lon="115.743117">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZsCH-01</name>
  <desc>WesternAustral1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65</url>
  <urlname>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S1 A Trotman Pattern Anchor by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-732" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S1 A Trotman Pattern Anchor</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Western Australian Shipwreck Museum&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A Trotman Pattern Anchor&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the name of the crayfishing boat referred to on the plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fb645ebf-5cab-4d31-9e9d-6b5fac061ff7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No ship would be complete without an anchor.  An example of a Trotman anchor can be found on the corner of the courtyard fronting the museum. This type of anchor was noted for  its compact nature and its strong holding power compared to earlier types of anchors.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/70cbc252-3d08-488b-af97-2c822f48a868" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With almost 21,000 kms of coastline, it is little wonder that shipwrecks feature prominently in the history of Western Australia. Much of this maritime history is on show at the WA Shipwreck Museum in the heart of beautiful Fremantle. Housed in a restored 1850's-era Commissariat building, this museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the Southern Hemisphere. 

The Adlab begins in the grounds outside the museum and continues through various themed  galleries inside. 

NB: Entry to the museum is free though a small donation is appreciated. The museum is generally open between 9.30am and 5pm each day.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0573" lon="115.74275">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZsCH-02</name>
  <desc>WesternAustral2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65</url>
  <urlname>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S2 The Eglinton Anchor by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-733" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S2 The Eglinton Anchor</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Western Australian Shipwreck Museum&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Eglinton Anchor&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In which month of 1852 was the Eglinton wrecked?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27119e96-c336-4f1c-b603-05eaa833a968" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you are standing close to the entry to the museum in front of the anchor from the Eglinton barque, you are in the right spot. This ship was wrecked off the coast of Wanneroo in 1852 on a voyage from London to Fremantle. Beneath the anchor is a brass plaque with the information required to answer the question.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/70cbc252-3d08-488b-af97-2c822f48a868" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With almost 21,000 kms of coastline, it is little wonder that shipwrecks feature prominently in the history of Western Australia. Much of this maritime history is on show at the WA Shipwreck Museum in the heart of beautiful Fremantle. Housed in a restored 1850's-era Commissariat building, this museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the Southern Hemisphere. 

The Adlab begins in the grounds outside the museum and continues through various themed  galleries inside. 

NB: Entry to the museum is free though a small donation is appreciated. The museum is generally open between 9.30am and 5pm each day.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.057167" lon="115.742717">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZsCH-03</name>
  <desc>WesternAustral8</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65</url>
  <urlname>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S3 The Batavia [Gallery 8] by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-734" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S3 The Batavia [Gallery 8]</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Western Australian Shipwreck Museum&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Batavia [Gallery 8]&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;For how many years were these timbers immersed?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9a5d19a8-0223-452f-85a7-c93d570e708f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Batavia was a ship of the Dutch East India Company that was wrecked on the Houtman Abrolhos islands. The tale of the Batavia shipwreck and subsequent mutiny and massacre  make for gruesome reading.
Make your way inside the museum to the Batavia gallery where some of the original timbers of the Batavia as well as cargo, replica portico and a skeleton of a crew member who met a fateful death are all masterfully displayed. If you are standing in front of a small information board entitled "Batavia timbers", you are in the right spot.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/70cbc252-3d08-488b-af97-2c822f48a868" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With almost 21,000 kms of coastline, it is little wonder that shipwrecks feature prominently in the history of Western Australia. Much of this maritime history is on show at the WA Shipwreck Museum in the heart of beautiful Fremantle. Housed in a restored 1850's-era Commissariat building, this museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the Southern Hemisphere. 

The Adlab begins in the grounds outside the museum and continues through various themed  galleries inside. 

NB: Entry to the museum is free though a small donation is appreciated. The museum is generally open between 9.30am and 5pm each day.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.057083" lon="115.742433">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
