  <desc>WesternAustral6</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65</url>
  <urlname>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S4 Xantho [Gallery 6] by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-735" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S4 Xantho [Gallery 6]</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Western Australian Shipwreck Museum&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Xantho [Gallery 6]&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This engine is an example of a gunboat engine from which war?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c6839d6e-65fd-4c1d-86d5-86efb35bacfe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;SS Xanthro was a steam ship used in the colony of Western Australia as a pearling transport and mothership, powered by a horizontal trunk engine This exhibit displays the conservation of the SS Xantho steam engine after more than a century under water. The information you require can be found on the info board in front of the engine.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/70cbc252-3d08-488b-af97-2c822f48a868" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With almost 21,000 kms of coastline, it is little wonder that shipwrecks feature prominently in the history of Western Australia. Much of this maritime history is on show at the WA Shipwreck Museum in the heart of beautiful Fremantle. Housed in a restored 1850's-era Commissariat building, this museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the Southern Hemisphere. 

The Adlab begins in the grounds outside the museum and continues through various themed  galleries inside. 

NB: Entry to the museum is free though a small donation is appreciated. The museum is generally open between 9.30am and 5pm each day.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.057083" lon="115.74245">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZsCH-05</name>
  <desc>WesternAustral4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65</url>
  <urlname>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S5 Duyfken model [Gallery4] by Paskersfind</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-736" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Western Australian Shipwreck Museum : S5 Duyfken model [Gallery4]</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Paskersfind</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Paskersfind</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Western Australian Shipwreck Museum&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Duyfken model [Gallery4]&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4c8a5085-39f0-48d2-b73f-b9386cfc4c65&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the surname of the Commander of this small vessel?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e82eef51-ce59-490d-a366-0805debec497" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Duyfken [little dove] was a small, fast ship built in the Dutch Republic. This ship entered the pages of Australian history when it became the first European vessel to make a recorded visit to the south land's shore in 1606.
In the glass case nearby is a replica model of the Duyfken.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/70cbc252-3d08-488b-af97-2c822f48a868" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With almost 21,000 kms of coastline, it is little wonder that shipwrecks feature prominently in the history of Western Australia. Much of this maritime history is on show at the WA Shipwreck Museum in the heart of beautiful Fremantle. Housed in a restored 1850's-era Commissariat building, this museum is recognized as the foremost maritime archaeology museum in the Southern Hemisphere. 

The Adlab begins in the grounds outside the museum and continues through various themed  galleries inside. 

NB: Entry to the museum is free though a small donation is appreciated. The museum is generally open between 9.30am and 5pm each day.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.66105" lon="116.662033">
  <ele>154.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBNizy-04</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin17</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam : S4 Old Northam Railway Station by Spark22</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910018" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam : S4 Old Northam Railway Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Spark22</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Spark22</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Old Northam Railway Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year were the train lines extended east ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6dc074d8-3832-4014-a300-c46175c64251" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Many thousands of people have walked the platform of the Old Northam Railway Station, from miners and hopefuls traveling to the Goldfields, early settlers to all parts of the Wheatbelt, and servicemen during World War Two and the Korean War. 
Officially closing in 1966 - the building is now a Railway museum open daily 11 - 3pm&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/21130ac3-43db-452d-81dc-093d6b83da95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab is part of the Golden Pipeline Trail from Mundaring to Kalgoorlie.
Northam townsite was set aside in November 1830. Northam achieved prominence with the extension of railway to the Goldfields in 1894. It became an important centre for the provision of public services such as railways, roads, and water supplies.
In 1949 after the 2nd World War - Northams Millitary Hospital and Army Camp became the  Commonwealth  Immigration Accommodation Centre accommodating
 thousands of refugees.  With the refugees later settling in Northam it gave the town a population mix quite different to most other rural towns. A number of them worked on the water supply scheme.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.65635" lon="116.6672">
  <ele>163.0</ele>
  <time>2022-09-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBNizy-05</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin18</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam : S5 The Mill by Spark22</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910020" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam : S5 The Mill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Spark22</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Spark22</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Mill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many swans are painted on the side of the mill?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7ad9467c-ab0d-452d-9ebb-568303ebd7fa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Northam Flour Mill has historical significance as a pioneer industry in the district, being the first mill in the town, built in 1867.

Turned into a canvas in 2020 inspired by the river running by it and the white swans introduced to the region in the early 1900s after being brought over by British colonists 30 years prior.

Northam is now the only location in the State where white swans survive and breed naturally.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/21130ac3-43db-452d-81dc-093d6b83da95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab is part of the Golden Pipeline Trail from Mundaring to Kalgoorlie.
Northam townsite was set aside in November 1830. Northam achieved prominence with the extension of railway to the Goldfields in 1894. It became an important centre for the provision of public services such as railways, roads, and water supplies.
In 1949 after the 2nd World War - Northams Millitary Hospital and Army Camp became the  Commonwealth  Immigration Accommodation Centre accommodating
 thousands of refugees.  With the refugees later settling in Northam it gave the town a population mix quite different to most other rural towns. A number of them worked on the water supply scheme.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
