   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Northam&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Locking bar pipe&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/231715c2-fe5d-4c55-a871-4681e643642a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did the Goldfields water supply scheme begin operating? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8bbe11bc-91e3-4d15-ba91-068cc95450d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is an original section of pipeline from the goldfields water supply scheme.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/21130ac3-43db-452d-81dc-093d6b83da95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This adventure lab is part of the Golden Pipeline Trail from Mundaring to Kalgoorlie.
Northam townsite was set aside in November 1830. Northam achieved prominence with the extension of railway to the Goldfields in 1894. It became an important centre for the provision of public services such as railways, roads, and water supplies.
In 1949 after the 2nd World War - Northams Millitary Hospital and Army Camp became the  Commonwealth  Immigration Accommodation Centre accommodating
 thousands of refugees.  With the refugees later settling in Northam it gave the town a population mix quite different to most other rural towns. A number of them worked on the water supply scheme.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
