 <wpt lat="-31.944967" lon="115.872483">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB1RBc-03</name>
  <desc>HighgateHistor3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f</url>
  <urlname>Highgate History : S3 Dilhorn by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910141" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Highgate History : S3 Dilhorn</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Highgate History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dilhorn&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What did Dilhorn serve as for over thirty years?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e07dfd0c-5fa5-4fed-9b86-0ed471eb0dd3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dilhorn has historic value for its associations with the growth and evolution of the Perth metropolitan area following the gold rush of the 1890's and, in particular the settlement and suburbanisation of the inner suburbs.

Dilhorn is associated with its first owner, William Thorley Loton, a prominent Perth identity who was involved in the commercial, cultural and political life of his times and with J. Talbot Hobbs, Dilhorn's designer, a prominent Perth architect, who was responsible for building many of Perth's grand homes at the turn of this century. It is named after his birthplace, Dilhorne in England.

Dilhorn is an elegant and confident example of a substantial Federation Queen Anne style house. The house displays many innovative and distinctive aesthetic elements that are a hallmark of J. Talbot Hobbs' domestic buildings between 1890 - 1910 and develops his architectural themes. Many of these themes were adopted in a simplified form in the mass housing market over the following twenty years.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a6f4212-17a5-4657-ae2d-1cbf9304c1f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you a brief walk around Highgate and its surrounding suburbs, to some of the unique historical sights that I am lucky enough to pass on my daily lunch break. There are some incredible homes and buildings in the area with varying architectural styles, which create such a unique suburb with incredible character.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.94485" lon="115.871717">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB1RBc-02</name>
  <desc>HighgateHistor2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f</url>
  <urlname>Highgate History : S2 Loton Park Tennis Club by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910139" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Highgate History : S2 Loton Park Tennis Club</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Highgate History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Loton Park Tennis Club&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The pavillion features what style of roof?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dcb0c51c-b35b-41dc-8b09-9cfd70633f93" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Post-indigenous occupation, Loton Park was originally owned by William Thorley Loton, Mayor of Perth (1901-2), MLC and well-known pastoralist. In 1904, William Loton sold the land to the City of Perth under the condition that it was set aside for recreational use. 

The tennis courts at this site were constructed in 1913-14 with the Loton Park Tennis Club established in 1916 and by 1917 it was granted permission to establish two additional courts.

The pavillion at the Tennis Club was erected in 1922 and is a fine example of the Interwar Arts and Craft style, to the design of Eales &amp; Cohen, foremost exponents of the style. Since construction it has undergone a minor extension and renovation to ensure longevity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a6f4212-17a5-4657-ae2d-1cbf9304c1f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you a brief walk around Highgate and its surrounding suburbs, to some of the unique historical sights that I am lucky enough to pass on my daily lunch break. There are some incredible homes and buildings in the area with varying architectural styles, which create such a unique suburb with incredible character.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.944317" lon="115.869833">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB1RBc-01</name>
  <desc>HighgateHistor1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f</url>
  <urlname>Highgate History : S1 Perth Oval by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910137" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Highgate History : S1 Perth Oval</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Highgate History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perth Oval&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which sporting club adopted the ground as its home ground in 1906?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4b353574-9c21-46d1-bc7f-6efbee32a7d2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The location of Perth Oval is a former site of indigenous occupation before being reclaimed to become lake and market garden. It was purchased by William Loton, and was offered to the City of Perth in 1904 for use as a public recreation area, known then as Loton Park. 

Perth Oval has been one of the long term premier sporting venues in metropolitan Perth from the early 1900s until the advent of more specialised sporting venues from the mid 1980s, including interstate football matches and cricket games. More recently it has been the home of Perth Glory soccer team. 

Perth Oval entry gates are one of the state's finest examples of the Interwar Mediterranean style for recreational purposes. The mature trees of the perimeter planting are representative of the Paradise landscape style of planting that is characteristic of public parks and gardens established in Perth at the turn of the century.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a6f4212-17a5-4657-ae2d-1cbf9304c1f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you a brief walk around Highgate and its surrounding suburbs, to some of the unique historical sights that I am lucky enough to pass on my daily lunch break. There are some incredible homes and buildings in the area with varying architectural styles, which create such a unique suburb with incredible character.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
