   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Highgate History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lincoln Street Ventilation Stack&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What style of architecture does the Stack represent?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2dd90c22-b46e-458f-b390-3d234b8e64b2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Lincoln Street Ventilation Stack was built to ventilate the newly introduced reticulated sewerage system in Perth and was an important step in the development and expansion of Perth’s metropolitan sewerage system during the 1930s and 1940s.

It was built by workers in receipt of sustenance payments during the Great Depression and played a major role in the Defence and Western Australian Police Service communications, as the antennae for the wireless were camoflagued in the vent structure during the World War II.

Lincoln Street Ventilation Stack was a failure in its own right. It closed after four weeks of operation when the hydrogen sulphide, which turns to sulphuric acid on contact with water, corroded the extractor fans so quickly as to make the operation of the tower financially unfeasible. Furthermore, the hydrogen sulphide, which is heavier than air, on exiting the tower dispersed to the surrounding area causing complaints from residents about the unpleasant odours.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a6f4212-17a5-4657-ae2d-1cbf9304c1f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you a brief walk around Highgate and its surrounding suburbs, to some of the unique historical sights that I am lucky enough to pass on my daily lunch break. There are some incredible homes and buildings in the area with varying architectural styles, which create such a unique suburb with incredible character.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.941633" lon="115.8709">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB1RBc-05</name>
  <desc>HighgateHistor5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f</url>
  <urlname>Highgate History : S5 Serbian Orthodox Church of Saint Sava by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910050" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Highgate History : S5 Serbian Orthodox Church of Saint Sava</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Highgate History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Serbian Orthodox Church of Saint Sava&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c922aac6-0447-415e-8440-e79b4606266f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The building has a feature that symbolises Holy Trinity, what is this feature?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/598cedbe-62a2-43e1-a700-620d01bc4ebf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Serbian Orthodox Church of Saint Sava is a fine example of the Postwar Immigrant Nostalgia style, based on the design principles of the Orthodox Church, applied to an ecclesiastical landmark. The Church demonstrates the cultural diversity of the community and provides a special place for the Serbian community, who constructed the place and use it as a place of worship and a focus of their society.

The Church was constructed in 1954 by the Serbian community, most of whom had fled Yugoslavia following World War II. It was named after Saint Sava, the first Bishop of Serbia and founder of the Serbian Orthodox Church. He is the most important saint in the Serbian Orthodox Church and the patron saint of education and medicine. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a6f4212-17a5-4657-ae2d-1cbf9304c1f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab takes you a brief walk around Highgate and its surrounding suburbs, to some of the unique historical sights that I am lucky enough to pass on my daily lunch break. There are some incredible homes and buildings in the area with varying architectural styles, which create such a unique suburb with incredible character.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.934767" lon="115.871833">
  <ele>30.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzYme-03</name>
  <desc>MtLawleyArtDec3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335</url>
  <urlname>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S3 Clarke's Building by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910036" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S3 Clarke's Building</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mt Lawley Art Deco&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Clarke's Building&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What establishment begun residing at this location in April 1999, prior to the Elford Hotel opening?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6c2d0a79-f1b7-432d-a0de-1622d5cc6980" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From as early as 1909, a wine saloon was run on Beaufort Street in this location by George H. Elford. For a period from at least 1915 to 1920 it was run by William W. Sloan and in 1929 there was a Mr H.P Best a grocer and wine &amp; spirit merchant. It is quite probable that one of these businesses held the liquor licence which was the precusor to current establishments.

Clarke's Building was erected on a site which was originally part of the Highgate Hill subdivision, which initially took place in the 1870's and was one of the first subdivisions of Perth. It was constructed in 1934 as a modern art deco commercial and retail premise. The construction of such a building at this time, in the middle of the Depression, must be considered unusual. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca7bc399-a1c4-4373-a312-535d71835830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you on a brief tour of the Mount Lawley's Beaufort Street Commercial Precinct and a surrounding landmark about one block away. The area features prominent art deco architectural style buildings throughout the neighbourhood that represent the Interwar period.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache, which is located within the Beaufort Street precinct.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.93445" lon="115.872317">
  <ele>30.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzYme-04</name>
  <desc>MtLawleyArtDec4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335</url>
  <urlname>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S4 Beaucott Buildings by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910133" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S4 Beaucott Buildings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mt Lawley Art Deco&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Beaucott Buildings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The building, prior to its refurbishment into art deco style in the 1930s, was constructed in what year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/75242cd2-722b-41a3-99eb-9459fb4be85b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Beaucott Building is a fine example of the Inter War Functionalist style, particularly in its symmetry, strong lineal emphasis, and continuous three dimensional massing, including cantilevered canopy and brick parapet, projecting lintels and relief brick banding. The obliqueness of the corner enables the south and west elevations to be viewed as a whole, emphasising the curved massing of the upper level of the façade, and contrasting glazed shop fronts at ground level. The red colour and texture of the brickwork, in contrast to the light rendered relief detailing and expanses of glass, also contributes towards its simple, aesthetic appeal.

The Beaucott Buildings as refurbished, were designed by the well known Perth architect W. G. Bennett, who was a leading exponent of the Inter War Functionalist and Art Deco styles in Western Australia at this period. It was first at the Beaufort/Walcott St intersection to be refurbished in the Inter War Functionalist style, establishing the character of the precinct.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca7bc399-a1c4-4373-a312-535d71835830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you on a brief tour of the Mount Lawley's Beaufort Street Commercial Precinct and a surrounding landmark about one block away. The area features prominent art deco architectural style buildings throughout the neighbourhood that represent the Interwar period.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache, which is located within the Beaufort Street precinct.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.934433" lon="115.87205">
  <ele>30.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzYme-02</name>
  <desc>MtLawleyArtDec2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335</url>
  <urlname>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S2 Alexander Buildings by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910034" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S2 Alexander Buildings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mt Lawley Art Deco&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Alexander Buildings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The building was adapted from what period architectural style?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b8c69334-a395-4dfc-8349-af5a49cef48f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Alexander Buildings are a substantial example of a 1938 Interwar Art Deco refurbishment and renaming of the Federation period, Broadway Building. They are a landmark at the important intersection of Walcott and Beaufort Streets. Simon Alexander was responsible for the design and construction of the Broadway Building and his son was responsible for its adaptation into the Alexander Buildings. The history, form, style and landmark qualities of the Alexander Buildings complement the Lyceum Buildings opposite, now the Astor Theatre, also designed, developed and adapted by Simon and John Alexander. These landmark buildings have long been considered the heart of the Mt Lawley town centre.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca7bc399-a1c4-4373-a312-535d71835830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you on a brief tour of the Mount Lawley's Beaufort Street Commercial Precinct and a surrounding landmark about one block away. The area features prominent art deco architectural style buildings throughout the neighbourhood that represent the Interwar period.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache, which is located within the Beaufort Street precinct.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.934217" lon="115.872267">
  <ele>31.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzYme-01</name>
  <desc>MtLawleyArtDec1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335</url>
  <urlname>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S1 Astor Theatre by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910032" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S1 Astor Theatre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mt Lawley Art Deco&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Astor Theatre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Astor Theatre was originally named &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b768a443-baae-4970-8227-6ee9d65c9ce9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Astor Theatre is a fine example of Inter-war Art Deco design and is illustrative of the resurgence of hope and prosperity between the Depression and World War II. Astor Theatre is closely associated with the Alexander family who were involved in the original construction of the building and its remodelling. It is also closely associated with architect William Leighton, who designed many of Western Australia's cinemas during the 1930s. While the authenticity of the original 1914 building is low, the authenticity of the remodelled Art Deco 1939 building is high as the main rooms - the auditorium, lobbies and stairs - are close to the original state.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca7bc399-a1c4-4373-a312-535d71835830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you on a brief tour of the Mount Lawley's Beaufort Street Commercial Precinct and a surrounding landmark about one block away. The area features prominent art deco architectural style buildings throughout the neighbourhood that represent the Interwar period.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache, which is located within the Beaufort Street precinct.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.93135" lon="115.870617">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzYme-05</name>
  <desc>MtLawleyArtDec5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335</url>
  <urlname>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S5 Mount Lawley Tennis Club (fmr) by Lachlan1.77</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910135" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Mt Lawley Art Deco : S5 Mount Lawley Tennis Club (fmr)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lachlan1.77</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Mt Lawley Art Deco&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mount Lawley Tennis Club (fmr)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0d855a22-860d-48ea-93ac-e6bc34528335&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;As part of the upgrade project, completed in the year 2000, what was installed at the site?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e877c5b-cd7a-415e-905a-02391ea758aa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Mount Lawley Tennis Club (fmr) is a fine example of modest public building in the
Inter-War Functionalist style. The building was designed by architectural firm Powell, Cameron &amp; Chisolm who were responsible for several notable buildings in Western Australia.

In 1998, the City of Stirling began a program to upgrade facilities at the Mount Lawley Bowling Club. The plans for the upgrade, by Bateman, Grundmann Wilson Architects, involved the demolition of the 1957 Function Hall and construction of a new, larger function room, a minor hall, offices, changing rooms, kitchens and stores, all to the east and south of the original Mount Lawley Tennis Club building. The extension was designed to imitate the Inter-war Functionalist architectural style original building and included curvilinear features to the new main entrance on the western elevation, matching finial details, a stepped pediment, and matching render and brickwork. The internal decoration was also designed to imitate the style.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca7bc399-a1c4-4373-a312-535d71835830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure Lab will take you on a brief tour of the Mount Lawley's Beaufort Street Commercial Precinct and a surrounding landmark about one block away. The area features prominent art deco architectural style buildings throughout the neighbourhood that represent the Interwar period.

As you answer each question at each stage of the Adventure Lab, make sure that you note the clues to locate the bonus final cache, which is located within the Beaufort Street precinct.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.962633" lon="115.84285">
  <ele>24.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBeQh5-03</name>
  <desc>KokodaTrackMem3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6</url>
  <urlname>Kokoda Track Memorial Walk : S3 Isurava by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910060" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kokoda Track Memorial Walk : S3 Isurava</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kokoda Track Memorial Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Isurava&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who was awarded the first Victoria Cross of the South-West Pacific campaign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4579044b-bc5a-4974-b43c-c59e25a518c4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Isurava was the location of the first major battle on the Kokoda Track 1150 Australian and Papuan troops came against a Japanese force nearly five times their number.

Being attacked from three fronts the Australian line held for four days before the overwhelming numbers eventually forced a retreat to Eroa Creek, however the battle halted the Japanese advance and the casualties sustained were contributing factors in their advancement south to Port Moresby being unsuccessful.


Image: VETERAN A.I.F. MEN OF A UNIT THAT FOUGHT IN THE WESTERN DESERT AND SYRIA ARE NOW IN ACTION AGAIN IN NEW GUINEA. (AWM013466 - Public Domain)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/96a72deb-548b-4bd5-b875-0f83021b9567" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Kokoda Track Memorial Walk is a tribute to the bravery of Australian troops who fought through atrocious conditions and against vastly superior numbers in the Papua New Guinea campaign of July 1942 - January 1943.

The Kokoda Trail was a path that linked Ower's Corner, approximately 40 km north-east of Port Moresby, and the small village of Wairopi, on the northern side of the Owen Stanley mountain range. Its name was derived from the village of Kokoda that stood on the northern side of the main range and was the site of the only airfield between Port Moresby and the north coast.

"Kokoda Trail" and "Kokoda Track" have been used interchangeably since the Second World War and the former was adopted by the Battles Nomenclature Committee as the official British Commonwealth battle honour in October 1957.

This memorial walk was opened in 2001 and rises 62m along 150 steps.

Upper Parking: S31 57.618 E115 50.534
Lower Parking: S31 57.793 E115 50.526
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
