   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kokoda Track Memorial Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Abuari&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Savage fighting took place between what dates? (DD _ _ _ DD MMMMMM YYYY)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/280d8ed6-fbd2-4a81-94fe-39d14e79b1cd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It was at Abuari that the Western Australian 2/16th battalion first engaged Japanese forces.

The engagement of the enemy impeded their progress and stopped encircling of Australian positions.

Over the next two weeks a fighting withdrawal slowed the Japanese advanced and helped to stop the Japanese from taking Port Moresby.


Image: Australians plod along "The Track" on the way to Buna. (AWM013620 - Public Domain)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/96a72deb-548b-4bd5-b875-0f83021b9567" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Kokoda Track Memorial Walk is a tribute to the bravery of Australian troops who fought through atrocious conditions and against vastly superior numbers in the Papua New Guinea campaign of July 1942 - January 1943.

The Kokoda Trail was a path that linked Ower's Corner, approximately 40 km north-east of Port Moresby, and the small village of Wairopi, on the northern side of the Owen Stanley mountain range. Its name was derived from the village of Kokoda that stood on the northern side of the main range and was the site of the only airfield between Port Moresby and the north coast.

"Kokoda Trail" and "Kokoda Track" have been used interchangeably since the Second World War and the former was adopted by the Battles Nomenclature Committee as the official British Commonwealth battle honour in October 1957.

This memorial walk was opened in 2001 and rises 62m along 150 steps.

Upper Parking: S31 57.618 E115 50.534
Lower Parking: S31 57.793 E115 50.526
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
