  <urlname>Kokoda Track Memorial Walk : S4 Imita Ridge by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476911164" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Kokoda Track Memorial Walk : S4 Imita Ridge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kokoda Track Memorial Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Imita Ridge&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aca2c5fb-c3c1-4a06-a761-dca21c6d3dd6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many steps make up the "Golden Stairs"?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/05001b52-4987-4703-9285-09016e2e8ffd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the site that finally halted the Japanese advance. 

Of the 1100 Australian troops of the 2/16th (Western Australia) and 2/14th (Victoria) battalions only 143 remained.
Being close to the main stores allowed for fresh troops and supplies to reinforce the Australian lines.

The Japanese force heavily depleted of supplies and troops were ordered to abandon their position and retreat to the north coast where two months earlier they had started their advance.

Australian forces pursued closely and there was limited resistance before Templeton's Crossing.


Image: 25-POUNDER GUNS OF B TROOP, 14TH FIELD REGIMENT, ROYAL AUSTRALIAN ARTILLERY, BEING PULLED THROUGH DENSE JUNGLE (AWM026851 - Public Domain)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/96a72deb-548b-4bd5-b875-0f83021b9567" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Kokoda Track Memorial Walk is a tribute to the bravery of Australian troops who fought through atrocious conditions and against vastly superior numbers in the Papua New Guinea campaign of July 1942 - January 1943.

The Kokoda Trail was a path that linked Ower's Corner, approximately 40 km north-east of Port Moresby, and the small village of Wairopi, on the northern side of the Owen Stanley mountain range. Its name was derived from the village of Kokoda that stood on the northern side of the main range and was the site of the only airfield between Port Moresby and the north coast.

"Kokoda Trail" and "Kokoda Track" have been used interchangeably since the Second World War and the former was adopted by the Battles Nomenclature Committee as the official British Commonwealth battle honour in October 1957.

This memorial walk was opened in 2001 and rises 62m along 150 steps.

Upper Parking: S31 57.618 E115 50.534
Lower Parking: S31 57.793 E115 50.526
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
