  <name>LB9Kym-05</name>
  <desc>CoogeeCapersS5T</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32</url>
  <urlname>Coogee Capers : S5 The Mighty Tuart by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-781" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coogee Capers : S5 The Mighty Tuart</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Capers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Mighty Tuart &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Tuart Trees can grow to how high according to the information board?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d44df0e4-9d06-43da-9696-3f4c24d1c355" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Tuart trees are found throughout Woodman point. Tuarts have shiny light green leaves and fibrous grey bark. These trees provide homes for birds, possums and bats. A layer of bushy shrubs and herbs beneath the tree also provide shelter to our native wildlife.
This allows Woodman point to be a highly valued refuge for wild life.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fcadfa12-a873-4c41-a962-0a7361395bb0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Woodman Point a area rich in history with hidden treasures waiting to be found. The MICKS crew have enjoyed exploring this area and would like to share some interesting areas with you through this Adventure Lab.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.12985" lon="115.759683">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB9Kym-02</name>
  <desc>CoogeeCapersS2F</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32</url>
  <urlname>Coogee Capers : S2 Forgotten Cemetry by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-778" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coogee Capers : S2 Forgotten Cemetry</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Capers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Forgotten Cemetry &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the second word on the first line of Sister O'Kane Memorial?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/997b7776-1fc0-4ad6-832d-0b4cf9b95919" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sister Rosa O'Kane trained as a nurse in Townsville and became a matron in western Queensland hospitals before World War I.
Rosa signed up to train as a battlefield nurse in 1915 and was called up in 1917. On 11 November 1918, she was bound for Europe on the SS Wyreema to serve as part of the Australian Army Nursing Service when the Armistice was declared. Following her ship’s return to Perth, she volunteered to tend to World War I servicemen suffering from pneumonic-influenza – the Spanish flu – who, while returning to Australia on board the transport ship SS Boonah, were struck down with it.
Between 1918 and 1920, the Spanish Flu plagued society. This pandemic caused more deaths than World War I.
To prevent the spread of the Spanish flu, those on board the SS Boonah were unable to disembark and reunite with family for some time. Sister O’Kane was tending to soldiers at the Woodman Point Quarantine Station to the south of Fremantle, WA, when she too became ill and died on 21 December 1918. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fcadfa12-a873-4c41-a962-0a7361395bb0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Woodman Point a area rich in history with hidden treasures waiting to be found. The MICKS crew have enjoyed exploring this area and would like to share some interesting areas with you through this Adventure Lab.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.1322" lon="115.760283">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB9Kym-01</name>
  <desc>CoogeeCapersS1E</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32</url>
  <urlname>Coogee Capers : S1 Explosive Magazine by I C Nothing</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-777" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Coogee Capers : S1 Explosive Magazine</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>I C Nothing</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">I C Nothing</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Coogee Capers&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Explosive Magazine &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a29728dc-def1-43df-8aeb-c8f306a0fd32&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Woodman Point was chosen as the ideal site for the explosives/ magazines due to its proximity to which location?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7b517320-fc42-43b7-9c2e-f50a28ab9184" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This area was once part of the Woodman Point magazine/ explosive storage depot.
It was constructed in the early 1900's and closed in 1983.
This depot was used extensively between World War 1 and 2.
The explosive magazines were set back from the beach in the sand dunes, surrounded by sandbag barriers. The red brick buildings have very thick walls and high set windows.
Remnants of these structures can still be found throughout Woodman Point today  
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fcadfa12-a873-4c41-a962-0a7361395bb0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Woodman Point a area rich in history with hidden treasures waiting to be found. The MICKS crew have enjoyed exploring this area and would like to share some interesting areas with you through this Adventure Lab.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.981017" lon="115.799667">
  <ele>29.0</ele>
  <time>2022-10-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBUkhN-05</name>
  <desc>PeaceMemorialR5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f188fa14-b315-4023-b01c-80de265b2cf9</url>
  <urlname>Peace Memorial Rose Garden : S5 Peaceful View by jinta29</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910107" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Peace Memorial Rose Garden : S5 Peaceful View</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jinta29</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jinta29</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Peace Memorial Rose Garden&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Peaceful View&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f188fa14-b315-4023-b01c-80de265b2cf9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f188fa14-b315-4023-b01c-80de265b2cf9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was Kath born?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e922fdff-f476-4d01-affb-5b46e464a9ec" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A seat dedicated to Kath Mackinnon&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/264b0418-c476-4227-9fb3-afed73ef57a7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A commemorative rose garden comprising formal rose plantings, hard and soft landscape elements, that was built over a period of years from 1948 to the early 1960s to the design of architect W.G. Bennett, and later modified in the 1970s and 1980s, has cultural heritage significance for the following reasons: 
- the place is rare as one of the earliest, and the largest and best known commemorative rose garden in Western Australia, dedicated to those who died in World War Two and to peace;  
- the rose garden was intended as a State Memorial, and commemorates service-people from throughout the State; 
- the place is highly valued by the community for social, cultural, spiritual and aesthetic reasons, as a landmark visible reminder and commemoration of the sacrifice made by those who fought in World War Two, and for its contribution to the community’s sense of place; and, it has associations with the National Rose Society, in particular past president, Charles Frost.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
