   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Swarbrick Wander &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Where the Wild Things Roam &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/930cdbde-8d95-45a5-b26f-b66ac0295e80"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/930cdbde-8d95-45a5-b26f-b66ac0295e80&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the black snake?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ebb56b0-6da6-45d5-be08-09ba3287faa7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The information for this stage is at the marker and the quote is from Peter Farmer.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1879aeaf-cd93-481d-927c-e415d1cb8813" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Swarbrick part of the Walpole Wilderness. Enjoy your walk through the bush and admire the amazing mirrored wall. This area was logged in the late 90s and there was wide spread protesting at the logging of native trees that included Yellow Tingle, a tree only found in a 50k radius of Walpole. Now we are lucky to have a beautiful, peaceful walk where you can see wildflowers and listen to the birds. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
