   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Convoy Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;First Convoy&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4e032777-9aa9-46e8-9ac2-eaeef28a5b6c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/4e032777-9aa9-46e8-9ac2-eaeef28a5b6c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the last ship on the sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f8285cd6-3e16-4685-91f1-393ab251ca00" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Convoy Walk features 21 interpretive signs giving info­rma­tion about the vessels that made up the convoy and their per­sonnel. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1e12e854-31da-4051-ac06-61d3cb90eb9b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Situated in the Albany Heritage Park, The Convoy Walk and lookout on Mount Adelaide was officially opened on 1 November 2014. The Convoy Walk leads to the Convoy Lookout with a view over King George Sound where troops gathered on ships before departing for the First World War. The walk contains 21 markers containing information about the ships in the First and Second Convoys, including the names and images of the troopships and escorts, and where they embarked troops. The lookout includes a shelter and interpretive material relating to the vessels in the convoy.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
