 <wpt lat="-31.270433" lon="119.97545">
  <ele>437.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB15mX-04</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin25</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S4 Life on the Line 3 by AZ09</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008888" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S4 Life on the Line 3</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AZ09</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AZ09</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Life on the Line 3&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;in the 3rd paragraph of Life on the line -  why do they put in a penny a week and buy this big tin of ...... ? (1 word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b2261f46-5195-436a-97e1-2dddaed25b8e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; Please see the plaque - there are three paragraphs on the right hand side.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6fd633b-228e-45ef-906a-888255005136" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The site of No 7 at Gilgai is fascinating, not despite but because all its buildings, including the pumping station, have been demolished.

The houses and school have gone, the railway line ripped up, the pumps sold for scrap metal and all that remains is the tank that received water from No 6. But a walk around the site of the former pumping station site at Gilgai (an Aboriginal place name) is a walk through time, a journey into the lives of the people who once lived and worked there, who went to the school, played tennis and held community celebrations. The buildings may have gone, but the reminders of these lives are all around. 

The end stop is all that remains of the railway line. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.270417" lon="119.975517">
  <ele>437.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB15mX-01</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelinex</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S1 Out of Exile by AZ09</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008882" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S1 Out of Exile</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AZ09</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AZ09</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Out of Exile&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;"our dear Mother sat down and wept" -  For how many years? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7d0f1fba-f3c8-40f3-82a3-442ce1de02ae" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Their absence makes it easier to appreciate why the isolated No 7 was known as Siberia and to gain respect for families who made lives here.

‘…when news of Dad’s transfer came through, our dear Mother sat down and wept…For ___ years she had gallantly fought the adversities of ‘Siberia’ (as No 7 was called), but now (with) the prospect of being close to doctors, schools and shops a reality, all her emotions came to the surface. So with great joy and anticipation we moved to No 6.’ Rose Birss (née Wall), resident 1930-36

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6fd633b-228e-45ef-906a-888255005136" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The site of No 7 at Gilgai is fascinating, not despite but because all its buildings, including the pumping station, have been demolished.

The houses and school have gone, the railway line ripped up, the pumps sold for scrap metal and all that remains is the tank that received water from No 6. But a walk around the site of the former pumping station site at Gilgai (an Aboriginal place name) is a walk through time, a journey into the lives of the people who once lived and worked there, who went to the school, played tennis and held community celebrations. The buildings may have gone, but the reminders of these lives are all around. 

The end stop is all that remains of the railway line. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.270467" lon="119.975533">
  <ele>436.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB15mX-02</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin23</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S2 Life on the Line 1 by AZ09</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008884" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S2 Life on the Line 1</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AZ09</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AZ09</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Life on the Line 1&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Uncle John made us one of the first wireless sets - what is his surname? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c4b5af6-405a-420c-9d2c-73138254183a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Please see the plaque - there are three paragraphs on the right hand side. 
 &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6fd633b-228e-45ef-906a-888255005136" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The site of No 7 at Gilgai is fascinating, not despite but because all its buildings, including the pumping station, have been demolished.

The houses and school have gone, the railway line ripped up, the pumps sold for scrap metal and all that remains is the tank that received water from No 6. But a walk around the site of the former pumping station site at Gilgai (an Aboriginal place name) is a walk through time, a journey into the lives of the people who once lived and worked there, who went to the school, played tennis and held community celebrations. The buildings may have gone, but the reminders of these lives are all around. 

The end stop is all that remains of the railway line. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
