  <name>LB15mX-05</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin26</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S5 Pipeline by AZ09</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008890" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai  : S5 Pipeline</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AZ09</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AZ09</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Gilgai &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pipeline &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/06d77e52-6adf-4ae1-aaa6-41f2cc414149&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;on right square brick - What black number is on the inside of the wall ...?  (Numbers only. Leave a space between two sets of numbers)  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dd49b267-9ec0-414c-808f-10702ed30c3f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are two square bricks for the pipeline. If you can see old plants - assume someone used to live there. 

The buildings may have gone, but the reminders of these lives are all around. Housing consisted of a line of four-room houses, all carbon copies of the one blueprint, and all constructed of tin, weatherboard and asbestos sheet. Since visitors were a novelty, and toilets an obvious necessity, there was only a dusty bike track, barely noticeable, that ran past the front of the houses, and a shady, sheltered and quite established track along the rear of houses. This serviced the row of dunnies, all in a line and 30 paces from the back doors.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6fd633b-228e-45ef-906a-888255005136" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The site of No 7 at Gilgai is fascinating, not despite but because all its buildings, including the pumping station, have been demolished.

The houses and school have gone, the railway line ripped up, the pumps sold for scrap metal and all that remains is the tank that received water from No 6. But a walk around the site of the former pumping station site at Gilgai (an Aboriginal place name) is a walk through time, a journey into the lives of the people who once lived and worked there, who went to the school, played tennis and held community celebrations. The buildings may have gone, but the reminders of these lives are all around. 

The end stop is all that remains of the railway line. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.106183" lon="120.738333">
  <ele>445.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-19T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBcYvy-01</name>
  <desc>GoldenPipelin32</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1d056ba-71e8-45e5-afe4-b3a955e1093b</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari  : S1 THE TRIP OF A LIFELINE by 21Outback</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008870" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari  : S1 THE TRIP OF A LIFELINE</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>21Outback</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">21Outback</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;THE TRIP OF A LIFELINE&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1d056ba-71e8-45e5-afe4-b3a955e1093b"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1d056ba-71e8-45e5-afe4-b3a955e1093b&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look at the picture map - there in the middle is No 8 pump station Dedari - ON left -  what is the suburb which starts with W ? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cb7dfd7b-af69-4ea8-8a4d-4a833614e0cf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a stop on the Golden Pipeline Heritage Trail. Access to the Pump Station and surrounding areas has been closed due to the presence of asbestos. The National Trust and Water Corporation are working together to ensure visitors still get to experience the incredible history of No 8 Pump Station.

 THE TRIP OF A LIFELINE

 The trail includes steam, pump stations numbered off from Mundaring Weir, the source of supply for the Goldfields Water Supply Scheme. Families at the stations referred to their homes as No 2 or No 5 and so on. No 8 was the last in line before Kalgoorlie.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ac715292-1b79-429e-9cc8-00e942b2310a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 8 Pump Station was last of the original steam powered pump stations to operate. When, in 1970, the boilers cooled and the engines were shut down for the last time, the workers simply walked out and the doors were closed. Today the pump station remains almost as it was that very day.

Dedari is the most intact of all the original pump stations. Inside there are the two boilers, the two engines and pumps, the economiser and a host of ancillary equipment and tools. Outside, the steel chimney still stands tall and the coal bin looks to be awaiting the next load of fuel.

On the northern side of the pump station building stands the timber framed and ironclad store and blacksmith shop which still contains the blacksmith’s forge. A number of other structures also remain including the suction tank, weighbridge, the weatherboard engineer’s office, and the brick venturi meter house which used to record the evenness of the pumping as well as the quantity of water sent on.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
