  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari  : S2 GOLD IN THEM THAR BRICKS by 21Outback</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99008872" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari  : S2 GOLD IN THEM THAR BRICKS</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>21Outback</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">21Outback</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail - Dedari &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;GOLD IN THEM THAR BRICKS&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1d056ba-71e8-45e5-afe4-b3a955e1093b"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b1d056ba-71e8-45e5-afe4-b3a955e1093b&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt; Look at the picture map - What are the courts there ? (6 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a98b149d-e26c-403b-8b51-1ab3c471419a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;"The Pumping Station was built for James Simpson and Co. an English Engineering firm to install their pumping engines and plant.'

"They are large horizontal, triple expansion steam engines directly coupled to the water plungers of the pump and have high duty oil compensators to use the steam more economically."

School project Marianne Seit. and Josephine Bartlett, 1954

Like Pump Stations 5, 6 and 7, No 8 Pump Station housed two pumps. Bricks for the building came from Coolgardie - some say made from gold bearing clay.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ac715292-1b79-429e-9cc8-00e942b2310a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No 8 Pump Station was last of the original steam powered pump stations to operate. When, in 1970, the boilers cooled and the engines were shut down for the last time, the workers simply walked out and the doors were closed. Today the pump station remains almost as it was that very day.

Dedari is the most intact of all the original pump stations. Inside there are the two boilers, the two engines and pumps, the economiser and a host of ancillary equipment and tools. Outside, the steel chimney still stands tall and the coal bin looks to be awaiting the next load of fuel.

On the northern side of the pump station building stands the timber framed and ironclad store and blacksmith shop which still contains the blacksmith’s forge. A number of other structures also remain including the suction tank, weighbridge, the weatherboard engineer’s office, and the brick venturi meter house which used to record the evenness of the pumping as well as the quantity of water sent on.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.1062" lon="120.738367">
