 <wpt lat="-32.276983" lon="125.431367">
  <ele>115.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7VhG-05</name>
  <desc>NullarborLinkss</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810</url>
  <urlname>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S5 Ciaguna ~ Blowhole by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99009152" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S5 Ciaguna ~ Blowhole</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Ciaguna ~ Blowhole&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Is the air breathing out of the blowhole hotter or colder than the ambient above ground temperature?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ac0797c8-03af-4c6e-9df3-63003c8f72a8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;They say that the hills have eyes, but are the caves alive? 
The answer is yes, but not for a frightening reason. When caves ‘breathe’, air pressure falls and rises. The periodic reversal of air flow is caused by the pressure equalisation between the underground cavities and the air pressure above the ground, while the speed of breathing is related to the volume of air contained in the cave as well as the cross-sectional area of a cave’s entrance.

The Caiguna blowhole, just like Nullarbor caves, breathe more vigorously than all the other caves in Australia – in fact, air movement at cave entrance has been measured to be around 72 kilometres per hour. As you get closer the the blowhole, you can feel the earth breathing through the air currents, which, according to Aboriginal legends, were believed to be the breath of spirits. You will also see that the Caiguna blowhole is connected to a tiny cave that has roughly 1.5 metres clearance.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1299bda9-577a-4113-b5cd-8b570e06f78c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As we travel across The Nullarbor Plain we are finding so much more to explore.
Caves,Cliffs,Geological sites and Homesteads - all the more for another Adventure Lab.
Crossing this huge plain is a quintessential Australian road trip experience and the incredible vast landscapes will make you feel in awe of the wonderful Country we are traveling across.
The name Nullarbor comes from the Latin word “nullus” meaning “nothing” and the word “arbor” meaning “tree”.
Wombats, emus and kangaroos call the Nullarbor home.
Can you imagine driving for 146.6km in a completely straight line, without a single bend or turn in the road? The Eyre Highway is where you will find this curve-free road, stretching between Caiguna and Balladonia.
As you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you could play 18 holes of golf along the world’s longest golf course – the Nullarbor Links.
Now as you cross the Nullarbor Plain, you can add Australia's longest Adventure Lab the 'Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way II', to your journey.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.713983" lon="128.8865">
  <ele>10.0</ele>
  <time>2022-12-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7VhG-04</name>
  <desc>NullarborLinksu</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810</url>
  <urlname>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S4 Eucla ~ The Old Telegraph Station by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99009150" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S4 Eucla ~ The Old Telegraph Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Eucla ~ The Old Telegraph Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many bricks in the Keystone above the doorway? written numeral&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/952e163e-eb94-4871-8856-c90354c95ff8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Be sure to check out the ghostly remains of the Old Telegraph Station in Eucla. Take a photograph of this constantly changing landmark before it disappears under the ever encroaching white sand dunes. Now it is little more than a few old stone walls protruding above the moving dunes.

The starkness of the surrounding white dunes creates a spectacular image, which can be seen to the south of the town of Eucla. The original town was established in 1877 as a manual repeater station for the overland telegraph.

The remainder of the original town is now under the sand, after a plague of rabbits ate most of the dune vegetation which then caused large sand drifts to encroach on the town site.

Whilst wandering around the ruins, tune into the supernatural, as the area is said to be haunted by a ghost (we've never seen the ghost - we'll be back!!)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1299bda9-577a-4113-b5cd-8b570e06f78c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As we travel across The Nullarbor Plain we are finding so much more to explore.
Caves,Cliffs,Geological sites and Homesteads - all the more for another Adventure Lab.
Crossing this huge plain is a quintessential Australian road trip experience and the incredible vast landscapes will make you feel in awe of the wonderful Country we are traveling across.
The name Nullarbor comes from the Latin word “nullus” meaning “nothing” and the word “arbor” meaning “tree”.
Wombats, emus and kangaroos call the Nullarbor home.
Can you imagine driving for 146.6km in a completely straight line, without a single bend or turn in the road? The Eyre Highway is where you will find this curve-free road, stretching between Caiguna and Balladonia.
As you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you could play 18 holes of golf along the world’s longest golf course – the Nullarbor Links.
Now as you cross the Nullarbor Plain, you can add Australia's longest Adventure Lab the 'Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way II', to your journey.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
