  <urlname>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S2 Yalata ~ Living of the Edge of the Nullabor Plain by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99009146" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two : S2 Yalata ~ Living of the Edge of the Nullabor Plain</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>South Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way ~ Number Two&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Yalata ~ Living of the Edge of the Nullabor Plain&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/745abbe8-55ee-4581-a365-a77eaa22f810&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the symbol in the lower left corner on the tallest sign? (a four letter word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/543259c9-78db-4c16-8a7b-0fe8069a180b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lying at the edge of the Great Victoria Desert on the southern margin of Australia's majestic Nullarbor Plain, Yalata Indigenous Protected Area (IPA), covers 456,300 hectares of coastal dunes, limestone cliffs, sand plains and mallee shrub lands.

Originally occupied by Wirangu and Mirning coastal communities, Yalata's Traditional Owners also comprise Kokata, Antakarinja, Pindiini, and Ngalea western desert peoples. These groups are linked through cultural affiliations and traditional practices.

Surrounded by national parks and reserves, and the Great Australian Bight Marine Park, Yalata forms part of a wider region identified for conservation purposes. Around 20,000 people visit Yalata each year to fish, camp and watch the migration of southern-right whales which arrive from Antarctica between June and October.

Yalata IPA Rangers monitor the large visitor numbers and have constructed platforms and boardwalks to alleviate environmental impacts to the fragile sand dunes and beach areas.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1299bda9-577a-4113-b5cd-8b570e06f78c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As we travel across The Nullarbor Plain we are finding so much more to explore.
Caves,Cliffs,Geological sites and Homesteads - all the more for another Adventure Lab.
Crossing this huge plain is a quintessential Australian road trip experience and the incredible vast landscapes will make you feel in awe of the wonderful Country we are traveling across.
The name Nullarbor comes from the Latin word “nullus” meaning “nothing” and the word “arbor” meaning “tree”.
Wombats, emus and kangaroos call the Nullarbor home.
Can you imagine driving for 146.6km in a completely straight line, without a single bend or turn in the road? The Eyre Highway is where you will find this curve-free road, stretching between Caiguna and Balladonia.
As you drive across the Nullarbor Plain, you could play 18 holes of golf along the world’s longest golf course – the Nullarbor Links.
Now as you cross the Nullarbor Plain, you can add Australia's longest Adventure Lab the 'Nullarbor Links ~ The Aussie Way II', to your journey.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
