   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Places 2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Point Heathcote&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dcccaf8c-0325-41cd-a347-209ffd5579d2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dcccaf8c-0325-41cd-a347-209ffd5579d2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the aboriginal name for Point Heathcote?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9682df44-68c5-4bce-a081-f4fb15df1231" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Point Heathcote was named by Captain James Stirling after Midshipman G.C. Heathcote, a member of the 1827 Swan River expedition, who was the first European person to set foot on the point.  Heathcote was one of the original places considered as an ideal location to site the capital city before the site at the foot of Mount Eliza was chosen as the location of Perth.

There are a number of information plaques around the area.  One of them has the answer to the question asked. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5bae889d-e961-46d5-be77-5328134fb8ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Art can improve the connectivity of a precinct and its interrelationship with the rest of the area. Good public art can contribute to a sense of place, enhancing public spaces and celebrating cultural identity and community aspirations. (Source Melville City Council)

Like many local governments around the Perth Metropolitan area the City of Melville have brought and commissioned many pieces of art that now dot the various public parks and pathways around the local government area.  They also have constructed various viewing platforms and memorials at significant locations with the city boundaries.

Image: Coming of the colours (Source: Melville City council website)&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.006033" lon="115.83265">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
