  <name>LBQXWj-01</name>
  <desc>LMelvillePlac21</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dcccaf8c-0325-41cd-a347-209ffd5579d2</url>
  <urlname>[L] Melville Places 2 : S1 Waiting in the Wings by .tbh</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-617" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] Melville Places 2 : S1 Waiting in the Wings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>.tbh</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">.tbh</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Melville Places 2&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Waiting in the Wings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dcccaf8c-0325-41cd-a347-209ffd5579d2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dcccaf8c-0325-41cd-a347-209ffd5579d2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the middle word on the middle line?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1adc319d-8430-4887-ab06-96afce0c849f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally part of the Cottesloe Sculpture by the Sea in 2018 the sculpture was purchased by the City of Melville and now sits at its new home in Applecross.

From https://www.capral.com.au/project/waiting-in-the-wings/ the artist writes about the sculpture.

'Cottesloe’s inspiring vista gifting a beautiful place to tarry, to muse whilst waiting between the wings.  The wings symbols of lightness and imagination are used as an expression of our aspiration towards a higher possibility.'&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5bae889d-e961-46d5-be77-5328134fb8ab" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Public Art can improve the connectivity of a precinct and its interrelationship with the rest of the area. Good public art can contribute to a sense of place, enhancing public spaces and celebrating cultural identity and community aspirations. (Source Melville City Council)

Like many local governments around the Perth Metropolitan area the City of Melville have brought and commissioned many pieces of art that now dot the various public parks and pathways around the local government area.  They also have constructed various viewing platforms and memorials at significant locations with the city boundaries.

Image: Coming of the colours (Source: Melville City council website)&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.934433" lon="117.174567">
  <ele>360.0</ele>
  <time>2023-01-01T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKTpv-05</name>
  <desc>NarroginMemori5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0b64cb0-a8e6-4e68-b3f3-cdb49d5fcbdc</url>
  <urlname>Narrogin Memorial Park : S5 Post War Immigration by EGB2020</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-180" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Narrogin Memorial Park : S5 Post War Immigration</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>EGB2020</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">EGB2020</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Narrogin Memorial Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Post War Immigration&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0b64cb0-a8e6-4e68-b3f3-cdb49d5fcbdc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0b64cb0-a8e6-4e68-b3f3-cdb49d5fcbdc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the plaque - How many families and single men migrated to Narrogin?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6fafd07e-ed75-47ff-a824-a3acad3a8dfa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Following the end of world War lI, around 280 Families and Single men migrated to Narrogin mainly from Displaced Persons Camps in Europe.
People came from Poland, Ukraine, Latvia, Lithuania, Estonia, Belarus, Italy Hungary, Yugoslavia, Czechoslovakia, The Netherlands, Germany and Russia.
For many of the Families, their first home in Narrogin was in one of the camps that were established to accommodate them.
The Families of men who worked for WAG lived in tents on railway land;
Main Roads Families lived in Nissen huts, tents and tin shacks within the Main Roads depot.
Single men lived in boarding houses and the Town Caravan Park.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7751f112-e216-4670-aaa2-8f2f6c5fedf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The park commemorates those who served in World War One.

The Memorial Park has associations with the development of Narrogin since the 1890s, originally being part of the police precinct. When Premier James Mitchell visited Narrogin on 8 July 1919, the idea of a memorial park was uppermost in the minds of the Town Council, and the Premier was asked if the Government would give the police paddock to the Town for that purpose. The Premier agreed to the request and an area of a little under 1.25 acres (approx 0.60 ha) was excised from the site and vested in the Town Council. It was gazetted Reserve 17532 as a Soldiers Memorial Site on 24 September 1920.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
