   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Foxes Lair Narrogin&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Arboretum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/406d90b7-7686-42c8-b23a-88f92fe26270"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/406d90b7-7686-42c8-b23a-88f92fe26270&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Has dolerite more or less quartz than granite?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/39c359f3-a1a4-4a4a-98e9-571130901586" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Forrests Department developed numerous demonstration plantings (arboreta) throughout the wheatbelt in the 1950's and 1960's, focusing on species for quality timber, dust control and for windbreaks.
Each tree has a unique number at the base.
Information boards and QR Codes give you valuable information about Foxes Lair and its attractions.
Visit Doug Sawkins website https://www.foxeslair.org/ for more 
