   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Eric Singleton Bird Sanctuary &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wildlife&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/679f672b-34aa-4634-a8ed-46b9c46fd043"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/679f672b-34aa-4634-a8ed-46b9c46fd043&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of animal number 1? (3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dd04401e-710a-4269-a464-3cd68af97ca7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A variety of animals live in the wetland.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/014274ec-d393-4067-a19c-7be4d90b1419" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Eric Singleton Bird Sanctuary is an important feeding, resting and breeding area for birds and other fauna. It also provides a unique natural space in our urban catchment for walking, bird watching, and a place for quiet contemplation.

Eric Singleton and his family were avid birdwatchers and observed over 70 species in this area. The wetland was declared a nature reserve in 1977.

Please note, this is a dog free zone.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
