   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Burns Beach Bimble&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Camping Reserve &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/709b51b1-984e-4eca-82b1-f441000a5647"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/709b51b1-984e-4eca-82b1-f441000a5647&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the plaque what year was the plan first Gazetted? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a8fee25b-3899-4e2b-a586-56f7a48dc78b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At this location you will find a brick plinth with a historical plaque on its surface. ….. approach and examine the plaque in detail to find your answer…..&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c95d52f7-af81-4e73-9b77-de765242f2bc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Though Burns Beach is recently developed suburb, the land was originally owned by the Midland Railway Company. In 1908, following a request from 50 local residents, the Wanneroo Road Board successfully applied for a 20-hectare (49-acre) reserve for camping and a health resort. By the 1920s, the area was well used by locals, who knew the area as "Burns Beach" after a farmer who ran sheep in the area.[3]

For many years it was accessible only by track or boat and functioned as a small coastal village, and tourism and fishing were the main occupations. After World War II, it was finally connected by road to Wanneroo Road.   The cover arial photo shows the suburb in the Mid 1970’s, surprisingly the exact same road layout can still be walked today, in the “Old Burns” part of the suburb. 

This adventure will take you on a walk showcasing some of the natural areas and interesting artworks. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
