   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Burns Beach Bimble&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Indian Ocean View Point &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/709b51b1-984e-4eca-82b1-f441000a5647"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/709b51b1-984e-4eca-82b1-f441000a5647&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the base of stairs there is a plaque. What year was the the park opened? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9bcee62c-5f35-45af-be64-c2993d6972c8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the view point, climb the steps and look out to the Ocean.  At the base of the steps there is a plaque…. Seek it out to find your answer. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c95d52f7-af81-4e73-9b77-de765242f2bc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Though Burns Beach is recently developed suburb, the land was originally owned by the Midland Railway Company. In 1908, following a request from 50 local residents, the Wanneroo Road Board successfully applied for a 20-hectare (49-acre) reserve for camping and a health resort. By the 1920s, the area was well used by locals, who knew the area as "Burns Beach" after a farmer who ran sheep in the area.[3]

For many years it was accessible only by track or boat and functioned as a small coastal village, and tourism and fishing were the main occupations. After World War II, it was finally connected by road to Wanneroo Road.   The cover arial photo shows the suburb in the Mid 1970’s, surprisingly the exact same road layout can still be walked today, in the “Old Burns” part of the suburb. 

