   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No 4. Pump Station - Receiving Tank&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the first two words written on the sign on the tubular barrier?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/82868cc2-df5f-43d6-b54c-bb7403e1a658" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Unlike all other stations, No. 4 had a circular, rather than rectangular receiving tank which held 4.5 million litres of water. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e5e4354-7e40-4ca2-8eae-71ddffaeea79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although fenced off due to asbestos issues, three generations of pump stations can be seen at this location. The original steam pump station, constructed at the turn of the century, is still an imposing landmark although it has not been used for more than 50 years. 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.491583" lon="118.24415">
  <ele>307.0</ele>
  <time>2023-07-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBv7g8-04</name>
  <desc>GoldenIeline001</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station : S4 No. 4. Pump Station - 2001 by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910140" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station : S4 No. 4. Pump Station - 2001</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;No. 4. Pump Station - 2001&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There are 5 valves; what are the letters and number on the second last line of the one nearest you?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2a9ff895-1719-40b7-8cfa-22909f5afb96" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 2001 the third generation pump station was constructed at a cost of $7.5 million. As running of the pump stations became more automated and road transport became easier, there was little reason for people to live onsite and consequently the houses which formed a thriving community have disappeared. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e5e4354-7e40-4ca2-8eae-71ddffaeea79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although fenced off due to asbestos issues, three generations of pump stations can be seen at this location. The original steam pump station, constructed at the turn of the century, is still an imposing landmark although it has not been used for more than 50 years. 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.476533" lon="118.291083">
  <ele>330.0</ele>
  <time>2023-07-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBv7g8-05</name>
  <desc>GoldenIelineT45</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3</url>
  <urlname>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station : S5 Merredin Peak Railway Dam by Boyzrule5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476910142" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station : S5 Merredin Peak Railway Dam</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Boyzrule5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Boyzrule5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Golden Pipeline Trail: No. 4 Pump Station&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Merredin Peak Railway Dam&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/251ea690-3bc7-4c84-a8f2-33887f1a50d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the title of the information plaque at this location?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6f143d2b-b68f-4ae7-8e05-0a70547fa600" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although technically not a part of the pipeline, this location is significant, pre-dates the pipeline and is listed on the trail. 
Large quantities of water were required to power steam locomotives pushing out into the goldfields. With no perennial streams or other reliable water sources in the region, rocks had to be harvested for water. The dam was originally built in conjunction with the railway in 1893. At the same time a stone contour wall was built around the Peak, it caught and directed the rainfall runoff into a large, 100m long stone lined channel which led to the dam. A sluice gate at the entrance of the channel controlled the flow of water into the dam. A second sluice gate located in the rock wall to the side of the channel allowed excess water to run off into open ground, ensuring the town would not be flooded. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e5e4354-7e40-4ca2-8eae-71ddffaeea79" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although fenced off due to asbestos issues, three generations of pump stations can be seen at this location. The original steam pump station, constructed at the turn of the century, is still an imposing landmark although it has not been used for more than 50 years. 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.2956" lon="115.041067">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
