 <wpt lat="-31.209517" lon="121.622">
  <ele>305.0</ele>
  <time>2023-09-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBuWKt-05</name>
  <desc>NickelInTheHea5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce</url>
  <urlname>Nickel in the heart of Kambalda : S5 Winder Wheels by Morgo the geo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-20" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nickel in the heart of Kambalda : S5 Winder Wheels</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Morgo the geo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Morgo the geo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nickel in the heart of Kambalda&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Winder Wheels&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many winder wheels are located here (digit)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/16782392-8d67-4640-823b-8f9d9959d643" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These wheels sit on top of a head frame above a shaft. Wire ropes pass through these to lift buckets (skips) of ore deep from underground. They have been at this location for over two decades.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/526ba332-58f6-474a-ad46-88b1cf3c1708" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally a small artisanal gold mining camp in the 1900’s, is wasn’t until January 1966 that Western Mining Corporation struck massive nickel ore in their first drill hole KD1 that transformed the region. Within 18 months, a town sprung up and nickel was being mined. This Adventure Lab will take you to a few spots which looks at the history of Kambalda.

There is a bonus cache on Red Hill, so keep a lookout for the coordinates.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.214717" lon="121.6266">
  <ele>305.0</ele>
  <time>2023-09-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBuWKt-01</name>
  <desc>NickelInTheHea1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce</url>
  <urlname>Nickel in the heart of Kambalda : S1 Underground Jumbo by Morgo the geo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-16" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Nickel in the heart of Kambalda : S1 Underground Jumbo</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Morgo the geo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Morgo the geo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nickel in the heart of Kambalda&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Underground Jumbo&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many holes have been drilled into the rock (digit)?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/403316d3-8acb-4cc1-8710-619d68c67b4c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This was the first jumbo I saw. It’s a mechanised underground rock drill used to create tunnels. This jumbo cut the Carnilya nickel mine portal and spent years underground at the Victory gold mine.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/526ba332-58f6-474a-ad46-88b1cf3c1708" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Originally a small artisanal gold mining camp in the 1900’s, is wasn’t until January 1966 that Western Mining Corporation struck massive nickel ore in their first drill hole KD1 that transformed the region. Within 18 months, a town sprung up and nickel was being mined. This Adventure Lab will take you to a few spots which looks at the history of Kambalda.

There is a bonus cache on Red Hill, so keep a lookout for the coordinates.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.214433" lon="121.6268">
  <ele>305.0</ele>
  <time>2023-09-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBuWKt-04</name>
  <desc>NickelInTheHea4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/004b018b-48f0-457f-802d-165f4d9952ce</url>
  <urlname>Nickel in the heart of Kambalda : S4 Rail Bogger by Morgo the geo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-19" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
