  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476912215" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Helena and Aurora Ranges : S5 The Way to the Top</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sazpazza</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sazpazza</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Helena and Aurora Ranges&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Way to the Top&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f7059ba0-3bf4-4259-ad7e-350219e24bdf"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f7059ba0-3bf4-4259-ad7e-350219e24bdf&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many bols on this sign?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7a3bdf7a-b5b8-49cc-a3c9-f0b939d99367" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Helena Aurora Range is also home to over 100 species of reptiles, mostly lizards, with 14 species of gecko, we have seen many of these little fellas turning over rocks and branches.

There are also 8 species of dragon to possibly spot out here, although we must admit to only seeing a few in our many trips out here.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b5bcddd4-446f-4e3c-823f-b801633c5b4b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sazpazza love coming out to the gorgeous Helena Aurora Range (Bungalbin). It is remote enough to get some peace and quiet yet close enough to Perth for a few days away. The region is the largest, highest and steepest banded ironstone formation in Western Australia. No higher land form can be seen in a 400km radius, making the Helena Aurora Range sight not to be missed when travelling through southern WA.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-30.34235" lon="119.670067">
  <ele>480.0</ele>
  <time>2024-01-15T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQF6H-04</name>
  <desc>HelenaAndAuror4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f7059ba0-3bf4-4259-ad7e-350219e24bdf</url>
  <urlname>Helena and Aurora Ranges : S4 The Fork in the Road by sazpazza</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476912213" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Helena and Aurora Ranges : S4 The Fork in the Road</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sazpazza</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sazpazza</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Helena and Aurora Ranges&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Fork in the Road&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f7059ba0-3bf4-4259-ad7e-350219e24bdf"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f7059ba0-3bf4-4259-ad7e-350219e24bdf&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where would you be if you went left according to the sign? (3 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/112a34eb-ac75-421b-9391-6df332dc4731" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Couple of options here,  so you head further north towards Mount Manning or continue on towards the magnificent Peak with stunning views

The Helena Aurora Range currently stands as one of the few regions in WA still not affected by mining.

It is a rich area composed of more than 350 species of plants and animals, including five endemic plant species, and the Critically Endangered Leucopogon spectablis, found only on the range.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b5bcddd4-446f-4e3c-823f-b801633c5b4b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sazpazza love coming out to the gorgeous Helena Aurora Range (Bungalbin). It is remote enough to get some peace and quiet yet close enough to Perth for a few days away. The region is the largest, highest and steepest banded ironstone formation in Western Australia. No higher land form can be seen in a 400km radius, making the Helena Aurora Range sight not to be missed when travelling through southern WA.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.865867" lon="115.7629">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2024-01-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBwiHC-03</name>
  <desc>CharlesRileyWa3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f</url>
  <urlname>Charles Riley Wander : S3 Soccer Club by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-809" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Charles Riley Wander : S3 Soccer Club</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Charles Riley Wander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Soccer Club&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year were the new clubrooms officially opened?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4fe9edba-f1e0-4d41-9409-1be34951ea83" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You are looking for a plaque on the clubhouse wall.

Founded in 1973, North Beach Soccer Club is one of the biggest soccer clubs in Western Australia. 

The senior men teams compete in the top WA Amateur League, Division 1,2,3 &amp; 4 of the Metropolitan North League and the WA Masters League. The club also has 3 senior women teams competing across the Perth &amp; Peel region and one of the largest junior soccer programs in WA.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27df700e-0cc6-4076-bcd7-8df556ca7d0b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A short wander around the Charles Riley Memorial Reserve. The journal entries will also lead you to a bonus cache (GCAJW8T).

Covering 8ha of sporting fields and recreation space, the Charles Riley reserve includes an impressive array of facilities, including:

Multiple sports clubs
Cricket nets
Stirling Community Centre
North Beach War Memorial
North Beach RSL Sub-branch
Two community play areas
BBQs &amp; picnic shelters
Fenced dog park
North Beach Toy Library
Accessible paths and amenities&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.86455" lon="115.759633">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2024-01-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBwiHC-02</name>
  <desc>CharlesRileyWa2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f</url>
  <urlname>Charles Riley Wander : S2 Tennis Club by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-808" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Charles Riley Wander : S2 Tennis Club</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Charles Riley Wander&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tennis Club&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f67c51e2-3901-46e8-b008-211340b5e52f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who are the two memorial plaques dedicated to?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5c4bf93f-b985-4b0f-a9b4-5120451d5b8f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just past the flagpole are two memorial plaques on the wall.

Founded in 1963 the North Beach Tennis Club boasts 24 tennis courts comprising of 4 plexi-pave hard courts, 4 gel courts, 4 synthetic grass courts and 12 premium grass courts.

Other sports club in this part of the reserve include Beach Volleyball and Lawn Bowls, along with the North Beach RSL Sub-branch.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27df700e-0cc6-4076-bcd7-8df556ca7d0b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A short wander around the Charles Riley Memorial Reserve. The journal entries will also lead you to a bonus cache (GCAJW8T).

Covering 8ha of sporting fields and recreation space, the Charles Riley reserve includes an impressive array of facilities, including:

Multiple sports clubs
Cricket nets
Stirling Community Centre
North Beach War Memorial
North Beach RSL Sub-branch
Two community play areas
BBQs &amp; picnic shelters
Fenced dog park
North Beach Toy Library
Accessible paths and amenities&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
