 <wpt lat="-34.368" lon="118.2429">
  <ele>455.0</ele>
  <time>2024-03-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRreh-01</name>
  <desc>BluffKnollStir1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8</url>
  <urlname>Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP : S1 Good time to check out the map! by sazpazza</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-276" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP : S1 Good time to check out the map!</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sazpazza</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sazpazza</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Good time to check out the map!&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the Distance to summit?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/232c4340-8bd8-45d1-a7fe-405a057d5c70" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is the start point of the walk,  take some time to look at the map and see what you are in for,  make sure you have appropriate clothing/footwear and plenty of water and read all the info. It is an amazing part of WA and well worth the climb. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a3b7bc83-9de0-4fc1-8bb6-e414e56934b4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bluff Knoll is the highest peak of the Stirling Range in the Great Southern region of Western Australia (WA). It is 1,099 metres (3,606 ft) above sea level, with a prominence of 650 m (2,130 ft).[1] The local Aboriginal people, the Mineng and Koreng/Goreng sub-groups of the Noongar Nation, call the mountain Boola Miyel or Pualaar Miial, meaning "great many-faced hill"[2] or "many eyes", as the peak has rock formations that resemble eyes looking out across the valley.

The Bluff Knoll trail is a round trip of about 6 kilometres (4 mi) taking three to four hours,[3] and has a Grade 4 rating of climbing difficulty, with some steep and rough steps.[3] In 2020 many parts of the trail were destroyed in the Black Summer bushfires, and has since been upgraded and improved.[4] The view from the summit encompasses the Stirling and Porongurup mountain ranges, as well as the coast near Albany.

Due to its altitude, Bluff Knoll and the surrounding peaks of the Stirling Ranges are some of only a few places to experience regular snowfalls in Western Australia.[5] When snow is forecast by the Bureau of Meteorology it attracts hikers from all over the southwest region to experience it.

The peak was given its name by Governor James Stirling as the highest point in the Stirling Range.[6] Although Bluff Knoll is sometimes claimed to be the highest point in Western Australia,[6] the highest peak is actually Mount Meharry in Karijini National Park in the Hamersley Range, which is 1,249 metres (4,098 ft) above sea level.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.3748" lon="118.255767">
  <ele>1039.0</ele>
  <time>2024-03-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBRreh-05</name>
  <desc>BluffKnollStir5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8</url>
  <urlname>Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP : S5 The Peak! Bluff Knoll! You made it! by sazpazza</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476912223" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP : S5 The Peak! Bluff Knoll! You made it!</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sazpazza</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sazpazza</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bluff Knoll - Stirling Range NP&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Peak! Bluff Knoll! You made it!&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8caf66c0-d97f-46a0-9418-bcf8858ee3c8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;According to the sign what do they recommend you to not throw off for safety?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1a4501f8-d4c2-416a-91ab-d7e5d6aba43c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Now your are here stop and enjoy the serenity! The views are amazing and you will be feeling pretty bloody awesome right now! Take loads of photos to send to your family and friends,  have a muesli bar or an apple if you brought one and just enjoy! And enjoy a big drink of H2O while standing still! &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a3b7bc83-9de0-4fc1-8bb6-e414e56934b4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Bluff Knoll is the highest peak of the Stirling Range in the Great Southern region of Western Australia (WA). It is 1,099 metres (3,606 ft) above sea level, with a prominence of 650 m (2,130 ft).[1] The local Aboriginal people, the Mineng and Koreng/Goreng sub-groups of the Noongar Nation, call the mountain Boola Miyel or Pualaar Miial, meaning "great many-faced hill"[2] or "many eyes", as the peak has rock formations that resemble eyes looking out across the valley.

The Bluff Knoll trail is a round trip of about 6 kilometres (4 mi) taking three to four hours,[3] and has a Grade 4 rating of climbing difficulty, with some steep and rough steps.[3] In 2020 many parts of the trail were destroyed in the Black Summer bushfires, and has since been upgraded and improved.[4] The view from the summit encompasses the Stirling and Porongurup mountain ranges, as well as the coast near Albany.

Due to its altitude, Bluff Knoll and the surrounding peaks of the Stirling Ranges are some of only a few places to experience regular snowfalls in Western Australia.[5] When snow is forecast by the Bureau of Meteorology it attracts hikers from all over the southwest region to experience it.

The peak was given its name by Governor James Stirling as the highest point in the Stirling Range.[6] Although Bluff Knoll is sometimes claimed to be the highest point in Western Australia,[6] the highest peak is actually Mount Meharry in Karijini National Park in the Hamersley Range, which is 1,249 metres (4,098 ft) above sea level.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
