   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old mines of the Collie fields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Phoenix Mine&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did this mine start?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/69289ee5-a7c3-4db1-a655-8047cd33b979" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Phoenix Coal Mine started in 1948, when the Griffin Coal Mining Company Ltd developed a test tunnel, and uncovered good seams of coal. The mine was just west of their Griffin (later known as Wyvern mine). The mine operated through the 1950's, but was a small producer. Coal was carted by truck to the Wyvern siding just west of the mine. Phoenix was one of the first mines at Collie to be mechanized, moving away from hand mining, and ore carted by ponies.

Take a walk into the bush to find the old mineshaft and workshop remains.

Cover Image: 816B/C/1493, Conveyor belts to surface from bins, Wyvern Colie Mine, Collie, 1947.State Library of Western Australia&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4c8a5d8f-8cba-4ede-8c55-01385cab4d14" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a step back in time and explore the old mine ruins of the past and transformation into today's society.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.383183" lon="116.136633">
  <ele>189.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqgUk-01</name>
  <desc>OldMinesOfTheC1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620</url>
  <urlname>Old mines of the Collie fields : S1 Griffin Mine by Adrianrobert88</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1184" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old mines of the Collie fields : S1 Griffin Mine</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Adrianrobert88</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Adrianrobert88</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old mines of the Collie fields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Griffin Mine&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did this mine cease operation?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e9d7987e-a54c-4701-80b6-e7e928a8e3e9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Griffin Coal Mining Syndicate was formed in 1926 to test a lease just south of the Collie River. The first mine manager was A.J. Stibbs.

The shareholders were local mining men working at other mines in the district. This co-operative arrangement was common on the coalfield, however somehow it did not prevent conflict with the Miner's Union, and strikes regularly occurred. Sources tend to mention the Griffin and Wyvern mines as separate, but were neighbours accessing the same lode. The Wyvern name starts in 1944. All the workings were closed in 1954, after the coal resources at the site were exhausted. The company moved to open pit mining at Muja, and Griffin is still with us today mining east of Collie.

In 1946 it is mentioned the Wyvern is one of the smallest on the field, employing 35 men, producing 500 tonnes of coal per week. Together the two mines employed about 153 men, producing 340 tonnes of coal per day.

Coal was being sent to the Collie power station. After the war, the mine was mechanised, doing away with the Dickensian hand mining, and ponies. Shuttle cars, a joy loader, and arc wall coal cutter were imported. With a certain sense of wonderment, the process was described as a face of coal 24 feet wide, with 20 holes bored with electric drills, and charged with gelignite.

Mechanical loaders deposited the ore onto conveyor belts, and shuttle cars to the surface. It is then deposited into a tumbler, with fine and large lumps of coal graded automatically by falling through different sized holes.

Cover Image: Griffin Mine entrance 21 June 1927, State Library of Western Australia b3507692&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4c8a5d8f-8cba-4ede-8c55-01385cab4d14" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a step back in time and explore the old mine ruins of the past and transformation into today's society.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.384683" lon="116.2288">
