  <name>LBqgUk-04</name>
  <desc>OldMinesOfTheC4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620</url>
  <urlname>Old mines of the Collie fields : S4 Stockton opencut by Adrianrobert88</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1187" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old mines of the Collie fields : S4 Stockton opencut</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Adrianrobert88</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Adrianrobert88</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old mines of the Collie fields&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stockton opencut&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7072d8da-3600-4f03-a26c-51ca9dfde620&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did underground operations cease at Stockton?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c0c8f77e-eeff-41ae-a156-9b6f8e039e33" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;open pit mining at Stockton comenced in 1943 and ceased in 1957 with underground operations continuing until 1960.

This was the first open cut coal mine at Collie. The coal was part of the Ewington Formation, with the main seam encountered at 37 feet below the surface, and a lesser top seam at 19 feet below the surface. 

The open cut saw mechanisation with drag lines, and diesel shovels, taken from the Big Bell and Burbidge gold mines, and Metropolitan Brickworks, elsewhere in the State. The mine was operated by Amalgamated Collieries of W.A. Ltd. It employed about 1000 men, and produced 260 tonnes of coal per day. The mine produced a total of just over 1.5 million tonnes of coal.

Ernest Robert Boyd (40) in 1939, William Blackford (21) in 1940, James Charles Parker (56), and William Bebbington (53) in 1941, were all killed from coal cave-ins.

There was no requirement at the time for the mine to be rehabilitated. It filled with water, and is presently used for camping, swimming, water skiing etc as a recreation reserve. The lake is 7 kilometres east of Collie, just south of the highway. The high acidity of the lake has seen it closed at times due to public health issues.

Cover image: Stockton open cut coal mine, Collie State Library of Western Australia 000158D&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4c8a5d8f-8cba-4ede-8c55-01385cab4d14" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a step back in time and explore the old mine ruins of the past and transformation into today's society.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
