 <wpt lat="-31.55035" lon="116.4652">
  <ele>136.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHcZT-05</name>
  <desc>NewRoadS5Connor</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9</url>
  <urlname>New Road : S5 Connor’s Mill by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915744" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>New Road : S5 Connor’s Mill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;New Road&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Connor’s Mill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a bench seat, who was it donated by?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8cce5f50-b62b-4732-816b-1c9abf15da41" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Connor's Mill is an icon of Toodyay and the legacy of one of the town's most prominent businessmen, Daniel Connor. It is a testimony to Toodyay's busy agricultural industry.

Connor's Mill, with its historic machinery, demonstrates for visitors the process of turning wheat into flour. It retells stories of the many families whose lives revolved around this grand convict era building. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14bf330f-4024-419b-8e59-a62fa9c3738e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stirling Terrace was originally known as the 'New Road,' surveyed in 1852 and cleared by convict labour.

During this time, the main street was nothing more than a dirt track, dusty in summer and bogged or flooded in winter. Pedestrians had to deal with open cesspits and livestock wandering the street, leading one local to complain whether: "something will be done to prevent pigs, goats and stray stock from patrolling the streets, at their own sweet will”

This adventure will take you to some historic sights along Stirling Terrace. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.550333" lon="116.466767">
  <ele>137.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHcZT-04</name>
  <desc>NewRoadS4Memori</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9</url>
  <urlname>New Road : S4 Memorial Hall by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915742" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>New Road : S4 Memorial Hall</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;New Road&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Memorial Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On the side entrance is a silver sign, “ Toodyay Memorial Hall Stone”, what year was the stone Re-installed?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1330e9d2-5b7e-4db0-90ed-6288eba0c4c7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;When Newcastle was made a municipality in 1877 the need for a Road Board meeting place arose. The board met in a number of places around town until the Municipal Chambers was constructed for this purpose in 1895. In 1910 Mayor Somers and the Councillors of the day added a town hall to the rear of the Municipal Chambers, forming the building now known as the Toodyay Memorial Hall.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14bf330f-4024-419b-8e59-a62fa9c3738e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stirling Terrace was originally known as the 'New Road,' surveyed in 1852 and cleared by convict labour.

During this time, the main street was nothing more than a dirt track, dusty in summer and bogged or flooded in winter. Pedestrians had to deal with open cesspits and livestock wandering the street, leading one local to complain whether: "something will be done to prevent pigs, goats and stray stock from patrolling the streets, at their own sweet will”

This adventure will take you to some historic sights along Stirling Terrace. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.550167" lon="116.46695">
  <ele>135.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHcZT-03</name>
  <desc>NewRoadS3Federa</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9</url>
  <urlname>New Road : S3 Federation Square by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915740" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>New Road : S3 Federation Square</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;New Road&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Federation Square&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Small plaque, under the pergola: “The Pioneer Settlers” - who came to the district in what century?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/51fb2dd5-7881-4e43-abc4-7a772f81c386" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;When the Toodyay Post Office was erected in 1897, a brick and stone well was sunk on this site to provide water to the Newcastle townspeople.

The remains of the well are still present today as a cement cap.
Federation Square stands as a memorial to the Indigenous people of Toodyay and the early European families who settled in the region.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14bf330f-4024-419b-8e59-a62fa9c3738e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stirling Terrace was originally known as the 'New Road,' surveyed in 1852 and cleared by convict labour.

During this time, the main street was nothing more than a dirt track, dusty in summer and bogged or flooded in winter. Pedestrians had to deal with open cesspits and livestock wandering the street, leading one local to complain whether: "something will be done to prevent pigs, goats and stray stock from patrolling the streets, at their own sweet will”

This adventure will take you to some historic sights along Stirling Terrace. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.550117" lon="116.467183">
  <ele>135.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBHcZT-02</name>
  <desc>NewRoadS2PostOf</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9</url>
  <urlname>New Road : S2 Post Office by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915738" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>New Road : S2 Post Office</groundspeak:name>
