   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;New Road&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fire Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13736dfe-5639-413f-9ac8-5d9579a46cd9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the plaque on the facade, what was the builders last name?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3fc7ac66-e871-4a91-bb3c-9e0ec39daf87" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Building was erected by the WA fire  brigades board and opened by James Pollitt ESQ. JP, Chairman of the Toodyay Road Board on August 20th, 1938&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/14bf330f-4024-419b-8e59-a62fa9c3738e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stirling Terrace was originally known as the 'New Road,' surveyed in 1852 and cleared by convict labour.

During this time, the main street was nothing more than a dirt track, dusty in summer and bogged or flooded in winter. Pedestrians had to deal with open cesspits and livestock wandering the street, leading one local to complain whether: "something will be done to prevent pigs, goats and stray stock from patrolling the streets, at their own sweet will”

This adventure will take you to some historic sights along Stirling Terrace. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
