  <name>LBL2dK-01</name>
  <desc>OldOnslowTowns1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7191bc3e-900f-497c-b130-4c7b14e773e6</url>
  <urlname>Old Onslow Townsite : S1 Cemetery by 80degrees</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915726" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old Onslow Townsite : S1 Cemetery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>80degrees</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">80degrees</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Onslow Townsite&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cemetery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7191bc3e-900f-497c-b130-4c7b14e773e6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7191bc3e-900f-497c-b130-4c7b14e773e6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On what Station is Elenor Stewart's husband John buried?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/57da8deb-56a7-422c-9ebb-edcf3a157dbb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lot 152 was gazetted as a cemetery in1914 however the earliest graves date to 1897.Graves were segregated based on their religion.  Most graves can be found in the Anglican Section.  The cemetery was used until the 1920's when the town was relocated to Beadon Point.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/535f7654-4696-4a79-8aa6-df6fa87aa727" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Adventure will take you for a journey through the original Onslow Township.  Onslow was established in 1883 to supply the surrounding sheep and cattle stations as well as the gold mines and pearling industry.  The town was gazetted in 1885 and it became important in the North West for the export of all of the above.
However with a rapidly silting river, large ships could no longer dock to load or unload goods.  In 1925 a new jetty opened at nearby Beardon Point ( now Onslow).  Onslow's people and many of its buildings relocated leaving behind the ghost town of today.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9063" lon="115.949233">
  <ele>19.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBiAiB-02</name>
  <desc>BassendeanTown2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f</url>
  <urlname>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S2 Bassendean Masonic Lodge (Former) by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-452" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S2 Bassendean Masonic Lodge (Former)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bassendean Masonic Lodge (Former)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many GROUPS of windows can you count along the south (Palmerston St) side of the building? (WORD)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1d0ca76f-f9c9-4c8e-b495-84a9a5bea28b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean Masonic Lodge was erected in 1934 by the members of the Bassendean Lodge No. 102 (formerly West Guildford Lodge 102) and Bassendean Chapter 20 for use by both as their Masonic home.  The building committee prepared the tenders for the various trades, selected the contractors and ordered materials. The bricks (49,000) were supplied by Whitemans Brickyard, and W Bro D Dells carted them to the site for 8 shillings per 1,000 bricks and Bro W H H Adie laid them at a cost of £1-17s-6p per 1,000. 

Since its construction very few alterations or additions have occurred to the building. In 1961, some internal renovations were carried out to the hall, and in 1972 a suspended ceiling was installed in the Lodge room. The Bassendean Masonic Lodge  was used continuously as a Masonic hall until 1988, when both the Bassendean Lodge No. 102 and the Bassendean Chapter No. 20 held their last meetings. 

The building was transferred to the Town of Bassendean in 1988 and until 2012 it was used intermittently for meetings and functions. In 2012, the place was converted into a private residence with a portion allocated for use as professional rooms. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/568dcf61-058f-4b50-8fef-3cb4f3578fd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean town centre was established and laid out as an extension of Guildford townsite in the 1840s but it was not until the 1890s that the town developed rapidly with the influx of workers during the gold boom period. The town (previously referred to as West Guildford) was named Bassendean in 1922 after one of the first farms in the district - ‘Bassendean’ - established by the First Colonial Secretary, Peter Broun. The town developed on either side of what was called the Perth Road and later, Guildford Road, which logically connected Perth to Guildford.

Once very much a working class suburb, in recent years Bassendean has been gentrified and Old Perth Road strip is now home to a number of eclectic bars, cafes and art spaces. Why not linger a little longer and enjoy a coffee or a beer after completing this Adventure Lab. 

(Cover Photo Credit - www.bassendean.net)
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.905717" lon="115.945933">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBiAiB-01</name>
  <desc>BassendeanTown1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f</url>
  <urlname>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S1 Bassendean House by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915746" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S1 Bassendean House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bassendean House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;You should be standing near the driveway of the private residence. What is the family name written above the door of the detached garage?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8979e70b-aaf3-4ce4-bd1f-f31e5295d36e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built in 1914, 1 Kathleen Street - "Bassendean House" - was the home of  Edward James Clay who made a significant contribution to the Bassendean community as a member of the Road Board and the Progress Association. Clay lived here with his wife Emma Mary Clay and their two children. Emma Clay was also active in the local community. She opposed the opening of the Bassendean Hotel and raised a petition against it with other concerned locals. Edward Clay died in 1925 and the family remained owners and occupiers of the house for many years.

This house demonstrates the form and scale of housing for professional men and their families in the early 1900s, and is a good example of a Federation Bungalow style residence. It continues as a private residence. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/568dcf61-058f-4b50-8fef-3cb4f3578fd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean town centre was established and laid out as an extension of Guildford townsite in the 1840s but it was not until the 1890s that the town developed rapidly with the influx of workers during the gold boom period. The town (previously referred to as West Guildford) was named Bassendean in 1922 after one of the first farms in the district - ‘Bassendean’ - established by the First Colonial Secretary, Peter Broun. The town developed on either side of what was called the Perth Road and later, Guildford Road, which logically connected Perth to Guildford.

Once very much a working class suburb, in recent years Bassendean has been gentrified and Old Perth Road strip is now home to a number of eclectic bars, cafes and art spaces. Why not linger a little longer and enjoy a coffee or a beer after completing this Adventure Lab. 

(Cover Photo Credit - www.bassendean.net)
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
