 <wpt lat="-31.9048" lon="115.948983">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBiAiB-03</name>
  <desc>BassendeanTown3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f</url>
  <urlname>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S3 Saint Mark's the Evangelist Church by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915748" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S3 Saint Mark's the Evangelist Church</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Saint Mark's the Evangelist Church&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the single word written under the cross on the metal gate in front of the stairs?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2cc3b7d7-c2e9-42ca-b827-f10bdc42c179" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Anglican Church had originally established a church in West Guildford in 1909 in Lord Street but this weatherboard building was destroyed in a windstorm in 1915.  The Bassendean site - on high ground nearer to the centre of the townsite -  was subsequently chosen for replacement building. 

In October 1915, foundations for the brick sanctuary and chancel were laid. The original weatherboard structure was re-erected as a meeting room and hall. On 15th January 1916, the new brick chancel was consecrated by the Archbishop of Perth C.O. L. Riley. The furnishings for the new portion were donated by friends and members of the congregation and the frontal was 'donated by the Cathedral and worked by Mrs Phillips of Claremont'. This portion is currently the sanctuary of the church. 

In 1937, the church was completed with the construction of a brick nave. The completed church was consecrated by Archbishop of Perth, Primate Dr H. F. Le Fanu on 12 December 1937. In the period following World War Two, Bassendean experienced a population growth and the town secured funds to enable the construction of a new hall on the site. It was opened on 19 July 1959. The church has continued to evolve as the needs and practices of the community have changed. In 1985, four stained glass windows were donated to the church. In 1991, the hall was rewired and new lighting was installed. In 2001, the interior of the church was rearranged to create a church in the round. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/568dcf61-058f-4b50-8fef-3cb4f3578fd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean town centre was established and laid out as an extension of Guildford townsite in the 1840s but it was not until the 1890s that the town developed rapidly with the influx of workers during the gold boom period. The town (previously referred to as West Guildford) was named Bassendean in 1922 after one of the first farms in the district - ‘Bassendean’ - established by the First Colonial Secretary, Peter Broun. The town developed on either side of what was called the Perth Road and later, Guildford Road, which logically connected Perth to Guildford.

Once very much a working class suburb, in recent years Bassendean has been gentrified and Old Perth Road strip is now home to a number of eclectic bars, cafes and art spaces. Why not linger a little longer and enjoy a coffee or a beer after completing this Adventure Lab. 

(Cover Photo Credit - www.bassendean.net)
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9041" lon="115.948117">
  <ele>23.0</ele>
  <time>2024-07-28T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBiAiB-04</name>
  <desc>BassendeanTown4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f</url>
  <urlname>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S4 Bassendean Hotel by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915750" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S4 Bassendean Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bassendean Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Look up at the circular Bassendean Hotel signs (featuring a black swan) jutting out from each side of the hotel. What YEAR is featured?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/845e6982-09ee-4487-b487-93f1df3c6895" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At least 5 attempts were made to establish a hotel in Bassendean (then West Guildford) and in one instance in 1905, there was an unsuccessful attempt to re-locate the licence of the Stirling Arms to the town. Several further attempts were made, but the inevitable decision was that West Guildford should stay dry.
Following the Gold Rushes and its perceived excesses, there was a strong reaction to the sale and consumption of alcohol and the temperance movement began to hold sway. Hotel licences were harder to obtain and temperance hotels and coffee palaces became more common. Finally, in I927 a 468 signature petition commenced a process that would eventually lead to the construction and licencing of the Bassendean Hotel.

The Bassendean Hotel was built in 1929 for Patrick Connolly by builders Blackmore Brothers to a design by architects J.H.O. Hargrave and E.S. Porter. 
 
The Hotel caused considerable controversy as many community members and churches in the district valued a teetotal lifestyle. An active opposition group formulated a well organised 'No License' campaign to keep Bassendean free of licensed hotels. The campaign failed to gain support with the legislators and the license was granted in 1930 after five attempts.  

Because it was built in the period of restricted trading hours, this and other hotels like and including the Bassendean Hotels were made robust to withstand the ‘six o’clock swill'.

The Hotel was extensively refurbished over a two year period from 2020, re-opening in September 2022. There is a considerable collection of photographs from the early days of the township displayed throughout the hotel which is well worth looking at. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/568dcf61-058f-4b50-8fef-3cb4f3578fd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean town centre was established and laid out as an extension of Guildford townsite in the 1840s but it was not until the 1890s that the town developed rapidly with the influx of workers during the gold boom period. The town (previously referred to as West Guildford) was named Bassendean in 1922 after one of the first farms in the district - ‘Bassendean’ - established by the First Colonial Secretary, Peter Broun. The town developed on either side of what was called the Perth Road and later, Guildford Road, which logically connected Perth to Guildford.

Once very much a working class suburb, in recent years Bassendean has been gentrified and Old Perth Road strip is now home to a number of eclectic bars, cafes and art spaces. Why not linger a little longer and enjoy a coffee or a beer after completing this Adventure Lab. 

(Cover Photo Credit - www.bassendean.net)
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.903733" lon="115.94755">
