  <urlname>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S5 Bassendean Padbury's Buildings by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476915752" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings : S5 Bassendean Padbury's Buildings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bassendean Town Centre Historical Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bassendean Padbury's Buildings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/348e501c-f21f-482b-857b-06890b5b703f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the YEAR written in the circular deco at the top of the Padbury's Buildings facade?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/21c72a1c-95b9-4c48-bedc-07052b52a548" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Located on the corner of Old Perth Road and Guildford Road, Bassendean,  Padbury's Building was built in 1918 by William Padbury (1867–1951) - a successful businessman, ship owner, pastoralist, politician and philanthropist throughout his life in the district. William Padbury was the nephew of Walter Padbury,  the notable nineteenth century entrepreneur who is associated with Padbury’s Colonial Stores in Guildford. 

The building is a good representative example of a medium sized Federation Free Classical style commercial building in a prominent location, and is a landmark building in Bassendean. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/568dcf61-058f-4b50-8fef-3cb4f3578fd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bassendean town centre was established and laid out as an extension of Guildford townsite in the 1840s but it was not until the 1890s that the town developed rapidly with the influx of workers during the gold boom period. The town (previously referred to as West Guildford) was named Bassendean in 1922 after one of the first farms in the district - ‘Bassendean’ - established by the First Colonial Secretary, Peter Broun. The town developed on either side of what was called the Perth Road and later, Guildford Road, which logically connected Perth to Guildford.

Once very much a working class suburb, in recent years Bassendean has been gentrified and Old Perth Road strip is now home to a number of eclectic bars, cafes and art spaces. Why not linger a little longer and enjoy a coffee or a beer after completing this Adventure Lab. 

(Cover Photo Credit - www.bassendean.net)
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
