   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Plain ~ Travel Trivia&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Theme: Space Junk&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was NASA fined $400 for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/62868113-b4d1-4ec4-bfd6-e56abcb1e009" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1979, a huge chunk of the (somewhat dodgy) Skylab space station fell to earth in remote regions of Western Australia, and in South Australia near Balladonia. This occurred after NASA technicians realised the device could land near a heavily populated area and changed its angle of flight to direct it towards the Indian Ocean. The then US President, Jimmy Carter, called the Balladonia Roadhouse to apologise for the mess. An Australian government inspector was sent out to review the situation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/44f55e29-0048-415c-82ff-0c5d3cb4ba1c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Nullarbor (Latin for no trees) trivia game. 

The aim is to break up your drive across the 90 mile straight. Australia’s longest straight road at 146.6km. This stretch of Eyre Highway runs from Caiguna (east) to Balladonia (west).

The Nullarbor Plain is a flat, treeless, expanse of limestone. ‘Tho unlikely you’ll see much (if any) wildlife during the day - I had to slow for a dingo early one morning as it padded across the highway. At the Head of Bight whales bask close to the spectacular Bunda Cliffs. 

All questions have multiple choice answers. You can log the waypoints in any order and either direction of travel.

Edward John Eyre (1841) described the Nullarbor Plain as “a hideous anomaly, a blot on the face of nature, the sort of place one gets into in bad dreams.” 

My view - It is one of the most extraordinary and peaceful places on Earth! 

Happy trails! 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.3777" lon="124.614683">
