   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Plain ~ Travel Trivia&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Theme: UFO Activity&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Mr Henley described the object as looking like an upside-down?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b605982e-ecc4-4758-bf16-1379c7e7ca46" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In January 1988, the Knowles family was travelling east across the Nullarbor Plain at night (never a good idea as the wildlife is out and about) and the car was buzzed by a brightly-lit object. The Knowles later claimed the car was lifted up and a tyre burst when it dropped back on to the road. The family was terrified and reported the encounter to police at Ceduna. And the family car was left coated in a fine dark dust of unknown composition. A truck driver named Mr Graham Henley also (independently) reported seeing this object on the same night.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/44f55e29-0048-415c-82ff-0c5d3cb4ba1c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Nullarbor (Latin for no trees) trivia game. 

The aim is to break up your drive across the 90 mile straight. Australia’s longest straight road at 146.6km. This stretch of Eyre Highway runs from Caiguna (east) to Balladonia (west).

The Nullarbor Plain is a flat, treeless, expanse of limestone. ‘Tho unlikely you’ll see much (if any) wildlife during the day - I had to slow for a dingo early one morning as it padded across the highway. At the Head of Bight whales bask close to the spectacular Bunda Cliffs. 

All questions have multiple choice answers. You can log the waypoints in any order and either direction of travel.

Edward John Eyre (1841) described the Nullarbor Plain as “a hideous anomaly, a blot on the face of nature, the sort of place one gets into in bad dreams.” 

My view - It is one of the most extraordinary and peaceful places on Earth! 

Happy trails! 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
