   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Nullarbor Plain ~ Travel Trivia&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Theme: S.O.S.&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fb8b50bf-d40f-47c6-b53e-29ecf52727ae&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Who uses emergency landing strips on the Eyre Highway?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c933643-b11a-4c89-86ca-12045d9b065a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;You’ll come across sections of the Eyre Highway that are signposted and marked out as emergency landing strips. At only 9 metres wide the road is narrower than the usual minimum for an airstrip. If a patient retrieval is required, local emergency services and police will close the highway to traffic in both directions. You won’t find your family vehicle or caravan competing for space with low-flying aircraft.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/44f55e29-0048-415c-82ff-0c5d3cb4ba1c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Nullarbor (Latin for no trees) trivia game. 

The aim is to break up your drive across the 90 mile straight. Australia’s longest straight road at 146.6km. This stretch of Eyre Highway runs from Caiguna (east) to Balladonia (west).

The Nullarbor Plain is a flat, treeless, expanse of limestone. ‘Tho unlikely you’ll see much (if any) wildlife during the day - I had to slow for a dingo early one morning as it padded across the highway. At the Head of Bight whales bask close to the spectacular Bunda Cliffs. 

All questions have multiple choice answers. You can log the waypoints in any order and either direction of travel.

Edward John Eyre (1841) described the Nullarbor Plain as “a hideous anomaly, a blot on the face of nature, the sort of place one gets into in bad dreams.” 

My view - It is one of the most extraordinary and peaceful places on Earth! 

Happy trails! 
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
