   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Kalamunda ARTWALK&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;SCARP&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aec90b74-e1b2-4b94-8c52-d34f4524922c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/aec90b74-e1b2-4b94-8c52-d34f4524922c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was this sculpture commissioned.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c586f620-14b5-4747-897b-9be82388bd64" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Artists: Malcolm Harris and Matthew Harding
Two timber screens were commissioned to mark the 
entrance and exit of the Zig Zag Cultural Centre for its 
opening. They are made from jarrah purlins that 
once supported the roofs of some of the Midland Railway Workshop buildings, embellished with slumped and UV coloured glass insets.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/146ebc3e-34cb-40f0-9813-24586f45d722" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Kalamunda ArtWalk takes you past public artworks within the Kalamunda Town Centre, with extensions to the Kalamunda Community Centre and the City of Kalamunda administration building. It begins at the Perth Hills Kalamunda Visitor Centre on Railway Road.

The ArtWalk brochure can be collected at the Visitor Centre, 50 Railway Road, Kalamunda or downloaded online. The Centre is open 7 days a week, 9am to 4pm during the week, and 10am to 4pm on weekends and most public holidays.

All stages can be accessed for free and are available 24/7.

There is a bonus cache (GCAXNGH) that can be located using information in the Journal.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
