  <name>LBijLZ-03</name>
  <desc>LeightonBatter3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e</url>
  <urlname>Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels : S3 Command Post by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1008" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels : S3 Command Post</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Command Post&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the sign “Leighton Battery Command Post”, what year was the battery closed&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/33fb7219-224e-41fb-a41d-b59ee71a73d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This command post was commission in 1948 to control the 5.25 inch guns at the modernized Leighton battery. 

These guns could engage both surface and air targets and the command post used both coastal and air warning radars to identify targets. 

Leighton battery was the only 5.25 inch battery to be commissioned in Australia and was used as the training battery for regular army gunners and national service men. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9469aeb4-c835-417f-909d-59b004366eeb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There is a Bonus Box for this Adventure Lab - GCAZ3AK

Leighton Battery at Buckland Hill in Mosman Park was part of the coastal defences of Australia during WWII and the Fremantle Fortress protecting Fremantle Harbour. 

The battery initially consisted of two 6-inch MK VII naval guns, operational from February 1943. These guns were removed in 1945 and replaced by three 5.25 inch dual purpose coastal artillery/anti aircraft guns. While it was envisioned to have all major ports in Australia protected by these type of guns, the Leighton Battery became the only one ever operational. The battery was dismantled and the equipment disposed of for scrap in 1963. 


The battery site was added to the Western Australian State Register of Heritage Places in August 1999 and was awarded an Engineering Heritage National Marker in November 2014. While some above-ground installations have been restored, the underground installations consisting of underground tunnels, rooms, observation post and semi-buried command post are considered an outstanding example of technical achievement.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
