  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e</url>
  <urlname>Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels : S4 3.7-inch Anti Aircraft Gun by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1009" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels : S4 3.7-inch Anti Aircraft Gun</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Leighton Battery WWII Tunnels&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;3.7-inch Anti Aircraft Gun&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8917b15f-7c95-4c18-9032-ea9d627c497e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the sign “3.7inch Heavy Anti Aircraft Gun”, how many personnel manned the gun in action? (Number)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/91a0e164-0c53-467e-9f8a-f0adf0c44062" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The 3.7 inch (94mm) Heavy anti-aircraft gun was designed in Britain shortly before the outbreak of World War 2 replacing the 3 inch gun that had been in service since World War 1. 

It was mass produced in Britain, Canada and Australia and used in all theatres of conflict during WW2 and in post war training in Australia until 1965. 

The fun fired a shell weighing 28lbs (12.7kg) to a maximum height of 32,000 feet (9755m). A fuse was set to explode at a predicted height against an attacking aircraft.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9469aeb4-c835-417f-909d-59b004366eeb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There is a Bonus Box for this Adventure Lab - GCAZ3AK

Leighton Battery at Buckland Hill in Mosman Park was part of the coastal defences of Australia during WWII and the Fremantle Fortress protecting Fremantle Harbour. 

The battery initially consisted of two 6-inch MK VII naval guns, operational from February 1943. These guns were removed in 1945 and replaced by three 5.25 inch dual purpose coastal artillery/anti aircraft guns. While it was envisioned to have all major ports in Australia protected by these type of guns, the Leighton Battery became the only one ever operational. The battery was dismantled and the equipment disposed of for scrap in 1963. 


The battery site was added to the Western Australian State Register of Heritage Places in August 1999 and was awarded an Engineering Heritage National Marker in November 2014. While some above-ground installations have been restored, the underground installations consisting of underground tunnels, rooms, observation post and semi-buried command post are considered an outstanding example of technical achievement.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
