 <wpt lat="-31.945017" lon="115.8308">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
  <time>2024-10-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7ozt-02</name>
  <desc>HomeOfFootball2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4</url>
  <urlname>The Home of Football : S2 Eagles Race by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918199" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Home of Football : S2 Eagles Race</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Home of Football&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Eagles Race&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where does the author remember watching from? (3 words as written including punctuation)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5f8b8ce4-4071-4f30-b5eb-ded4507b46eb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the late 1990s, Subiaco Oval underwent a series of significant redevelopments to modernize the venue and increase its seating capacity. One of the major changes during this period was the completion of the grandstands at the city end of the field, which had previously been an open grassed hill for spectators to sit or stand on. This project effectively filled in the final sections of the stadium, creating a fully enclosed seating arrangement that enhanced the atmosphere for fans and players alike.

At the same time, the West Coast Eagles built new club headquarters at the oval. This relocation also impacted the team’s match-day routine, as the new facilities were positioned at the city end of the stadium, and the Eagles began running out onto the field from this location.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df723db2-d3d4-40aa-ba44-1c78a10e77c6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For over 100 years Subiaco Oval was the home of football in Western Australia.

Established in 1908, it served as the home ground for the Subiaco Football Club and later became the main venue for Australian Football League (AFL) matches in Western Australia.

The oval was initially a modest facility but underwent multiple redevelopments over the years to increase its capacity and improve its amenities. By the time it was fully developed, Subiaco Oval could accommodate over 43,000 spectators, making it the largest stadium in Western Australia.

It hosted many memorable AFL games, including home matches for the West Coast Eagles (from their inception in 1987) and Fremantle Dockers (from 1995). The oval also hosted other sports like rugby, cricket, and soccer, as well as concerts by international artists.

In 2018, Subiaco Oval was officially closed following the opening of the new Perth Stadium, which took over as the primary venue for major sports events in Perth. Following its closure, the oval was partially demolished, but the playing surface and some of its heritage features were preserved as part of a redevelopment plan to integrate the site into the new Bob Hawke College and surrounding community areas.

Subiaco Oval holds a special place in the hearts of Western Australians, symbolizing over a century of local and national sporting achievements.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9442" lon="115.8308">
  <ele>41.0</ele>
  <time>2024-10-20T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB7ozt-01</name>
  <desc>HomeOfFootball1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4</url>
  <urlname>The Home of Football : S1 Let There Be Light by Cirrus_WA</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918197" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>The Home of Football : S1 Let There Be Light</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Cirrus_WA</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Home of Football&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Let There Be Light&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the result for the first AFL fixture played under flood lights in 1997? (As written including punctuation)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cadbc681-3790-4a77-aa19-8c94560083ad" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For much of the venue's history, night fixtures were notably absent from Subiaco Oval due to the lack of ground lighting. As a result, the WACA Ground in East Perth, despite being traditionally a cricket venue, became the primary location for night games due to its existing lighting infrastructure.

This changed ahead of the 1997 AFL season, when significant upgrades were made to Subiaco Oval. Four large lighting towers were constructed near the flanks at each end of the ground, enabling night football to be held at Subiaco for the first time. These towers were strategically placed to ensure optimal illumination of the field, allowing both the fans in attendance and television broadcasts to clearly follow the fast-paced action of the game.

Although the original towers have since been replaced, the current towers are positioned in the same locations as the originals.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df723db2-d3d4-40aa-ba44-1c78a10e77c6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For over 100 years Subiaco Oval was the home of football in Western Australia.

Established in 1908, it served as the home ground for the Subiaco Football Club and later became the main venue for Australian Football League (AFL) matches in Western Australia.

The oval was initially a modest facility but underwent multiple redevelopments over the years to increase its capacity and improve its amenities. By the time it was fully developed, Subiaco Oval could accommodate over 43,000 spectators, making it the largest stadium in Western Australia.

It hosted many memorable AFL games, including home matches for the West Coast Eagles (from their inception in 1987) and Fremantle Dockers (from 1995). The oval also hosted other sports like rugby, cricket, and soccer, as well as concerts by international artists.

In 2018, Subiaco Oval was officially closed following the opening of the new Perth Stadium, which took over as the primary venue for major sports events in Perth. Following its closure, the oval was partially demolished, but the playing surface and some of its heritage features were preserved as part of a redevelopment plan to integrate the site into the new Bob Hawke College and surrounding community areas.

Subiaco Oval holds a special place in the hearts of Western Australians, symbolizing over a century of local and national sporting achievements.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9449" lon="115.829317">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
