   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;The Home of Football&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Subiaco Oval Pavilion&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1513cc67-dd91-458b-ad70-d415a2fe69f4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What Blocks are listed on the sign at the bottom of the walkway? ( _ _ _ &amp; _ _ _ )&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/88efdd81-f7c9-4446-8d64-1e0deec18a2d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Subiaco Oval Pavilion, located on the northern side of the oval, stands as one of the most well-preserved sections of the original stadium. It retains much of the infrastructure that once defined the venue, including original bench seating, concrete seating tiers, and signage. Notably, the players' and officials' dugouts also remain intact.

Behind the seating area, visitors can explore an open-air museum dedicated to Western Australian football history. This space showcases photographs and exhibits that celebrate the legacy of football at Subiaco Oval and across the state. In addition, the area offers modern amenities, including a kiosk, change rooms, and bathroom facilities, as well as a playground, making it a family-friendly destination. The preserved elements of the Subiaco Oval Pavilion offer fans and visitors a nostalgic connection to one of Western Australia's most iconic sporting venues.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df723db2-d3d4-40aa-ba44-1c78a10e77c6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For over 100 years Subiaco Oval was the home of football in Western Australia.

Established in 1908, it served as the home ground for the Subiaco Football Club and later became the main venue for Australian Football League (AFL) matches in Western Australia.

The oval was initially a modest facility but underwent multiple redevelopments over the years to increase its capacity and improve its amenities. By the time it was fully developed, Subiaco Oval could accommodate over 43,000 spectators, making it the largest stadium in Western Australia.

It hosted many memorable AFL games, including home matches for the West Coast Eagles (from their inception in 1987) and Fremantle Dockers (from 1995). The oval also hosted other sports like rugby, cricket, and soccer, as well as concerts by international artists.

In 2018, Subiaco Oval was officially closed following the opening of the new Perth Stadium, which took over as the primary venue for major sports events in Perth. Following its closure, the oval was partially demolished, but the playing surface and some of its heritage features were preserved as part of a redevelopment plan to integrate the site into the new Bob Hawke College and surrounding community areas.

Subiaco Oval holds a special place in the hearts of Western Australians, symbolizing over a century of local and national sporting achievements.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.944083" lon="115.829483">
  <ele>53.0</ele>
