  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/798602c9-7976-4e1d-b1bf-89d262d721a4</url>
  <urlname>Harewood Forest Walk Trail : S4 Free land! by richlink</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918193" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Harewood Forest Walk Trail : S4 Free land!</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Harewood Forest Walk Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Free land!&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/798602c9-7976-4e1d-b1bf-89d262d721a4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/798602c9-7976-4e1d-b1bf-89d262d721a4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many rooms did a typical group settlement cottage have? (digit)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e92fd14b-3156-4b43-9c61-7935f40164c8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;During the 1920's the WA Government offered free land to settlers from the UK under the Group Settlement Scheme.
The karri forest, with fertile soils and high rainfall seemed a farmer's paradise. Settlers thought that soils that grew huge trees should produce bumper agricultural crops.
Settlers came to the Denmark area - but most ended up walking off the land because of the back breaking work of clearing the trees and then finding the soil depleted and useless for farming.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/95b8cddc-dbe0-4bbe-970f-a5bcfda09104" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Logged of all large timber in the late 1800's, Harewood Forest has recovered and is today a great example of regrowth karri forest.
Now you can walk among the tall karris and the whispery fronds of the karri oak with their lush undergrowth of tassel flower and karri hazel. Small birds such as re-winged fairy-wrens and white-breasted robins' flit through the under-storey while purple-crowned lorikeets chatter loudly in the tree tops when the karri is in bloom.

Well defined circuit trail that takes around 1 hour to complete. Dogs are welcome but must be on a lead.

Mobile reception can be sketchy so ensure you have opened the LAB adventure before setting off!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.92645" lon="117.291933">
