   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Harewood Forest Walk Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Growing rich on resources&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/798602c9-7976-4e1d-b1bf-89d262d721a4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/798602c9-7976-4e1d-b1bf-89d262d721a4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the name of the Timber &amp; Trading Company (one word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/901e01be-07f8-4d66-8209-79ffd0fbc131" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;WA is blessed with many natural resources.
Since English colonial settlement at Albany in 1826, resourceful people travelled to the surrounds to make a living from fishing, farming or the timber industry.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/95b8cddc-dbe0-4bbe-970f-a5bcfda09104" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Logged of all large timber in the late 1800's, Harewood Forest has recovered and is today a great example of regrowth karri forest.
Now you can walk among the tall karris and the whispery fronds of the karri oak with their lush undergrowth of tassel flower and karri hazel. Small birds such as re-winged fairy-wrens and white-breasted robins' flit through the under-storey while purple-crowned lorikeets chatter loudly in the tree tops when the karri is in bloom.

Well defined circuit trail that takes around 1 hour to complete. Dogs are welcome but must be on a lead.

Mobile reception can be sketchy so ensure you have opened the LAB adventure before setting off!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
