  <urlname>Diving into Dunsborough  : S1 Diving into Dunsborough by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918936" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Diving into Dunsborough  : S1 Diving into Dunsborough</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Diving into Dunsborough &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Diving into Dunsborough &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9c2e9861-9fd2-4d0b-b964-a866c82d5779"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9c2e9861-9fd2-4d0b-b964-a866c82d5779&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the school mentioned on the mural? 3 words&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/864f66f2-1220-4b15-bb21-24b70efa4939" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This mural depicting a diver diving into the unknown depths of the ocean.
Diving around Dunsborough showcases some amazing sites, plenty of marine life, and the HMAS Swan Wreck, which is one of the largest and most accessible dive wrecks in the Southern Hemisphere! After serving the Royal Australian Navy for two decades, the 113 metre decommissioned naval destroyer was scuttled off the coast of Dunsborough in 1997. It now rests on the ocean floor, transformed into one of Australia’s most popular dive wreck sites a teeming with life.
Sitting in 32 metres of water at the deepest point, the HMAS Swan reaches up 24m from the sea floor, with the crows nest sitting in 8m of turquoise water. This means there is ample exploring to be done at all levels, suitable for open water divers through to technical divers. Being sunk purposely as a dive wreck, it is truly a spectacular dive, with many large hull cut-outs allowing you to explore inside and out!
Beyond the wreck itself, there is a diverse range of marine life to be seen. From large grouper and batfish outside to schools of bullseye fish and Western Blue Devils inside the ships rooms.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cccc8088-294e-470c-b58e-db52c9030b45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Diving into Dunsborough will show you some of the murals that have been painted around town to enhance the ‘arty vibe’ throughout this iconic holiday destination.
The murals have been painted by an Australian artist exploring beauty through nature, characters and details. He creates work on a variety of scales using acrylics, aerosol, and inks. Abstract brushstrokes and layered backgrounds are detailed with entertaining whimsical illustrations of characters, trees, birds, animals, colliding universes and patterns. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.8574" lon="114.986717">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
  <time>2024-11-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEHni-02</name>
  <desc>GracetownS2Grac</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8</url>
  <urlname>Gracetown  : S2 Gracetown Lookout by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918744" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Gracetown  : S2 Gracetown Lookout</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Gracetown &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gracetown Lookout&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Giant Humpback Whales and Southern  Right Whales depend on what microscopic food &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/86f7e9f1-b918-4eb9-8587-53a21224523e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Gracetown, you will find a very laid back town that revolves around surfing, tourism and wineries. 
Enough to make your lay days a little bit more pleasant.

The best time to surf the West Australia coast is pretty much all year long. Ground swells are consistently pushing decent waves on the coast throughout the year. 
However, in winter, waves will be bigger than summer and one should expect colder water temperature. 
The summer months will also be a little more crowded.

From the lookout up here, we have just been the whales breaching and frolicking about as they traveled south.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e84b086a-3b84-4b94-b434-3d548f1fcaf4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Time to unwind and enjoy the waters of Gracetown.
A waterfront seaside holiday town, set on the shores of the Indian Ocean where the Cowaramup Brook meets the Ocean.
The waters here at Gracetown are great for swimming, snorkeling, diving and surfing.
There are safe beaches, great surf breaks, interesting reefs for snorkeling and diving.
There’s also a dedicated shark patrol helicopter that patrols the coastline and safety features to warn swimmers or surfers to get out of the water if there is any imminent danger.

There’s also plenty of fishing off the coastline.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-33.861533" lon="114.988917">
  <ele>6.0</ele>
  <time>2024-11-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEHni-01</name>
  <desc>GracetownS1Cowa</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8</url>
  <urlname>Gracetown  : S1 Cowaramup Reef by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918742" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Gracetown  : S1 Cowaramup Reef</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Gracetown &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cowaramup Reef&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/82759dca-2020-447d-8654-35e67b7e09f8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many bollards are roped together on the eastern side of the gazebos.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dba9b315-4099-420a-a9b3-d06d54e772e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first recorded use of the area was  as a holiday area and later in 1957 it was proposed that the area should be developed into a camping and caravan park. 
The area was surveyed and the town of Gracetown was gazetted in 1963, subsequently named in honour of Grace Bussell.
The camping area was designated on the other side of the main road into town, with access to the local beach here, where the Cowaramup Brook meets the Indian Ocean.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e84b086a-3b84-4b94-b434-3d548f1fcaf4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Time to unwind and enjoy the waters of Gracetown.
A waterfront seaside holiday town, set on the shores of the Indian Ocean where the Cowaramup Brook meets the Ocean.
The waters here at Gracetown are great for swimming, snorkeling, diving and surfing.
There are safe beaches, great surf breaks, interesting reefs for snorkeling and diving.
There’s also a dedicated shark patrol helicopter that patrols the coastline and safety features to warn swimmers or surfers to get out of the water if there is any imminent danger.

There’s also plenty of fishing off the coastline.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
