   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Yallingup &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Torpedo Rocks&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5bea9843-e065-451b-8ed2-fd50eefaa2b2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5bea9843-e065-451b-8ed2-fd50eefaa2b2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There is a statue here representing a Wadandi Songline, her name is?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d52185c4-3bc9-4f96-9c2f-3cfa967e4354" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Torpedo Rocks is between Yallingup and Smiths Beach. 
A great spot to watch the whales breach and frolic in the waters and watch the sunset over the Indian Ocean.
It is a popular but potentially dangerous fishing location that offers the opportunity to access deeper water in an area of very rocky coastline with significant swells. 
It can also be a good place to view the beautiful scenery in this area. It is easily accessed by a short detour off the Cape to Cape walking trail that passes through the Yallingup coastal hills. 
This is a late afternoon scene looking south towards Smiths Beach.
There is a statue here representing a Wadandi Songline.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/90e35f59-b81b-422c-820e-45489461b9a1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to Yallingup
A surfer’s paradise.
Surfers have been coming to Yallingup since the late 1940’s.

Yallingup Beach has it all – from big waves offshore, to a sheltered lagoon perfect for safe family swimming. It's also a popular place to learn to surf.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
