   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bayswater Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Ellis House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number on the letterbox?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/814f156d-9652-49ed-aeb9-4dc549ba18ef" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Construction on Ellis House first began in 1912, and the weatherboard building originally served the area as a local dairy owned by the Ellis family who were British migrants of 1911. Today there are few traces of the local dairy industry which was a major activity in the Bayswater area, making this an old homestead of great historic value.

In 1995, the City of Bayswater restored the property which today is open to the public as a community
art centre and gallery.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c152985a-03e2-4f2f-a62d-3e05b7c48485" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Despite the establishment of the Swan River Colony in 1829, Bayswater remained relatively undeveloped (other than land lots for gardening and farming) until the early 1900s. Once the Midland Railway Workshops opened, an influx of workers from the eastern states and Great Britain led to the establishment of many more permanent and substantial structures across the locality including a number of commercial properties along King William Street. 

This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the Heritage-listed locations both in the town centre and close to the Swan River. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.925433" lon="115.9195">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2024-12-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbBaB-05</name>
  <desc>BayswaterHerit5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c</url>
  <urlname>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S5 Leighton (Halliday) House by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918321" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S5 Leighton (Halliday) House</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bayswater Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Leighton (Halliday) House&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When was the Bayswater Road Board first elected? (Format as per plaque - month day, year)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/69823c49-3625-4c8e-9709-b1756e005282" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Halliday House (or ‘Leighton House’ as it was originally named) was built by the Halliday family in the 1890’s during Bayswater’s pioneering phase. ‘Leighton House’ was a substantial development in Federation Queen Anne style , reflecting the family’s resources and faith in Bayswater’s future. The house also served as meeting place for the small local Baptist congregation. 

In recent years, the house has been restored and was transferred to the Bayswater Historical Society who maintain the house as a museum and headquarters of the historical society.

Look for the plaque outside the front gate to the property. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c152985a-03e2-4f2f-a62d-3e05b7c48485" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Despite the establishment of the Swan River Colony in 1829, Bayswater remained relatively undeveloped (other than land lots for gardening and farming) until the early 1900s. Once the Midland Railway Workshops opened, an influx of workers from the eastern states and Great Britain led to the establishment of many more permanent and substantial structures across the locality including a number of commercial properties along King William Street. 

This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the Heritage-listed locations both in the town centre and close to the Swan River. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9194" lon="115.912683">
