  <desc>BayswaterHerit2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c</url>
  <urlname>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S2 Billiard Saloon by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918315" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S2 Billiard Saloon</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bayswater Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Billiard Saloon&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What number King William Street are you outside? (numeral)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f04e6ef7-d3db-4709-b113-e120eb017e7c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A small billiard saloon was opened by Jack Smart on this site around 1919. In 1921, the Marshall family moved from the Goldfields and bought Smart's saloon, increasing the size of the operation and adding a hairdressing business in an adjacent shed. In 1922, the front of the site was further developed into two stores - a women's and a men's hairdressing salon.

Originally the building was meant to be two storeys, but the Marshall's young son Bob was prone to sleep walking, so it was decided that a single storied building was the safer option! Bob received his early billiards' training in this saloon, and went on to become an international billiard's champion.

Look for the plaque at your feet outside the front door of the building. 



&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c152985a-03e2-4f2f-a62d-3e05b7c48485" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Despite the establishment of the Swan River Colony in 1829, Bayswater remained relatively undeveloped (other than land lots for gardening and farming) until the early 1900s. Once the Midland Railway Workshops opened, an influx of workers from the eastern states and Great Britain led to the establishment of many more permanent and substantial structures across the locality including a number of commercial properties along King William Street. 

This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the Heritage-listed locations both in the town centre and close to the Swan River. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.91875" lon="115.91195">
