  <urlname>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S1 Inglis's Buildings by familysteiner</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918313" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Bayswater Heritage Buildings : S1 Inglis's Buildings</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>familysteiner</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">familysteiner</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Bayswater Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Inglis's Buildings&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/01d023b9-9d1e-418f-af96-0892f47d173c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the year of construction featured on this plaque?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/db396fe9-7913-4eb8-82ea-819e78216f86" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Designed by architect Henry Halliday, these premises were the first business to be constructed along the railway line in Bayswater. Over time there have been a number of tenants, including Campbells Grocery Store (until 1919), and another grocery store owned by the McLeish family during WW2. In the late 1950s, Jack Percival relocated his chemist shop from further up Whatley Crescent, and a pharmacy continues to operate from this site to this day.

Look for the plaque at your feet outside the front door of the building. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c152985a-03e2-4f2f-a62d-3e05b7c48485" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Despite the establishment of the Swan River Colony in 1829, Bayswater remained relatively undeveloped (other than land lots for gardening and farming) until the early 1900s. Once the Midland Railway Workshops opened, an influx of workers from the eastern states and Great Britain led to the establishment of many more permanent and substantial structures across the locality including a number of commercial properties along King William Street. 

This Adventure Lab will take you to some of the Heritage-listed locations both in the town centre and close to the Swan River. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.965683" lon="115.82205">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2024-12-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtauE-03</name>
  <desc>AncientKingsPa3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492</url>
  <urlname>Ancient Kings Park : S3 Mega Marsupial by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918327" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Ancient Kings Park : S3 Mega Marsupial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Ancient Kings Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mega Marsupial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the last word in the paragraph?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ac3e3c7d-49ee-4eda-ac26-b4c8a8b6c471" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Diprotodon is from the Pleistocene epoch. The earliest finds date back to 1.77 million years ago.

Diprotodon is the largest-known marsupial to have ever lived; it greatly exceeds the size of its closest living relatives wombats and koalas. 

It is the only marsupial and metatherian that is known to have made seasonal migrations. Large herds, usually of females, seem to have marched through a wide range of habitats to find food and water, walking at around 6 km/h (3.7 mph). Diprotodon may have formed polygynous societies, possibly using its powerful incisors to fight for mates or fend off predators, such as the largest-known marsupial carnivore Thylacoleo carnifex. Being a marsupial, the mother may have raised her joey in a pouch on her belly, probably with one of these facing backwards, as in wombats.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71620841-cd5a-4128-a31e-55b99a3e7830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland (or Synergy Parkland), in Kings Park, has to be a favourite spot amongst Perth families. Also known as the “Dinosaur Park”, thanks to it’s many life-sized dinosaur statues. 

There is little zig-zagged bridges lead to the island in the middle of the pond, Lycopod Island. Sprouting up on the island like Robinson Crusoe’s treehouse is a playground, the first of two children’s playgrounds at Synergy Parkland Kings Park. To the North of the Lake is another playground - Arthur Fairall Playground. On a weekend and school holidays this park is popular for children.

Parent's may be able to enjoy Zamia Cafe, is an ideal location for a casual family breakfast or lunch - but can be very busy too!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.965417" lon="115.821833">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
