  <desc>AncientKingsPa2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492</url>
  <urlname>Ancient Kings Park : S2 Living Rocks by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918325" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Ancient Kings Park : S2 Living Rocks</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Ancient Kings Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Living Rocks&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where were the first living stromatolites discovered in Western Australia?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19086063-a32b-424e-a501-cefc178eaaf8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stromatolites and Thrombolites are lumpy layered living rocks. 

What is the difference?

Stromatolites have a finely laminated structure, meaning they are made up of layers. They are formed by colonies of cyanobacteria, microscopic organisms that photosynthesize.

Thrombolites have a non-laminated, clotted structure. They are formed by biofilms of microorganisms, especially cyanobacteria, trapping, binding, and cementing sedimentary grains. Thrombolites are massive with a macroscopic clotted fabric. And the patterns can be seen by the naked eye.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71620841-cd5a-4128-a31e-55b99a3e7830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland (or Synergy Parkland), in Kings Park, has to be a favourite spot amongst Perth families. Also known as the “Dinosaur Park”, thanks to it’s many life-sized dinosaur statues. 

There is little zig-zagged bridges lead to the island in the middle of the pond, Lycopod Island. Sprouting up on the island like Robinson Crusoe’s treehouse is a playground, the first of two children’s playgrounds at Synergy Parkland Kings Park. To the North of the Lake is another playground - Arthur Fairall Playground. On a weekend and school holidays this park is popular for children.

Parent's may be able to enjoy Zamia Cafe, is an ideal location for a casual family breakfast or lunch - but can be very busy too!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.96495" lon="115.8223">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2024-12-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtauE-04</name>
  <desc>AncientKingsPa4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492</url>
  <urlname>Ancient Kings Park : S4 Mutta..burra...whata? by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918329" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Ancient Kings Park : S4 Mutta..burra...whata?</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Ancient Kings Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Mutta..burra...whata?&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many million years ago were the Muttaburrasaurus last seen? (number)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e1b6d92-f3c3-44e4-9857-b4ad90f3411f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Muttaburrasaurus was a large, plant-eating ornithopod from the Early Cretaceous of eastern Australia. It is one of the most complete dinosaurs from Australia and the first to be cast and mounted for display. Muttaburrasaurus had an unusual skull with a long, rounded snout.

Muttaburrasaurus had many other features seen in other basal ornithopods, including reduced forelimbs and a long, stiffened tail. Based on the length and strength of its limbs, Muttaburrasaurus may have been able to move on either its two back legs or on all four legs.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71620841-cd5a-4128-a31e-55b99a3e7830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland (or Synergy Parkland), in Kings Park, has to be a favourite spot amongst Perth families. Also known as the “Dinosaur Park”, thanks to it’s many life-sized dinosaur statues. 

There is little zig-zagged bridges lead to the island in the middle of the pond, Lycopod Island. Sprouting up on the island like Robinson Crusoe’s treehouse is a playground, the first of two children’s playgrounds at Synergy Parkland Kings Park. To the North of the Lake is another playground - Arthur Fairall Playground. On a weekend and school holidays this park is popular for children.

Parent's may be able to enjoy Zamia Cafe, is an ideal location for a casual family breakfast or lunch - but can be very busy too!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.965517" lon="115.821433">
