  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492</url>
  <urlname>Ancient Kings Park : S1 Bullockornis (dromornis planei) by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918323" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Ancient Kings Park : S1 Bullockornis (dromornis planei)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Ancient Kings Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bullockornis (dromornis planei)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What name is on top of the sign which describes this bird (1 word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4f2e2109-324c-4259-abf6-5c86e5bbf3d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dromornis planei was a massive bird with a formidable bill and lived in the Miocene Epoch (24 million - 5 million years ago). It belonged to a uniquely Australian family of extinct flightless birds, the dromornithids (mihirungs). Because of the close relationship between mihirungs and ducks, Dromornis planei has been nicknamed the 'Demon Duck of Doom'. 

Identification
Dromornis planei was a heavily built bird with a long neck and enormous legs. It was as tall as an ostrich but far more massive. The skull of Dromornis planei was similar in size to a horse’s head and had a deep, curved bill. It about 2.5m tall and 300kg in weight.

Most of the wing bones are unknown for Dromornis planei, and what we know about its sternum (breastbone) comes from small fragments. Like other mihirung species, Dromornis planei probably also had stubby little wings and lacked a keel on its sternum. These features are associated with flightlessness.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71620841-cd5a-4128-a31e-55b99a3e7830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland (or Synergy Parkland), in Kings Park, has to be a favourite spot amongst Perth families. Also known as the “Dinosaur Park”, thanks to it’s many life-sized dinosaur statues. 

There is little zig-zagged bridges lead to the island in the middle of the pond, Lycopod Island. Sprouting up on the island like Robinson Crusoe’s treehouse is a playground, the first of two children’s playgrounds at Synergy Parkland Kings Park. To the North of the Lake is another playground - Arthur Fairall Playground. On a weekend and school holidays this park is popular for children.

Parent's may be able to enjoy Zamia Cafe, is an ideal location for a casual family breakfast or lunch - but can be very busy too!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.96495" lon="115.821733">
