  <urlname>Ancient Kings Park : S5 Armoured and Dangerous by Leg5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918331" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Ancient Kings Park : S5 Armoured and Dangerous</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Leg5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Leg5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Ancient Kings Park&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Armoured and Dangerous&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7365baad-7ec2-4aa1-a5e2-d1587cccf492&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What does this animal name mean? (2 words)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/06f8178e-1a29-4c72-9618-8a72c180190f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Phytosaur were a heavily armoured semiaquatic reptiles found as fossils from the Late Triassic Period (about 229 million to 200 million years ago). Phytosaurs were not dinosaurs; rather both groups were archosaurs, a larger grouping that also includes crocodiles and pterosaurs (flying reptiles).

Phytosaurs were able to move about easily on land, and, although they were not ancestral to the crocodiles, they were distantly related and resembled crocodiles in appearance and probably in habits as well. The long, pointed jaws were armed with numerous sharp teeth, and it is probable that the phytosaurs preyed largely upon fishes. Like crocodiles, they had several rows of bony armour embedded in the skin along the back. The nostrils in the phytosaurs were set on a crest high on the skull in front of the eyes; this adaptation allowed them to float just underneath the water’s surface, with only the nostrils protruding above it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/71620841-cd5a-4128-a31e-55b99a3e7830" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;May Drive Parkland (or Synergy Parkland), in Kings Park, has to be a favourite spot amongst Perth families. Also known as the “Dinosaur Park”, thanks to it’s many life-sized dinosaur statues. 

There is little zig-zagged bridges lead to the island in the middle of the pond, Lycopod Island. Sprouting up on the island like Robinson Crusoe’s treehouse is a playground, the first of two children’s playgrounds at Synergy Parkland Kings Park. To the North of the Lake is another playground - Arthur Fairall Playground. On a weekend and school holidays this park is popular for children.

Parent's may be able to enjoy Zamia Cafe, is an ideal location for a casual family breakfast or lunch - but can be very busy too!!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
