 <wpt lat="-32.0496" lon="115.838433">
  <ele>15.0</ele>
  <time>2025-01-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqRTp-01</name>
  <desc>WetlandWalkS1Pa</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba</url>
  <urlname>Wetland Walk : S1 Paperbark by monkeywand</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918423" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wetland Walk : S1 Paperbark</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>monkeywand</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">monkeywand</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wetland Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Paperbark&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What did the Aboriginal people use paperbark for? To make... (phrase as written)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b29699d5-ef30-4cb8-bcaf-08078e53d118" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Swamp Paperbark (Melaleuca raphiophylla) is found in abundance at Piney Lakes, surrounding the wetland. It has loose, papery bark that makes a perfect home for a variety of invertebrates that shelter underneath the bark. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc3fcebf-4b1f-4c38-81f3-7979ae1d4de1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Piney Lakes Wetland Walk. This area was once part of a larger wetland, but due to climate changes and urbanization, it is now a seasonal dampland.

Walk around the natural wetland and through the paperbark forest. See local plants and
animals including birds, quendas and orchids, and listen out for frog calls! 

The walk is approximately 650m which should take about 30mins. The walk contains sandy trails and uneven ground make it unsuitable for prams or wheelchairs. Dogs and bikes are strictly prohibited from going into the natural wetland area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.050633" lon="115.837883">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2025-01-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqRTp-02</name>
  <desc>WetlandWalkS2Ma</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba</url>
  <urlname>Wetland Walk : S2 Marri by monkeywand</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918425" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wetland Walk : S2 Marri</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>monkeywand</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">monkeywand</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wetland Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Marri&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Noongar people would use the sap from the trees as medicine for what? (phrase as written)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2807c45d-4f98-43e7-a295-990a935441af" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Marri Tree (Corymbia calophylla) has rough bark on part or all of the trunk, lance-shaped adult leaves, branched clusters of cup-shaped or pear-shaped flower buds, each branch with three or seven buds, white to pink flowers, and relatively large oval to urn-shaped fruit, colloquially known as honky nuts.

Marri wood has had many uses, both for Aboriginal people, and in the construction industry.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc3fcebf-4b1f-4c38-81f3-7979ae1d4de1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Piney Lakes Wetland Walk. This area was once part of a larger wetland, but due to climate changes and urbanization, it is now a seasonal dampland.

Walk around the natural wetland and through the paperbark forest. See local plants and
animals including birds, quendas and orchids, and listen out for frog calls! 

The walk is approximately 650m which should take about 30mins. The walk contains sandy trails and uneven ground make it unsuitable for prams or wheelchairs. Dogs and bikes are strictly prohibited from going into the natural wetland area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.050683" lon="115.837167">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2025-01-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqRTp-03</name>
  <desc>WetlandWalkS3Qu</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba</url>
  <urlname>Wetland Walk : S3 Quenda by monkeywand</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918427" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wetland Walk : S3 Quenda</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>monkeywand</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">monkeywand</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wetland Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Quenda&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Quenda is the Noongar name for what kind of marsupial? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/53a41446-7f00-48cc-b3b9-65fa37fc5efd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Quenda (Isoodon obesulus fusciventer) are small, nocturnal marsupials with a long pointed muzzle. They make their home at Piney Lakes, often found near bodies of water. Quendas dig to find fungi and other invertebrates to eat. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc3fcebf-4b1f-4c38-81f3-7979ae1d4de1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Piney Lakes Wetland Walk. This area was once part of a larger wetland, but due to climate changes and urbanization, it is now a seasonal dampland.

Walk around the natural wetland and through the paperbark forest. See local plants and
animals including birds, quendas and orchids, and listen out for frog calls! 

The walk is approximately 650m which should take about 30mins. The walk contains sandy trails and uneven ground make it unsuitable for prams or wheelchairs. Dogs and bikes are strictly prohibited from going into the natural wetland area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.050083" lon="115.83585">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
  <time>2025-01-10T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqRTp-05</name>
  <desc>WetlandWalkS5Ta</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba</url>
  <urlname>Wetland Walk : S5 Tawny Frogmouth by monkeywand</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918431" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Wetland Walk : S5 Tawny Frogmouth</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>monkeywand</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">monkeywand</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wetland Walk&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tawny Frogmouth&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6a0dd693-f6d2-4d22-8f10-e6ccc0da40ba&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Tawny Frogmouths are also known as native what?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9d3be8b9-f1d0-4d76-8282-90593c9d58b8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Tawny Frogmouth (Podargus strigoides) is a species of frogmouth native to the Australian mainland and Tasmania and found throughout. It is a big-headed, stocky bird often mistaken for an owl due to its nocturnal habits and similar colouring. Owls catch their prey with their talons whereas a Tawny Frogmouth uses its excellent reflexes to catch prey with their beaks. They remain sitting very still on a low perch, and wait for food such as insects, spiders, mice, rats, frogs and scorpions to come to them. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc3fcebf-4b1f-4c38-81f3-7979ae1d4de1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Piney Lakes Wetland Walk. This area was once part of a larger wetland, but due to climate changes and urbanization, it is now a seasonal dampland.

Walk around the natural wetland and through the paperbark forest. See local plants and
animals including birds, quendas and orchids, and listen out for frog calls! 

The walk is approximately 650m which should take about 30mins. The walk contains sandy trails and uneven ground make it unsuitable for prams or wheelchairs. Dogs and bikes are strictly prohibited from going into the natural wetland area. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.049783" lon="115.83555">
  <ele>10.0</ele>
