 <wpt lat="-31.962" lon="115.8254">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfj4v-03</name>
  <desc>KoreanWarMemor3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd</url>
  <urlname>Korean War Memorial : S3 Nurses in Korea by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918487" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Korean War Memorial : S3 Nurses in Korea</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Korean War Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Nurses in Korea&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did the last Australian nurses return to Australia&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fec39797-4824-439a-bf4b-6f373222ea64" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A total of 153 Australian nurses served in Commonwealth hospital units during the Korean War: While based mainly in Japan, many also served in Korea. Trained, civilian nurses joined the Australian Army and the Royal Australian Air Force for a four year period, tending to battle casualties, health problems and the transportation of patients&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/457d1f46-04c4-4298-b20b-3207e1886a60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just five years after the Second World War, Australia was “called to arms” to fight in a foreign country, Korea. The Australian Government under Prime Minister Robert Menzies immediately offered military assistance to the United Nations Security Council. From 25 June 1950 until 27 July 1953, 17,000 Australians in the Army, Navy and Air Force served as part of the United Nations (UN) multinational force, defending South Korea from the Communist forces of North Korea.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.96195" lon="115.82545">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfj4v-04</name>
  <desc>KoreanWarMemor4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd</url>
  <urlname>Korean War Memorial : S4 Australian involvement by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918489" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Korean War Memorial : S4 Australian involvement</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Korean War Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Australian involvement &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many Australian casualties were there?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bcdd8ad4-2668-4453-b4de-25c04e1d1c45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Australian military served with distinction during the war. At the Battle of Kapyong an Australian battalion (approximately 800 soldiers) along with another from Canada defeated an entire Chinese division (approximately 15,000 men) and prevented it from taking Seoul. Both battalions were awarded US Presidential Unit Citations.
Nearly 18,000 Australian soldiers, sailors, airmen and nurses served in the war.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/457d1f46-04c4-4298-b20b-3207e1886a60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just five years after the Second World War, Australia was “called to arms” to fight in a foreign country, Korea. The Australian Government under Prime Minister Robert Menzies immediately offered military assistance to the United Nations Security Council. From 25 June 1950 until 27 July 1953, 17,000 Australians in the Army, Navy and Air Force served as part of the United Nations (UN) multinational force, defending South Korea from the Communist forces of North Korea.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.961933" lon="115.82535">
  <ele>39.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfj4v-01</name>
  <desc>KoreanWarMemor1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd</url>
  <urlname>Korean War Memorial : S1 In memory of all veterans by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918483" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Korean War Memorial : S1 In memory of all veterans</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Korean War Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;In memory of all veterans&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the base of the stone is a plaque, it was dedicated by the minister for ________ and veterans affairs&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/101c2f90-7f10-4cd7-9ac5-14322f777c45" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In appreciation of your sacrificial efforts and support of the Australian Government and its people, the people of the Republic of Korea and their Government express their deep and enduring gratitude.
We are deeply indebted to you for the democratic freedom we are enjoying now which was possible through your sheer determination and willpower.
While we cherish in our hearts the memory of your boundless brotherly love towards us, this stone from the battlefield of Kapyong will stay as evidence reaffirming our mutual respect and triendship for generations to come&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/457d1f46-04c4-4298-b20b-3207e1886a60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just five years after the Second World War, Australia was “called to arms” to fight in a foreign country, Korea. The Australian Government under Prime Minister Robert Menzies immediately offered military assistance to the United Nations Security Council. From 25 June 1950 until 27 July 1953, 17,000 Australians in the Army, Navy and Air Force served as part of the United Nations (UN) multinational force, defending South Korea from the Communist forces of North Korea.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.961967" lon="115.8254">
  <ele>38.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-07T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBfj4v-02</name>
  <desc>KoreanWarMemor2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd</url>
  <urlname>Korean War Memorial : S2 Memorial stone by Barnzy12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918485" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Korean War Memorial : S2 Memorial stone</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barnzy12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barnzy12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Korean War Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Memorial stone&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Name of the county (one word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/334a6a55-3b11-4e09-a726-f514c193c3f8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The stone is from the area where the Battle of Kapyong took place in 1951. It was a critical year for the Korean War and the men of the 3rd Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment showed great courage in this significant battle. Michael Kelly, an historian at the Australian War Memorial wrote an article for the Sydney Morning Herald in 2016 and said that: “The Battle of Kapyong turned the tide of the Korean War, the legacies of which can still be felt today”.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/457d1f46-04c4-4298-b20b-3207e1886a60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just five years after the Second World War, Australia was “called to arms” to fight in a foreign country, Korea. The Australian Government under Prime Minister Robert Menzies immediately offered military assistance to the United Nations Security Council. From 25 June 1950 until 27 July 1953, 17,000 Australians in the Army, Navy and Air Force served as part of the United Nations (UN) multinational force, defending South Korea from the Communist forces of North Korea.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
