   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Korean War Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Family connection &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d5259acb-dbcc-421a-8711-0146b4ebb2bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Listed on the second plaque, second column between BELL and BENDING, what was his last name?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fa711bcc-47be-4cac-9c25-9cf586067ae9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My grandfather served in Korea. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/457d1f46-04c4-4298-b20b-3207e1886a60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just five years after the Second World War, Australia was “called to arms” to fight in a foreign country, Korea. The Australian Government under Prime Minister Robert Menzies immediately offered military assistance to the United Nations Security Council. From 25 June 1950 until 27 July 1953, 17,000 Australians in the Army, Navy and Air Force served as part of the United Nations (UN) multinational force, defending South Korea from the Communist forces of North Korea.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.75715" lon="115.796167">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKRsG-05</name>
  <desc>SixSeasonsS5Kam</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2</url>
  <urlname>Six Seasons : S5 Kambarang by FelixII</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918481" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Six Seasons : S5 Kambarang</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>FelixII</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">FelixII</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Kambarang&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;One of the most striking displays of flowers to be seen during this season will be the ? (first word only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bf1819f7-ee6b-47ba-8734-a2fd408871ba" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;October &amp; November

During the Kambarang season longer dry periods accompany a definite warming trend with many things undergoing transformation and an abundance of colours and flowers exploding around us.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/995cf9a9-d1c3-40cb-b161-abed3224971f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The local Nyoongar Aboriginal people living in spiritual harmony with the land travelled back and forth across the coastal plain according to their six seasons. With the change of these seasons their food resources changed as well. Places of fresh water bear special significance, not only for animal and plant communities, but also to the Nyoongar's spiritual connection to 'Boodja' (Earth Mother)

Take a walk and explore the seasons.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.7556" lon="115.795483">
  <ele>22.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKRsG-04</name>
  <desc>SixSeasonsS4Dji</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2</url>
  <urlname>Six Seasons : S4 Djilba by FelixII</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918479" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Six Seasons : S4 Djilba</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>FelixII</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">FelixII</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Djilba&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The woodland birds will still be nestbound. Hence the ? of the Koolbardi, Djidi Djidi and Chuck-a-luck. (one word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/34298731-4a1a-48cb-8d13-967e7de448ff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;August &amp; September

Djilba season is a transitional time of the year, with some very cold, clear days combining with warmer, wet and windy days and the occasional sunny day or two. This is the start of the massive flowering explosion in the South West, beginning with the yellow flowering plants such as the Acacias, plus creams and some vivid, striking blues.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/995cf9a9-d1c3-40cb-b161-abed3224971f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The local Nyoongar Aboriginal people living in spiritual harmony with the land travelled back and forth across the coastal plain according to their six seasons. With the change of these seasons their food resources changed as well. Places of fresh water bear special significance, not only for animal and plant communities, but also to the Nyoongar's spiritual connection to 'Boodja' (Earth Mother)

Take a walk and explore the seasons.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.753783" lon="115.7943">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKRsG-03</name>
  <desc>SixSeasonsS3Dje</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2</url>
  <urlname>Six Seasons : S3 Djeran &amp; Makuru by FelixII</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918477" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Six Seasons : S3 Djeran &amp; Makuru</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>FelixII</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">FelixII</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Djeran &amp; Makuru&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Root bulbs of the ?  (one word only)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/721768b6-0d68-4358-bdef-353c37095c81" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;April &amp; May

Djeran season at last sees a break in the really hot weather. A key indicator of the change of season is the cool nights that once again bring morning dew. The winds have also changed, especially in their intensity, with light breezes being the go and generally swinging from southerly directions (ie southeast to southwest). Flying ants can be seen cruising around in the light winds.

June &amp; July

Makaru sees the coldest and wettest time of the year come into full swing. Traditionally, this was a good time to move back inland from the coast as the winds turned to the west and south bringing the cold weather, rains and occasionally snow on the peaks of the Stirling and Porongorup Ranges.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/995cf9a9-d1c3-40cb-b161-abed3224971f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The local Nyoongar Aboriginal people living in spiritual harmony with the land travelled back and forth across the coastal plain according to their six seasons. With the change of these seasons their food resources changed as well. Places of fresh water bear special significance, not only for animal and plant communities, but also to the Nyoongar's spiritual connection to 'Boodja' (Earth Mother)

Take a walk and explore the seasons.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.752967" lon="115.79445">
  <ele>21.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKRsG-02</name>
  <desc>SixSeasonsS2Bun</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2</url>
  <urlname>Six Seasons : S2 Bunuru by FelixII</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918475" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Six Seasons : S2 Bunuru</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>FelixII</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">FelixII</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Six Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bunuru&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9f789354-18ec-46b9-9d1d-ed66378255b2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The scientific name of the striking female&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/682969d4-2a97-46bc-8ed7-79c2f3f956e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;February &amp; March

Bunuru is the hottest time of the year with little to no rain. Hot easterly winds continue with a cooling sea breeze most afternoons if you're close to the coast. Therefore, traditionally this was, and still is, a greast time for living and fishing by the coast, rivers and estuaries. Because of this, freshwater foods and seafood made up major parts of the diet during this time of year.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/995cf9a9-d1c3-40cb-b161-abed3224971f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The local Nyoongar Aboriginal people living in spiritual harmony with the land travelled back and forth across the coastal plain according to their six seasons. With the change of these seasons their food resources changed as well. Places of fresh water bear special significance, not only for animal and plant communities, but also to the Nyoongar's spiritual connection to 'Boodja' (Earth Mother)

Take a walk and explore the seasons.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.7522" lon="115.7939">
  <ele>20.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBKRsG-01</name>
