 <wpt lat="-35.02505" lon="117.894567">
  <ele>181.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJ8Ma-03</name>
  <desc>ANZACMemorialS3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c</url>
  <urlname>ANZAC Memorial : S3 That wonderful dawn service by MummaBear5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918457" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>ANZAC Memorial : S3 That wonderful dawn service</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>MummaBear5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">MummaBear5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;ANZAC Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;That wonderful dawn service&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of the person pictured?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ab91b85c-ba91-4c7e-9287-ae4df2bc417c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Reverend Arthur Ernest White started the Dawn Service tradition at Albany
He joined the Australian Imperial Force in March 1916 and was appointed Chaplain of the
44th Battalion. Padre White saw service in France and returned to Western Australia for
medical discharge on 12 February 1918.
Twelve days later on the 24 February 1918, White celebrated a requiem mass for the battle
dead in St John's Church. Afterwards a walk to the summit of Mt Clarence took place.
White had arranged for a boatman to cast a wreath into the waters of King George Sound.
He is reputed to have said, 'Albany was the last sight of land our troops saw of Australia..
Perhaps we should commemorate them this way every Anzac Day.
Reverend White left Western Australia in 1919, but returned to Albany in 1929 to become
Rector of St John's Church.
The following year, on Anzac Day, White conducted a dawn service at St John's Church
followed by a wreath laying ceremony at the local war memorial (as recorded in St John's
Church register). In 1931 he repeated this service then climbed Mt Clarence to observe the
dropping of a wreath into King George Sound.
Reverend White continued this tradition until he left Albany in 1938. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0198e668-c06c-497d-ab67-220af0f041fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Enjoy an informative walk at the ANZAC Memorial, the site of the first Dawn Service.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-35.024817" lon="117.89535">
  <ele>175.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJ8Ma-05</name>
  <desc>ANZACMemorialS5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c</url>
  <urlname>ANZAC Memorial : S5 A resurrected memorial by MummaBear5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918461" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>ANZAC Memorial : S5 A resurrected memorial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>MummaBear5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">MummaBear5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;ANZAC Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A resurrected memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where was the original ANZAC Memorial located?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/146d0168-ba0d-4ace-b3fb-c29aa447f589" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Desert Mounted Corps Memorial is a new version of a statue that
once stood in Port Said, Egypt
The original memorial is believed to have been paid for by light
horsemen, mounted infantrymen, cameleers and army nurses who
each subscribed a day's pay. The Australian and New Zealand
Governments also contributed to the memorial
During the Suez Crisis, on the night of 26 December 1956 a crowd
attacked the monument. It Was pulled from its base and smashed
beyond repair.
Nhen peace returned, the damaged memorial and plinth were
shipped to Australia, where Raymond Boultwood Ewers, a Melbourne
sculptor, created a new statue based on the original. The plaster
moulds were then shipped to ltaly for the bronze casting
After much argument, the decision was made to locate the
resurrected memorial in Albany. It was unveiled on Mt Clarence in
October 1964, by Sir Robert Menzies.
Those attending the ceremony included Desert Mounted Corps veterans.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0198e668-c06c-497d-ab67-220af0f041fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Enjoy an informative walk at the ANZAC Memorial, the site of the first Dawn Service.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-35.024967" lon="117.895283">
  <ele>175.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBJ8Ma-04</name>
  <desc>ANZACMemorialS4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c</url>
  <urlname>ANZAC Memorial : S4 Lone Pine Memorial by MummaBear5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918459" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>ANZAC Memorial : S4 Lone Pine Memorial</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>MummaBear5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">MummaBear5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;ANZAC Memorial&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lone Pine Memorial&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f0c3fdb0-8900-4662-b70e-cb286e4b6e0c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was Albany's Lone Pine Memorial planted?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d41170f-4531-4a0c-bc16-348b16cb3cbe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Albany's Lone Pine Memorial was planted in 1974 to commemorate
the dlamond jubilee of the departure of the first contingent of the
First Australian Imperlal force.
When Australian troops landed at Gallipoli on 25 April 1915 they saw
a stunted pine tree growing on the outhern lobe of the 400 Plateau.
The soldiers called it Lone Pine, and the position retained the name
long after the tree was destroyed by shellfire.
Lone Pine was the scene of a major attack launched by the First
Australlan Infantry Division on 6 August 1915. In four days of fighting
the Australians lost more than 2,000 men. The Turks suffered over
6,000 casualties.
Two Australian soldiers, L/Cpl BenjamIn Srnith and Sgt Keith
McDowell recovered pine cones from the enemy trenches and
brought them home.
Six trees were subsequently grown from the seeds of these cones.
Many more have since been grown from their seed and grafting techniques.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0198e668-c06c-497d-ab67-220af0f041fe" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Enjoy an informative walk at the ANZAC Memorial, the site of the first Dawn Service.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-35.025317" lon="117.89585">
  <ele>167.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBkecH-05</name>
  <desc>QuotationFromA5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/723f82f6-0f37-4af4-9f37-b3a3496d406c</url>
  <urlname>Quotation from Anzac's : S5 A Fortunate Life by Chookman8</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918471" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Quotation from Anzac's : S5 A Fortunate Life</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chookman8</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chookman8</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Quotation from Anzac's&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A Fortunate Life&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/723f82f6-0f37-4af4-9f37-b3a3496d406c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/723f82f6-0f37-4af4-9f37-b3a3496d406c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Whom was the quote from?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/091fb72f-22e4-4c2a-ba6b-03643489248a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The order to line up on the
beach was forgotten. We all
ran for our lives over the strip
of the beach and got into the
scrub and bush. Men were
falling all around me.
We were stumbling over
bodies-running blind.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6bdf135f-9261-41a1-8c1d-f96365ea26b9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Take a walk up to the Desert Mounted Corps Memorial. Taking in some nice views and some quotes from letter sent home from the Anzac's.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
