 <wpt lat="-31.850533" lon="115.7604">
  <ele>13.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBDNZJ-03</name>
  <desc>StarSwampHeri39</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78</url>
  <urlname>Star Swamp Heritage Trail : S3 Site 9: Aboriginal Camp by iluvtrekking</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-998" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Star Swamp Heritage Trail : S3 Site 9: Aboriginal Camp</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>iluvtrekking</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">iluvtrekking</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Star Swamp Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Site 9: Aboriginal Camp &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Australia's original inhabitants used bark to make a variety of items including what? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8a3c0c7b-b006-40d8-b7af-fb217dfb4fc2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp has a long history as a Noongar camping area, with fresh water and abundant bush tucker and seafood. Trees that show evidence of scarring by the Noongar people can still be seen in the wetland areas to the west of this point.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/47bb10d8-7327-409e-83ad-192611d95825" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp Bushland Reserve provides countless opportunities for birdwatching, spotting native wildflowers and orchids, chance encounters with Quenda and Rainbow Bee Eaters, and photography, all situated in the middle of suburbia.

It has been the site of many bushfires. The most recent occurred on 21 March 2023 and burnt approximately 6.5 hectares of the 96 hectare reserve. The fire was believed to be deliberately lit.

This Adventure Lab has been prepared with children in mind, it encourages bushwalking, highlights conservation and offers an opportunity to learn some local history.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.849167" lon="115.759383">
  <ele>14.0</ele>
  <time>2025-02-16T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBDNZJ-02</name>
  <desc>StarSwampHer210</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78</url>
  <urlname>Star Swamp Heritage Trail : S2 Site 10: Drovers’ Tree by iluvtrekking</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-997" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Star Swamp Heritage Trail : S2 Site 10: Drovers’ Tree</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>iluvtrekking</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">iluvtrekking</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Star Swamp Heritage Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Site 10: Drovers’ Tree &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/897fd310-db34-4a5d-b2d3-c9bc18418f78&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What did Drovers do here in the early 1800’s and 1900’s? (one word)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/33eb9ef7-755f-4e53-be2d-b6308fc75f46" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This area served as a stopping point and watering hole along the Coastal Stock Route between Dongara and Fremantle. Cattle drovers frequented the area and Afghan camel drivers were a common sight after the opening of the goldfields in the 1890s.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/47bb10d8-7327-409e-83ad-192611d95825" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Star Swamp Bushland Reserve provides countless opportunities for birdwatching, spotting native wildflowers and orchids, chance encounters with Quenda and Rainbow Bee Eaters, and photography, all situated in the middle of suburbia.

It has been the site of many bushfires. The most recent occurred on 21 March 2023 and burnt approximately 6.5 hectares of the 96 hectare reserve. The fire was believed to be deliberately lit.

This Adventure Lab has been prepared with children in mind, it encourages bushwalking, highlights conservation and offers an opportunity to learn some local history.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.8485" lon="115.7595">
  <ele>17.0</ele>
