   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Karratha’s Artwork&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Walgu Park&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3750a827-d411-443c-8ac0-1f30cd3809e3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3750a827-d411-443c-8ac0-1f30cd3809e3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;There are two (2) different animals painted. Name the most painted on and how many (e.g:  Cat 15)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6ba4bb5a-8304-4273-a41c-4eb2a8e3c992" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Walgu Park was officially opened on 15 September 2019.

I took more notice of this little park early one morning while walking the grand fur baby. It is on the corner of what can be a busy roundabout and a walk over the drain bridge from the shopping centre.
The Red Earth Arts Precinct (REAP) with library and cinema is across the road.

You wouldn’t know there was any artwork here unless you walk down the ramp or stairs.

I tried to find out more about this art but sadly neither the Council nor library knew whom painted it and what it represents&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/604f02ae-3076-43d4-a494-25bc6a5b5532" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Driving and walking around town I’ve come across different forms of art.
This AdLab is featuring three along Dampier Highway that you can’t miss, one on Tank Hill and the other in a little park that is very easy to miss.

Karratha is the first city in the North West region of Western Australia.
Karratha was named after a pastoral station in the area and was established in 1968 to accommodate the processing and exploration workforce of the Hamersley Iron mining company and, in the 1980s the petroleum and liquified natural gas operations of the North West Shelf Venture.

On the first of July 2014 Karratha became a city. It covers an area of fifteen thousand square kilometres and has a population of around 20,000.
Karratha has the expected facilities of a major regional area with shopping centres, health campus and medical services, both public and private schools, airport, restaurants and cafes, hotels/motels and an arts precinct.

The weather here is divine most of the year except when the humidity is unbearable and when cyclones come through (mostly Nov - Mar). Plenty of warm sunny weather to enjoy with the laid back lifestyle ?? &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
