   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Footsteps in time&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dutch Exploration - 1697&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91681382-4532-4138-9269-394c51328bdb"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/91681382-4532-4138-9269-394c51328bdb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is hanging on the left side of his jacket ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d266ba93-dc2b-4d0f-bf12-f8252716944e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1697, Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh and his crew explored the Swan River and Dirk Hartog Island in Western Australia. They are thought to be the first Europeans to see black swans and to travel up the Swan River. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/40b91d62-e11a-4ade-b2eb-6a839b43f6eb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;NOTE : If your GPS signal is not working here, I have put large geo-fences in. Start at the man, furthest from the road.

The sculpture group commemorates the 175th anniversary of Western Australia and symbolises the business people who have built this thriving CBD
The five men are: the Dutch from the 17th century, who first explored the region; Anglo-Celtic (the English), who colonised the Swan River Settlement, now Perth; the gold rush days of the mid to late 19th century, which brought money and people to Western Australia; the post Second World War days, when the state again experienced a large influx of people; and last, but certainly not least, the busy and important business man of the early 21st century.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
