 <wpt lat="-31.925383" lon="115.880967">
  <ele>29.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvAvH-05</name>
  <desc>InglewoodHerit5</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72</url>
  <urlname>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S5 Inglewood Hotel by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918569" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S5 Inglewood Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Inglewood Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Inglewood Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In total, how many 'barber's stripes' are there on the building's south-west chimney?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/93e46c1d-51eb-4873-8810-2c28f764499d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Inglewood Hotel (1935) is one of the last eclectic buildings, fusing Spanish and Dutch influences into a unique structure. Eclectic architecture draws inspiration from various historical and geographical styles, resulting in something fresh and original. 

The hotel’s Dutch Gables reveal an unexpected origin. Architects Parry &amp; Clifton sought elements for the Inglewood Hotel, they turned to the Cape Dutch Gable. Combining it with arched details from the Spanish Mission style—often associated with California—they fashioned a very mid-1930s hotel design. This style didn’t endure, however, as later hotels embraced a more functionalist approach.

Images courtesy of the State Library of WA and the Inglewood Hotel.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c8217ec-f76e-479f-8687-425f0910c49b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a name thought to have been derived from the Norwegian barque Inglewood, the suburb of Inglewood began when land was first granted to John Gregory in 1831 and initially developed by Gold Estates of Australia.

A number of significant landmarks buildings were developed over the years, including the prominent clock tower.  This Adventure Lab will take you on a short stroll down Beaufort Street to look at a couple of these buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.921717" lon="115.885367">
  <ele>28.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvAvH-04</name>
  <desc>InglewoodHerit4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72</url>
  <urlname>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S4 Post Office by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918567" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S4 Post Office</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Inglewood Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Post Office&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Locate the PO boxes on the eastern side of the building.  What are the lowest and highest box numbers? ( _ _ _-_ _ _)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3eaf3b64-c218-45b2-9a53-27113dd95b28" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Inglewood Post Office is one of the most elegant Post Offices remaining from its era anywhere in Australia. It is an important Modern Movement design, as well as being one of the earliest Modern post offices designed by the Commonwealth Department of Works.

Opened in 1949, Inglewood Post Office has an elegantly composed front to the street corner. Australian Modernism arose from the works of the De Stijl movement and buildings by Dutch architect, Willem Marius Dudok. Originally an art movement, De Stijl (‘The Style’) stressed simple horizontal and vertical lines, which were combined with other simple forms to give a strong idea of harmony and order. One of the leading exponents in the art world was Piet Mondrian, whose simple rectangles and lines are among the most famous modern works of art. 

All of these philosophical/art-theory ideas can be seen on the Beaufort Street frontage which has two planes in sienna face brickwork. These planes are capped with a light concrete cornice which is visually linked to the cantilevered concrete hood over the main entrance and strip window.

Images courtesy of the Battye Library and Inglewood on Beaufort Community Group.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c8217ec-f76e-479f-8687-425f0910c49b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a name thought to have been derived from the Norwegian barque Inglewood, the suburb of Inglewood began when land was first granted to John Gregory in 1831 and initially developed by Gold Estates of Australia.

A number of significant landmarks buildings were developed over the years, including the prominent clock tower.  This Adventure Lab will take you on a short stroll down Beaufort Street to look at a couple of these buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.922033" lon="115.884267">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvAvH-03</name>
  <desc>InglewoodHerit3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72</url>
  <urlname>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S3 Police Station and Quarters by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918565" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S3 Police Station and Quarters</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Inglewood Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Police Station and Quarters &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What are the two words above the entryway of the building? (6 and 7 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0a3e37ff-ebd7-4a5d-ba2b-046d69257943" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Older police buildings have three main elements: places to live, places to work and places to detain people. Over time, there were changes in how these elements were negotiated, and these changes can be seen in the heritage police buildings that remain with us.

So in the Inglewood Police Station (1940), the residence was placed on the corner with Beaufort Street and the Station and Lock Up further along down Eighth Avenue. Designed by the Public Works Department, both buildings were constructed by William Clark, who was also responsible, among other places, for Bassendean Grandstand (1932), Bassendean Fire Station (1934), and South Perth Fire Station (1936).

Images courtesy of the WA Police Historical Society and Inglewood on Beaufort Community Group.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c8217ec-f76e-479f-8687-425f0910c49b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a name thought to have been derived from the Norwegian barque Inglewood, the suburb of Inglewood began when land was first granted to John Gregory in 1831 and initially developed by Gold Estates of Australia.

A number of significant landmarks buildings were developed over the years, including the prominent clock tower.  This Adventure Lab will take you on a short stroll down Beaufort Street to look at a couple of these buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.920167" lon="115.887533">
  <ele>27.0</ele>
