  <desc>InglewoodHerit2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72</url>
  <urlname>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S2 Piccadilly Gardens - Civic Theatre by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918563" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S2 Piccadilly Gardens - Civic Theatre</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Inglewood Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Piccadilly Gardens - Civic Theatre&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What type of 'designed' building was the theatre and clock? (one word, 8 letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d2f7e401-4024-487f-bf7b-6132b1d9af96" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Piccadilly Gardens was an outdoor theatre where local residents watched films from deck chairs in the 1920s. The Gardens were the forerunner of the Piccadilly Theatre, which was built on this site in the mid 1930s.  The original building was designed, built, and operated by Tom Snooks (1890–1958) who also operated theatre in the 1910s to the 1940s.  

Later the building became known as the Civic Theatre and in 1976 entertainer Max Kay (1936 – 2019) opened the Civic Theatre Restaurant, where he performed shows he wrote and produced himself until the early 1980s.

Images courtesy of Cinema Treasures and Inglewood on Beaufort Community Group.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c8217ec-f76e-479f-8687-425f0910c49b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a name thought to have been derived from the Norwegian barque Inglewood, the suburb of Inglewood began when land was first granted to John Gregory in 1831 and initially developed by Gold Estates of Australia.

A number of significant landmarks buildings were developed over the years, including the prominent clock tower.  This Adventure Lab will take you on a short stroll down Beaufort Street to look at a couple of these buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.91755" lon="115.890667">
  <ele>29.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBvAvH-01</name>
  <desc>InglewoodHerit1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72</url>
  <urlname>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S1 Civic Hotel by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918561" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Inglewood Heritage Buildings : S1 Civic Hotel</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Inglewood Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Civic Hotel&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ef4a3f80-575f-495d-b77a-92eb03b6ed72&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Name the tallest structure on the building, above the Hotel Rooms sign     (F _ _ _   P _ _ _)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c1820b60-52b8-4553-93a2-74b90616f64a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Civic Hotel is a great example of modernist architecture. Where the hotel used rectangular planes and space in a style known as ‘Modern Recilinear’.

Designed by Marshall Clifton and Reginald Summerhayes and opening in March 1940, the Civic Hotel signified the new direction architecture was taking. Two other hotels opened that year which shared the same design philosophy: the Carlisle Hotel and the Highway Hotel in Claremont. 

Images courtesy of Trove and the Perth Museum.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1c8217ec-f76e-479f-8687-425f0910c49b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a name thought to have been derived from the Norwegian barque Inglewood, the suburb of Inglewood began when land was first granted to John Gregory in 1831 and initially developed by Gold Estates of Australia.

A number of significant landmarks buildings were developed over the years, including the prominent clock tower.  This Adventure Lab will take you on a short stroll down Beaufort Street to look at a couple of these buildings.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-34.958833" lon="117.35535">
  <ele>8.0</ele>
