 <wpt lat="-17.97015" lon="122.23675">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB649G-05</name>
  <desc>BroomeArtTra599</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0</url>
  <urlname>Broome Art Trail : S5 9 Zeros, 9 Stories by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918627" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome Art Trail : S5 9 Zeros, 9 Stories</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome Art Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;9 Zeros, 9 Stories&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Charlie had no… ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/77f70b30-b16d-44b5-8670-2e3996dc0a0c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A collaboration between the Perth artist Paula Hart and Broome local Chris Maher, this sculpture is a tribute to the many people affected by and killed in the 1942 air raid attacks. 

From the artist’s website: 
The work aims to reveal the many perspectives of the Japanese attack on Broome, on 3 March 1942. It stands as a silent legacy to those who lost their lives, and the impact on this remote Australian community. In the past, war memorials have tended to hero the leaders or remember the deceased. However, this approach often misses the multifaceted human stories behind these episodes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf6a13b-4349-4357-bdc5-a1f8533a29bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Broome is known for many features: camels, pearls, its multicultural vibe, access to the Kimberley, and of course the glorious Cable Beach. It is also an hub of creativity and draws artists from across the country. Let's go on a meander to find some of the town’s artworks. 

Keep track of the completion messages to find the key word to unlock the coordinates of the bonus cache. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-17.953217" lon="122.233717">
  <ele>12.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB649G-04</name>
  <desc>BroomeArtTrail4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0</url>
  <urlname>Broome Art Trail : S4 G-AUGJ by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918625" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome Art Trail : S4 G-AUGJ</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome Art Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;G-AUGJ&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The pioneer aviator named on the plaque is Sir… ? Two words. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/995db3b2-5882-4f43-a01b-f4a93411a413" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;What is Broome known for? Australia’s first scheduled air service, of course! And perhaps some other things… 

The artist Sobrane, a former Broome resident now living in Mt Barker WA, characterises herself as a “pathological freedom fighter.” This mural depicts a cheeky camel, perhaps a tad surprised at the metal creature buzzing over its head. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf6a13b-4349-4357-bdc5-a1f8533a29bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Broome is known for many features: camels, pearls, its multicultural vibe, access to the Kimberley, and of course the glorious Cable Beach. It is also an hub of creativity and draws artists from across the country. Let's go on a meander to find some of the town’s artworks. 

Keep track of the completion messages to find the key word to unlock the coordinates of the bonus cache. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-17.955333" lon="122.240483">
  <ele>9.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB649G-03</name>
  <desc>BroomeArtTrail3</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0</url>
  <urlname>Broome Art Trail : S3 Her Rules, Her Game by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918623" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome Art Trail : S3 Her Rules, Her Game</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome Art Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Her Rules, Her Game&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Turn around. What are the clouds saying? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a635dc7b-5fbf-4180-9c8e-b421f7cd2594" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This stunning mural was a 2018 NAIDOC collaboration between the Kimberley Aboriginal Medical Service and the West Kimberley Football League. It features two strong and dedicated high-school AFL players from the Dampier Peninsula and Looma community, Erin Victor and Sanchez Augustine. The artist, Matt Adnate, has mural artwork across Australia, and won the Archibald Packing Room Prize in 2024 for his portrait of hip-hop artist Baker Boy. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf6a13b-4349-4357-bdc5-a1f8533a29bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Broome is known for many features: camels, pearls, its multicultural vibe, access to the Kimberley, and of course the glorious Cable Beach. It is also an hub of creativity and draws artists from across the country. Let's go on a meander to find some of the town’s artworks. 

Keep track of the completion messages to find the key word to unlock the coordinates of the bonus cache. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-17.955333" lon="122.242917">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB649G-02</name>
  <desc>BroomeArtTrail2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0</url>
  <urlname>Broome Art Trail : S2 Fusion by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918621" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Broome Art Trail : S2 Fusion</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Broome Art Trail&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fusion&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/13c377b8-d48a-4757-9929-bb5d7dc69ce0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;At the sculpture’s base, there are two rectangular steel plates. How many holes are in each? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/10bad9e0-b2d8-4ef8-a958-70c2c80b8a0c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Unveiled in August 2021, this sculpture stands at the intersection of Carnarvon St and Napier Tce. It is perfectly positioned so that twice a year - usually April and August - the full moon rises directly over the peak of the sculpture. Fusion represents the interwoven cultures and pearling history of Broome, using jarrah, steel, rope and pitch, and over 200kg of pearl shell. The sculptors are based in the Leschenault area near Bunbury, and also created the Wardandi Boodja artwork on the Koombana Bay foreshore.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edf6a13b-4349-4357-bdc5-a1f8533a29bf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Broome is known for many features: camels, pearls, its multicultural vibe, access to the Kimberley, and of course the glorious Cable Beach. It is also an hub of creativity and draws artists from across the country. Let's go on a meander to find some of the town’s artworks. 

Keep track of the completion messages to find the key word to unlock the coordinates of the bonus cache. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-17.9533" lon="122.244833">
  <ele>11.0</ele>
  <time>2025-03-24T08:00:00Z</time>
