   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Chancellor Park - Seasons&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;February / March&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/658abece-e318-43aa-a7e4-20e9fd5bbf96"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/658abece-e318-43aa-a7e4-20e9fd5bbf96&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Bunuru is the ...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6820ff8d-12ed-4aa5-84d5-0a4744696454" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;February / March is Bunuru season!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/257404ae-b3fc-45e9-87fd-0b147300d46f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Chancellor Park serves to conserve the regions native vegetation and local biodiversity. This 24 hectare park provides a valuable habitat for many plants and animals.

In the Southwest of Australia, the Noongar seasonal calendar includes six different seasons in a yearly cycle.  These are Birak, Bunuru, Djeran, Makuru, Djilba and Kambarang.  Each of the six seasons represents and explains the seasonal changes we see annually.

This walk will take you to information on each of the season.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.546917" lon="115.714167">
  <ele>7.0</ele>
  <time>2025-04-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBbGN4-04</name>
  <desc>SamphireCoveRe4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ffc35fee-2375-464d-b1d5-bea864e93f6e</url>
  <urlname>Samphire Cove Reserve : S4 Microbat Boxes by fratra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1451" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Samphire Cove Reserve : S4 Microbat Boxes</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>fratra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">fratra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Samphire Cove Reserve&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Microbat Boxes&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ffc35fee-2375-464d-b1d5-bea864e93f6e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ffc35fee-2375-464d-b1d5-bea864e93f6e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What do the bats have a healthy appetite for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e99835e4-feae-49b6-a6c3-c64c93480854" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The microbat boxes in 
various trees and the paver art you will see on 
the ground along the trail are courtesy of the 
John Tonkin College Bushrangers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3d08a5e4-5c2d-40f2-8d42-ba086c4bf88a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On completion check the Journal to locate https://coord.info/GCB5K44

Samphire Cove Nature Reserve is an ‘A’ Class 
Nature Reserve and a wetland of international 
significance within the Peel-Yalgorup wetland 
system.
It is a Threatened Ecological Community due 
to the endangered samphire that grows here, 
and also due to the critically endangered Far
Eastern Curlew (widjoonong) which is often
seen feeding and putting on weight for the
return journey to their breeding grounds in the 
Arctic Circle, in common with other migratory 
shorebirds.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.547433" lon="115.714033">
