 <wpt lat="-32.0529" lon="115.741333">
  <time>2025-05-25T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBH7ZR-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa01L</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #010 : S1 Lets Explore Western Australia - Fremantle by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1368" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #010 : S1 Lets Explore Western Australia - Fremantle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #010&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lets Explore Western Australia - Fremantle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the Post code for Fremantle?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b4acd61f-9e6c-4412-a585-736c4b5e414e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Visited by Dutch explorers in the 1600s, Fremantle was the first area settled by the Swan River colonists in 1829,[3]: 11  and is named after Captain Charles Fremantle, an English naval officer who claimed the west coast of New Holland as British territory.[4] The settlement struggled in its first decades, and in 1850, with the advent of penal transportation to the colony, Fremantle became Australia's primary destination for convicts. The convict-built Fremantle Prison operated long after transportation of convicts ended in 1868, and is now a World Heritage Site.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/15fa4bb5-6e43-4bed-832c-d2155620ecd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052983" lon="115.7412">
  <time>2025-05-25T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBH7ZR-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa012</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #010 : S2 Wining and dining by neva2old</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1369" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #010 : S2 Wining and dining</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>neva2old</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">neva2old</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #010&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wining and dining&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/dfc54184-8622-46d7-b5e0-287e85178253&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is wine made from?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/58e51e7a-c1b8-41f8-b3b8-2b7d6cc85320" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Swan Valley is a region in the upper reaches of the Swan River between Guildford and Bells Rapids, Western Australia. It is bordered to the east by the Darling Scarp. Both Ellenbrook and Jane Brook lie within the region and discharge into the Swan River. There are seven suburbs within the region: Baskerville, Belhus, Caversham, Henley Brook, Herne Hill, Millendon and West Swan. It is part of the City of Swan local government area.

The Swan Valley Australian Geographical Indication is a sub-region of the larger Swan District wine region. Swan Valley was registered on 6 January 2003 following Swan&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/15fa4bb5-6e43-4bed-832c-d2155620ecd6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
