 <wpt lat="-32.052483" lon="115.74085">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBoVyU-05</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa02d</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #026 : S5 Tarndanya by Kyzabra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918814" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #026 : S5 Tarndanya</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kyzabra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kyzabra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #026&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Tarndanya&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What do we know Tarndanya as  ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2731a6ad-e56f-420d-a1ac-e247939addd7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Australia’s capital cities have ancient origins that predate European settlement. Indigenous cultures and languages thrived across the continent, each leaving their own mark on the landscape. By exploring the traditional names of these cities, we can acknowledge the enduring connection of Aboriginal peoples to the land and appreciate the rich heritage they bring to the nation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6dc6d8d-e092-4b30-9c83-b3752bf3e420" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This AdLab is themed around traditional place names.

Using traditional place names is truth-telling in action. It's a step towards acknowledging First Nations sovereignty.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052333" lon="115.7408">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBCv5C-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa02W</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S2 Weird Place Names - Point Torment by chickenhawk07</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918721" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S2 Weird Place Names - Point Torment</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chickenhawk07</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chickenhawk07</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #027&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Weird Place Names - Point Torment&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was it that attached the early explorers ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d86f2c6-c43d-4a2d-bbf3-f9e6e6314de6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia has a chequered history, but the naming of Point Torment is not based on any form of settler instigated activity that occurred in the area. Located 32km north of Derby, Point Torment was so named because explorer J L Stokes was subject to attacks from swarms of mosquitoes.

According to Landgate, here is what he had to say about the place back in February, 1838: "A name was soon found for our new territory, upon which we, with rueful unanimity, conferred that of Point Torment, from the incessant and vindictive attacks of swarms of mosquitoes, by whom it had evidently been resolved to give the newcomers a warm welcome". Stokes' description of mosquitoes as vindictive is right on the money, and with my unwarranted appeal to mosquitoes, I for one will be avoiding Point Torment for the foreseeable future.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/454e2a1a-eaa0-4856-86e1-ef11f2d79bd2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052283" lon="115.740733">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBCv5C-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa023</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S3 Weird Place Names - Useless Loop by chickenhawk07</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918723" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S3 Weird Place Names - Useless Loop</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chickenhawk07</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chickenhawk07</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #027&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Weird Place Names - Useless Loop&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What item is useless to a geocacher ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f0f4afa6-98d4-4d7c-9126-8b0dfa2d9e81" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Useless Loop could be used to describe any number of the road layouts implemented by local governments looking to spend their budgets before the next reporting period. However, the real Useless Loop is located on the Heirisson Prong in the Southern Region of Shark Bay.

The first half of Useless Loop's unusual name was bestowed upon it by French explorer Henri-Louis de Saulces de Freycinet who remarked on the large sandbank seemingly blocking access to an inviting harbour. Today though the town is far from useless, boasting world-class fishing and a beautiful marine park on your doorstep, and it has a school, a shop and community hall.

Intrepid travellers beware, Useless Loop is a closed company town, with 70 employees and their families servicing the Solar Salt Operation Shark Bay.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/454e2a1a-eaa0-4856-86e1-ef11f2d79bd2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
