 <wpt lat="-32.052483" lon="115.740783">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBoVyU-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa048</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #026 : S4 Garamilla by Kyzabra</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918812" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #026 : S4 Garamilla</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Kyzabra</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Kyzabra</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #026&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Garamilla&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/1f721226-f9ba-4865-b784-2153b4c506cf&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What do we know Garamilla as  ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b71aa908-ffc0-4c2d-8e21-852408eae384" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Australia’s capital cities have ancient origins that predate European settlement. Indigenous cultures and languages thrived across the continent, each leaving their own mark on the landscape. By exploring the traditional names of these cities, we can acknowledge the enduring connection of Aboriginal peoples to the land and appreciate the rich heritage they bring to the nation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e6dc6d8d-e092-4b30-9c83-b3752bf3e420" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This AdLab is themed around traditional place names.

Using traditional place names is truth-telling in action. It's a step towards acknowledging First Nations sovereignty.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052217" lon="115.740667">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBCv5C-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa02e</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S4 Weird Place Names - Innaloo by chickenhawk07</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918792" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #027 : S4 Weird Place Names - Innaloo</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chickenhawk07</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chickenhawk07</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #027&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Weird Place Names - Innaloo&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which of the following is not a reference to a Loo ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c2ceb071-60f3-44ec-9bdd-2f147a08320c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Innaloo was originally named Njookenbooroo (sometimes spelt Ngurgenboro, Noorgenbora or similar variants), believed to be derived from the local Noongar name for Herdsman Lake or a nearby wetland.

The spelling and pronunciation (e.g. "ny-ooken-borra") were deemed to be difficult to those unfamiliar with the name; in 1927, the local progress association asked welfare worker and anthropologist Daisy Bates to compile a list of possible alternative names, drawn from various Aboriginal languages. Bates' rendering of the personal name of a woman, "Innaloo" (from Dongara) was chosen.

Nevertheless, some local landmarks are still named "Nookenburra" – another variation of the original name.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/454e2a1a-eaa0-4856-86e1-ef11f2d79bd2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
