   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #027&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Weird Place Names - Disaster Bay&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d918570b-3b7b-4a91-bc60-95620a86add5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What would be a real disaster ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0f8c26cd-6a04-4ff2-8ed7-4b4c53e91e69" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Disaster Bay, located on the Dampier Peninsula in Western Australia, holds a unique place in the region's history, particularly related to the French Trappist mission. The bay was named by Captain Phillip Parker King of HMS Bathurst, who encountered difficult navigation and strong currents in the area. The Trappists established a mission and outrigger station at Disaster Bay, part of their broader mission work in the Kimberley region. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/454e2a1a-eaa0-4856-86e1-ef11f2d79bd2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
