 <wpt lat="-32.0528" lon="115.739667">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBPYxN-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa051</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S1 Fire by atCatpacity</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918904" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S1 Fire</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>atCatpacity</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">atCatpacity</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #051&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fire&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which season is represented by the colour red as it symbolises heat, sun and fire?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bfb7750a-8d5f-46b1-9a82-abb0a69b2f7f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;December-January

During this season the rain eases and the warm weather really starts to take hold. The afternoons are cooled by the sea breezes from the south west.

Traditionally this was the fire season. An almost clockwork style of easterly winds in the morning and sea breezes in the afternoon meant that this was burning time of the year for Noongar people and they would burn the country in mosaic patterns.

There are several reasons for this, including fuel reduction, increasing the grazing pastures for animals, to aid seed germination and to make it easier to move across the country.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed272129-11bc-4aef-97af-984fd14fa53e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

There are six Noongar seasons in Western Australia’s South West region, which are indicated by changes in local plants and animals. 

Each of the AdLab stages refers to one of the seasons. The final season is Djilba (August-September). This season is represented by the colour pink as it symbolises growth of wildflowers and plants.
This is the start of the massive flowering explosion that happens in the South West. Beginning with the yellow flowering plants such as the acacias.

Djilba is a transitional time of the year, with some very cold and clear days combining with warmer,rainy and windy days mixing with the occasional sunny day or two.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0528" lon="115.7396">
