  <name>LBPYxN-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa05t</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S2 Rain by atCatpacity</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918906" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S2 Rain</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>atCatpacity</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">atCatpacity</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #051&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Rain&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which season is represented by the colour dark blue as it symbolises rain and cold weather?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6526f9e6-a8d3-4bcb-ac56-d18387e281cd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;June-July

This season sees the coldest and wettest time of the year in the South West. Traditionally, this was a good time of the year to move back inland from the coast as the winds turned to the west and south bringing the cold weather, rains and occasionally snow on the peaks of the Stirling and Porongurup Ranges.

As the waterways and catchments started to fill, people were able to move about their country with ease and their food sources changed from the sea, estuarine and lake foods to those of the land, in particular the grazing animals such as the kangaroo.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed272129-11bc-4aef-97af-984fd14fa53e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

There are six Noongar seasons in Western Australia’s South West region, which are indicated by changes in local plants and animals. 

Each of the AdLab stages refers to one of the seasons. The final season is Djilba (August-September). This season is represented by the colour pink as it symbolises growth of wildflowers and plants.
This is the start of the massive flowering explosion that happens in the South West. Beginning with the yellow flowering plants such as the acacias.

Djilba is a transitional time of the year, with some very cold and clear days combining with warmer,rainy and windy days mixing with the occasional sunny day or two.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0528" lon="115.739567">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBPYxN-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa050</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S3 Flowers by atCatpacity</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918908" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S3 Flowers</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>atCatpacity</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">atCatpacity</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #051&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Flowers&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which season is represented by the colour yellow as it symbolises the return of the hot weather?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/654bc5d2-abc0-4ec2-8a3c-9867dde36f10" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; October-November

During this season,we see an abundance of colours and flowers exploding all around us.

The yellows of many of the acacias continue to abound, along with some of the banksias and many other smaller delicate flowering plants including the kangaroo paw and orchids.

During this time the balgas will continue to flower, especially if they've been burnt in the past year or closely shaved.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed272129-11bc-4aef-97af-984fd14fa53e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

There are six Noongar seasons in Western Australia’s South West region, which are indicated by changes in local plants and animals. 

Each of the AdLab stages refers to one of the seasons. The final season is Djilba (August-September). This season is represented by the colour pink as it symbolises growth of wildflowers and plants.
This is the start of the massive flowering explosion that happens in the South West. Beginning with the yellow flowering plants such as the acacias.

Djilba is a transitional time of the year, with some very cold and clear days combining with warmer,rainy and windy days mixing with the occasional sunny day or two.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
