 <wpt lat="-32.0528" lon="115.739517">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBPYxN-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa055</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S4 Wind by atCatpacity</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918910" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #051 : S4 Wind</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>atCatpacity</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">atCatpacity</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #051&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wind &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5a7a2cbb-3361-4a2d-a051-5afbc4e975a8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which season is represented by the colour orange and is the hottest time of the year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a3534807-d952-4e76-b1fc-f12cd8388d3b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; February-March

This is the hottest time of the year with little to no rain. Hot easterly winds continue with a cooling sea breeze most afternoons – if you're close to the coast.

Traditionally this was, and still is, a great time for living and fishing by the coast, rivers and estuaries. Because of this, freshwater foods and seafood made up major parts of the Noongar people’s diet during Bunuru.

This is also a time of the white flowers with lots of white flowering gums in full bloom, including jarrah, marri and ghost gums.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ed272129-11bc-4aef-97af-984fd14fa53e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

There are six Noongar seasons in Western Australia’s South West region, which are indicated by changes in local plants and animals. 

Each of the AdLab stages refers to one of the seasons. The final season is Djilba (August-September). This season is represented by the colour pink as it symbolises growth of wildflowers and plants.
This is the start of the massive flowering explosion that happens in the South West. Beginning with the yellow flowering plants such as the acacias.

Djilba is a transitional time of the year, with some very cold and clear days combining with warmer,rainy and windy days mixing with the occasional sunny day or two.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
