   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #22&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Quokka Shakka (A Colony of Quokkas)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b29dfbd1-890f-4f29-b9ab-38234dacd0d7"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b29dfbd1-890f-4f29-b9ab-38234dacd0d7&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where is the declining colony of quokkas?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6847c9e8-9a74-4da1-9c32-421d18304bb8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;An estimated 4,000 quokkas live on the mainland, with nearly all mainland populations being groups of fewer than 50.

Although one declining group of over 700 occurs in the southern forest between Nannup and Denmark.

In 2015, an extensive bushfire near Northcliffe nearly eradicated one of the local mainland populations, with an estimated 90% of the 500 quokkas dying.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d875d50d-1b5f-45c2-a690-11efe6ee8232" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0527" lon="115.739033">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBWHNq-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa05m</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d813470f-d2b7-4ff9-9482-f41e6cf236e4</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #055 - Pommie Adventures in Aus : S3 A pleasant walk along a very long jetty! by RegPlate</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1470" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #055 - Pommie Adventures in Aus : S3 A pleasant walk along a very long jetty!</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>RegPlate</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">RegPlate</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #055 - Pommie Adventures in Aus&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A pleasant walk along a very long jetty!&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d813470f-d2b7-4ff9-9482-f41e6cf236e4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d813470f-d2b7-4ff9-9482-f41e6cf236e4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Did you enjoy the walk to the end? How far is it back to dry land?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/708ec1fc-6a5d-4a62-9899-ec83d221b254" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We've dropped in here many times and seen the jetty in all its glory, brilliant sun and a howling rain storm. I recommend the sun option if you want to walk to the end!
If the water is clear, why not visit the underwater observatory and see the wildlife in all its natural environment.
And if its too far to walk back, perhaps the train is an alternative mode of transport.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e9a25770-161f-49fc-81aa-998143705f25" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

Hi all, my daughter &amp; family emigrated to WA Australia many years ago and we have been visiting regularly to see our grandaughters growing up.
This AdLab covers some places we have visited, well known landmarks and features in Western Australia. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.053133" lon="115.738967">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-30T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBaivg-05</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab45</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0b4437a3-da91-48fa-8ca8-c9cfc0093695</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #41 : S5 A short but exciting life. by megawat60</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1581" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #41 : S5 A short but exciting life.</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>megawat60</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">megawat60</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #41&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;A short but exciting life.&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0b4437a3-da91-48fa-8ca8-c9cfc0093695"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/0b4437a3-da91-48fa-8ca8-c9cfc0093695&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which country did Bon die?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ef347108-950b-4dde-b094-191ed5d35798" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On 15 February 1980, Scott attended a session where Malcolm and Angus Young were working on the beginnings of two songs that would later be recorded on the Back in Black album: "Have a Drink On Me" and "Let Me Put My Love Into You", with Scott accompanying on drums rather than singing or writing lyrics.  Young has also claimed Scott played drums on "Hells Bells" while AC/DC drummer Simon Wright has claimed Angus Young played him a demo of Scott playing drums on "Back in Black".

Days earlier, Scott had gone with Mick Cocks to visit their friends the French group Trust in the Scorpio Sound studio in London, where they recorded the album Répression; Scott was working on the English adaptation of texts by Bernie Bonvoisin for the English version of the album. During this visit, the musicians did a jam session of "Ride On". This improvised session was Scott's last recording.

Sometime during the late evening of 18 February and early morning of Tuesday, 19 February 1980, Scott passed out and died at the age of 33. He had just visited a London club called the Music Machine (currently known as KOKO). He was allegedly left to sleep in a Renault 5 owned by his friend Alistair Kinnear, at 67 Overhill Road in East Dulwich.  Later that day, Kinnear found Scott unconscious and alerted the authorities. Scott was taken to King's College Hospital in Camberwell, where he was pronounced dead on arrival. The official report of the coroner concluded that Scott had died of "acute alcohol poisoning" and classified it as "death by misadventure".

As part of the funeral arrangements, Scott's body was embalmed by Desmond Henley; it was later cremated, and Scott's ashes were interred by his family at Fremantle Cemetery in Fremantle.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/00af1bc3-dabe-47e1-a8dd-dfde7061dba3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

In the world of Rock n Roll probably one of the best know names is Ronald Belford "Bon" Scott, so continuing my AdLabs for this Quokka Art, who better to feature than the man himself!
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
