  <name>LBVRyX-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab22</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b29dfbd1-890f-4f29-b9ab-38234dacd0d7</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #22 : S1 Quokka Colonies by pood</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1488" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #22 : S1 Quokka Colonies</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>pood</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">pood</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #22&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Quokka Colonies &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b29dfbd1-890f-4f29-b9ab-38234dacd0d7"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b29dfbd1-890f-4f29-b9ab-38234dacd0d7&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where is the location of the largest population of quokkas found in their natural environment?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9bbec8f2-650a-4426-88dc-8bdc4d87f35c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Quokkas are native to Western Australia and can be found on both Rottnest Island and the mainland, with Rottnest Island being their most well-known and largest population centre. 

Rottnest Island is essentially free of natural predators that prey on quokkas, such as dingoes, red foxes, and feral cats. This allows the quokka population to thrive and grow. 
The island provides quokkas with a wide variety of food sources, including grasses, leaves, and shrubs. 

Rottnest Island is a small island off the coast of Western Australia, which has helped preserve its native wildlife, including the quokka population. 

While quokkas are also found on the mainland, their populations there have declined due to habitat loss, predation by introduced species, and other factors. The conditions on Rottnest Island have created a more stable and favorable environment for quokkas, making it their most significant stronghold. 
There is over 10,000 quokkas on Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d875d50d-1b5f-45c2-a690-11efe6ee8232" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
