   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #021&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Eurogliders&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c660ff50-2779-4e41-9a91-7d89a41a084e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/c660ff50-2779-4e41-9a91-7d89a41a084e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did these guys form?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5af560fb-b25c-4d1d-b234-8a803875bfcc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Eurogliders are a band formed in Perth, Western Australia, which included Grace Knight on vocals, Bernie Lynch on guitar and vocals, and Amanda Vincent on keyboards. In 1984, Eurogliders released an Australian top ten album, This Island, which spawned their No. 2 hit single, "Heaven (Must Be There)". "Heaven" also peaked at No. 21 on the United States Billboard Mainstream Rock charts and appeared on the Hot 100.  Another Australian top ten album, Absolutely, followed in 1985, which provided two further local top ten singles, "We Will Together" and "Can't Wait to See You". They disbanded in 1989, with Knight having a successful career as a jazz singer., Australian rock music historian Ian McFarlane described Eurogliders as "the accessible face of post-punk new wave music". The band's sophisticated brand of pop was traditional in its structure, but displayed the decidedly 'modern veneer' (hip clothes, heavy use of synthesiser)".  The band reformed in 2005 releasing two new albums followed in 2014 by their seventh album.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1b0b66cb-4c08-4ac4-9310-a772f797f827" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
This AdLab hopefully will celebrate some of the wonderful musicians and bands of Perth and Western Australia.  As the most isolated city in the world, WA certainly punches way above its weight in turning out some great and original bands.  I have picked 5 that resonated with me during my early days in Perth, having arrived in the late 70’s from Sydney.  All this information was from my memory or mostly stolen off Wikipedia!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052767" lon="115.73855">
