  <name>LBMsnH-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa062</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35c5c2ce-4a7f-4afc-890c-cb615c5579d6</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #061 : S2 WA's animal emblem by ullyssia</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1484" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #061 : S2 WA's animal emblem</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ullyssia</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ullyssia</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #061&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WA's animal emblem&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35c5c2ce-4a7f-4afc-890c-cb615c5579d6"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/35c5c2ce-4a7f-4afc-890c-cb615c5579d6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is WA's animal emblem?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e7e3af0a-e4ae-4721-819e-3bbc126055a2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia's animal emblem was once found across southern Australia but is now extinct throughout most of these areas. Today, it is found living in small patches of forest in the south-west of the State.

The destruction of habitat through clearing for farming and the introduction of foxes with European settlement led to the decline of the species.  While still endangered and listed as vulnerable, there has been some improvement. New populations have been established in nature reserves and forests, and the Perth Zoo is breeding the animals for release into protected habitat. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca56feb7-d1e9-48e1-a6fd-dbb425dba7e3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.


To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052467" lon="115.7384">
