 <wpt lat="-32.052467" lon="115.738383">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-26T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBEB6J-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa01o</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b76d406f-87c1-437d-9fdf-ddace6c028d4</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #019 : S2 Moondyne Joe by Rach2</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918756" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #019 : S2 Moondyne Joe</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Rach2</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Rach2</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #019&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Moondyne Joe &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b76d406f-87c1-437d-9fdf-ddace6c028d4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b76d406f-87c1-437d-9fdf-ddace6c028d4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did Moondyne Joe Make his escape&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0c0c7439-60d5-466f-97a0-39bd0f0cdecc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Joseph Bolitho Johns (c. February 1826 – 13 August 1900), better known as Moondyne Joe, was an English convict and Western Australia's best-known bushranger. He is remembered as a person who had escaped multiple times from prison.
In early 1867, due to his diminishing health, Johns was set to work breaking stone in the open air but, rather than permit him to leave the prison, the acting comptroller-general ordered that the stone be brought in and dumped in a corner of the prison yard, where Johns worked under the constant supervision of a warder. Governor John Hampton was so confident of the arrangements, he was heard to say to Johns: "If you get out again, I'll forgive you". However, the rock broken by Johns was not removed regularly, and eventually a pile grew up until it obscured the guard's view of him below the waist. Partially hidden behind the pile of rocks, he occasionally swung his sledgehammer at the limestone wall of the prison.

On 7 March 1867, Johns escaped through a hole he had made in the prison wall and it was two years before he was recaptured. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/73b523d5-5ee5-40cb-ae62-0fb6a05dd917" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
The section of the AdLab is based around Fremantle Prison and some of its famous inmates. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
