   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #063&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Lakes&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/88b248a6-b78b-4089-b480-73b188d92210"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/88b248a6-b78b-4089-b480-73b188d92210&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the largest natural lake in WA?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19445eee-112d-41b3-af0f-ab14a7c23eee" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Even though most of the state receives less than 400mm (16inches) of rain per year, with evaporation rates in excess of 3m (9ft) per year, WA is still home to many thousands of lakes, both intermittent and perennial. These include the man-made Lake Argyle, a reservoir so vast it's classed as an inland sea, and Lake Ballard, home to the world’s largest outdoor art gallery.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79f52816-70e6-4713-9929-b5327d247bd3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
