  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918699" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #017 : S5 Munda Biddi Trail</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>jinta29</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">jinta29</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #017&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Munda Biddi Trail&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ce6093cf-fe71-484d-86f5-7f4d95fb74cc"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ce6093cf-fe71-484d-86f5-7f4d95fb74cc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What town is at the halfway point of the trail and is home to tame kangaroos and emus?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/75261249-2db5-4544-a2fc-78a4b690bc60" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Munda Biddi translates to 'path through the forest' in the indigenous Noongar language spoken in South West of Western Australia. The Trail is a 1,072km off-road adventure cycling trail, like no other, traversing WA's scenic Darling Range and southern forests through eucalypt bushland, granite outcrops and unique coastal landscapes. 

South-West WA is recognised as a global biodiversity hotspot with unique endemic flora and fauna that is just incredible. There are few places in the world where a trail of this length could be built in a relatively undeveloped natural corridor. 

The window of cycling weather is generally good between late March to November in the south west of WA, although the summer in the northern jarrah forests can get extremely hot (over 40 degrees).  
The Trail has varied terrain (rail trail sections, single track and forest roads) suiting a range of riders from beginners to the more experienced, with many accessible points and road crossings that allow for easy drop offs, making your adventures more convenient. 

All information provided by https://mundabiddi.org.au/&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a6e8836a-1b0c-49bc-93f2-757ab313c984" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052517" lon="115.738067">
