   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #032&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Humpback Whales&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d4cb9158-78fb-41a3-8979-868e0cbcf2fb"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d4cb9158-78fb-41a3-8979-868e0cbcf2fb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How did the Humpback whale get its name?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a0d2c445-1efb-41d2-b53b-48adfcab6689" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Several species of animals migrate past Western Australia, including whales, birds and other marine life.

Humpback Whales:
It's estimated that 40,000 humpbacks travel along the coast of Western Australia Annually.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ec0b28c8-fa05-4883-9075-c86effbb437e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
