   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #032&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Blue Whale&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d4cb9158-78fb-41a3-8979-868e0cbcf2fb"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/d4cb9158-78fb-41a3-8979-868e0cbcf2fb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Blue Whales are ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/01cbd5f8-237f-478f-9f46-13b53e48347b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Several species of animals migrate past Western Australia, including whales, birds and other marine life

The Blue Whale migrates along the Coast of Western Australia and you often spot when from March to May.
The blue whale is not only one of the most well-known whale species, but it’s also the largest known whale in existence, growing to lengths of over 100 ft and weighing more than 150 tons, although 60 – 80 ft. is more common. They are on the Endangered Animal list.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ec0b28c8-fa05-4883-9075-c86effbb437e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
