  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918757" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S2 Most Common Orchid</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #087&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Most Common Orchid&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The cowslip orchid is part of which orchid family?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8e2253d4-c257-47ae-94c9-95a457879b50" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The most common orchid in Western Australia is the Cowslip orchid (Caladenia flava). It is known for its bright yellow flowers and widespread distribution across WA, from Geraldton to east of Esperance. It has a long flowering period, making it easier to spot during various times of the year. Its bright yellow colour makes it relatively easy to locate, even for beginners.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/191bff51-c256-4c38-83e0-2fd688482d13" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

In this ad Lab we will be looking at Western Australia's orchids.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0522" lon="115.7413">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSaUh-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa08R</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S3 Rarest Orchid by Lumpyjrb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918759" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S3 Rarest Orchid</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #087&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Rarest Orchid&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The Queen of Sheba orchid can take how long to flower?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3ac2ed34-020e-4641-a135-9c93f99de92e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Queen of Sheba orchid (Theylmitra variegata) is one of Western Australia's rarest and most iconic orchids. It is endemic to WA, with only a few locations known to contain it. The eastern Queen of Sheba orchid (Thelymitra speciosa) also a rare species native to southwestern WA. Although rare it is thriving, but because it is only found in two locations it is also highly threatened. It is part of the sun orchid family.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/191bff51-c256-4c38-83e0-2fd688482d13" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

In this ad Lab we will be looking at Western Australia's orchids.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052217" lon="115.741317">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSaUh-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa08h</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S4 The Strangest Orchid by Lumpyjrb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918761" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S4 The Strangest Orchid</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #087&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Strangest Orchid&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The underground orchid is the only plant in the world that is pollinated by .....&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/09921617-a61c-4c5b-a442-6a6762595f95" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Western Australian underground orchid, Rhizanthella gardneri, is often considered the strangest orchid in WA due to its unique lifestyle and appearance. It spends its entire life cycle, including flowering, below the soil surface, emerging only slightly with a head of small, hidden red flowers. This makes it quite different from the typical orchids with vibrant, above-ground blooms. Its key Charateristic is it is epiparasitic, meaning it doesn't photosynthesize and obtains nutrients from mycorrhizal fungi, which in turn get nutrients from the roots of the broom honey myrtle.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/191bff51-c256-4c38-83e0-2fd688482d13" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

In this ad Lab we will be looking at Western Australia's orchids.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05225" lon="115.74135">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-05-31T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSaUh-05</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa08e</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S5 Perfumed Orchids by Lumpyjrb</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918763" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #087 : S5 Perfumed Orchids</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Lumpyjrb</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #087&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perfumed Orchids&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/04018f99-f74c-4b70-8c60-75a756924fa5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The purple Enamel Orchid flowers between .....&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/09fe8671-8ed9-4eaa-9e15-41410b065f5e" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although not known for their perfume, several Western Australian native orchids are known for their fragrant scents. Two notable examples are the  Lemon-scented Sun Orchid or Vanilla Orchid (Thelymitra antennifera) and the Purple Enamel Orchid (Elythranthera brunonis). Lemon-scented Sun Orchid is native to Western Australia, South Australia, Victoria, and northern parts of Tasmania, and is known for its lemon-like fragrance. The Purple Enamel Orchid, an endemic species of the south-west of Western Australia, and some fairy orchids also emit a scent. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/191bff51-c256-4c38-83e0-2fd688482d13" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

In this ad Lab we will be looking at Western Australia's orchids.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05215" lon="115.74125">
