  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa09B</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2ba2f8e7-331c-4fab-b57e-ed76f222efd0</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #099 : S1 BIG SNAIL - Bremer Bay WA by Snoopy1976</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918845" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #099 : S1 BIG SNAIL - Bremer Bay WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Snoopy1976</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Snoopy1976</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #099&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;BIG SNAIL - Bremer Bay WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2ba2f8e7-331c-4fab-b57e-ed76f222efd0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2ba2f8e7-331c-4fab-b57e-ed76f222efd0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What does the Big Snail Weigh?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5b7528f8-e210-428c-85a2-b8b66b43bd9d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A concrete snail has emerged at Bremer Bay to celebrate an under-appreciated native snail.

Bremer-based landscape ecologist Nathan McQuoid, co-ordinating the project as contractor to the Fitzgerald Biosphere Group, said the sculpture was of a South Coast Land Snail, or Bothriembryon Melo.

“One of the most common things you see along the trail here is these snail shells, and they’re actually a native land snail of which there’s many species,” he said.

“They’re often overlooked and people don’t realise they’re a native and they’re a key part of the invertebrate ecology.

“So we thought we’d trumpet them a bit, give them a platform, give them some profile and make a super-sized snail sculpture.”

Mr McQuoid said the snail was very common around Bremer’s bushlands.

“You very rarely see them alive, but if it’s drizzly or humid or moist, you’ll see them out and about, cruising around,” he said.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e44a9fc8-cf07-4dcc-a66c-beee2a460849" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure is about Notable WA "Big Things&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
