   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #099&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;GIANT RAM - Wagin WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2ba2f8e7-331c-4fab-b57e-ed76f222efd0"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2ba2f8e7-331c-4fab-b57e-ed76f222efd0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which monument is taller?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/47558248-5cd5-4f00-9f6c-ff443d21eccc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Built in 1985, Wagin's Giant Ram is part of the the network of Big Things across Australia's rural and regional areas, although unlike many of the country's whimsical giants, this ram is anatomically correct.

Sculptor Andrew Hickson was commissioned to build the ram to commemorate the district's role in the Australian wool industry. Measuring in at 30 feet high, the beast's scale is second only to Goulburn's Giant Merino, likely the biggest ram in the world. However, what the Wagin ram lacks in height it makes up in genitalia size. While the Goulburn ram was not built with any visible reproductive organs, the proud Wagin ram sports a rather impressive faux-woolen pair between its oversized hind legs. The big balls are a popular photo opportunity and while sculptural scrotums are not unheard of (see the Wall Street Bull), they are almost never featured on giant-size installation. The ram even stands on a pavilion so that visitors can stroll beneath its bulk and inspect its undercarriage. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e44a9fc8-cf07-4dcc-a66c-beee2a460849" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure is about Notable WA "Big Things&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051167" lon="115.740717">
