   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #097&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Largest Fish Caught&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8ae704c0-e5c4-42ee-adda-4fc79f8e0e60"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/8ae704c0-e5c4-42ee-adda-4fc79f8e0e60&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the largest fish caught in Western Australia?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/04a2b8ad-a7e6-43fd-8521-121130b64c2b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In fishing-related terms, have you ever heard the expressions 'the one that got away' or 'it was this big'?

The largest fish caught in Western Australia, was a Blue Marlin weighing 494 kilograms (1089 pounds). It was caught off the coast of Exmouth in January 2018, by angler, Clay Hilbert.

According to IGFA records, the largest fish ever caught in Australia was a Great White Shark that weighed 1,208.389 kg (2,664 pounds). Caught off the coast of Ceduna, South Australia, in 1959, by angler Alfred Dean.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5d8f415a-095a-4433-9ed6-c3c9a05a51e8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051083" lon="115.740683">
