   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #071&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Death of Tom Edwards&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/65dc793a-8aaf-4175-81a7-53e90db1229c"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/65dc793a-8aaf-4175-81a7-53e90db1229c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;On what date did the Fremantle riots take place? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1981ec30-8b00-4929-8d3d-341cf2ae3efb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Tom Edwards was the only person who died as a result of violence in the riot on the Fremantle wharf on in May 1919, killed  by a blow to the head from a police rifle-butt or baton.

There was a confrontation between members of the Lumpers Union led by Bill Renton, and police brought personally to Fremantle by the Premier, Hal Colebatch, the lumpers having refused to allow strike-breakers to unload a particular ship, the SS Dimboola.

His funeral procession was attended by thousands. 

In memory of his sacrifice, a fountain was sculpted by Pietro Porcelli outside the Fremantle Trades Hall. It was moved to Adelaide Street near Town Hall, where it still commemorates "Comrade Tom Edwards, Working Class Martyr."&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7a027cb5-953b-4bcb-aa16-94006b15712d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.  This series will take you on a quick tour of some of my fav public Art installations in Fremantle. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051467" lon="115.740283">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBo37y-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa00g</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #007 : S1 Stage 1 - Lake Hillier by JKJK20</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1313" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #007 : S1 Stage 1 - Lake Hillier</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>JKJK20</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">JKJK20</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #007&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stage 1 - Lake Hillier&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Why is Hillier Lake pink?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/607287b6-dcaf-44aa-8067-55bb0703bab8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; Location: Middle Island, near Esperance, WA

Lake Hillier is famous for its vibrant pink color, which comes from special algae and bacteria that live in the salty water. What’s even more amazing is that the water stays pink even if you take it out of the lake! Surrounded by white sand and blue ocean, this lake looks like something out of a fairy tale — and it can only be seen by boat or air.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7205d174-5558-4b79-ab98-b3f740519b03" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05155" lon="115.74035">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-02T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBo37y-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa00y</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #007 : S2 Stage 2 - Lake Argyle by JKJK20</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1314" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #007 : S2 Stage 2 - Lake Argyle</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>JKJK20</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">JKJK20</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #007&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stage 2 - Lake Argyle&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/26579967-cdc9-4ac3-896a-32cc1cffd89a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was Lake Argyle built for, and what kind of animals live in it now?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0f240ab6-aafe-45c5-b001-28003d33064b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; Location: East Kimberley, WA

Lake Argyle is one of the largest man-made lakes in the Southern Hemisphere. It was built by damming the Ord River to provide water for farming and electricity. Today, it’s home to thousands of freshwater crocodiles, many types of birds, and fish. From above, the lake looks like a maze of islands and blue water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7205d174-5558-4b79-ab98-b3f740519b03" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.050917" lon="115.740183">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
