 <wpt lat="-32.052633" lon="115.738983">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-01T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrD1H-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab57</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S1 The Western Ringtail Possum by ringtailposs</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918827" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S1 The Western Ringtail Possum</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ringtailposs</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ringtailposs</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #57&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Western Ringtail Possum&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where is the Western Ringtail Possum predominatly found.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19fc601b-a24e-4f9b-a9a3-e1bda21fe263" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scientific name Pseudocheirus Petegrinus Occidentalis
Idigenous name Ngwayir
The Ringtail possum is only found in small pockets in the Southwest of WA , many in Busselton an surrounding areas. The possum its self has a small stocky body ,that ranges from dark grey to bownish. Its belly is typically white. It has a prehensile tail that has a white tip. Its ears are small and rounded. The adult in the wild can live to 4 to 6 years but longer in captivity. It will grow to about 1kilo for a female and up to 1.5kilo for a male cosiderably smaller then their Brushtail counterpart. Ringtail possums are territorial and singular creatures unlike the Brushtails that live in families.Ringtail possums have a prehrnsile tail that can be used as an extra hand, the end curls to form a ring ,that can be used to build their dreys and for juat hanging around.
It is illegal to keep Ringtail possums as pets as they are a protected native species.
Please enjoy our wildlife as wildlife and lers all help to protect them.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6e82859-eca9-4e67-84b3-bb622d92c854" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - Which intendsto form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth.
There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
This ones about another of our beautiful marsupials, the Western Ringtail Possum. Some will tell you its a load of baloney and absolutly detest them but then theres others whom see them as the endangered species that they are ,and how beautiful they actually are. Most of us that do see them this way have had the pleasure of hand raising one ,two or 300. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052617" lon="115.738983">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
