  <name>LBrD1H-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab5t</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S2 Western Ringtail Possum Feeding by ringtailposs</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918829" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S2 Western Ringtail Possum Feeding</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ringtailposs</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ringtailposs</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #57&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Western Ringtail Possum Feeding&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Coprophagy is the act that the Western Ringtail Possum perform to maximise nutrient saturation .What does this mean.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1434fe4d-11d2-41f5-98be-717450a2e7d2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Western Ringtail Possum is a herbivore and eats leavrs and flowers. In my backyard i have 6 dreys and a couple of possums who help themseves to my strawberries,roses,fejohs and the new shoots and leaves on my plants  i dont care but some people detest them for this reason.They love peppi leaves and bottlebrush flowers
Where i live we definatly have plenty of peppi trees. They forage in the treetop canopy and generally dont come to ground.
Baby ringtails eat the new shoots off the peppi leaves as they have a special oil in them that develops their brains. 
Toxic foods for possums are citrus, cheese, green potatoes and proccessed foods. Toxic foods cause problems with their digestive systems and can be fatal.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6e82859-eca9-4e67-84b3-bb622d92c854" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - Which intendsto form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth.
There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
This ones about another of our beautiful marsupials, the Western Ringtail Possum. Some will tell you its a load of baloney and absolutly detest them but then theres others whom see them as the endangered species that they are ,and how beautiful they actually are. Most of us that do see them this way have had the pleasure of hand raising one ,two or 300. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052633" lon="115.73895">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-01T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrD1H-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab55</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S3 Western Ringtail Possum babies by ringtailposs</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918831" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S3 Western Ringtail Possum babies</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ringtailposs</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ringtailposs</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #57&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Western Ringtail Possum babies&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;If a Western Ringtail Possum mum is scared or suffers trauma and the baby falls off  ,what will happen&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fa751326-b782-40e8-bc7e-7cbe737c97e5" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ringtail Possum babies are born like most mammals. Born and then they migrate to the pouch where they attatch to a teat.
A Ringtail Possum can have 1 babies sometime 2 very rarely 3 . The baby is born without fur and is called a Pinky, as they grow they will grow fur and will be attatched to the teat.
When they are old enough and fully furred the baby will spend short amounts of time outside the pouch, but can still feed by pulling the teat out of the pouch. 
When they are old enough they leave the pouch and ride on mums back. Baby possums will start chewing on the baby leaves to get the oil to develop their brains. If you are in an are where there are Rungtails you maybe lucky enough to see a baby or 2 riding on mums back at night.
When a mum suffers trauma or is frightened ,she may run or jump and loose the baby off her back. That baby may never be reunited with mum as she may not come back. This is when volunteer carers step in to handraise the baby.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6e82859-eca9-4e67-84b3-bb622d92c854" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - Which intendsto form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth.
There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
This ones about another of our beautiful marsupials, the Western Ringtail Possum. Some will tell you its a load of baloney and absolutly detest them but then theres others whom see them as the endangered species that they are ,and how beautiful they actually are. Most of us that do see them this way have had the pleasure of hand raising one ,two or 300. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0526" lon="115.73895">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-01T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrD1H-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab5S</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S4 Western Ringtail Possum Enviroment by ringtailposs</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918833" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #57 : S4 Western Ringtail Possum Enviroment</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ringtailposs</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ringtailposs</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #57&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Western Ringtail Possum Enviroment&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6aefa9f5-402e-4323-98e6-98e589f12709&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;A Western Ringtails nest is called what&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/bd13f2c3-6518-4351-acba-f7ea55450571" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Western Ringtail Possum lives in the treetop canopy, a possum found on the ground is possibly not well,dehydrated or a juvenile that has been dropped and left behind. Possums live in nests called dreys . They build 2 different types of dreys the first being a flat standing or feeding drey and the second is like a cacoon. The cacoon is built by the possum by intertwining sticks and leaves and bark. It makes a nice safe enviroment for mum and baby. Quiet often when hot the possum will lay flat out on its back in the drey and like its feet and belly to cool down. They will have multiple dreys cause you never know where you will be caught out when the sun comes up. 
Part of Busseltons planning is to have possum coridors, so there is plenty of canopy for possums to move from one place to another, without the need to come down on the ground. Even to the point of building possum bridges across the highway. These possum bridges are built from treetop height on one side of road to other side so no need for ppossums to come down. Possums are at great risk on the ground as they are slow movers and become prey to cats dogs ,cars and birds of prey.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b6e82859-eca9-4e67-84b3-bb622d92c854" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - Which intendsto form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth.
There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
This ones about another of our beautiful marsupials, the Western Ringtail Possum. Some will tell you its a load of baloney and absolutly detest them but then theres others whom see them as the endangered species that they are ,and how beautiful they actually are. Most of us that do see them this way have had the pleasure of hand raising one ,two or 300. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
