   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #45&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fish #2&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/088444e3-ad3d-479a-a28d-874872871882"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/088444e3-ad3d-479a-a28d-874872871882&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which fish am I?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6917c16b-9d30-4679-922f-c8eaca955926" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This fish is another popular recreational fish in WA waters. Their distribution ranges from Exmouth in northern part of WA to Albany in the south. They have a missile shaped head, forked tail and a powerful streamlined frame, its well suited to the turbulent waters of surf beaches. They are pack hunters and have very sharp teeth. In 1996 the largest recorded was at The Aquarium of WA (AQWA), it was believed to be 11 years old and reached a length of more than 1m and weighed in at 15.7kg.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/be0c4763-e30d-4df6-953f-a8138b34f844" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the4 WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
