  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLab48</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/088444e3-ad3d-479a-a28d-874872871882</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #45 : S4 Fish #4 by LOLOACacheIC</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1621" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #45 : S4 Fish #4</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>LOLOACacheIC</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">LOLOACacheIC</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #45&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fish #4&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/088444e3-ad3d-479a-a28d-874872871882"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/088444e3-ad3d-479a-a28d-874872871882&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What fish is this?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f736ad16-ab66-461d-aa31-3b5d17e63387" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This fish is found in coastal waters of Japan, China, Taiwan, Phillippines, New Zealand and Australia. In WA, this fish is found from Karratha in the north, through to the Great Australian Bight. This is mainly a bottom dwelling fish found at depths of 200-300m along the continental shelf, however they do spend some of their lives in mid to upper water levels. They feed on small fish, crustaceans, worms, molluscs, jellyfish, sea stars, sea urchins and algae. They are slow growing but long living fish that can live around 40 years or so. Their slow growth means they are less able to recover from overfishing. The photo shows a juvenile and adult.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/be0c4763-e30d-4df6-953f-a8138b34f844" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the4 WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
To join this project, please contact the project admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
