   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #100&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Fremantle - Perth Train Service&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b4a08bbc-4bd8-4504-a994-ab3f3be58f00"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b4a08bbc-4bd8-4504-a994-ab3f3be58f00&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When did the station in Cliff Street open?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0da802f5-1e5f-45cd-ab00-391d5fe923b7" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The first train service between Fremantle and Perth was part of the Eastern Railway which opened in 1881 and was later extended and became the basis for the modern day Fremantle and Midland lines. The cost of a train ticket in 1881 was between 1 and 2 shillings. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e871336a-ecef-43a2-a284-02737accb5ac" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
 
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
