   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #100&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;North West Ore Trains &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b4a08bbc-4bd8-4504-a994-ab3f3be58f00"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b4a08bbc-4bd8-4504-a994-ab3f3be58f00&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How long was the train?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/73277048-66a1-49b8-89c0-5b77868e6768" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the North West of Western Australia, rail is often used to transport ore from mine sites to ports to export overseas. In September 2024, a train transported 82,000 metric tonnes of iron ore between the Yandi mine and Port Hedland in the Pilbara region of Western Australia. The world’s longest train was made up of 682 wagons powered by 8 diesel locomotives. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e871336a-ecef-43a2-a284-02737accb5ac" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.
 
To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
