  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa06y</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #065 : S3 Bicycle Delivery by WA K Team</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1525" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #065 : S3 Bicycle Delivery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>WA K Team</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">WA K Team</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #065&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Bicycle Delivery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What animal was depicted on the second set of stamps used by &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/79858c8d-a0bc-4c6a-81cb-f95b82c40df2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Coolgardie Cycle Express Co. was a delivery service that began with a mail delivery from Coolgardie to Ora Banda on a bicycle in 1893. In early 1894 they expanded the service to Southern Cross and other goldfields locations.  Sadly, the company was only short lived as the telegraph line reached Coolgardie &amp; Kalgoorlie from Southern Cross in August 1894 the company was liquidated. However, the company continued to operate to other locations.
The company issue stamps reflecting the letter delivery charge.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9d97a8a5-760a-4abc-bf42-cde9646d96d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

This AdLab explores "The Postal History of Western Australia' - ie WA post &amp; stamps prior to federation.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0522" lon="115.738833">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjDtw-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa06x</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #065 : S4 Taxing Stamps by WA K Team</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1526" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #065 : S4 Taxing Stamps</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>WA K Team</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">WA K Team</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #065&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Taxing Stamps&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was the highest value Revenue stamp used by WA prior to 1966&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f7991aa5-c798-42ec-9dd2-c0b8810286f2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;WA issued a number of revenue stamps of different designs depicting the black swan.  
From 1881 to 1904 overprinted postage stamps were used.  In 1904 the iconic "long swans" were introduced and used up until 1940 with various values up to one hundred pounds.  In 1941 the smaler format "small swans" were introduced and used until the introduction of decimal currency in 1965.

Revenue stamps were used to pay for state government services and to collect various state taxes such as payroll taxes, property and company stamp duty etc.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9d97a8a5-760a-4abc-bf42-cde9646d96d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

This AdLab explores "The Postal History of Western Australia' - ie WA post &amp; stamps prior to federation.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052217" lon="115.738867">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-03T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBjDtw-05</name>
