   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #065&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;First Post Offices&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/cd919853-e9e3-4aec-9723-3d2dd1992f84&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was the Post Office in Perth established as the main post office?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/59a40970-2ca3-42a2-b646-ec6355e95026" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Western Australia established its postal service soon after settlement in 1829. In December of that year, Fremantle's harbourmaster was appointed postmaster. A post office in Albany, Western Australia opened on 14 October 1834, and the main post office moved to Perth with James Purkis appointed as the first Postmaster.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9d97a8a5-760a-4abc-bf42-cde9646d96d8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

This AdLab explores "The Postal History of Western Australia' - ie WA post &amp; stamps prior to federation.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052833" lon="115.738883">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-05T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBzZL2-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa10O</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/03a0c9c8-f42b-4e9a-a9cf-e8c1b2d19428</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #109 : S1 Our nearest neighbours by chrishere</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918952" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #109 : S1 Our nearest neighbours</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>chrishere</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">chrishere</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #109&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Our nearest neighbours&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/03a0c9c8-f42b-4e9a-a9cf-e8c1b2d19428"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/03a0c9c8-f42b-4e9a-a9cf-e8c1b2d19428&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;From an orbital perspective, which two planets are Earth's nearest neighbours?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/49a6351e-10a2-44e7-a1bd-4d31266f26ce" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The solar system is our cosmic neighborhood, made up of the sun and everything that orbits it — planets, moons, asteroids and comets. The solar system formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a giant rotating cloud of gas and dust.  Within our solar system there are 9 planets and 5 named dwarf planets.

(Image credits: NASA and National Geographic)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4e3e243e-5860-481e-86a9-7686ba28c313" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal. 

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This Adventure Lab is located in the southern part of Western Australia which has low humidity levels and virtually no air pollution, creating pristine night skies and the ideal location for stargazing.  In this Adveture Lab we have a quick look at a couple of our neighbours in the Solar System.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052817" lon="115.738883">
