   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #101 : S3 World's Happiest Animal, the Quokka</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>MummaBear5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">MummaBear5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #101&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;World's Happiest Animal, the Quokka&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12cba197-dfa2-433c-830e-b841ce0ffc13"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12cba197-dfa2-433c-830e-b841ce0ffc13&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many quokkas are left in the world?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a90c8ebf-6f55-465a-ae41-c736b12ff5b3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Playful, adorable, friendly, and cute as a button, quokkas have recently won the title of the happiest animals on earth. But, while quokkas are undeniably cheerful, they are gravely endangered, with less than 15,000 left in the world. Native to Western Australia, most of these fluffy creatures live on Rottnest Island, a short boat ride from Perth.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/667dc5c3-5fa9-4675-a5d4-44ea6f7788f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051067" lon="115.740417">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBZmpT-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa157</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12cba197-dfa2-433c-830e-b841ce0ffc13</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #101 : S4 Emus Rule Western Australia by MummaBear5</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918935" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #101 : S4 Emus Rule Western Australia</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>MummaBear5</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">MummaBear5</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #101&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Emus Rule Western Australia&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12cba197-dfa2-433c-830e-b841ce0ffc13"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/12cba197-dfa2-433c-830e-b841ce0ffc13&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was The Great Emu War?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5013d1eb-49fa-4ab6-a569-203558ce2b4b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the later part of 1932, the Royal Australian Artillery went to war, not with invaders, but with enormous flightless birds native to the country, known as the emu.
The conflict broke out after World War I veterans turned farmers started settling in Western Australia. They intended to till the land, but emus overrun the area, destroying the crops. Thus, the Great Emu War began. In the end, the military exercise proved futile, and the emus were the apparent victors.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/667dc5c3-5fa9-4675-a5d4-44ea6f7788f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
