   <groundspeak:owner id="">JKJK20</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #102&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Stage Four - South Fremantle Power Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5f44b592-9006-48a7-898f-917c1e03b3c1"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5f44b592-9006-48a7-898f-917c1e03b3c1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where was most of the power station machinery manufactured?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/00f126ea-db98-47da-88c5-dc24debe75ef" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Fremantle's most haunted places  
The South Fremantle Power Station is widely considered haunted, with many reports and stories suggesting paranormal activity. While there's no definitive proof of ghostly activity, the building's eerie history, abandonment, and association with alleged events like suicides, satanic worship, and murder have contributed to its reputation as a haunted location. 
Eerie History:
The South Fremantle Power Station was built in the 1950s and closed in 1985. It now stands mostly abandoned, visited by explorers and ghost hunters. There are numerous rumors surrounding the building, including tales of suicides, satanic rituals, and murders, which have fueled the belief that it's haunted. 
Paranormal Activity Reports:
Some individuals have reported experiencing unusual sensations, like feeling like someone is going to jump out and stab them, especially in certain areas of the building. 
Visiting the Power Station is Trespassing. It's important to note that visiting the South Fremantle Power Station is trespassing and considered dangerous. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0ff39bf4-4d8e-416d-93d9-9ed5881ed321" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.053083" lon="115.739267">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-11T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBV2LZ-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa152</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/14ab7efc-d146-4c30-96ce-d5f2fa2aff82</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #105 : S4 Beacon Battery by Two+Four</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1386" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #105 : S4 Beacon Battery</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Two+Four</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Two+Four</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #105&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Beacon Battery&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/14ab7efc-d146-4c30-96ce-d5f2fa2aff82"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/14ab7efc-d146-4c30-96ce-d5f2fa2aff82&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where did they consider moving the Battery to?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1a2a3f14-4e04-4226-a3bc-7612cfd8ed11" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Beacon Battery was located east of the Challenger Battery, near the northern tip of Garden Island. Manned by personnel of the nearby Challenger Battery, it consisted of two 4-inch Mk XIX naval guns. The battery was tasked with protecting the boom net on the northern end of Cockburn Sound and was operational from March 1943. During 1944, it was considered to move the battery to Carnac Island but this was not carried out and, like Challenger and Peron batteries, it was placed into care and maintenance in December 1944.

Beacon Battery	Garden Island	32.156667°S 115.665833°E	2 × 4-inch Mk XIX naval guns&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/83483b1a-aea5-4267-b815-7edbc63d2b6f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

This lab is themed on Fremantle Fortress

Fremantle Fortress was the combined coastal defences protecting the harbour of Fremantle, Western Australia, since the mid-1930s and, predominantly, during World War II. The coastal defences of the Fremantle Fortress stretched along the coastline of Perth from Cape Peron to Swanbourne and also included installations on Garden Island and Rottnest Island. While the first coastal batteries of the future Fremantle Fortress were installed at Arthur Head in 1906, the military installations protecting the harbour were expanded in the 1930s, being eventually dismantled again by 1963.

During World War II, Fremantle Harbour, guarded by the Fremantle Fortress, was home to the Fremantle submarine base, which was the second-most important submarine base in the Pacific War after Pearl Harbor.

The coastal batteries were manned by members of the Royal Australian Artillery, the Royal Australian Engineers and the Volunteer Defence Corps. Apart from these men, members of the Australian Women's Army Service also served at the Fremantle Fortress.

Of the former installations, the Oliver Hill Battery and its associated railway have been preserved, remaining a tourist attraction on Rottnest Island. Parts of the Leighton Battery, Buckland Hill, have been restored and its underground facilities are accessible. A bunker and one of the Panama mounts of the Peron Battery are also preserved and accessible.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
