  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa169</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S1 Australian White Ibis (Threskiornis molucca) by SkyCat12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918968" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S1 Australian White Ibis (Threskiornis molucca)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SkyCat12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SkyCat12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #058&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Australian White Ibis (Threskiornis molucca)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;By which name is this bird colloquially known?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e1eebf40-eb4a-43cc-94ca-34632e34fc85" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Australian White Ibis have long, down-curved beaks, which are perfectly adapted for probing through mud, sand, and weeds to find food. They also use their beaks to hammer open shells of mussels and shellfish.

Originally associated with wetlands and marshes, it is now commonly seen in urban areas scavenging from available human food sources.

Fun fact: flying at low altitude between foraging and roosting sites, the Australian white ibis can reach speeds of up to 30kms per hour

Image credits @ Ian Davies, Geoffrey Groom (eBird)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e00df3e-02c0-4a3a-8588-3d6ec26cea34" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This educational journey explores the diverse birdlife found along the coastal regions of South West Western Australia and Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
