</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052583" lon="115.738883">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtjQA-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa170</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S2 Greater Crested Tern (Thalasseus bergii) by SkyCat12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918970" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S2 Greater Crested Tern (Thalasseus bergii)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SkyCat12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SkyCat12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #058&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Greater Crested Tern (Thalasseus bergii)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Which feature would enable you to differentiate this bird from a Caspian Tern?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/44dcfcae-9573-419a-9627-f44957b2ec8c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There are several different tern species commonly sighted along the WA coast - the most frequent visitor between Fremantle and Rottnest is the Greater Crested Tern.

Best viewing spots are at the North and South Mole and the beaches and rocks of Rottnest.

Fun fact: they hover a few meters above the water before diving into the sea to catch fish for their dinner.

Image credits @ Hayley Alexander, Doug Cameron (eBird)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e00df3e-02c0-4a3a-8588-3d6ec26cea34" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This educational journey explores the diverse birdlife found along the coastal regions of South West Western Australia and Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
