 <wpt lat="-32.052617" lon="115.738867">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtjQA-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa05y</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S3 Banded Stilt (Cladorhynchus leucocephalus) by SkyCat12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918972" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S3 Banded Stilt (Cladorhynchus leucocephalus)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SkyCat12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SkyCat12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #058&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Banded Stilt (Cladorhynchus leucocephalus)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Rottnest Island is home to what percentage of the world's Banded Stilt population?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b92bd266-be42-4d4e-be58-7a3ce049ace2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These shorebirds gather in some of the largest concentrations of any species, with flock numbers of over 150,000.

They will fly long distances to remote salt lakes if sufficient water is present.  You can reliably find them all year round in the wetlands of Rottnest Island.  

Fun fact: historically nicknamed the Rottnest Snipe due to it's prevalence on the island.

Image credits @ Adam Fry, David Tytherleigh (eBird)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e00df3e-02c0-4a3a-8588-3d6ec26cea34" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This educational journey explores the diverse birdlife found along the coastal regions of South West Western Australia and Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052583" lon="115.738833">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-08T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBtjQA-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa171</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S4 Osprey (Pandion haliaetus) by SkyCat12</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918974" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S4 Osprey (Pandion haliaetus)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SkyCat12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SkyCat12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #058&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Osprey (Pandion haliaetus)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the Noongar name for the Osprey?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5e2b53a2-03d6-4035-a9f0-419501d9cfea" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Osprey is a large, mostly white raptor that cruises over lakes, rivers, and coastal waterways in search of fish. Head and underparts are mostly white, back feathers and tail are a dark brown. In flight they hold their wings with a kink in the wrist (shaped like an "M"). 

There are several Osprey nests located around the island: they are semi-permanent structures built from sticks and tend to be maintained and reused each season. The number of breeding pairs varies from year to year.  

Fun fact: often seen plunging feet-first into water from high in the air to grab fish.

Image credits @ David Irving, David Brown (eBird)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e00df3e-02c0-4a3a-8588-3d6ec26cea34" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This educational journey explores the diverse birdlife found along the coastal regions of South West Western Australia and Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0526" lon="115.738833">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
