  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1712" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #058 : S5 Rainbow Bee-eater (Merops ornatus)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SkyCat12</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SkyCat12</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #058&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Rainbow Bee-eater (Merops ornatus)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/efa82f0c-0122-4e42-a0ee-ce142d51151e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This bird is typically rather vocal.  What does its call sound like?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/73312e5f-11f8-4ccd-b23e-b78a6b4380fb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This brightly coloured bee-eater has a yellow face and black mask markings.  They have green and blue underparts with elongated black tail streamers, and rust-colored underwings which are obvious in flight. 

Rainbow Bee-eaters often perch in numbers in trees, powerlines and on wires to facilitate hunting of flying insects - be sure to look up from time to time when walking around to view these beautiful birds. 

Fun fact: you will most likely hear them before you see them, due to their distinctive call.

Image credits @ Mat Gilfedder, Chris Wills (eBird)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2e00df3e-02c0-4a3a-8588-3d6ec26cea34" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka
off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs
to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry
Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on
WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This educational journey explores the diverse birdlife found along the coastal regions of South West Western Australia and Rottnest Island.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
