 <wpt lat="-32.052833" lon="115.73875">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgheP-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa164</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S1 Natural Wealth by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1303" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S1 Natural Wealth</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #108&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Natural Wealth&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the largest mine in WA in terms of sheer scale?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8eb26a36-7419-405c-8a61-c75f375a85ce" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Following the first discovery of lead ore in 1848 near Northampton, mining operations in Western Australia have grown massively in scale, annual production and value. With more than 50 different minerals being produced from 134 predominantly higher-value and export-oriented mining projects (plus hundreds of smaller quarries and mines), mining in Western Australia now accounts for 62% of the total Australian output, with an annual value in excess of $180bn.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc132de7-e532-45ec-b47f-fa8d76f9d138" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05285" lon="115.7388">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgheP-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa165</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S2 Gold Rush by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1304" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S2 Gold Rush</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #108&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gold Rush&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the largest gold nugget found in WA? (So far!)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2be72fe0-a956-45a7-aeba-2f52187fcaeb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gold mining in Western Australia dates back to the 1880s but became a significant industry in the 1890s, following gold discoveries at Cue in 1891 and Coolgardie in 1892. In 1893 a new claim was registered further east and within 3 days 750 men were prospecting the area. The gold rush that followed saw a town quickly spring up, initially called Hannans, and later renamed Kalgoorlie. Today, gold mining is the third largest commodity sector in Western Australia behind iron ore and LNG, and accounts for almost 70% of all gold sold in Australia. The allure of gold also still attracts between 15,000 and 20,000 individual prospectors each year who head out into the WA bush in the hope of finding the next big one.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc132de7-e532-45ec-b47f-fa8d76f9d138" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052833" lon="115.7388">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-09T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgheP-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa166</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S3 Diamonds Are Forever by Barco</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1305" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #108 : S3 Diamonds Are Forever</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Barco</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Barco</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #108&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Diamonds Are Forever&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ece5ba8b-3732-4395-8044-41239cc446f3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the largest rough diamond mined in WA?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c8a8d863-854a-45ed-a2af-1a7e52c0f6ca" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Diamond samples were first discovered in 1979 in the floodplain of a small creek that flowed into Lake Argyle. Over the next few years The Argyle Diamond Mine grew to eventually become the largest diamond producer in the world by volume (14 million carats in 2018). It was the only known significant source of pink and red diamonds (producing over 90% of the world's supply), and additionally provided a large proportion of other naturally coloured diamonds, including champagne, cognac and rare blue diamonds. The mine closed in 2020 after 37 years of operation during which it produced more than 865 million carats of rough diamonds.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/cc132de7-e532-45ec-b47f-fa8d76f9d138" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
