  <desc>ThornlieCockbu1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0c57ea5-8b75-400a-b8ed-e04ef18e2c2e</url>
  <urlname>Thornlie-Cockburn Link : S1 Cockburn Central by InternalLights</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-980" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Thornlie-Cockburn Link : S1 Cockburn Central</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>InternalLights</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">InternalLights</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Thornlie-Cockburn Link&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Cockburn Central &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0c57ea5-8b75-400a-b8ed-e04ef18e2c2e"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a0c57ea5-8b75-400a-b8ed-e04ef18e2c2e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What platform does the Thornlie-Cockburn link depart from? NUMBERS ONLY&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/7e201daf-7f7d-4d93-b628-c39df799e323" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Trains on the Thornlie-Cockburn Link will terminate at Cockburn Central Station and turn back towards Thornlie, while providing passenger interchange to the Mandurah Line.

Cockburn Central’s existing platform will be extended 162m to create the terminating platform and provide direct services for passengers to Perth Stadium without needing to transfer through Perth.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2154cef3-fe4a-4fdc-8506-2cec735488d9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Thornlie-Cockburn Link will be Perth’s first east-west cross line connection, making travel more flexible while providing a higher level of public transport service to Perth’s south-eastern suburbs.

Connecting the Mandurah and Armadale-Thornlie lines will open new opportunities for longer-term developments around the new Nicholson Road and Ranford Road stations.

The Thornlie-Cockburn Link will support growth and accessibility across the south-eastern suburbs by providing direct access to employment and recreation opportunities while supporting about 1,680 jobs through the new stations and upgrades to the existing Thornlie, Cockburn Central and Stadium stations.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051167" lon="115.7406">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSukt-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa09Q</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S1 WAQuokka #095.1 - New Norcia, WA by sacada</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476919006" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S1 WAQuokka #095.1 - New Norcia, WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sacada</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sacada</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #095&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WAQuokka #095.1 - New Norcia, WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What unexpected drama unfolded near the oval in New Norcia?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/1f48c6e7-2086-4c46-af40-e792d9714038" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Tucked away in the wheatbelt of Western Australia, New Norcia is not your average outback town — it’s Australia’s only monastic village. Founded by Spanish Benedictine monks in the 1840s, the place hums with history, European architecture, and the faint scent of freshly baked monk-made bread. With ancient stone buildings, olive groves, and an eerie calm that makes you whisper without knowing why, it feels like someone accidentally dropped a Mediterranean village in the middle of rural WA.

We rolled in with our trusty car and caravan, aiming for a peaceful lunch by the oval. Instead, we performed an accidental demonstration of “how not to park on wet grass.” Moments later, both car and van were gloriously bogged. No worries, though — some friendly campers joined the rescue party. Spades in hand, we dug, pushed, swore politely, and cheered as we finally got free. Just as we finished, one of the would-be rescuers casually said he had a Trax in his truck parked nearby... but didn’t want to get it dirty. I guess some adventures are best left to the mud-loving amateurs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b73b084c-5410-4bff-a301-9fd61a1bc64b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

There are lots of amazing places in Western Australia. Here are some of my adventures.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051217" lon="115.740617">
  <ele>4.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSukt-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa09k</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S2 WAQuokka #095.2 - Meekatharra, WA by sacada</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918961" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S2 WAQuokka #095.2 - Meekatharra, WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sacada</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sacada</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #095&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WAQuokka #095.2 - Meekatharra, WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What surprise did Meekatharra throw at us?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f1c07d28-e634-4f73-8132-5770d7738362" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the heart of the Murchison region, Meekatharra is a true-blue outback town where red dirt rules, road trains thunder through, and the sky stretches wider than your sense of direction. Once a gold rush hub, now it’s a rugged stopover for prospectors, adventurers, and those chasing caches in the middle of nowhere. It's the kind of place where the pub is still the news centre and the only traffic jam involves a stray emu.

Our visit to Meeka took an unexpected turn when we made a quick detour to the local hospital. False alarm, but the friendly nurses were thrilled to have someone to chat with — apparently patients are rarer than rain. Speaking of which, it had been raining. The red dirt turned into thick, clingy mud that loved tyres more than gravity. A short detour off the road for a cache became a bog-fest, and we later discovered we'd unknowingly taken a scenic route down a road officially closed. Oops. Classic Meeka mischief.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b73b084c-5410-4bff-a301-9fd61a1bc64b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

There are lots of amazing places in Western Australia. Here are some of my adventures.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051133" lon="115.740617">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSukt-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa173</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S3  WAQuokka #095.3 - Laverton, WA by sacada</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918963" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S3  WAQuokka #095.3 - Laverton, WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sacada</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sacada</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #095&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt; WAQuokka #095.3 - Laverton, WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What warning did prospectors give in Laverton?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27a0baa4-e041-4ec0-9395-325119a0f943" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Laverton sits on the edge of Western Australia's wild east, where the bitumen ends and the red tracks to the unknown begin. It's a land of gold, grit, and grand tales, where prospectors still chase fortune and the desert whispers stories of those who came before. With the Central Desert Highway stretching out like the world's most ambitious shortcut, Laverton is a launchpad for outback dreams—or dramas.

We arrived just in time for a downpour and found ourselves stranded in the local van park, surrounded by grizzled prospectors and very muddy tyres. The word was that venturing off the road during the wet could cost you $1000 per tyre. So, we all huddled around a campfire, spinning yarns and waiting out the weather. I made the rookie mistake of asking where they do their prospecting. The silence was immediate. They all stared, then one muttered, “Mate, we don’t even tell each other.” Classic goldfield code. The next day, the roads dried just enough for us to make our escape up the Central Highway—where we promptly stopped to help fellow travellers stuck at water crossings. Outback karma.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b73b084c-5410-4bff-a301-9fd61a1bc64b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

There are lots of amazing places in Western Australia. Here are some of my adventures.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051083" lon="115.740617">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSukt-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa09d</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S4  WAQuokka #095.4 - Gibb River Road, WA by sacada</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918965" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S4  WAQuokka #095.4 - Gibb River Road, WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sacada</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sacada</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #095&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt; WAQuokka #095.4 - Gibb River Road, WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Why didn’t we cross the river on the Gibb?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/edce165e-2c1c-4a7c-8c5b-52accb65bab4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The legendary Gibb River Road cuts through the heart of the Kimberley in outback Western Australia—a 660km-long test of nerve, suspension, and whether your travel insurance covers water crossings and airborne fridges. It’s one of those tracks that’s equal parts bucket-list and break-your-axle.

We rolled in from the east, ready to conquer the mighty Gibb... until we met our match: a river crossing that looked more like a moving quarry. Croc-infested, rocky, and hundreds of metres wide, it had its own cheering squad of campers on deck chairs. We watched a 4WD bounce through like it was on a mechanical bull. Then came the tow truck—car on the tray, another casualty. “Third one today,” a spectator casually noted, guzzling beer. After enough rubbernecking, we made the difficult but wise call: we liked our car and caravan in one piece. So we turned back… via the El Questro road. Which, judging by the van-shattering corrugations, may have actually been worse. We didn’t make it far before declaring defeat again. Sometimes the outback wins, and you just shake the red dust off and try another day.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b73b084c-5410-4bff-a301-9fd61a1bc64b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

There are lots of amazing places in Western Australia. Here are some of my adventures.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.0511" lon="115.740617">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-17T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBSukt-05</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa09J</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S5 WAQuokka #095.5 - Gascoyne Junction, WA by sacada</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918967" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #095 : S5 WAQuokka #095.5 - Gascoyne Junction, WA</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>sacada</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">sacada</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #095&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;WAQuokka #095.5 - Gascoyne Junction, WA&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/847d54ec-6814-4951-afe8-f4f42c462fe2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What did we do in Kennedy Range NP?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/331b5e73-a12a-45c9-bd57-e5aa408e0b53" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Tucked in the vast heart of outback Western Australia, Gascoyne Junction is a remote speck of civilization where red dirt stretches for days and the road signs start getting lonely. It’s the kind of place where driving “just down the road” could mean a 300km detour, and where flat tyres and busted axles are almost part of the welcome sign.

On my first journey through, I passed through the Junction on a backroads mission to Murchison, only to come across a stranded crew with an entire axle and wheel missing from their car. They’d been waiting a day and a half, with a tow truck expected sometime tomorrow… maybe. On a later trip, we ventured into Kennedy Range National Park, arriving just before sunset. Most would’ve waited for daylight, but we figured, why not do a steep rocky climb now? As darkness swallowed the trail, we wandered the eerie flat-topped range, unsure where the cliff edges were—perfect conditions for reflective thinking... and nervous laughter. Caches found, limbs intact, nerves slightly frayed—we called it a win.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b73b084c-5410-4bff-a301-9fd61a1bc64b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

There are lots of amazing places in Western Australia. Here are some of my adventures.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052733" lon="115.73915">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX1Cy-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa05q</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #054   : S1  Meckering earthquake by Chookman8</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1707" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #054   : S1  Meckering earthquake</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chookman8</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chookman8</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #054  &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt; Meckering earthquake&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How long did the earthquake last&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/663a65f5-aae1-4a6a-9bd0-dc55997fa810" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Meckering earthquake, 14 October 1968

In the Wheatbelt town of Meckering, on a sultry day in 1968, the town’s 600 residents were enjoying the Queen’s Birthday public holiday when, at 10.59am, the ground began to shake.

A powerful magnitude-6.9 earthquake ripped through homes, streets and paddocks. For 40 seconds the ground shook and heaved, destroying almost every building. The PDF symbol
quake’s shallow depth
meant the damage was complete, wiping Meckering out.

The few homes and buildings that weren’t flattened were extensively damaged. Power and telephone lines were cut and in places the earth cracked and rose up almost 2.5 metres.

Seventeen Meckering residents were injured but remarkably no one was killed.

It is one of the largest seismic events ever recorded in Australia, felt throughout southern WA. Buildings in York and Northam sustained damage and in York three people were also injured. In Perth, cracks appeared in buildings and roads, including Kwinana Freeway. Tremors were felt as far away as Albany and Esperance.

Though rebuilding began soon after, many Meckering residents chose to leave.

Meckering's population today is less than 250. Remnants of the damage have been preserved and a fault line is still visible 12km south of the town on the York-Meckering Road.
A man is touching the outside of a crumpled car, which is buried under a pile of bricks from a neighbouring building that has been destroyed in the Meckering earthquake
The Meckering earthquake is still one of the largest seismic events ever recorded in Australia&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5eaa658d-5150-44de-9f47-b6ce249b4180" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;?	Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
