 <wpt lat="-32.052733" lon="115.7391">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-14T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBX1Cy-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa176</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #054   : S4 Perth hailstorm, 22 March 2010 by Chookman8</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918973" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #054   : S4 Perth hailstorm, 22 March 2010</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chookman8</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chookman8</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #054  &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Perth hailstorm, 22 March 2010&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ecb13a2a-adf2-412e-acde-d1f8ae20ae07&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt; The total damage bill was estimated at what amount?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a1185bb0-f9df-4bd1-be48-90f2993dd5b2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;While the Bureau of Meteorology expected a damaging storm on that autumn day in March 2010, the scale of the damage was completely unforeseen.

Hailstones, some the size of golf balls, pelted the Perth metropolitan area smashing windows, roofs and cars. There was also heavy rain - in the 24 hours from 9am on the 22nd, the Perth metropolitan area registered its
fifth highest March daily rainfall

since records began in 1876. Homes and buildings were flooded, and a landslide was triggered by the downpour in Kings Park damaging two apartment buildings below and leading to evacuations.

In addition to the hailstones and flooding, lightning damaged powerlines and brought many down causing hazardous conditions. Adding to the chaos, the storm hit during the weekday peak hour when many people were on the road. More than 100,000 homes were without power across a large part of the metropolitan area.

Insurance claims across the State topped $100 million and the total damage bill was estimated to be $1.08 billion. The event was officially declared a natural disaster by the State Government allowing those worst affected to receive disaster relief funds.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/5eaa658d-5150-44de-9f47-b6ce249b4180" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;?	Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
