   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #076&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The XY Falcon&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/59a8c712-5721-4133-a14a-83c06a9cc6c3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/59a8c712-5721-4133-a14a-83c06a9cc6c3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many GT HO Phase lll Falcons were produced?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8c8c8223-f0e7-4533-9056-a8a8a8cf8f48" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The XY Falcon was produced between 1970-1972. These vehicles were produced with a 351 cubic inch V8 engine. The sedan version made in 1971 was known as the GT HO phase lll . There were only 300 GT HOs produced. The GT HO phase all is the greatest ever Australian High Performance sport sedan in motoring culture.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/33ad5cf7-3901-4fbc-84a8-236d58a9d1f2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Quokka AdLab series- which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on the stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05145" lon="115.740933">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBgQuV-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa194</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/59a8c712-5721-4133-a14a-83c06a9cc6c3</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #076 : S3 The XA Falcon by Falcon351</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1474" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #076 : S3 The XA Falcon</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Falcon351</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Falcon351</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #076&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The XA Falcon&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/59a8c712-5721-4133-a14a-83c06a9cc6c3"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/59a8c712-5721-4133-a14a-83c06a9cc6c3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many versions of the XA Falcon were there?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4dfad9ef-a520-4ee4-b778-49be96773846" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The XA Falcon was a crucial model for the Ford Motor Company in Australia. This car came in 5 different versions . The sedan, coupe, panel van ute and station wagon.The 2 door hard top was created with the vision of going racing, with its 4.9 litre V8 engine. The XA coupe would go on to win Bathurst in both 1973 and 1974 . It became the highest selling Falcon of its time. 
We also owned a XB panel van&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/33ad5cf7-3901-4fbc-84a8-236d58a9d1f2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Quokka AdLab series- which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on the stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05135" lon="115.740917">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-27T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBpK3v-05</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa200</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773</url>
  <urlname>WA Quokka AdLab Art #074 : S5 05 - Mt Henry Bridge(s) by Team Red Roo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918985" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WA Quokka AdLab Art #074 : S5 05 - Mt Henry Bridge(s)</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Team Red Roo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Team Red Roo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WA Quokka AdLab Art #074&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;05 - Mt Henry Bridge(s)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When as the first Mt Henry Bridge opened to traffic&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/525840b9-4ae1-4afa-8616-bf3677f11f70" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like the Narrows Bridges, Mt Henry Bridge is actually two road bridges - the first opened in 1982 and the second in 2006. These bridges also hold a rail section in between allowing trains to run from Perth City to as far south as Mandurah.

Best views are from the Esplanade, in Mt Pleasant and there is parking available underneath the bridge on the southern side if you want to try cycling to Canning Bridge and back around the Mt Pleasant foreshore. There are plenty of excellent caches around this cycle track.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df3ac083-3eac-43e5-85f5-4706261a8cb9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like many major cities around the world, Perth is situated on a river with it's population and business centers being on both sides of the river. For business and the general public to access quickly it requires bridges across the river at various points and a road system to cope with the traffic levels. 
This Lab introduces you to five of the main bridge systems across the Swan and Canning Rivers several of which are tourist attractions. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05145" lon="115.7409">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
