   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WA Quokka AdLab Art #074&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;03 - The Causeway / Boorloo Bridges&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year was Boorloo Bridge opened to the public&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/42d8698a-6453-4568-bced-8f5e16b8596c" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The causeway bridges consists of two road bridges (one after the other) spanning the Canning River between Victoria Park and East Perth using Heirisson Island in the center of the river. The bridge system was first opened in 1843. 
More recently a pedestrian &amp; cycleway (Boorloo Bridge) system has been build on the south west side of the causeway bridges and opened in 2024, allowing for cycle and pedestrian traffic to cross the river in relative safety. 

Views may be had of both bridge systems from Heirisson Island and both Boorloo Bridges, with parking available on east side of Heirisson Island, between the two road bridges.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df3ac083-3eac-43e5-85f5-4706261a8cb9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like many major cities around the world, Perth is situated on a river with it's population and business centers being on both sides of the river. For business and the general public to access quickly it requires bridges across the river at various points and a road system to cope with the traffic levels. 
This Lab introduces you to five of the main bridge systems across the Swan and Canning Rivers several of which are tourist attractions. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051333" lon="115.7409">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
