  <urlname>WA Quokka AdLab Art #074 : S4 04 - Windan and Matagarup Bridges by Team Red Roo</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1505" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WA Quokka AdLab Art #074 : S4 04 - Windan and Matagarup Bridges</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Team Red Roo</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Team Red Roo</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WA Quokka AdLab Art #074&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;04 - Windan and Matagarup Bridges&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/5e5028b6-5b09-4770-a899-2fd811acd773&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;When was Matagarup Bridge opened to the public&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3878b088-89b2-402b-8896-db5c7e3ee260" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Windan Bridge is a road bridge taking traffic from the freeway north across the Swan River along the Graham Farmer Freeway and via the Graham Farmer Tunnel. This bridge was opened in 2000 and sits next to the Goongoongup Rail Bridge which was finished 5 years earlier.

Views can be taken from the walk/cycle track along either side of the river or from Matagarup Bridge - a wide pedestrian/cycle bridge across the river to the south of Windan Bridge, giving easy access to the sports stadium. The land on the east side of the river now holds the sports stadium and tennis courts and has been redesigned and landscaped to become a major feature for Perth. Matagarup Bridge was opened in 2018

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/df3ac083-3eac-43e5-85f5-4706261a8cb9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like many major cities around the world, Perth is situated on a river with it's population and business centers being on both sides of the river. For business and the general public to access quickly it requires bridges across the river at various points and a road system to cope with the traffic levels. 
This Lab introduces you to five of the main bridge systems across the Swan and Canning Rivers several of which are tourist attractions. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.05205" lon="115.740467">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrUxh-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa06j</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S1 Ngarrijirr / Night Parrot by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1567" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S1 Ngarrijirr / Night Parrot</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Ngarrijirr / Night Parrot&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Where would a Night Parrot build their nest?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/28332d4a-8ab9-4017-9dcc-aab300a79f9b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Night Parrot (Manyjilyjarra name: Ngarrijirri, scientific name: Pezoporus occidentalis) is a mostly ground-dwelling parrot that’s only found in Australia. For around 100 years they were feared extinct. They are nocturnal and hide in clumps of spinifex by day and emerge after sunset to forage for food. They build theirs nests under the very prickly and spikey Triodia spinifex, with 2-4 white eggs being produced. Night Parrots are very difficult to monitor and remain one of Australia’s most cryptic species: cryptic meaning hard to see, well camouflaged.

The Night Parrot has a conservation status of Critically Endangered, which means a species has an extremely high chance of dying out or becoming extinct in the future. In 2024, Ngururrpa Rangers and the University of Western Australia published their project based on audio recordings, that identified up to 50 Night Parrots in the Great Sandy Desert. 

Ref: Night Parrot Information Sheet, WA Museum (March 2023)
https://www.uwa.edu.au/news/article/2024/september/breakthrough-discovery-indigenous-rangers-in-outback-wa-find-up-to-50-night-parrots-one-of-australias-most-elusive-birds&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87aa6925-19fd-4e89-9a62-cdb7cc07b2d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure highlights some lesser-known but very cool Western Australian animals from the Kimberley and Pilbara, which are under threat of extinction. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
