  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918989" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S2 Wirridanyniny / Galwany / Freshwater Sawfish</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wirridanyniny / Galwany / Freshwater Sawfish&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What type of fish is a sawfish? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2383ff57-7a1a-45c3-9c37-538db404c1cc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The rivers of North-West WA are home to Pristis microdon, the Freshwater Sawfish. This is the largest freshwater fish in Australia, and is critically endangered - sometimes called “the world’s most endangered fish.” The sawfish belongs to stingray family, and can grow up to 7 metres long. They use the “saw” to sense prey using electrical signals in the water, and they, unlike sharks, they do not have replacement teeth. 

The sawfish is protected from hunting, unless by Indigenous traditional owners. The larger sawfish have been an important supply of food for Aboriginal people for millennia, however can be too big to carry and are generally only taken if there are no other fish available.

The female sawfish give birth to live pups into coastal areas near freshwater rivers - the babies have a special cover on the “saw” to protect mum during birth! The young then travel upriver where they grow for 4-6 years, before eventually migrating back to the ocean. The level of water in the river is a key factor in their ability to travel between larger pools, and for overall survival of the juvenile fish. 

https://library.dbca.wa.gov.au/FullTextFiles/628158.pdf
Photo: https://www.marineconservation.org.au/sawfish/&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87aa6925-19fd-4e89-9a62-cdb7cc07b2d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure highlights some lesser-known but very cool Western Australian animals from the Kimberley and Pilbara, which are under threat of extinction. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
