  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S3 Minyjirri / Gouldian Finch by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918991" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S3 Minyjirri / Gouldian Finch</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Minyjirri / Gouldian Finch&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What face colour is NOT usual for a wild Gouldian finch? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4f8471ec-e714-45fc-9bbc-8dff0953d5de" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Gouldian Finch (Miriwoong: Minyjirri, scientific name Erythrura gouldiae) is understandly one of the most recognisable birds of Northern Australia. For a century it was sought for international export, with demand for aviaries high due to its stunning colours. This, combined with a mite infection and ongoing habitat loss from fire and grazing, has led to dwindling numbers and being named on Australia’s Endangered Species List. 

The birds grow to around 12-14cm long, and have several natural colour variations in the wild, with black, red and yellow faces. The males have a bright purple breast, while the females have a duller mauve colour. Interestingly, mating between black- and red-headed birds can result in changes to the sex balance of offspring, with many more males produced due to a level of genetic incompatibility. The birds feed on grass seeds from savannah areas, nesting in hollow eucalypts. 

https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/biodiversity/threatened/publications/gouldian-finch-erythrura-gouldiae-2008
https://australian.museum/learn/animals/birds/gouldian-finch/
Photo: Martin Pot, via Wikipedia &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87aa6925-19fd-4e89-9a62-cdb7cc07b2d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure highlights some lesser-known but very cool Western Australian animals from the Kimberley and Pilbara, which are under threat of extinction. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
