 <wpt lat="-32.051767" lon="115.74015">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-06-22T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBrUxh-04</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa203</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S4 Odorr / Dugong by banthoms</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="1476918993" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals : S4 Odorr / Dugong</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>banthoms</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">banthoms</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Odorr / Dugong&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What dugong is known as a “sea… “ what? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/75bf7a77-438c-461f-ba82-63c52a651aaa" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The dugong (Bardi / Jawi: Odorr, scientific name Dugong dugon) is a member of the Sirenia family, related to the manatee - the sirens of Greek legend. The Australian museum describes a dugong as a “large, grey brown bulbous animal” - perhaps not the stereotypical mermaid. They are also well described as the “sea cow” - large (up to 400kg), slow moving and eating grass. 

In this case, it’s seagrass, which has reduced due to pollution, changes in sea temperatures and other uses, eg land reclamation. The dugong uses its snout to snuffle across seagrass meadows, leaving a sand plume in its wake. Its slow reproductive cycle of 12-14 months gestation and up to 7 years between calving also means that human activities such as gillnetting can have a large effect on dugong populations. Dugongs can travel internationally for migration, and it is listed as “vulnerable” internationally and is on the Convention of International Trade in Endangered Species register. 

In the Kimberley, the Indigenous Saltwater Advisory Group, comprising traditional owners of the length of the NW coastline, have led the Indigenous Turtle &amp; Dugong Initiative. The vision is to support “healthy and  sustainable habitat and populations of sea turtle and dugong in Kimberley waters, using Western science and Indigenous knowledge for this culturally significant animal.

Resources: Australian Museum, worldwildlife.org, ISWAG Indigenous Turtle &amp; Dugong Initiative Implementation Plan 2022-2032
Photo: Australian Museum

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87aa6925-19fd-4e89-9a62-cdb7cc07b2d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure highlights some lesser-known but very cool Western Australian animals from the Kimberley and Pilbara, which are under threat of extinction. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
