   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #069: NW endangered animals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Wiminji / Northern Quoll&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/ed93de7d-5227-40d4-89cd-e3749647d40f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Why do male Northern Quolls live only one year? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f2d1ea40-ee0c-4fc7-9892-0265fa4522cd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Northern Quoll (Martu: Wiminji, scientific name Dasyurus hallucatus) is the smallest of Australia’s four quoll species. They are only 0.5-1kg in size, and mostly carnivorous, eating intertebrates, small mammals, reptiles and fruits. Despite this small size, they are largest known mammal to exhibit semelparity, where an individual expends so many resources to breed that they die after a single breeding season. The males live only one year and are thought to die from walking long distances in search of a mate, sleep deprivation, fighting and lack of eating. However, the females tend to be more vigilant and rest more often, living up to three years. 

Wiminji are under threat from feral cats, uncontrolled fires, and being poisoned by eating cane toads. A key conservation measure is the “waterless fence” between the Kimberley and the Pilbara, which may protect the Pilbara from cane toad invasion. Traditional burning, to prevent out-of-control late season fires, and feral animal containment will also help protect the sparse Northern Quoll populations.

https://www.australianwildlife.org/animals/northern-quoll
https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.221180#d1e1100
Photo: B Leue, T Sayers - Australian Wildlife Conservancy &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/87aa6925-19fd-4e89-9a62-cdb7cc07b2d1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com

This adventure highlights some lesser-known but very cool Western Australian animals from the Kimberley and Pilbara, which are under threat of extinction. 

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
