   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #043&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Nigilgi Cave&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9b58ce02-7869-4e6f-a798-02744b023a76"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9b58ce02-7869-4e6f-a798-02744b023a76&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What does Yallingup mean in Aboriginal?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b4d266b6-66ad-4540-b8d6-e6f3afa9fc74" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ngilgi Cave, formerly known as Yallingup Cave, has a rich history intertwined with both Aboriginal Dreamtime stories and European discovery. 

It's one of the oldest show caves in Western Australia, with a history dating back over half a million years. 

The cave is significant in Wardandi Aboriginal culture as the setting for a Dreamtime story about the good spirit Ngilgi battling the evil spirit Wolgine.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/378568d3-abe1-413c-a381-19d06c1b18a6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
