   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #037&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Hutt Lagoon Pink Lake, Kalbarri&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7b75c005-3b03-45f0-b5ba-4a60d0d78581"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7b75c005-3b03-45f0-b5ba-4a60d0d78581&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the best way to get to Hutt Lagoon from Kalbarri?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e5896684-e0e6-4947-9712-b3c37b4f1b20" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hutt Lagoon is a very popular (and photogenic!) tourist destination in Western Australia’s Coral Coast region.

Visiting the lake is free and going for a dip is allowed, making this one of the most visitor-friendly pink lakes in WA.

This reliable, year-round pink lake is a must-see on your WA road trip. Hutt Lagoon is located an hour drive from Geraldton, 40 minute drive from Kalbarri, not far from Northampton or only a 5.5-hour drive north of Perth and one of many natural highlights on the west coast of Australia, alongside Lancellin Sand Dunes, The Pinnacles, and Nature’s Window in Kalbarri National Park.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0a2f565e-9d3e-4f26-b6d6-8a6a97258d9f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
