 <wpt lat="-32.0521" lon="115.7405">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-07-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBwUkF-01</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdla02V</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka Adlab #028 : S1 Pimbee Road via Wahroonga Station by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1661" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka Adlab #028 : S1 Pimbee Road via Wahroonga Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka Adlab #028&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Pimbee Road via Wahroonga Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;This windmill is located very close to cattle grid&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/19f073f3-facd-4b7a-b9e6-0aff000a22e0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a gravel road from North West Coastal Highway.   North of the Woomamel River  to the Gascoyne Junction.  It is advised not to drive this way if wet or raining.
There is 18 cattle grids along this track.  With not many cattle in sight.  This trip none. Only 1 kangaroo that was going to race me or hit my front fender.  Lucky escape for both &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b51a0bf9-96a5-4dc8-9e7d-cf0f5dd5cfbf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.052033" lon="115.740433">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
  <time>2025-07-18T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBwUkF-02</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdla223</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka Adlab #028 : S2 Gascoyne Junction by ebenie</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1662" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka Adlab #028 : S2 Gascoyne Junction</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>ebenie</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">ebenie</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka Adlab #028&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gascoyne Junction &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/05150a41-7e21-4703-979c-01629ee0f8b4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The new Gascoyne Bridge was finished in&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/8037ae5c-3466-478a-88ba-30901b3b1521" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The township of Gascoyne Junction is on the south side of the river which is mainly the business side EG pub, caravan park, roadhouse and shire offices, and rec centre with a few houses
Most people live on the North side of the river and are know as the river people.
The first bridge built the bridge across the river bed in 1931 with access cutting the traffic from north to the mainly pub.  Most have dinghy’s for this purpose but a flying Fox was installed somewhere. Or swim to which Buddy Williams wrote a song called Les Dingo.  The river was cut of in floods and cyclones that are prone to the area 
In 2010 Gasoyne Junction was wiped out by a massive flood and River people were evacuated along with the town people. 
Eventually the township has been rebuilt with a higher bridge  
But the iconic pub will always be missed
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b51a0bf9-96a5-4dc8-9e7d-cf0f5dd5cfbf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.051983" lon="115.740367">
  <ele>1.0</ele>
