  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #040 : S5 Hotham Valley Railway by dezzabills</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1884" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #040 : S5 Hotham Valley Railway</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>dezzabills</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">dezzabills</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #040&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Hotham Valley Railway&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number of the steam train used at HVR&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d268eaee-1316-4317-9280-2b526c44f658" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Hotham Valley Tourist Railway (commonly Hotham Valley Railway) is a tourist and heritage railway in the Peel region of Western Australia. We operate over a 32 kilometer section of the original Pinjarra to Narrogin railway line, and has its origins in a small group of enthusiasts who met together in 1974 with the object of preserving both Western Australian steam locomotives and the railway line itself. Dwellingup is now the primary centre of Hotham Valley's operations.
Today we operate both steam and diesel locomotive hauled trains on a variety of services and is staffed exclusively by volunteers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dce8047e-8208-4ecf-856d-6ffb56ed31a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.053117" lon="115.738667">
  <time>2025-07-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBqG35-03</name>
  <desc>WAQuokkaAdLa220</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26</url>
  <urlname>WAQuokka AdLab Art #040 : S3 Holyoake by dezzabills</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1882" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>WAQuokka AdLab Art #040 : S3 Holyoake</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>dezzabills</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">dezzabills</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #040&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Holyoake&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Holyoake emerged as a timber settlement around 1910, it was linked to whom?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c3c062a3-0d2e-4f53-a0af-49c39ff9165a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Holyoake, located near Dwellingup in Western Australia, was a timber town that thrived in the early 20th century, particularly around the South West Timber Hewers Co-operative Society's mill. The town was established in the same area as other timber settlements like Nanga Brook and Marrinup. It was a busy railway siding, with timber being transported out daily. The town was largely destroyed by the devastating 1961 Dwellingup bushfires, along with other nearby mill towns. While Dwellingup was rebuilt, Holyoake and the other mill towns were not, and their sites are now popular recreation areas. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dce8047e-8208-4ecf-856d-6ffb56ed31a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
