   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #040&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Dwellingup Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e247ea17-2884-4cd0-a895-5cb94449fa26&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What does the term "staff and ticket" refer to?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a0468419-b47e-4b44-9070-19a758fc2d04" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Dwellingup Railway Station has a rich history tied to the timber industry and the Hotham Valley Railway. Today, it's a popular destination for heritage railway enthusiasts, offering scenic train rides through the Jarrah forest. 

On the original Pinjarra to Narrogin rail line, Dwellingup is the first town after leaving Pinjarra. It came into existence after the arrival of the railway in 1910, and it had the first triangle for turning locomotives, which still exists today, it became a crucial hub for timber transport and a bustling town center. A major bushfire in 1961 devastated the area, but Dwellingup and its station were rebuilt, unlike many of the surrounding timber mills. Originally a temporary station building was erected at Dwellingup, but after World War Two, more permanent structures were established. 

On the 5th September 1911, Dwellingup became a block station under staff and ticket regulations and this is still used today. 

On the 1st January 1912 the first station master was appointed, with the last station master being withdrawn on the 7th May 1971.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dce8047e-8208-4ecf-856d-6ffb56ed31a3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
