   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;WAQuokka AdLab Art #039&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Our Fave Places in WA - Perth Mint&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a2c6432d-a028-4959-b56a-16e13ab76dc9"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/a2c6432d-a028-4959-b56a-16e13ab76dc9&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The 93kg King Henry on display contains how much gold?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b963ce19-f110-43f9-811d-c2298b48310a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Initially a branch of the British Royal Mint producing coins for the British Empire, Perth Mint is now the worlds only government owned and guaranteed precious metals enterprise.
It is a major gold and silver refiner providing investment products and operating a retail shop where visitors can purchase metal products and souvenirs.
The tours are a must where it is possible to see a traditional gold pour and explore exhibitions and displays of gold and precious metals.

Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art - Please join in by contacting WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9e7fcfd4-1f93-433b-8214-caeafb5bfe30" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the WAQuokka AdLab art series - which intends to form a giant quokka off the coast of Perth. There is a 500m geofence on stages which allows the AdLabs to be solved from between the Fremantle Maritime Museum and the Rottnest Ferry Terminal.

To join this project, please contact the project Admins on WAQuokkaAdLab@gmail.com&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.551567" lon="115.71865">
  <time>2025-07-13T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBLZMT-01</name>
  <desc>LBOUNDARYISLAN1</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f6433565-d520-41be-bbe3-d19f71a0c860</url>
  <urlname>[L] BOUNDARY ISLAND MARKERS  : S1 CRABS by OOH YEH</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-1995" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>[L] BOUNDARY ISLAND MARKERS  : S1 CRABS</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>OOH YEH</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">OOH YEH</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;BOUNDARY ISLAND MARKERS &lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;CRABS &lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background:#990000;color:#fff;border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px;"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f6433565-d520-41be-bbe3-d19f71a0c860"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/f6433565-d520-41be-bbe3-d19f71a0c860&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number on this channel marker ?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/fb9a48c0-00b5-4f38-94f1-e961e73c1c2f" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Crabs are one of the many crustaceans in this area .
Blue Swimmer Crabs have large clawed front legs that they use for both hunting and defense. Their back legs are smaller and shaped like paddles, making them powerful swimmers.?The upper part of their shell is known as the carapace (ca-ra-pace). The carapace is broad, flat and edged in sharp teeth-like ridges. The last teeth on each side are pronounced, resembling horns.
Although each crab is unique in its colour vibrancy and shell pattern, males are always blue whilst females are a mottled brown.
Adult Blue Swimmer Crabs inhabit the sandy floors of inshore marine waters, such as bays and estuaries. During the early stages of their life cycle the larvae float at the water’s surface up to 80 kilometres from the coastline.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/819db19f-4d7f-4e64-94f1-284c0fca345d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Boundary island 
One of many islands in the Mandurah estuary 
You can get to this place by boat, paddle board, kayak or swim .
The island is a breeding ground for many of the bird life in the area .
To obtain the bonus you must complete this lab by obtaining the necessary numbers from the channel markers .&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
