  <groundspeak:cache id="-462" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Whistler Walk 1 : S2 Australian Raven</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>Chwiliwr</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">Chwiliwr</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Whistler Walk 1&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Australian Raven&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/90591efc-8a80-4354-8a75-4903bcf3db15"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/90591efc-8a80-4354-8a75-4903bcf3db15&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the scientific name.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/d02b7e42-39bf-4292-9db9-b4f09f1ca1a6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A large, all-black bird glossed with purple and bluish-green and with long pointy feathers on the throat. The eye is whist in adults and brown in immatures.

They are found throughout southern and eastern Australia, in Western Australia north to the Wooramel River, the Eastern Goldfields and southern fringe of the Nullabor Plain;  they are common in Bungedore Park.

(Image by Peter Rowland)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/59d90e7c-b537-43d0-9122-5a9586cd4423" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Whistler Walk is a loop trail through the northern area of Bungendore Park in the Wungong Regional Park.

Take a walk beneath Jarrah, Marri, Casuarina and Banksia trees. Enjoy the whistles and calls of the park’s diverse birdlife and the beautiful blossoms.

The one-directional loop trail is well sign posted with boot cleaning stations along the trail to help prevent the spread of Phtytophthora Dieback.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.172286" lon="116.043111">
