   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Whistler Walk 1&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Snottygobble&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/90591efc-8a80-4354-8a75-4903bcf3db15"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/90591efc-8a80-4354-8a75-4903bcf3db15&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What colour are the flowers?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/250e0733-cefb-4df3-9b23-8b3ff373efbc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Snottygobble, Persoonia Longifolia, is and attractive small tree with long narrow leaves found throughout the Jarrah forest.  It is single-stemmed and grows from 2m to 5m in height with a roundish bushy crown.

This beatifully barked tree is so named for its sticky succulent fruita.  It generally grows in association with another snottygobble, Persoonia elliptica, which has oval shaped leaves.

(Image by Caro Telfer, Photographer)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/59d90e7c-b537-43d0-9122-5a9586cd4423" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Whistler Walk is a loop trail through the northern area of Bungendore Park in the Wungong Regional Park.

Take a walk beneath Jarrah, Marri, Casuarina and Banksia trees. Enjoy the whistles and calls of the park’s diverse birdlife and the beautiful blossoms.

The one-directional loop trail is well sign posted with boot cleaning stations along the trail to help prevent the spread of Phtytophthora Dieback.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.955667" lon="115.89975">
  <ele>28.0</ele>
  <time>2025-08-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBc884402</name>
  <desc>YarloopHistory2</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844</url>
  <urlname>Yarloop History : S2 Yarloop Railway Station by dezzabills</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-2036" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Yarloop History : S2 Yarloop Railway Station</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>dezzabills</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">dezzabills</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Yarloop History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Yarloop Railway Station&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What was completed in November 1898?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/28a5f184-0041-474d-a3dd-5712d71a9704" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yarloop Railway Station's history is closely tied to the development of the town's timber industry. Initially a siding on the Perth-Bunbury railway line, it became a vital hub with the establishment of Millar's timber mill and its extensive railway system. The station served not only as a passenger stop but also as a crucial link for transporting timber and equipment, including the operation of the extensive Millar's private railway network. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dbf5b27d-dbb7-4008-a6b6-896088bd3660" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yarloop, established in 1894, is a town with a rich history rooted in the timber industry. Originally a timber milling town, it became known for its steam workshops which housed machinery for the timber industry. The name is thought to be derived from the local Bindjareb Aboriginal language, though an alternative theory suggests it comes from the term "yard loop" related to the railway. The town was significantly impacted by the 2016 bushfires, which destroyed many buildings, including the historic workshops. Efforts are underway to preserve its heritage.

This Adventure Lab is only a small portion of the Yarloop Heritage Trail.
more information can be obtained here:
 https://trailswa.com.au/trails/trail/yarloop-heritage-walk-trail&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
