   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Yarloop History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Yarloop Town Hall&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What did the main hall have?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/05ea28ec-e3b8-4675-9d8a-c18ed7690eb0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Yarloop Town Hall, originally built in 1938, was a significant landmark in the timber town of Yarloop, Western Australia. It was designed by Powell, Cameron and Chisholm, the same architectural firm that designed the Brunswick and Harvey Town Halls. The hall was known for its Art Deco facade and was a hub for community events, including dances, balls, and other gatherings. Sadly, the hall was severely damaged by the devastating 2016 bushfires, with only the facade and a fireplace remaining. However, the Yarloop Community Resource Centre, which now stands on the site, incorporates the surviving facade and fireplace, preserving a piece of the town's history. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dbf5b27d-dbb7-4008-a6b6-896088bd3660" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yarloop, established in 1894, is a town with a rich history rooted in the timber industry. Originally a timber milling town, it became known for its steam workshops which housed machinery for the timber industry. The name is thought to be derived from the local Bindjareb Aboriginal language, though an alternative theory suggests it comes from the term "yard loop" related to the railway. The town was significantly impacted by the 2016 bushfires, which destroyed many buildings, including the historic workshops. Efforts are underway to preserve its heritage.

This Adventure Lab is only a small portion of the Yarloop Heritage Trail.
more information can be obtained here:
 https://trailswa.com.au/trails/trail/yarloop-heritage-walk-trail&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.957667" lon="115.900733">
  <ele>32.0</ele>
  <time>2025-08-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBc884404</name>
  <desc>YarloopHistory4</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844</url>
  <urlname>Yarloop History : S4 Nobs Hill - Snobs Hill by dezzabills</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="-2038" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Yarloop History : S4 Nobs Hill - Snobs Hill</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>dezzabills</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">dezzabills</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Yarloop History&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Nobs Hill - Snobs Hill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/3dc60093-f51d-4fb1-aac6-cc2ec13c8844&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;what was "The Cottege" built for?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/2c024c5c-0adc-405e-b8fd-f0a45d4020b9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Nobs Hill (also known as Snob’s Hill) was the colloquial name for a group of houses in Yarloop, which included the Millars’ Timber and Trading Co Manager’s house built in 1906 for Mr R Driver.
The Mill Manager’s house, located on the rise overlooking the town and Yarloop Workshops, sat next to The Cottage, which provided accommodation for personnel from Perth or distinguished visitors. A housekeeper and her husband, who usually worked for Millars, lived on the premises.

Over time, they added several houses to Nobs Hill, which were considerably larger than the workers’ cottages and had higher ceilings, more amenities and were better finished.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dbf5b27d-dbb7-4008-a6b6-896088bd3660" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yarloop, established in 1894, is a town with a rich history rooted in the timber industry. Originally a timber milling town, it became known for its steam workshops which housed machinery for the timber industry. The name is thought to be derived from the local Bindjareb Aboriginal language, though an alternative theory suggests it comes from the term "yard loop" related to the railway. The town was significantly impacted by the 2016 bushfires, which destroyed many buildings, including the historic workshops. Efforts are underway to preserve its heritage.

This Adventure Lab is only a small portion of the Yarloop Heritage Trail.
more information can be obtained here:
 https://trailswa.com.au/trails/trail/yarloop-heritage-walk-trail&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-32.957733" lon="115.899217">
  <ele>26.0</ele>
