   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Mill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;The Old Mill&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Governor James Stirling was invited to lay the foundation stone in what year?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/80295cf1-bc8e-4e68-afa3-9b82a976fa7b" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill and Mill Cottage are among the state's oldest surviving colonial buildings.
William Shenton had a wooden mill built in 1833 but soon replaced it with the limestone structure you see today.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea95cd61-6612-4848-8c28-ace95570ce73" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill in South Perth is one of Perth’s best-known landmarks. Built in 1835 and operating until 1859, the mill produced 680kg of flour per day at its peak. Since ceasing production, the site has been used as a hotel, a residence, a wine saloon and a poultry farm.

In 1992, the Old Mill was granted to the City of South Perth to commemorate the centenary of the founding of Local Government in South Perth (1892). The stone buildings and site were repaired and upgraded and were then vested in the National Trust.

The site has local and state cultural heritage significance due to the Old Mill being the oldest surviving colonial structure in the Perth area.

There is a Bonus Box for this adventure lab - GCBBMPX which is located within a stage of this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.966233" lon="115.8469">
  <ele>2.0</ele>
  <time>2025-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB80a7902</name>
  <desc>OldMillS2Willia</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79</url>
  <urlname>Old Mill : S2 William Shenton by crabzy11</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000202" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old Mill : S2 William Shenton</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>crabzy11</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">crabzy11</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Mill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;William Shenton&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was Shenton granted the lease?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4c958ea4-d76f-41db-af45-742eeeae99e1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;William Shenton arrived in the Swan River Colony in 1829 and established its first flourmill in Fremantle. This operation relied on supplies of imported grain, but soon an alternative source became available - crops grown inland, in the vicinity of Guildford.

In the absence of roads extending inland, the river was the settlement's main cargo route. Conveying the grain to Fremantle imposed heavy transport costs upon growers, so Shenton initially sought government assistance to relocate his milling business to Guildford. This didn't transpire, but instead he was granted a lease to establish his operation here in South Perth.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea95cd61-6612-4848-8c28-ace95570ce73" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill in South Perth is one of Perth’s best-known landmarks. Built in 1835 and operating until 1859, the mill produced 680kg of flour per day at its peak. Since ceasing production, the site has been used as a hotel, a residence, a wine saloon and a poultry farm.

In 1992, the Old Mill was granted to the City of South Perth to commemorate the centenary of the founding of Local Government in South Perth (1892). The stone buildings and site were repaired and upgraded and were then vested in the National Trust.

The site has local and state cultural heritage significance due to the Old Mill being the oldest surviving colonial structure in the Perth area.

There is a Bonus Box for this adventure lab - GCBBMPX which is located within a stage of this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.96605" lon="115.847083">
  <ele>5.0</ele>
  <time>2025-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB80a7905</name>
  <desc>OldMillS5Miller</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79</url>
  <urlname>Old Mill : S5 Miller’s Pool by crabzy11</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000208" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old Mill : S5 Miller’s Pool</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>crabzy11</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">crabzy11</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Mill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Miller’s Pool&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;In what year was Miller’s Pool reconstructed &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6d1da1e9-581b-4447-b285-3a1bf444960a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On the eastern side of Shenton's mill, a shallow water basin became known as Miller's Pool. Opening into the river, it was accessible by small boats known as 'flats', transporting sacks of grain from the Guildford area or Fremantle.

Transferring grain from these boats to a storage area and then into the mill itself was backbreaking work. In 1837, Shenton orchestrated the construction of a 45-metre channel or spur from the edge of Miller's Pool, enabling sacks to be relayed directly to the mill.

&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea95cd61-6612-4848-8c28-ace95570ce73" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill in South Perth is one of Perth’s best-known landmarks. Built in 1835 and operating until 1859, the mill produced 680kg of flour per day at its peak. Since ceasing production, the site has been used as a hotel, a residence, a wine saloon and a poultry farm.

In 1992, the Old Mill was granted to the City of South Perth to commemorate the centenary of the founding of Local Government in South Perth (1892). The stone buildings and site were repaired and upgraded and were then vested in the National Trust.

The site has local and state cultural heritage significance due to the Old Mill being the oldest surviving colonial structure in the Perth area.

There is a Bonus Box for this adventure lab - GCBBMPX which is located within a stage of this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.966083" lon="115.846933">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
