  <name>LB80a7903</name>
  <desc>OldMillS3Miller</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79</url>
  <urlname>Old Mill : S3 Miller’s Cottage by crabzy11</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000204" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old Mill : S3 Miller’s Cottage</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>crabzy11</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">crabzy11</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Mill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Miller’s Cottage&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;“A humble home turned haven for art, law, and ____ __ ___ _____”&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3aad05ba-4f97-47c7-ae75-bdd8671a6089" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With the mill completed, William Shenton turned his attention to accommodation for the resident miller. A simple affair, the miller's cottage was soon completed in 1836.

After the decline of the mill, the cottage saw many uses, such as a meeting place for the Wilgie Sketch Club (which later became the West Australian Society of Arts - the oldest art society in WA).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea95cd61-6612-4848-8c28-ace95570ce73" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill in South Perth is one of Perth’s best-known landmarks. Built in 1835 and operating until 1859, the mill produced 680kg of flour per day at its peak. Since ceasing production, the site has been used as a hotel, a residence, a wine saloon and a poultry farm.

In 1992, the Old Mill was granted to the City of South Perth to commemorate the centenary of the founding of Local Government in South Perth (1892). The stone buildings and site were repaired and upgraded and were then vested in the National Trust.

The site has local and state cultural heritage significance due to the Old Mill being the oldest surviving colonial structure in the Perth area.

There is a Bonus Box for this adventure lab - GCBBMPX which is located within a stage of this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.9661" lon="115.846983">
  <ele>3.0</ele>
  <time>2025-08-23T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LB80a7904</name>
  <desc>OldMillS4Feedin</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79</url>
  <urlname>Old Mill : S4 Feeding The Colony by crabzy11</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99000206" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Old Mill : S4 Feeding The Colony</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>crabzy11</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">crabzy11</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Old Mill&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Feeding The Colony&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/7cdb1440-9a7f-4c31-b2b9-ff591dd80a79&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many bushels of wheat was the mill grinding per day on 1837?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ca198ad6-7dc5-4626-88fa-80365106bda9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The infant Swan River Colony was heavily dependent on flour as a food source, which could only be obtained by grinding grain. Initially, this tedious job had to be done by hand.
To operate his mill, William Shenton hired experienced miller, William Steele.

The mill's rotating sails harnessed the wind to powerits rotary quern. This comprised two circular stones, with one fixed and the other rotating above it. Grain was poured through a central hole known as the "swallow' or ‘eye’ of the quern.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ea95cd61-6612-4848-8c28-ace95570ce73" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Old Mill in South Perth is one of Perth’s best-known landmarks. Built in 1835 and operating until 1859, the mill produced 680kg of flour per day at its peak. Since ceasing production, the site has been used as a hotel, a residence, a wine saloon and a poultry farm.

In 1992, the Old Mill was granted to the City of South Perth to commemorate the centenary of the founding of Local Government in South Perth (1892). The stone buildings and site were repaired and upgraded and were then vested in the National Trust.

The site has local and state cultural heritage significance due to the Old Mill being the oldest surviving colonial structure in the Perth area.

There is a Bonus Box for this adventure lab - GCBBMPX which is located within a stage of this Adventure Lab&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.899167" lon="115.97065">
