   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wrights Lake - Champion Lakes&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Champion Lakes Boating Club&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/eb2ff215-10c1-45a4-844c-021ee15d8cd2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/eb2ff215-10c1-45a4-844c-021ee15d8cd2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number on the electrical cabinet? &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/27dfa72c-a32f-43bf-949c-f29b958314be" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 2009 Champion Lakes Boating Club made its first appearance in competitive regattas. The clubs 4 inaugural racers were Jason Steven, Huntz Theime, Daniel Foucar and Pamela Pearson. 
With only 4 competitors, CLBC was destined to finish last, but it’s original 4 showed promising signs for the future. 
Daniel won the clubs first state title in the single sculls at the state sprint championships. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/97f243f0-989c-4a11-8524-4e2ef5d8f03a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wrights Lake in Kelmscott was named for an early settler Charles Wright, who received the land grant in the 1830’s. The lake was originally a natural drainage line and timber reserve.
The lake and it’s surrounding parkland were developed into an aquatic facility with an international rowing course, with the master plan endorsed in 2000 and completed after the Tonkin Highway extension in 2005. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
