   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Wrights Lake - Champion Lakes&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Just to the first marker&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/eb2ff215-10c1-45a4-844c-021ee15d8cd2"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/eb2ff215-10c1-45a4-844c-021ee15d8cd2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the number on the electrical cabinet?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/05d88bc1-fc25-4bdb-b46e-ba3a503fa159" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The international-standard rowing and water sports venue was opened in 2009. Construction of the A$30 million centre involved the development of a large lake, a 2000m buoyed course and surrounding infrastructure for rowing, canoeing, dragon boating and triathlons. The lake is surrounded by a 5km walking path. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/97f243f0-989c-4a11-8524-4e2ef5d8f03a" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wrights Lake in Kelmscott was named for an early settler Charles Wright, who received the land grant in the 1830’s. The lake was originally a natural drainage line and timber reserve.
The lake and it’s surrounding parkland were developed into an aquatic facility with an international rowing course, with the master plan endorsed in 2000 and completed after the Tonkin Highway extension in 2005. &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
