  <desc>TimeZoneS1AirHo</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/08fb7129-d063-47c1-9928-7adca040a82f</url>
  <urlname>Time Zone : S1 Air Hockey by SirFancy</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="99002436" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Time Zone : S1 Air Hockey</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>SirFancy</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">SirFancy</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Time Zone&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Air Hockey&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/08fb7129-d063-47c1-9928-7adca040a82f"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/08fb7129-d063-47c1-9928-7adca040a82f&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Radius: 500m&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kind of question: single&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name brand of the air hockey located in this store? (upper case letters)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f8dea259-d1be-4412-a86b-dd5632395486" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Invention of Air Hockey:

Air hockey was invented in the late 1960s by a group of engineers named Bob Lemieux, Phil Crossman, and Brad Baldwin. The engineers were working on a project to create a game that could simulate ice hockey without the need for ice. They developed a prototype of the game, which they called "Puck Bowler," and it quickly gained popularity among players.

The game&amp;#039;s popularity grew rapidly, and the engineers decided to rename it to "Air Hockey" and sell the game to arcade manufacturers. The first commercial air hockey table was produced in 1972 by the Brunswick Corporation, and it quickly became a hit with arcade-goers. By the mid-1970s, air hockey had become a staple of arcades across America.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/143e0ba2-4982-42ec-baf3-4a5ce73370d0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This Location is open and free to enter during most week days from 9am to 10pm.

In 1958, Malcolm Steinberg&amp;#039;s father gave him 21 pinball machines. In Perth, the 18-year-old struck deals to place the pinballs in fish and chip shops and milk bars in exchange for half the earnings.[1]

Steinberg&amp;#039;s company Leisure &amp;amp; Allied Industries (LAI), which manufactured and imported arcade games, entered the video game retail market in the early 1970s with its first two stores, Blue Pumpernickel and Crystal Palace. In 1978, Steinberg opened the first Timezone — his first arcade — on Murray Street, Perth. He also relaunched the stores under the new brand. Timezone was successful, reaching a peak of eight stores in Western Australia.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
