   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">&lt;h3&gt;Geocaching Muesum Adventure Lab&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Gallery 2: Mystery / Puzzle Cache&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/668319d0-fa9b-40a0-8c2e-c8bc1c557771"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/668319d0-fa9b-40a0-8c2e-c8bc1c557771&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Radius: 200m&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kind of question: multiple choice&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is another common name for a Mystery Cache?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/a1fc9ed9-bde6-4769-a6b4-5bc57b33afb8" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This gallery is filled with riddles, codes, and puzzles.

Mystery (or Puzzle) Caches require solving something before you can find the final.

WA Example: Noongar Boodjar #5 in the Southwest asks you to solve a crossword about local Indigenous language groups before heading to the final location.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Descriptions&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b1d01425-2f5c-430d-9ec8-61bcf97c9cfc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Geocaching Museum! Across ten galleries, you'll learn about the different cache types that geocachers can hide or find. Each gallery features an example from Western Australia, connecting global cache types with local flavour. After each exhibit, you'll answer a quick question to test your knowledge.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
