   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Penguin Island&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Resort Restoration&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9c015f6d-980f-4f74-bb94-6512dbb5d8ed" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/9c015f6d-980f-4f74-bb94-6512dbb5d8ed&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What year did the resort close&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/acfb08b3-b7a4-4a30-ac72-90b5bcddc1dd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Seaforth Mckenzie made it his
home from 1914 to 1926, drawing many guests and becoming a local celebrity. In 1954, the Gill family built a resort on the island for 100 guests and ferried visitors from the mainland on ex-World War Two amphibious Army Ducks.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
