                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Discovering Dinosaurs&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Pterodactylus&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fc4300c3-f706-480d-8bf1-cc5234fa7959" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/fc4300c3-f706-480d-8bf1-cc5234fa7959&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Find the false fact:&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/c340f7c7-84d7-443e-bc5b-d5b0b4240cbc" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;There’s no such thing as a Pterodactyl. 

It&amp;#039;s unclear at what point "pterodactyl" became the pop-culture synonym for pterosaurs in general but palaeontologists never use the term "pterodactyl," instead of focusing on individual pterosaur genera such as Pterodactylus or Pteranodon. 

Pterodactylus translates from Ancient Greek to ‘winged finger’ whilst Pteranodon means winged and toothless. &lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/e9d84869-fc11-4f30-bb1d-fef849ce40c2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Dinosaurs are a diverse group of reptiles that were the dominant terrestrial life form on Earth during the Mesozoic Era, about 245 million years ago. Dinosaurs went into decline near the end of the Cretaceous Period, about 66 million years ago. 

The name dinosaur comes from the Greek words deinos (“terrible” or “fearfully great”) and sauros (“reptile” or “lizard”). 

Test your knowledge of dinosaurs with these fun and quirky facts! &lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
