 <wpt lat="-31.9555" lon="115.864367">
  <ele>31.0</ele>
  <time>2026-02-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQN5R-5</name>
  <desc>ArtWalkPerth115</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3</url>
  <urlname>Art Walk Perth 1 : S5 Westin Face by AndrewB1973</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="9705558" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Art Walk Perth 1 : S5 Westin Face</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AndrewB1973</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AndrewB1973</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Art Walk Perth 1&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Westin Face&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 50m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;The top of her shoulder starts on how many panels down on the right hand side?&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/0fef2d24-ebb0-49c9-97d5-3873ccd65d91" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Artist: Rone     
Instagram: @r_o_n_e                                                                                      
Finding the friction point between beauty and decay is a thread that runs through much of Rone’s work. As a street artist best known for his haunting, stylised images of women’s faces, he understands better than most that beauty can be fleeting. Seeing his artworks gradually worn away by natural and human elements has taught him to appreciate the unexpected beauty of an image as it begins to blend back into its more prosaic surroundings. Rone has gone from spearheading Melbourne’s fledgling street art movement in the early 2000s, as a member of the Everfresh crew, to being a celebrated fixture on the international street art scene. An inveterate traveller, his distinctive female muses have followed him around the world and can be found – in various states of decay – peering out from beneath overpasses and emblazoned on walls everywhere from New York to New Zealand and many places in between. These days, Rone’s work is found as often in galleries as it is on the streets. His work has been acquisitioned by the National Gallery of Australia, commissioned by the National Gallery of Victoria to work with Jean Paul Gaultier and shown by galleries including Stolen Space in London, Urban Nation in Berlin and Allouche Gallery in New York.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dfc9eecf-12e7-4ca9-b228-8ef353526a4d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Walking around the City of Perth, you’re likely to stumble across a colourful mural or be captivated by some street art. 

As our urban art scene evolves, new works are appearing across the city, adding vibrancy to the streets. Some of the works are ephemeral, which means they may only last for a short time before being painted over or replaced with something new. 

The City has gained international attention in recent years, with works from some of the world’s best street artists gracing its buildings and lining laneways.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.95505" lon="115.86275">
  <ele>29.0</ele>
  <time>2026-02-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQN5R-4</name>
  <desc>ArtWalkPerth114</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3</url>
  <urlname>Art Walk Perth 1 : S4 Neon by AndrewB1973</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="9705557" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Art Walk Perth 1 : S4 Neon</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AndrewB1973</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AndrewB1973</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Art Walk Perth 1&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Neon&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 50m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is written on Snoopy’s shirt?&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f3182538-a4c4-461a-a292-ec76b70762bb" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Artist: Drew Straker
Instagram: @muralist
Straker had an eye for visual design from an early age and as he grew older he dedicated a lot of his time to skateboarding with his friends. As he explored the streets of Perth, he became more familiar with street art, one day he decided to pick up a spray can and try it for himself. Straker believes that “Graffiti art creates a window of opportunity to youth that would not otherwise have connection with paintings, let alone the world around them, graffiti provided me with the fundamentals of mural painting and incidentally forced me and my friends to become avid photographers". Straker generally works in a handful of styles sometimes with sentiment, sometimes for show, and occasionally to shock. Straker&amp;#039;s unique murals appear to light up streets like neon signs, they’re created using only spray paint and a ‘neon glow’ technique (white lines under a transparent colour) his work can be seen in hundreds of locations all over Western Australia. Straker is very open with his love for Perth and has enjoyed seeing it evolve even with its “Build and destroy mentality”.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dfc9eecf-12e7-4ca9-b228-8ef353526a4d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Walking around the City of Perth, you’re likely to stumble across a colourful mural or be captivated by some street art. 

As our urban art scene evolves, new works are appearing across the city, adding vibrancy to the streets. Some of the works are ephemeral, which means they may only last for a short time before being painted over or replaced with something new. 

The City has gained international attention in recent years, with works from some of the world’s best street artists gracing its buildings and lining laneways.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-31.955033" lon="115.8625">
  <ele>25.0</ele>
  <time>2026-02-12T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBQN5R-2</name>
  <desc>ArtWalkPerth112</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3</url>
  <urlname>Art Walk Perth 1 : S2 The Pier Street Trees by AndrewB1973</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="9705555" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Art Walk Perth 1 : S2 The Pier Street Trees</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>AndrewB1973</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">AndrewB1973</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Art Walk Perth 1&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;The Pier Street Trees&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/6f0f5829-16b3-451d-afef-a3142c6c13d3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 50m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;How many trees are in the mural?&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/943b787a-bce9-4467-8770-3c78740a969d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Artist: Joanna Brown 
Instagram: @laelinestudio
Designed as a cooling, pleasing, lush ‘jewel-like’ respite from the urban environment it aims to promote wellbeing and lift the spirit. The mural also hints at many shared references from the surrounding natural and built environment.
The plus symbol (+) is similar to the first aid symbol, denoting the healing and cooling effect of trees. The repeated square motif reflects the decorative features found on the facades of surrounding buildings and makes abstract reference to urban street blocks and the formality of nearby Cathedral Square.
The colour palette draws on the blue of Jon Tarry’s ‘Cygna’ sculpture at the corner of Hay and Pier Street and incorporates the green, grey tones of our native Eucalyptus lane-poolei (Salmon White Gum). These trees are depicted in the mural and planted along Hay Street.
The artwork is warmer at the top (orange elements) and cooler at the bottom (blue). The colours are cooled as they travel from top to bottom through the centre of the tree, reflecting the importance of trees in cooling and shading our city.
In the words of the artist the mural is ultimately ‘conceived as a celebration of trees in support of the goals of the WA Tree Festival’.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/dfc9eecf-12e7-4ca9-b228-8ef353526a4d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Walking around the City of Perth, you’re likely to stumble across a colourful mural or be captivated by some street art. 

As our urban art scene evolves, new works are appearing across the city, adding vibrancy to the streets. Some of the works are ephemeral, which means they may only last for a short time before being painted over or replaced with something new. 

The City has gained international attention in recent years, with works from some of the world’s best street artists gracing its buildings and lining laneways.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
