   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;North Perth Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;North Perth Primary School &lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b2844e9f-1136-4866-aacd-08282acd3d0e" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b2844e9f-1136-4866-aacd-08282acd3d0e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    single
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;WHAT were Edmund E. Jarvis and John R. Hall? (Two words)&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/9f5ec90f-5abd-448f-b616-dec02ece45ee" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;North Perth Primary School, one of Western Australia’s longest continually operating public schools, was established on 30 January 1899 as the North Perth District School (originally known as Woodville School) to serve the rapidly growing residential community following Perth’s late-19th-century expansion. Built on land purchased in 1897 in the Toorak Estate, the original brick classrooms soon proved too small as the suburb’s population surged, and by 1921 it was the largest primary school in the state, prompting the addition of new classrooms and the separate Infant School in 1922. The school’s buildings, significant examples of early Public Works Department design, have been largely retained and were added to the State Register of Heritage Places in 2005;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4ca77558-4821-4026-870a-224310403b71" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;North Perth developed from low-lying seasonal wetlands significant to the Whadjuk Noongar people into farmland and market gardens in the mid-19th century before rapid residential growth during the WA gold boom of the 1890s. Land was subdivided under enticing names like Toorak and Woodville around 1895–1901, and the area was officially named North Perth in 1901; its early civic infrastructure included the North Perth Primary School (opened 1899), North Perth Town Hall (1902/1910), churches, shops and pubs, aided by tram connections to the city. North Perth briefly operated as its own municipality from 1901 until amalgamation into the City of Perth in 1914, and continued expansion between the world wars brought local businesses and community facilities. Post-World War II migration diversified the population, with European and later Asian communities contributing to its cultural tapestry, and today the suburb is appreciated for its heritage architecture, village-style streets and close-in urban living.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
