   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;North Perth Heritage Buildings&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Church of Saint Nikola&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b2844e9f-1136-4866-aacd-08282acd3d0e" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b2844e9f-1136-4866-aacd-08282acd3d0e&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    single
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the HOUSE NUMBER on the front wall of the Hall located at the rear of the main Church building? (2 digits)&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/f68e58a3-f5ca-4b18-8dfa-56161893247d" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;The Macedonian Orthodox Church of St Nikola has been a central institution for the Macedonian-Australian community in North Perth since the late 1960s, reflecting decades of cultural, spiritual and social life. An initial church was opened in 1968–69 on land associated with the Macedonian Community Centre, consecrated by senior clergy as a place where new migrants could worship in their own tradition and language and celebrate baptisms, weddings and national feasts. In the 1970s a split within the community led to the purchase of a former Methodist church on Angove Street, which was rededicated as St Nikola; subsequently, in the 1990s the community acquired land on Macedonia Place and built the present church facility, officially opened in February 1996, funded largely through donations and volunteer effort. The church remains active within the Macedonian Orthodox Diocese of Australia, serving both religious and cultural needs and anchoring the identity of Macedonian migrants and their descendants in Western Australia&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/4ca77558-4821-4026-870a-224310403b71" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;North Perth developed from low-lying seasonal wetlands significant to the Whadjuk Noongar people into farmland and market gardens in the mid-19th century before rapid residential growth during the WA gold boom of the 1890s. Land was subdivided under enticing names like Toorak and Woodville around 1895–1901, and the area was officially named North Perth in 1901; its early civic infrastructure included the North Perth Primary School (opened 1899), North Perth Town Hall (1902/1910), churches, shops and pubs, aided by tram connections to the city. North Perth briefly operated as its own municipality from 1901 until amalgamation into the City of Perth in 1914, and continued expansion between the world wars brought local businesses and community facilities. Post-World War II migration diversified the population, with European and later Asian communities contributing to its cultural tapestry, and today the suburb is appreciated for its heritage architecture, village-style streets and close-in urban living.&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
