  <urlname>Katanning History : S3 First Electric Street Lights by monkeywand</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="9731652" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>Katanning History : S3 First Electric Street Lights</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>monkeywand</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">monkeywand</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Katanning History&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;First Electric Street Lights&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e192b975-a5e3-486f-b4ab-976c6aadacca" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/e192b975-a5e3-486f-b4ab-976c6aadacca&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    single
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Of whom is the statue across the street? (Full name only)&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/3e9409a6-3063-4fe8-acb7-5ecc725d0b74" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;In 1891, Katanning made history by being the first town in Western Australia to have electric street lights. 

Although taken over by the by the State Electricity Commission in 1950, the Flour Mill continued to provide the town’s electricity supply until 1959 when it was taken over by the Western Australian State Government. Today, Katanning is part of the state electrical grid with electricity being generated at Muja near Collie and carried by wires around the southern half of the state. &lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/436aad31-0d35-472b-afed-92e8da76e4ac" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;KAAT-NYINY is a local Noongar word meaning the head sits here. It comes from the Dreamtime story of Mulka, a giant man whose body parts are embedded in country throughout the Great Southern Region.

Katanning was the junction of three Aboriginal tribal grounds long before European settlement. The first Europeans to explore the Katanning area were Governor James Stirling and Surveyor General John Septimus Roe who travelled through the area in 1835 en route from Perth to Albany.  

Katanning is located in the Upper Great Southern and is at the heart of one of the most productive agricultural regions in Western Australia.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
