 <wpt lat="-31.908183" lon="116.152967">
  <ele>321.0</ele>
  <time>2026-05-24T08:00:00Z</time>
  <name>LBT22F-5</name>
  <desc>NuttyWanderS5Sp</desc>
  <url>https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b5965fce-4f7d-4630-a94f-71aff724ecf6</url>
  <urlname>A Nutty Wander : S5 Spiky by 15Moonbeams</urlname>
  <sym>Geocache</sym>
  <type>Geocache|Lab Cache</type>
  <groundspeak:cache id="9746779" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
   <groundspeak:name>A Nutty Wander : S5 Spiky</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>15Moonbeams</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">15Moonbeams</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country></groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;A Nutty Wander&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Spiky&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b5965fce-4f7d-4630-a94f-71aff724ecf6" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/b5965fce-4f7d-4630-a94f-71aff724ecf6&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    multiple choice
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;What is the name of these unique plants?&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/43c1c1e9-89c0-4d3e-ab21-216105c0d0c9" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Dotted through the bush around here are striking plants with black trunks and long, thin foliage at the top, collectively referred to as its crown or thatch. As these narrow, wiry leaves die, they don&amp;#039;t fall off; instead, they hang down around the trunk, forming a thick protective layer known as a skirt. The spikes are botanically called flower spikes (or "spears"). They are made up of a long stem (scape) and a dense flowering section packed with thousands of tiny white or cream flowers. They grow in spring and summer, and will often rapidly shoot up (sometimes up to several centimetres a day).&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/b900aede-bd77-4645-9e96-7b6c207a7877" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Take a wander through this beautiful part of Mundaring, enjoy the bush and maybe find a nutty cache or two along the way. (Remember to bring a pen with you!)
If you have time, you can continue your wander along a network of tracks.
If you&amp;#039;re lucky (and quietly patient), you might see and hear some local wildlife.
Enjoy!&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
