   <groundspeak:name>Bullara Station : S5 Shearer’s Lodge</groundspeak:name>
   <groundspeak:placed_by>richlink</groundspeak:placed_by>
   <groundspeak:owner id="">richlink</groundspeak:owner>
   <groundspeak:type>Lab Cache</groundspeak:type>
   <groundspeak:container>Virtual</groundspeak:container>
   <groundspeak:attributes>
   </groundspeak:attributes>
   <groundspeak:difficulty>1</groundspeak:difficulty>
   <groundspeak:terrain>1</groundspeak:terrain>
   <groundspeak:country>Australia</groundspeak:country>
   <groundspeak:state>Western Australia</groundspeak:state>
   <groundspeak:short_description html="True"> </groundspeak:short_description>
   <groundspeak:long_description html="True">
                                                                                                                                        &lt;h3&gt;Bullara Station&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;h4&gt;Shearer’s Lodge&lt;/h4&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2181d775-d5e2-4ef0-ade8-4199b0d7c4aa" target="_blank"&gt;https://labs.geocaching.com/goto/2181d775-d5e2-4ef0-ade8-4199b0d7c4aa&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Radius: 20m&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Stages: 5&lt;/p&gt;


    &lt;p&gt;Kind of question:
                    single
            &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Question:&lt;br /&gt;Observing the sheep mural - what is the first name of the artist? (5 letters)&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Waypoint Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/ce8b9252-7008-4b6a-879d-75a31149c6f4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;From the 1920’s -  Merino were run for wool production from the historic shearing sheds and quarters.
With the downturn in wool prices in the 1990’s, the Damara breed of sheep were introduced as they were resilient to the outback challenges and were farmed for meat.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;hr /&gt;
    &lt;h5&gt;Adventure Lab Description&lt;/h5&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://gsmediadata.blob.core.windows.net/mediacontainer/6ae2ef1e-4cae-409f-80af-5203b35cf2dd" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;PLEASE NOTE;
This adventure is only accessible when Bullara is open to the public from 1st April to mid-October each year.

Bullara Station has been a family run working cattle property since the 1950&amp;#039;s. It is the culmination of a dream to share the experience of life on a cattle station in an authentic, environmentally sustainable and comfortable way. It has stemmed from a passion for the red dirt and connecting with adventurous travellers.
The station is a must see, either just a day trip to check out the cafe and its amazing menu or a longer stay to appreciate the station.
&lt;/p&gt;</groundspeak:long_description>
    <groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
   <groundspeak:logs>
  </groundspeak:logs>
   <groundspeak:travelbugs>
   </groundspeak:travelbugs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>
 <wpt lat="-22.681267" lon="114.039483">
